const defaultQuestionBank = [{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The core of a transformer is laminated primarily to reduce which loss?","options":["Copper Loss","Hysteresis Loss","Eddy Current Loss","Friction Loss"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Laminations break conductive paths, effectively reducing circulating eddy current loops."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The synchronous speed (Ns) of a 4-pole induction motor at 50 Hz supply is:","options":["1000 RPM","1500 RPM","3000 RPM","750 RPM"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ns = 120 * f / P = (120 * 50) / 4 = 1500 RPM."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an alternator operating at leading power factor, the effect of armature reaction is:","options":["Purely demagnetizing","Purely cross-magnetizing","Purely magnetizing","Both demagnetizing and cross-magnetizing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At leading power factor, the armature flux is in direct phase assistance with the main rotor pole flux, causing pure magnetization."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 50 Hz induction motor has a full-load slip of 4%. If rotor resistance is doubled, the maximum torque developed will:","options":["Be doubled","Be halved","Remain unchanged","Increase by 40%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maximum torque (Tmax) of an induction motor is independent of rotor circuit resistance; only the slip at which Tmax occurs shifts."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Corona discharge in extra-high voltage transmission lines can be minimized by:","options":["Solid copper wire","Bundled conductors","Thin conductors","Increasing frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bundled conductors effectively increase cross-sectional equivalent radius, mitigating electric stress."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Ferranti effect on long transmission lines occurs when the line is:","options":["Heavily loaded","Operating at zero lagging pf","Lightly loaded or on open circuit","Short-circuited at receiving end"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Due to line charging capacitance, receiving-end voltage exceeds sending-end voltage under light-load conditions."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a lossless transmission line having surge impedance Zc = 400 Ω operating at 400 kV, its Surge Impedance Loading (SIL) is:","options":["100 MW","200 MW","400 MW","1600 MW"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SIL = V_L² / Zc = (400 × 10³)² / 400 = 160,000 / 400 = 400 MW."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The breaking capacity of a high-voltage circuit breaker is conventionally expressed in:","options":["kVA","kW","MVA","Amperes only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rated breaking capacity is expressed in symmetrical short-circuit MVA (√3 * V * I)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Which distance relay is preferred for the protection of very long EHV transmission lines?","options":["Mho relay","Impedance relay","Reactance relay","Plain overcurrent relay"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mho relays (admittance relays) are least affected by power swings, making them ideal for long lines."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the power factor of a purely capacitive AC circuit?","options":["Zero Lagging","Unity (1.0)","Zero Leading","0.8 Leading"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Current leads voltage by 90° in a pure capacitor, giving power factor = 0 leading."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a series RLC circuit with R = 10 Ω, L = 0.1 H, and C = 10 µF, the Quality Factor (Q) at resonance is:","options":["10","100","5","50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Q = (1/R) * √(L/C) = (1/10) * √(0.1 / 10⁻⁵) = (1/10) * √(10,000) = 100 / 10 = 10."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a linear time-invariant system to be bounded-input bounded-output stable, all poles must lie:","options":["On the imaginary axis","In the left-half of s-plane","In the right-half of s-plane","At infinity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Roots in the left-half of the s-plane yield decaying exponential transients."},{"subject":"Measurements & Instrumentation","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Permanent Magnet Moving Coil (PMMC) instrument can measure:","options":["DC quantities only","AC quantities only","Both AC and DC","Pulsating AC only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PMMC deflects proportional to average value, which is strictly zero for symmetrical AC."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Silicon Controlled Rectifier (SCR) turns OFF naturally when:","options":["Gate pulse is removed","Anode current drops below holding current","Gate voltage is inverted","Supply voltage doubles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Once fired, the gate loses control; turning it off requires anode current to drop below holding current (Ih)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a DC generator, the generated EMF is dynamically induced according to:","options":["Fleming's Right-Hand Rule","Fleming's Left-Hand Rule","Faraday's Law & Fleming's Right-Hand Rule","Ampere's Circuital Law"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Magnitude is given by Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction, and direction is determined by Fleming's Right-Hand Rule."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At standstill (starting condition), the slip of a 3-phase induction motor is:","options":["Zero","0.5","1.0","Infinity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At standstill, rotor speed Nr = 0. Therefore, slip s = (Ns - Nr) / Ns = (Ns - 0) / Ns = 1.0."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary function of a commutator in a DC generator is to:","options":["Convert DC to AC","Convert induced AC in armature to DC in external circuit","Reduce armature reaction","Increase magnetic flux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Armature conductors always generate alternating current (AC). The commutator acts as a mechanical rectifier converting AC to DC."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"To control the speed of a DC shunt motor above its rated (base) speed, which method is used?","options":["Armature resistance control","Field flux control","Armature voltage control (Ward-Leonard)","Tapped field control"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Speed N is inversely proportional to field flux (N ∝ Eb / Φ). By weakening the field flux, speed rises above rated speed."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A transformer core is constructed using high-grade silicon steel primarily to:","options":["Reduce eddy current loss","Reduce hysteresis loss","Eliminate copper loss","Improve mechanical strength"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Silicon steel possesses high magnetic permeability and a narrow hysteresis loop, significantly reducing hysteresis loss."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The speed of an alternator running at 50 Hz with 2 poles is:","options":["1500 RPM","3000 RPM","1000 RPM","750 RPM"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ns = 120 * f / P = (120 * 50) / 2 = 3000 RPM."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which test on a transformer is performed with rated current circulating at reduced voltage?","options":["Open-circuit test","Short-circuit test","Back-to-back (Sumpner's) test","Insulation resistance test"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SC test is performed at low voltage (typically 5-10% of rated voltage) to circulate full rated current and measure full-load copper losses."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Swinburne's test cannot be performed on which of the following machines?","options":["DC Shunt Motor","DC Compound Motor","DC Series Motor","DC Shunt Generator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Swinburne's test is a no-load test. A DC series motor can never be started on no-load because it develops dangerously high speed."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an autotransformer with transformation ratio k (k = V2/V1 < 1), the fraction of power transferred inductively is:","options":["k","(1 - k)","(1 + k)","k / (1 - k)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In an autotransformer, power transferred conductively is k * Total Power, and power transferred inductively is (1 - k) * Total Power."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"V-curves of a synchronous motor demonstrate the relationship between:","options":["Armature current and power factor","Armature current and field current","Field current and power factor","Torque and speed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"V-curves plot Armature Current (Ia) versus DC Field Current (If). Inverted V-curves plot Power Factor (cos Φ) versus Field Current."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Cogging in 3-phase squirrel cage induction motors occurs when:","options":["Number of stator slots equals number of rotor slots","Supply voltage drops by 50%","Motor is started on full load","Rotor bars are heavily skewed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the number of stator slots is equal to or an integral multiple of rotor slots, magnetic locking (cogging) occurs, preventing rotation."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A synchronous condenser is essentially a synchronous motor operating at:","options":["Under-excitation with mechanical load","Over-excitation with mechanical load","Over-excitation at no mechanical load","Critical excitation at full load"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An over-excited synchronous motor running at zero shaft load draws leading reactive current from the bus, acting as a synchronous capacitor/condenser."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The maximum starting torque in a 3-phase slip-ring induction motor is achieved when:","options":["R2 = X2","R2 = s * X2","R2 = 0","X2 = 0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At starting, slip s = 1. Torque is maximum when rotor resistance per phase equals standstill rotor reactance (R2 = s * X2 = X2)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a transformer, tertiary windings are primarily provided to:","options":["Increase voltage transformation ratio","Provide a path for zero-sequence harmonic currents and stabilize neutral","Reduce copper usage","Eliminate core losses completely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A delta-connected tertiary winding provides a circulating closed path for 3rd harmonic currents, unbalancing loads, and suppressing neutral voltage shifts."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 4-pole, 50 Hz, 3-phase induction motor runs at 1440 RPM. What is the frequency of the rotor-induced EMF?","options":["50 Hz","25 Hz","2 Hz","5 Hz"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ns = (120 * 50) / 4 = 1500 RPM. Slip s = (1500 - 1440) / 1500 = 60 / 1500 = 0.04. Rotor frequency fr = s * f = 0.04 * 50 = 2 Hz."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 500 kVA transformer has constant core loss of 2 kW and full-load copper loss of 8 kW. The kVA load for maximum efficiency is:","options":["250 kVA","353.5 kVA","400 kVA","500 kVA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Load at η_max = Rated kVA * √(Pi / Pcu_fl) = 500 * √(2 / 8) = 500 * √(1 / 4) = 500 * 0.5 = 250 kVA."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"If the excitation of a synchronous generator connected to an infinite bus is increased, it results in:","options":["Increase in real power output (MW)","Decrease in real power output","Increase in lagging reactive power delivery (MVAR)","Increase in frequency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Active power (P) is governed solely by prime-mover governor steam/water input. Field excitation governs reactive power (Q) exchange and terminal voltage."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"During the plugging method of braking a 3-phase induction motor, the slip value is approximately:","options":["Zero","Between 0 and 1","Between 1 and 2","Greater than 2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Plugging is achieved by reversing any two stator supply phases, flipping field rotation to -Ns. Slip becomes s = (-Ns - Nr) / (-Ns) = (Ns + Nr) / Ns ≈ (2 - s) (i.e. between 1 and 2)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase alternator has a fractional pitch coil shorted by 30° electrical. The pitch factor (kp or kd) for the fundamental wave is:","options":["cos(15°)","sin(15°)","cos(30°)","sin(30°)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pitch factor kp = cos(chording angle / 2) = cos(30° / 2) = cos(15°) ≈ 0.966."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The primary cause of hunting in synchronous motors and its remedy are:","options":["Load fluctuation; remedied by damper windings","High excitation; remedied by field rheostat","Supply frequency change; remedied by governors","Harmonic currents; remedied by chokes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sudden periodic load variations cause the rotor to oscillate about its steady-state load angle. Damper windings placed in pole faces develop induction torque to suppress hunting."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Interpoles (commutating poles) in a DC machine are connected in:","options":["Series with armature winding","Parallel with armature winding","Series with shunt field winding","Parallel with shunt field winding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interpoles carry the full armature current to develop an opposing MMF that neutralizes reactance voltage and improves commutation."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Universal Motor is capable of operating on:","options":["AC supply only","DC supply only","Both AC and single-phase DC supply","3-phase AC supply only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A universal motor is a modified DC series motor built with laminated cores to operate on either DC or single-phase AC."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For distribution transformers, which efficiency metric is considered most critical?","options":["Commercial efficiency at full load","All-day efficiency (energy efficiency)","Maximum efficiency at unity pf","Half-load efficiency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distribution transformers remain energized 24 hours a day with fluctuating loads; hence, all-day efficiency (kWh output / kWh input over 24h) is prioritized."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When starting a 3-phase induction motor using a Star-Delta starter, starting current is reduced to:","options":["50% of DOL starting current","33.3% (1/3) of DOL starting current","57.7% (1/√3) of DOL starting current","25% of DOL starting current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In star connection, phase voltage is Vline / √3, causing phase current to drop by 1/√3 and line current to drop to (1/√3) * (1/√3) = 1/3 (33.3%)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which fractional horsepower single-phase motor operates with the least noise and vibration?","options":["Universal motor","Repulsion motor","Hysteresis motor","Capacitor start motor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hysteresis motors have smooth, unslotted cylindrical rotors of high-retentivity chrome steel, producing no mechanical or magnetic cogging noise."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an alternator, the Synchronous Impedance (EMF) method yields a voltage regulation value that is:","options":["Always lower than actual (optimistic)","Always higher than actual (pessimistic)","Exactly equal to actual regulation","Zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The EMF method assumes magnetic circuits remain unsaturated, yielding an exaggerated synchronous reactance value and pessimistic (higher) regulation."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Compensating windings in heavy-duty DC machines are placed in:","options":["Armature core slots","Pole shoes and connected in series with armature","Commutator riser segments","Yoke frame in shunt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compensating windings sit directly in pole face slots carrying armature current in the opposite direction to neutralize cross-magnetization under pole faces."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Crawling in a squirrel-cage induction motor at approximately 1/7th synchronous speed is caused by:","options":["5th space harmonic flux","7th space harmonic flux","3rd time harmonic current","Rotor slot skewing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 7th stator space harmonic creates a forward-rotating field at Ns/7, producing a stable harmonic torque dip that traps the motor at 1/7th base speed."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An alternator with a high Short Circuit Ratio (SCR) exhibits:","options":["Poorer voltage regulation and lower stability limit","Better voltage regulation and higher steady-state stability limit","High synchronous reactance Xd","Smaller physical air gap and lower size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SCR is inversely proportional to Xd. High SCR implies lower Xd, larger physical air gap, superior voltage regulation, and enhanced synchronizing power."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The primary advantage of a double squirrel-cage induction motor rotor design is:","options":["Low starting torque and high running efficiency","High starting torque combined with high running efficiency","Higher operating power factor than synchronous motors","Elimination of slip completely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At startup, current flows in the outer high-resistance cage (high starting torque). At rated speed, current shifts to the low-resistance inner cage (high efficiency)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The magnetizing inrush current of a power transformer during switching is characterized by a high content of:","options":["3rd harmonic component","2nd harmonic component","5th harmonic component","7th harmonic component"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformer inrush current is rich in DC offset and 2nd harmonic content, which differential protection relays use for harmonic restraint."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Hopkinson's test for testing DC shunt machines requires:","options":["One machine operating at full load","Two identical machines mechanically and electrically coupled","A calibrated auxiliary dynamometer","A variable frequency AC power source"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hopkinson's back-to-back regenerative test couples two identical machines so the generator supplies the motor, drawing only total losses from the mains."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Under which power factor condition can an alternator exhibit negative voltage regulation?","options":["Lagging power factor only","Unity power factor","Leading power factor","Zero power factor lagging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At leading power factors, the magnetizing armature reaction boosts terminal voltage under load (V > E), yielding negative voltage regulation."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 100 kVA transformer has full-load copper loss of 1600 W. At 75% of full load, the copper loss will be:","options":["1200 W","900 W","800 W","400 W"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Copper loss varies with the square of the loading fraction: Pcu = (3/4)² * 1600 = (9/16) * 1600 = 900 W."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A stepper motor has 4 stator phases and a rotor with 50 teeth. The step angle of the motor is:","options":["1.8°","2.5°","3.6°","7.5°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Step angle β = 360° / (m * Nr) = 360° / (4 * 50) = 360° / 200 = 1.8°."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Potier method (ZPF method) for alternator regulation is widely considered the most accurate because it separates:","options":["Stator core loss from friction loss","Armature leakage reactance and armature reaction MMF","Eddy current loss from hysteresis loss","Field resistance from brush resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Potier triangle separates the true leakage reactance drop (I * Xal) from the armature reaction MMF (Far) under actual saturated magnetic conditions."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In an induction motor, if the rotor is driven mechanically in reverse direction against stator field rotation, operating slip is:","options":["Between 0 and 1","Negative (s < 0)","Greater than 1 (s > 1)","Zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When rotor speed Nr is negative, s = (Ns - (-Nr)) / Ns = (Ns + Nr) / Ns > 1. This represents the induction braking/plugging quadrant."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 6-pole, 50 Hz alternator has 72 stator slots with 2-layer winding. The distribution factor (kd) for the fundamental is:","options":["0.957","0.966","0.866","1.000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Slots/pole = 72 / 6 = 12. Slots/pole/phase m = 12 / 3 = 4. Slot pitch γ = 180° / 12 = 15°. kd = sin(mγ/2) / [m * sin(γ/2)] = sin(30°) / [4 * sin(7.5°)] ≈ 0.957."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 3-phase core-type transformer, 3rd harmonic magnetizing current is absent because:","options":["Delta connection blocks it","Zero-sequence flux cannot establish a closed iron path and returns via air/tank with high reluctance","Core saturation eliminates harmonics","Neutral grounding drains 3rd harmonics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In 3-phase 3-limb core-type designs, 3rd harmonic fluxes in all limbs are in-phase and must return through air or tank oil, facing high magnetic reluctance."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two identical DC shunt machines are tested via Hopkinson's test. The motor armature current is 50 A, and generator armature current is 40 A. If supply voltage is 200 V, total copper and stray armature circuit losses are:","options":["2000 W","1800 W","1000 W","8000 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power drawn from mains supplies total losses: P_loss = V * (I_motor - I_gen) = 200 V * (50 A - 40 A) = 200 * 10 = 2000 W."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a power transformer, dry silica gel in the breather is blue and turns _____ when saturated with moisture:","options":["Pink","Yellow","Dark brown","White"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cobalt chloride impregnated silica gel is active blue when completely dry and turns pale pink when saturated with water vapor."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens to the back EMF (Eb) of a DC motor if the speed is halved while field flux is kept constant?","options":["Doubles","Remains unchanged","Halves","Becomes zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Back EMF is directly proportional to speed and flux (Eb ∝ Φ * N). If flux is constant and speed halves, Eb also halves."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a capacitor-start single-phase induction motor, the centrifugal switch disconnects the auxiliary winding at approximately what speed?","options":["20% to 30% of synchronous speed","50% of synchronous speed","70% to 80% of synchronous speed","95% of synchronous speed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The auxiliary start winding provides phase split for starting torque and is automatically opened at 70% to 80% of rated speed by centrifugal force."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The normal operating region of a 3-phase induction motor on its torque-slip characteristic curve is:","options":["High-slip unstable region","Low-slip stable linear region","Braking region (s > 1)","Generating region (s < 0)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under normal steady running conditions, the motor operates at very low slip (typically 2% to 5%) where torque is directly proportional to slip."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why can a standard 3-phase synchronous motor NOT self-start?","options":["No rotating magnetic field is produced","Rotor has high inertia and cannot accelerate during rapid stator field reversals","Starting winding is open-circuited","Terminal voltage drops to zero at startup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Due to rotor inertia, the rotor cannot accelerate to synchronous speed within one half-cycle, resulting in zero average starting torque."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The standard electrode gap used in an oil testing cup for measuring transformer oil dielectric breakdown voltage is:","options":["2.5 mm","10 mm","0.5 mm","25 mm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"As per IS:6792 / IEC standards, transformer dielectric oil testing is performed across polished spherical electrodes separated by a 2.5 mm gap."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Scott (T-T) connection, the teaser transformer has a primary tapping at what percentage of total turns?","options":["50%","86.6% (√3/2)","57.7% (1/√3)","70.7% (1/√2)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The teaser transformer primary operates at √3/2 (86.6%) of the main transformer turns to maintain balanced 3-phase to 2-phase conversion."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To increase the speed of a DC series motor above rated speed, which method is used?","options":["Connecting a diverter resistance across the series field","Inserting resistance in series with armature","Reducing applied terminal voltage","Reversing supply polarity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A diverter resistor in parallel with the series field diverts part of the current, weakening field flux (Φ) and increasing speed (N ∝ 1/Φ)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a cylindrical-rotor synchronous generator, maximum steady-state power transfer occurs at a load angle (δ) of:","options":["0°","45°","90°","180°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Real power is P = (E * V / Xs) * sin(δ). Power reaches its maximum theoretical steady-state stability limit when sin(δ) = 1 (δ = 90°)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When an induction machine is operated as an induction generator, it must:","options":["Supply lagging reactive power to the grid","Absorb reactive power from the grid to establish its magnetic field","Operate at positive slip (s > 0)","Run at exactly synchronous speed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An induction generator cannot self-excite without external capacitors or grid connection because it requires leading/reactive VARs to establish excitation flux."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If one transformer in a delta-delta bank of three 100 kVA transformers is damaged and removed, the V-V (open-delta) capacity is:","options":["100 kVA","173.2 kVA","200 kVA","150 kVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Open-delta bank capacity is 57.7% of the original 3-transformer bank rating: Capacity = 300 kVA * 0.577 = 173.2 kVA (or 86.6% of the 2 remaining units)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a DC generator without interpoles, to obtain sparkless commutation, brushes must be shifted:","options":["In the direction of armature rotation","Opposite to the direction of armature rotation","To the geometric neutral axis (GNA) permanently","90° electrical away from MNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Armature reaction distorts magnetic flux forward in the direction of rotation in a generator. Brushes must shift forward past the Magnetic Neutral Axis (MNA)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The direction of rotation of a shaded-pole single-phase induction motor is always from:","options":["Shaded part to unshaded part of the pole","Unshaded part to shaded part of the pole","Determined by supply polarity","Reversible via external switch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flux in the shaded portion lags behind flux in the unshaded portion due to induced circulating ring currents, establishing rotation from unshaded to shaded."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Zero voltage regulation in a practical power transformer occurs at what power factor?","options":["Unity power factor","Zero power factor lagging","Leading power factor","Zero power factor leading only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Regulation is approx (R_pu * cos Φ ± X_pu * sin Φ). For zero regulation, tan Φ = -R/X, which is only possible at a leading power factor."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a salient-pole synchronous generator, the reluctance power component is proportional to:","options":["sin(δ)","sin(2δ)","cos(δ)","cos(2δ)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reluctance power arises due to unequal d-axis and q-axis reluctances and is mathematically expressed as Prel = 0.5 * V² * (1/Xq - 1/Xd) * sin(2δ)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In the circle diagram of a 3-phase induction motor, the vertical intercept between the output line and the torque line represents:","options":["Stator copper loss","Rotor copper loss","Core loss","Friction and windage loss"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The torque line divides total internal copper loss into stator copper loss (below torque line) and rotor copper loss (between output and torque lines)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Retardation Test (running-down test) on a DC machine is conducted to isolate and determine:","options":["Armature copper losses","Mechanical friction, windage, and iron losses (stray losses)","Insulation resistance of windings","Commutator contact resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By shutting off power and monitoring the kinetic energy deceleration rate (dω/dt), stray rotational losses (W = J * ω * dω/dt) are calculated."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In Sumpner's (back-to-back) test on two identical transformers, the total power drawn from the primary supply represents:","options":["Full-load copper losses of both transformers","Core (iron) losses of both transformers","Combined core and copper losses of one transformer","Zero power"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The primary winding is excited at rated voltage with secondary loops opposing, so primary power supplies iron losses of both units."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The high starting torque of a deep-bar squirrel-cage induction motor is due to:","options":["Increased rotor leakage flux at high speed","Skin effect forcing current to outer rotor layers at startup frequency","Lower rotor resistance at high frequency","Large physical air gap"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At startup, rotor frequency equals 50 Hz. High reactance in bottom bar portions forces current to the top outer edges (skin effect), elevating effective resistance."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A synchronous machine connected to an infinite bus is hunting. The natural frequency of hunting oscillations is directly proportional to:","options":["Rotor moment of inertia (J)","Synchronizing power coefficient (Psyn)","Square root of synchronizing power coefficient (√Psyn)","Armature resistance (Ra)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Natural angular frequency of undamped hunting oscillation is ωn = √(Psyn / M), where Psyn is synchronizing power coefficient and M is inertia constant."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Dummy coils are provided in the armature of a DC machine to:","options":["Improve commutation","Provide mechanical balance to the armature","Increase generated EMF","Reduce armature reaction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dummy coils are placed in wave-wound armatures solely to maintain mechanical balance of the rotating armature core; they are not connected into the active electrical circuit."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Prior to magnetic saturation, the torque developed by a DC series motor is proportional to:","options":["Armature current (Ia)","Square of armature current (Ia²)","Square root of armature current (√Ia)","Independent of current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a series motor, field flux is proportional to armature current (Φ ∝ Ia). Torque T ∝ Φ * Ia ∝ Ia² prior to magnetic saturation."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The function of a conservator tank mounted above a power transformer is to:","options":["Prevent moisture absorption directly","Accommodate expansion and contraction of insulating oil with temperature changes","Cool the circulating oil through radiators","Act as a reservoir for arcing gases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conservator tank provides adequate volume for insulating oil to expand and contract freely during temperature variations without exposing the main tank to atmospheric oxygen."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a 3-phase induction motor, slip speed is defined as the:","options":["Synchronous speed (Ns)","Rotor speed (Nr)","Difference between synchronous speed and rotor speed (Ns - Nr)","Ratio of rotor speed to synchronous speed (Nr / Ns)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Slip speed is the relative mechanical speed between the rotating magnetic field and the actual rotor: Slip speed = Ns - Nr (RPM)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of single-phase motor has the lowest efficiency and poorest power factor?","options":["Capacitor-start capacitor-run motor","Permanent split capacitor motor","Shaded-pole motor","Universal motor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shaded-pole motors suffer from high copper losses in shading coils and poor magnetic coupling, yielding very low efficiencies (typically 5% to 35%) and low lagging power factors."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The voltage regulation of an alternator is defined as:","options":["(V_full_load - V_no_load) / V_no_load","(V_no_load - V_full_load) / V_full_load","(V_no_load - V_full_load) / V_no_load","V_no_load / V_full_load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voltage regulation is the percent rise in terminal voltage when rated full load is dropped at constant speed and excitation: %VR = [(V_nl - V_fl) / V_fl] * 100."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Equalizer rings (equalizer connections) are employed in the armature windings of DC machines with:","options":["Wave winding only","Lap winding only","Both lap and wave windings","Frog-leg winding only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In lap winding, the number of parallel paths equals the number of poles (A = P). Slight air-gap inequalities cause unequal induced voltages; equalizer rings bypass heavy circulating currents away from brushes."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the voltage and frequency of a transformer are both doubled (V/f = constant), what happens to core losses?","options":["Hysteresis loss doubles, Eddy current loss quadruples","Both hysteresis and eddy current losses double","Both losses remain unchanged","Hysteresis loss quadruples, Eddy current loss doubles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With V/f constant, maximum flux density Bmax is constant. Hysteresis loss (Ph ∝ f * Bmax^1.6) doubles. Eddy current loss (Pe ∝ f² * Bmax²) quadruples."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A self-excited DC shunt generator fails to build up terminal voltage. Which of the following is a possible cause?","options":["Field circuit resistance is less than critical resistance","Armature speed is greater than critical speed","Field circuit resistance exceeds critical field resistance (Rf > Rc)","Presence of excess residual magnetism"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For voltage build-up, the field resistance line must fall below the linear portion of the magnetization curve; if field resistance exceeds critical resistance (Rf > Rc), voltage cannot build up."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Under-excited synchronous motors operate at what power factor?","options":["Leading power factor","Lagging power factor","Unity power factor","Zero power factor leading"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When excitation is reduced below normal (under-excited), the motor must draw lagging reactive current from the AC supply to support its internal magnetization."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an induction motor, if an external EMF at slip frequency is injected into the rotor circuit in exact phase opposition to the induced rotor EMF, the motor speed:","options":["Increases above synchronous speed","Decreases below normal running speed","Remains unchanged","Stalls immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Injecting an opposing slip-frequency voltage into the rotor reduces net rotor current; the rotor must slip further back (higher slip, lower speed) to induce enough voltage to balance the mechanical load."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Subtransient reactance (Xd''), transient reactance (Xd'), and synchronous reactance (Xd) of an alternator satisfy which relationship?","options":["Xd'' > Xd' > Xd","Xd > Xd' > Xd''","Xd' > Xd > Xd''","Xd = Xd' = Xd''"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During a sudden fault, damper windings and field windings initial flux trap effects suppress armature reaction, making Xd'' the lowest impedance. As eddy currents decay, reactance increases: Xd'' < Xd' < Xd."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Cumulative Compound DC Motor, as mechanical load increases, the operating speed:","options":["Increases substantially","Remains strictly constant","Drops more sharply than a shunt motor but less than a series motor","Rises to infinity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"As load current rises, the series field assists the shunt field flux (Φ_total = Φ_sh + Φ_se). Because speed N ∝ Eb / Φ, increasing flux causes speed to drop more noticeably than in a shunt motor."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Which 3-phase transformer connection creates a +30° phase displacement between secondary line voltage and primary line voltage?","options":["Yy0","Dd0","Yd11 (or Dy11)","Yd1 (or Dy1)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In standard vector grouping (clock convention), group 11 (such as Dyn11 or Yd11) produces a secondary line voltage that leads the primary line voltage by 30° (+30°)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An alternator requires a field current of 100 A to produce rated open-circuit voltage and 80 A to circulate rated short-circuit current. Its Short Circuit Ratio (SCR) is:","options":["0.80","1.25","1.00","0.64"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SCR = (Field current for rated O.C. voltage) / (Field current for rated S.C. current) = 100 A / 80 A = 1.25."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 1000 kVA, 11 kV / 400 V, 3-phase transformer has a percentage leakage impedance of 5%. The short-circuit fault MVA at the secondary terminals is:","options":["5 MVA","10 MVA","20 MVA","50 MVA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fault MVA = Base MVA / Z_pu = 1.0 MVA / 0.05 = 20 MVA."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 6-pole, 50 Hz, 3-phase induction motor develops 28.5 kW gross mechanical power while rotor copper loss is 1.5 kW. The operating slip is:","options":["2.5%","4.0%","5.0%","6.5%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P_rotor_Cu / P_mech = s / (1 - s). Here: 1.5 / 28.5 = 1 / 19. Therefore: s / (1 - s) = 1 / 19 => 19s = 1 - s => 20s = 1 => s = 1/20 = 0.05 (5.0%)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a salient-pole alternator operating with load angle δ, reluctance torque develops because:","options":["Damper winding resistance is high","Magnetic reluctance along direct axis (d-axis) is lower than along quadrature axis (q-axis)","Armature leakage reactance is zero","Field winding is unexcited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Salient poles create non-uniform air gaps: minimum reluctance exists along the d-axis (iron core) and maximum reluctance along the q-axis (interpoler space), producing reluctance torque proportional to sin(2δ)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase slip-ring induction motor has a standstill rotor resistance of 0.04 Ω/phase and standstill reactance of 0.2 Ω/phase. The external resistance per phase added to obtain maximum starting torque is:","options":["0.04 Ω","0.16 Ω","0.20 Ω","0.24 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For maximum starting torque (s = 1), total rotor resistance must equal standstill reactance: R2_total = X2 = 0.20 Ω. External resistance required = 0.20 - 0.04 = 0.16 Ω/phase."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a synchronous machine, the Potier reactance (Xp) is strictly:","options":["Slightly greater than armature leakage reactance (Xal)","Equal to synchronous reactance (Xs)","Equal to zero","Directly proportional to field winding resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because leakage flux paths saturate under heavy field excitation during zero-power-factor testing, the measured Potier reactance (Xp) is slightly larger than the true armature leakage reactance (Xal)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary purpose of insulating oil used in a power transformer is to provide:","options":["Lubrication and cooling","Insulation and cooling","Protection against lightning","Mechanical damping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformer mineral oil serves two vital functions: electrical insulation between live conductors/tank and transferring internal heat away to cooling radiator fins."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a DC machine, the armature magnetic field (MMF wave) is:","options":["Rotating at armature speed with respect to stator","Stationary in space with respect to the main field poles","Alternating at supply frequency","Rotating at twice armature speed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Although the armature rotates, the mechanical commutator continually inverts conductor currents under the brush line, keeping the armature MMF peak stationary in space relative to the stator field poles."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The rotor of a 3-phase induction motor always rotates in:","options":["The same direction as the stator rotating magnetic field","The opposite direction to the stator rotating magnetic field","Either direction depending on starting torque","A stationary pulsing state"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to Lenz's law, the rotor accelerates in the direction of the rotating magnetic field (RMF) to reduce the relative speed and induced rotor EMF."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which single-phase motor is most widely used in domestic electric ceiling fans?","options":["Shaded-pole motor","Permanent Split Capacitor (PSC) motor","Universal motor","Repulsion-induction motor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Permanent Split Capacitor (PSC) motors have a permanently connected run capacitor across the auxiliary winding, ensuring quiet operation, high running efficiency, and low maintenance."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an open-circuit (O.C.) test of a transformer, the wattmeter records predominantly:","options":["Full-load copper loss","Core (iron) loss only","Combined copper and stray load loss","Friction and windage loss"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because rated voltage is applied with secondary open-circuited, the no-load primary current is negligible (2-5%), making primary copper loss unmeasurably small and leaving only core loss."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The speed of a DC shunt motor can be raised above rated base speed by:","options":["Increasing armature circuit resistance","Decreasing field current (flux weakening)","Increasing applied terminal voltage","Reversing armature connections"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Motor speed is inversely proportional to field flux (N ∝ Eb / Φ). Inserting a field rheostat decreases field current, weakening Φ and driving speed above base speed."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two single-phase transformers operating in parallel share a common load inversely proportional to their:","options":["kVA ratings","Per-unit leakage impedances","Core losses","Magnetizing currents"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For balanced load sharing without overloading either unit, transformers must share load according to their ratings, which dictates that load current divides inversely with their per-unit impedances."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The torque developed by a 3-phase induction motor under running conditions is directly proportional to:","options":["Stator terminal voltage (V)","Square of stator terminal voltage (V²)","Cube of stator terminal voltage (V³)","Square root of stator terminal voltage (√V)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotor induced EMF is proportional to terminal voltage (E2 ∝ V). Developed electromagnetic torque T ∝ s * E2² ∝ s * V²."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a synchronous machine, the direct-axis synchronous reactance (Xd) is greater than quadrature-axis reactance (Xq) because:","options":["Direct axis air gap length is smaller, producing lower magnetic reluctance","Quadrature axis air gap is smaller","Armature resistance is higher on direct axis","Damper windings cancel q-axis flux"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Salient poles project along the d-axis where the physical iron air gap is narrowest (lowest reluctance, maximum permeance), resulting in higher flux and higher inductance (Xd > Xq)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What happens if the shunt field winding of a running DC shunt motor suddenly open-circuits under no-load?","options":["The motor stops immediately","The motor burns out instantly due to high resistance","The motor accelerates to a dangerously high (runaway) speed","The motor continues running at exact rated speed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Field flux collapses to residual magnetism (Φ ≈ 0). Because N ∝ Eb / Φ, the motor accelerates dangerously to produce enough back EMF, risking centrifugal rotor explosion."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When controlling an induction motor below base speed using variable frequency drives (VFD), the V/f ratio is kept constant to:","options":["Maintain constant core flux and avoid magnetic saturation","Reduce rotor copper loss to zero","Increase starting frequency","Operate at unity power factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stator flux Φm ≈ V / (4.44 * f * N * kw). If frequency f decreases without reducing voltage V, flux skyrockets, causing severe core saturation and excessive magnetizing current."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A fractional-pitch armature winding is preferred in synchronous alternators over a full-pitch winding because it:","options":["Eliminates unwanted higher space harmonics and saves copper in end-connections","Increases fundamental voltage output","Increases slot leakage reactance","Eliminates need for field poles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chording coils by an angle ε suppresses specific harmonics (e.g., 5th or 7th) from the terminal EMF waveform while shortening coil end-turns, reducing total copper weight."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an alternator feeding an infinite bus, increasing the prime mover mechanical input without changing field excitation leads to:","options":["Increase in real power output (MW) and increase in load angle (δ)","Increase in reactive power output (MVAR) only","Increase in terminal voltage with constant MW","Increase in operating frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prime-mover torque advances the rotor magnetic axis relative to the stator bus wave (increasing power angle δ), directly increasing real active electrical power export (P = E*V/Xs * sin δ)."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The primary role of a tertiary delta-connected winding in a star-star (Y-Y) power transformer is to:","options":["Allow zero-sequence 3rd harmonic currents to circulate, preventing neutral voltage oscillation","Increase secondary terminal voltage by 73%","Eliminate stray leakage reactance","Provide mechanical bracing against short circuits"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The closed delta tertiary winding traps in-phase 3rd harmonic magnetizing currents, preventing 3rd harmonic voltages from appearing on phase legs and stabilizing the neutral point."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase induction motor has an input power of 50 kW to its rotor. If the motor operates at a slip of 4%, the gross mechanical power developed by the rotor is:","options":["2 kW","48 kW","52 kW","46 kW"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotor power relation: P_rotor_input : P_copper_loss : P_mech_gross = 1 : s : (1 - s). P_mech = (1 - s) * P_in = (1 - 0.04) * 50 kW = 0.96 * 50 = 48 kW."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a salient-pole synchronous generator, the steady-state power angle (δ) corresponding to maximum power output is:","options":["Strictly equal to 90°","Less than 90° (typically 60° to 75°)","Greater than 90° (typically 120°)","Equal to 180°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Power P = (E*V/Xd)*sin(δ) + 0.5*V²*(1/Xq - 1/Xd)*sin(2δ). The reluctance component sin(2δ) peaks at δ = 45°, shifting total composite peak power to an angle less than 90°."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 1-phase transformer has primary resistance R1 = 0.1 Ω and secondary resistance R2 = 0.004 Ω. If transformation ratio K = N2/N1 = 0.2, equivalent resistance referred to the primary is:","options":["0.104 Ω","0.200 Ω","0.116 Ω","0.024 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R01 = R1 + (R2 / K²) = 0.1 + (0.004 / 0.2²) = 0.1 + (0.004 / 0.04) = 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.200 Ω."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 4-pole lap-wound DC generator has 720 armature conductors. If flux per pole is 0.05 Wb and speed is 1200 RPM, generated EMF is:","options":["360 V","720 V","1440 V","180 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For lap winding, number of parallel paths A = poles P = 4. Generated EMF Eg = (Φ * Z * N * P) / (60 * A) = (0.05 * 720 * 1200 * 4) / (60 * 4) = 0.05 * 720 * 20 = 720 V."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"DC injection dynamic braking of a 3-phase induction motor operates on the principle of:","options":["Reversing phase sequence to reverse stator field rotation","Disconnecting AC supply and feeding DC into the stator to create a stationary magnetic field","Reconnecting stator windings in delta","Shorting rotor slip rings through capacitors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Injecting DC creates a spatially fixed, stationary magnetic field. The spinning rotor cuts this static field, inducing currents that generate a strong braking torque dissipating energy in rotor resistance."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 3-phase cylindrical-rotor synchronous motor, taking armature resistance (Ra) into account, maximum mechanical power develops at load angle δ equal to:","options":["90°","180° - θ (where θ = tan⁻¹(Xs / Ra))","θ (internal impedance angle)","45°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With armature resistance considered, power developed is P = [E*V/Zs]*cos(θ - δ) - [E²*Ra/Zs²]. Differentiating with respect to δ yields maximum mechanical power when δ = 180° - θ."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"ACSR (Aluminum Conductor Steel Reinforced) conductors are widely used in transmission lines because the central steel core provides:","options":["High electrical conductivity","High mechanical tensile strength","Protection against lightning surges","Reduced skin effect"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In ACSR conductors, outer aluminum strands carry the current, while the central galvanized steel core provides high mechanical tensile strength to permit longer spans and reduce sag."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Pin-type insulators are generally limited for use in overhead lines up to an operating voltage of:","options":["11 kV","33 kV","66 kV","132 kV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pin insulators become uneconomical, heavy, and physically bulky above 33 kV; hence, suspension-type disc insulators are preferred for 66 kV and above."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The sag (S) of a transmission line conductor suspended between two level supports of span length L and tension T is given by:","options":["S = w * L / (8 * T)","S = w * L² / (8 * T)","S = w * L² / (2 * T)","S = 8 * w * L / T"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conductor sag is given by S = (w * L²) / (8 * T), where w is weight per unit length, L is span length, and T is horizontal conductor tension."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Skin effect in an AC conductor results in:","options":["Uniform current distribution across the cross-section","Higher current density near the conductor surface than at the core","Higher current density at the center than at the surface","Decrease in effective AC resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alternating current induces higher internal magnetic flux linkage at the conductor center, increasing core reactance and forcing current to flow predominantly along the outer surface (skin)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The string efficiency of a suspension insulator string can be improved by:","options":["Using a longer cross-arm and a guard ring","Decreasing the number of insulator discs","Increasing the pin-to-earth capacitance","Reducing conductor spacing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"String efficiency increases as shunt capacitance (pin-to-tower) decreases relative to mutual disc capacitance. Longer cross-arms reduce shunt capacitance, and grading rings equalize voltage distribution across discs."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Bundled conductors are predominantly employed in EHV and UHV transmission lines to:","options":["Increase line inductance","Reduce corona loss, radio interference, and line reactance","Reduce mechanical tower loading","Eliminate skin effect completely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bundling multiple sub-conductors increases the geometric mean radius (GMR), lowering surface electrical gradient, mitigating corona loss, and decreasing overall line series inductance."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If the operating voltage of a transmission line is doubled while transmitting the same power, line conductor volume is reduced to:","options":["50% (1/2)","25% (1/4)","12.5% (1/8)","70.7% (1/√2)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Volume of conductor material required is inversely proportional to the square of transmission voltage (V ∝ 1 / V²). Doubling the voltage reduces conductor material to (1/2)² = 1/4 (25%)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a symmetrical and reciprocal 2-port transmission network, the ABCD parameters satisfy:","options":["A = D and AD - BC = 1","A = B and CD - AB = 1","A = D and AD - BC = 0","B = C and AD - BC = 1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reciprocity condition requires AD - BC = 1, and symmetry condition (line appears identical from either sending or receiving end) requires A = D."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a nominal-T representation of a medium-length transmission line:","options":["Total line capacitance is lumped at the sending end","Total line capacitance is lumped at the receiving end","Total line capacitance is lumped at the middle of the line","Capacitance is divided equally at both ends"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the nominal-T model, total line impedance Z is divided into two halves (Z/2) at both ends, and total line shunt admittance Y (capacitance) is concentrated at the center."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an underground cable, maximum dielectric electrostatic stress occurs at:","options":["The outer lead sheath surface","The middle of the dielectric insulation","The surface of the inner conductor","The armor layer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Electric stress g = V / [x * ln(R/r)]. Stress is maximum at minimum radius x = r (the conductor surface) and minimum at outer sheath radius x = R."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Peterson coil (arc suppression coil) is used in a power system to:","options":["Neutralize capacitive charging current during single line-to-ground faults","Limit 3-phase symmetrical short-circuit current","Damp power system oscillations","Improve receiving-end voltage regulation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A tunable reactor (Peterson coil) connected between system neutral and ground supplies an inductive current that cancels out the line-to-ground capacitive fault current, quenching arcing grounds."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In symmetrical components analysis, a balanced set of negative sequence currents has:","options":["Zero phase displacement between phases","Phase sequence identical to the original system (R-Y-B)","Phase sequence opposite to the original system (R-B-Y)","Currents existing only in the neutral wire"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Negative sequence components consist of three balanced phasors equal in magnitude, displaced by 120°, with phase sequence opposite to positive sequence (R-B-Y instead of R-Y-B)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The critical disruptive voltage (Vd) of an overhead transmission line increases when:","options":["Conductor radius (r) and spacing (d) both increase","Air density factor (δ) decreases","Conductor surface roughness increases","Supply frequency increases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Vd = m0 * g0 * δ * r * ln(d/r). Increasing conductor radius r and conductor spacing d increases the critical disruptive voltage, delaying corona onset."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Ferranti effect in long transmission lines can be compensated and voltage rise controlled using:","options":["Series capacitors","Shunt reactors","Shunt capacitors","Series reactors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under light load or open circuit conditions, line capacitance causes receiving-end voltage to exceed sending-end voltage. Shunt reactors absorb leading reactive VARs, pulling down terminal voltage."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 400 kV overhead transmission line has a Surge Impedance Loading (SIL) of 400 MW. If the operating line voltage is upgraded to 800 kV, the new SIL is:","options":["800 MW","1200 MW","1600 MW","2000 MW"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Surge Impedance Loading is proportional to the square of operating voltage: SIL = V² / Zc. Doubling voltage from 400 kV to 800 kV quadruples the SIL: 400 MW * (800 / 400)² = 400 * 4 = 1600 MW."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For an unsymmetrical Single Line-to-Ground (L-G) fault without fault impedance, the sequence networks are connected in:","options":["Parallel connection of positive, negative, and zero sequence networks","Series connection of positive, negative, and zero sequence networks","Positive and negative in series, zero sequence isolated","Positive and negative in parallel, zero sequence in series"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For an L-G fault, boundary conditions dictate Ia1 = Ia2 = Ia0 = Ia / 3, which is satisfied only when positive, negative, and zero sequence networks are connected in series."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 11 kV, 10 MVA generator has a subtransient reactance of Xd'' = 0.15 pu on its own base. The short-circuit fault MVA for a solid 3-phase fault at its terminals is:","options":["15 MVA","66.67 MVA","150 MVA","100 MVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Short-circuit fault MVA = Base MVA / Xd''_pu = 10 MVA / 0.15 = 66.67 MVA."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Equal Area Criterion for transient stability analysis of a Single Machine Infinite Bus (SMIB) system states that the system remains stable if:","options":["Accelerating area is strictly greater than decelerating area","Accelerating area is equal to or less than maximum available decelerating area","Rotor angle remains below 45°","Kinetic energy of the rotor remains zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During and after a disturbance, the rotor accelerates (storing transient kinetic energy A1). The system is transiently stable if the network can provide an equal decelerating area (A2 ≥ A1) before the critical angle limit."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In interconnected Automatic Generation Control (AGC), the Area Control Error (ACE) for an area is given by:","options":["ACE = ΔP_tie - B * Δf","ACE = ΔP_tie + B * Δf","ACE = ΔP_tie / (B * Δf)","ACE = Δf + B * ΔP_tie"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Area Control Error represents the mismatch between tie-line power and frequency bias: ACE = ΔP_tie + B * Δf, where B is the area frequency bias constant."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The break-even distance beyond which HVDC transmission becomes more economical than HVAC transmission for overhead lines is typically:","options":["50 to 100 km","200 to 300 km","600 to 800 km","1500 to 2000 km"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Due to high converter and inverter station terminal costs, HVDC becomes cheaper than HVAC for overhead lines only when line savings over distance offset terminal costs, typically at 600 to 800 km (and 40 to 50 km for submarine cables)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The load factor of a power generating station is defined as the ratio of:","options":["Average load to maximum demand","Maximum demand to average load","Average load to connected load","Connected load to plant capacity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Load factor = (Average load over a given period) / (Maximum demand during that period). A higher load factor lowers the overall cost per unit of electricity generated."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The diversity factor in a power distribution network is always:","options":["Equal to 1","Less than 1","Greater than 1","Zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Diversity factor = (Sum of individual maximum demands) / (Coincident maximum demand of the entire system). Because consumer peak demands do not occur at the exact same instant, it is always greater than 1."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Periodic transposition of 3-phase overhead transmission line conductors is carried out primarily to:","options":["Balance line mutual capacitances and reduce communication interference","Reduce conductor sag","Increase mechanical tensile strength","Prevent wind-induced conductor galloping"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transposing conductors equalizes the mutual inductance and capacitance of all three phases, balancing phase voltage drops and minimizing electromagnetic noise induced in parallel telephone lines."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An isolator (disconnecting switch) in an electrical substation must strictly be operated under:","options":["Full load current","Short-circuit fault current","No-load condition only","Overload current"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Isolators possess no arc-quenching medium. They are strictly mechanical switches operated only after the circuit breaker has interrupted current flow (no-load condition)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which secondary distribution system provides the highest reliability of electrical supply to consumers?","options":["Radial system","Ring main system","Tree distribution system","Single-end spur system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a ring main system, each distribution transformer is fed through two parallel ring paths, ensuring uninterrupted power even if one line section develops a fault."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of power generating plant is most technically and economically suited to operate as a base-load plant?","options":["Gas turbine peaking plant","Pumped-storage hydro plant","Nuclear power plant","Diesel generator station"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nuclear power stations involve heavy capital costs, very low incremental fuel costs, and long startup/shutdown cycles, making them ideal for steady round-the-clock base-load operation."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an electrical substation, a surge diverter (lightning arrester) is placed closest to:","options":["The power transformer terminals","The generator busbars","The main isolator switch","The substation boundary fence"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Surge arresters are positioned directly adjacent to transformer bushings to divert incoming steep-fronted lightning and switching overvoltages before they can stress the expensive winding insulation."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The typical surge impedance of an underground power cable compared to an overhead transmission line is:","options":["Much higher (approx. 1000 Ω)","Roughly equal (approx. 400 Ω)","Much lower (approx. 40 to 60 Ω)","Zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because conductors in a cable are tightly spaced inside a high-permittivity dielectric, shunt capacitance (C) is large and inductance (L) is low. Surge impedance Zc = √(L/C) is ~40-60 Ω, compared to ~400 Ω for overhead lines."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The zero-sequence impedance (Z0) of an overhead transmission line without ground wires is typically:","options":["Equal to positive sequence impedance (Z1)","Much less than positive sequence impedance (Z1)","2 to 3.5 times greater than positive sequence impedance (Z1)","Strictly zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Zero-sequence currents return through the earth. Because the ground return path has a deep equivalent penetration depth and higher resistance/reactance, Z0 is 2 to 3.5 times larger than Z1."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the spacing (D) between conductors of a 3-phase line is doubled while conductor diameter remains unchanged, line inductance per phase will:","options":["Double","Halve","Increase slightly by a logarithmic factor","Decrease to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Line inductance per phase is L = 2 × 10⁻⁷ * ln(GMD / GMR). Doubling the spacing doubles the GMD, but because GMD is inside the natural logarithm (ln), overall inductance increases only marginally."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an unsymmetrical Line-to-Line (L-L) fault without ground contact, the sequence networks are connected in:","options":["Series with zero-sequence network","Parallel opposition with positive and negative sequence networks (zero sequence open)","Series with positive and negative sequence networks only","All three sequence networks in parallel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For an isolated L-L fault, no current flows to ground (Ia0 = 0). The boundary conditions dictate Ia1 = -Ia2 and Va1 = Va2, which corresponds to connecting positive and negative sequence networks in parallel."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a 2-conductor bundled phase with sub-conductor radius r and spacing d, the equivalent self GMR (Ds) of the bundle is:","options":["√(r' * d)","r' * d","√(r * d)","(r' + d) / 2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a 2-conductor bundle, Ds = (r' * d * r' * d)^(1/4) = √(r' * d), where fictitious radius r' = 0.7788 * r accounts for internal flux linkages."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The per-unit inertia constant (H) of a synchronous generator represents:","options":["Stored kinetic energy in Mega-Joules at synchronous speed per MVA rating","Rotor mechanical speed in RPM","Direct-axis synchronous reactance per unit","Governor droop regulation percentage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inertia constant H = (Stored kinetic energy at rated synchronous speed in MJ) / (Machine rating in MVA). It typically ranges from 2 to 8 MJ/MVA for commercial generators."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Which Flexible AC Transmission System (FACTS) controller is connected in series with the line to dynamically control active power flow by modifying line reactance?","options":["STATCOM","Static VAR Compensator (SVC)","Thyristor Controlled Series Capacitor (TCSC)","Unified Power Quality Conditioner (UPQC)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A TCSC alters the net series capacitive reactance of the transmission circuit, thereby directly controlling the total transfer reactance (X) and transmitted active power P = (V1*V2/X) * sin δ."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a Single Machine Infinite Bus (SMIB) system where P = Pmax * sin δ, a 3-phase fault drops electrical power to zero. If initial angle is δ0 and maximum angle is δmax = π - δ0, the critical clearing angle (δc) satisfies:","options":["cos δc = (π - 2δ0) * sin δ0 + cos δ0","cos δc = (π - 2δ0) * sin δ0 - cos δ0","cos δc = cos δ0 / 2","cos δc = sin δ0 / (π - δ0)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equating accelerating area A1 = Pm*(δc - δ0) with decelerating area A2 = ∫[δc to δmax](Pmax*sin δ - Pm)dδ, and substituting Pm = Pmax*sin δ0, yields: cos δc = (π - 2δ0)*sin δ0 - cos δ0."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In the Newton-Raphson load flow method for an N-bus system containing 1 slack bus and m voltage-controlled (PV) buses, the size of the Jacobian matrix is:","options":["(2N - 2) × (2N - 2)","(2N - 2 - m) × (2N - 2 - m)","(N - 1) × (N - 1)","(2N - 1) × (2N - 1)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unknowns consist of (N - 1) voltage angles (δ) and (N - 1 - m) voltage magnitudes (|V|), since slack bus V and δ are fixed, and m PV buses have fixed |V|. Total size = (N - 1) + (N - 1 - m) = 2N - 2 - m."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Fast Decoupled Load Flow (FDLF) formulation is valid primarily due to which transmission line characteristic?","options":["High line resistance (R >> X)","High reactance-to-resistance ratio (X >> R) and small phase angle differences","Equal real and reactive power demands","Negligible series inductance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FDLF decouples active power/voltage angle (P-δ) and reactive power/voltage magnitude (Q-V) by assuming X >> R (Gij ≈ 0), cos(δi - δj) ≈ 1, and Qij << |Vi|² * Bij."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-core underground cable operates at 33 kV. If conductor radius r = 1 cm and internal lead sheath radius R = 2.718 cm (e cm), maximum dielectric stress at the conductor surface is:","options":["12.14 kV/cm","33.00 kV/cm","66.00 kV/cm","18.50 kV/cm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maximum dielectric stress g_max = V / [r * ln(R/r)]. Substituting r = 1 cm and R/r = e yields: g_max = 33 / [1 * ln(e)] = 33 / 1 = 33 kV/cm."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A lossless overhead line has series inductance L = 1.6 mH/km and shunt capacitance C = 10 nF/km. The surge impedance (Zc) of the line is:","options":["160 Ω","250 Ω","400 Ω","500 Ω"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Surge impedance Zc = √(L / C) = √(1.6 × 10⁻³ / 10 × 10⁻⁹) = √(1.6 × 10⁵) = √160,000 = 400 Ω."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In economic dispatch with transmission losses, the coordination equation for generator i incorporating penalty factor Li is:","options":["(dCi / dPi) = λ","Li * (dCi / dPi) = λ","(dCi / dPi) / Li = λ","(∂Ploss / ∂Pi) = λ"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With transmission losses included, equal incremental fuel cost requires Li * (dCi / dPi) = λ, where penalty factor Li = 1 / [1 - (∂Ploss / ∂Pi)]."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a steam (thermal) power plant, the function of an economizer is to:","options":["Extract heat from flue gases to preheat boiler feed water","Preheat combustion air entering the boiler furnace","Superheat steam exiting the boiler drum","Condense exhaust steam from the turbine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An economizer extracts waste thermal energy from hot flue gases leaving the boiler to preheat incoming boiler feed water, raising overall plant thermal efficiency."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For short transmission lines (length < 80 km and operating voltage < 20 kV), which line parameter is conventionally neglected?","options":["Series resistance","Series inductance","Shunt capacitance","Both series resistance and inductance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because line length is short and voltage is relatively low, line charging leakage currents are negligible; hence, shunt capacitance is ignored."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an electrical distribution system, a distributor is primarily designed based on the criteria of:","options":["Thermal current-carrying capacity","Permissible voltage drop","Corona loss limit","Short-circuit MVA rating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Feeders are sized based on thermal current-carrying capacity, whereas distributors directly feed consumer service connections and are designed strictly based on permissible voltage variation limits (typically ±5%)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a two-part electrical tariff, the consumer bill consists of charges based on:","options":["Connected load and power factor","Maximum demand (kW/kVA) and total energy consumed (kWh)","Energy consumed only","Running plant hours only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A two-part tariff assesses a fixed demand charge per kW/kVA of maximum demand (recovering fixed capital costs) plus an energy charge per kWh consumed (recovering operating/fuel expenses)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The continuous earth wire (ground wire) placed along the peak of transmission towers protects phase conductors against:","options":["Direct lightning strokes","Switching surges only","Line charging currents","Harmonic currents"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The overhead shield wire creates a protective shielding angle that intercepts incoming direct lightning strokes and diverts lightning currents safely to ground through tower footings."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A surge tank in a high-head hydroelectric power plant is installed to:","options":["Store excess water permanently","Relieve water hammer pressure transients in the penstock during sudden load reduction","Increase water velocity entering the impulse turbine","Filter out suspended silt and abrasive debris"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When turbine gates close rapidly during sudden load rejections, the surge tank absorbs the sudden water momentum, preventing catastrophic water hammer pressure bursts in the penstock."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Stockbridge dampers installed near insulator suspension clamps on overhead lines are used to suppress:","options":["Low-frequency, high-amplitude conductor galloping","High-frequency, low-amplitude Aeolian wind vibrations","Conductor swinging caused by cross-winds","Sag variations caused by seasonal temperature rise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Stockbridge dampers dissipate kinetic energy via inter-strand friction within their messenger cables, dampening high-frequency (5 to 50 Hz) vortex-shedding Aeolian wind vibrations."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The velocity of electromagnetic wave propagation along a lossless overhead transmission line in air is approximately:","options":["1.5 × 10⁸ m/s","3.0 × 10⁸ m/s (speed of light)","3.0 × 10⁶ m/s","1.0 × 10⁵ m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a lossless TEM line in air, wave velocity is v = 1 / √(μ0 * ε0) = c ≈ 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. In underground cables, dielectric permittivity slows this down to c / √εr."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Capacitance grading of an extra-high voltage underground cable is accomplished by:","options":["Using metallic intersheaths maintained at external potentials","Using dielectric layers of different permittivities, with permittivity decreasing outward from the conductor","Using a single homogeneous dielectric with increasing permittivity outward","Increasing the conductor core diameter only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By layering dielectrics such that relative permittivity εr decreases from the inner conductor surface to the outer sheath, the product (εr * r) is kept nearly constant, equalizing peak dielectric stresses across layers."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a power flow study, the variables specified (known) at a Load (PQ) Bus are:","options":["Real power (P) and voltage magnitude (|V|)","Voltage magnitude (|V|) and voltage phase angle (δ)","Net real power (P) and reactive power (Q)","Reactive power (Q) and voltage phase angle (δ)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At a load (PQ) bus, active power demand P and reactive power demand Q are specified, while voltage magnitude |V| and phase angle δ are the unknown state variables computed iteratively."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Proximity effect between parallel conductors carrying alternating current results in:","options":["Non-uniform current distribution across the conductor cross-section caused by mutual magnetic flux of adjacent conductors","A reduction in effective AC resistance","Complete elimination of skin effect","An increase in shunt capacitance to infinity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Flux generated by current in adjacent conductors distorts current distribution across nearby conductors, crowding currents toward the inner or outer edges and elevating AC resistance."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In single-core underground cables carrying heavy AC currents, metallic sheath circulating currents and sheath losses are minimized by:","options":["Solidly grounding both ends of the sheath","Cross-bonding the metallic sheaths at periodic transposition intervals","Eliminating the outer protective lead sheath","Increasing line frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cross-bonding transposes metallic sheaths among the three phases so that induced sheath voltages cancel over three consecutive minor sections, eliminating sheath circulating currents."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Using standard symmetrical component transformation, total 3-phase complex power (S = P + jQ) is expressed as:","options":["S = Va1*Ia1* + Va2*Ia2* + Va0*Ia0*","S = 3 * [Va1*Ia1* + Va2*Ia2* + Va0*Ia0*]","S = √3 * [Va1*Ia1* + Va2*Ia2* + Va0*Ia0*]","S = 3 * [Va1*Ia1 + Va2*Ia2 + Va0*Ia0]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using standard non-power-invariant sequence transformation, total 3-phase complex power equals three times the sum of individual sequence complex powers: S = 3 * (Va0*Ia0* + Va1*Ia1* + Va2*Ia2*)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In overhead line distance protection, which relay type is least affected by ground arc resistance?","options":["Mho relay","Impedance relay","Reactance relay","Offset mho relay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A reactance relay measures only the imaginary component (X) of fault impedance and plots as a horizontal boundary on the R-X diagram, making its operating reach independent of arc resistance R."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A rectangular traveling wave of voltage magnitude V travels on a line of surge impedance Zc and hits an open-circuit terminal. The reflected voltage wave and total terminal voltage are:","options":["Reflected = -V, Total = 0","Reflected = +V, Total = 2V","Reflected = 0, Total = V","Reflected = +0.5V, Total = 1.5V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For an open-circuit termination (ZL = ∞), the voltage reflection coefficient is Γv = (ZL - Zc) / (ZL + Zc) = +1. The reflected surge is +V, causing terminal voltage to double to V + V = 2V."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An impedance has a value Z_old on base (MVA_old, kV_old). Its converted value Z_new on a new base (MVA_new, kV_new) is:","options":["Z_old * (MVA_new / MVA_old) * (kV_old / kV_new)²","Z_old * (MVA_old / MVA_new) * (kV_new / kV_old)²","Z_old * (MVA_new / MVA_old) * (kV_new / kV_old)","Z_old * (kV_old / kV_new)² / (MVA_new / MVA_old)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Base impedance is Zb = kV² / MVA. Converting per-unit impedance yields: Z_new = Z_old * (Zb_old / Zb_new) = Z_old * (MVA_new / MVA_old) * (kV_old / kV_new)²."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a solid Double Line-to-Ground (L-L-G) fault at generator terminals, the sequence currents satisfy:","options":["Ia1 = Ia2 = Ia0","Ia1 + Ia2 + Ia0 = 0","Ia1 = -Ia2 and Ia0 = 0","Ia1 = Ia2 and Ia0 = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In an L-L-G fault, the positive, negative, and zero sequence networks are connected in parallel (Va1 = Va2 = Va0). By Kirchhoff's Current Law at the common parallel junction, Ia1 + Ia2 + Ia0 = 0."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 50 Hz, 200 MVA alternator has a per-unit inertia constant H = 5.0 MJ/MVA. The stored kinetic energy in the rotor at synchronous speed is:","options":["200 MJ","500 MJ","1000 MJ","2000 MJ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stored kinetic energy is the product of generator MVA rating and per-unit inertia constant: Ek = S_base * H = 200 MVA * 5.0 MJ/MVA = 1000 MJ."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A transmission branch between Bus 1 and Bus 2 has a series impedance Z = j0.25 pu with zero mutual coupling. In the bus admittance matrix (Y_bus), the mutual element Y12 is:","options":["j4.0 pu","-j4.0 pu","j0.25 pu","-j0.25 pu"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Branch admittance is y12 = 1 / z12 = 1 / (j0.25) = -j4.0 pu. In Y_bus formulation, off-diagonal elements are defined as Y12 = -y12 = -(-j4.0) = +j4.0 pu."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"If mechanical power input to an alternator connected to an infinite bus is 0.8 pu and the power-angle equation is Pe = 1.6 * sin(δ) pu, the initial steady-state power angle (δ0) prior to any fault is:","options":["15° (0.262 rad)","30° (0.524 rad)","45° (0.785 rad)","60° (1.047 rad)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In steady-state equilibrium, electrical output matches mechanical input: Pe = Pm => 1.6 * sin(δ0) = 0.8 => sin(δ0) = 0.8 / 1.6 = 0.5. Hence, δ0 = sin⁻¹(0.5) = 30° (0.524 rad)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The safety factor of an insulator disc used in overhead transmission lines is defined as the ratio of:","options":["Flashover voltage to puncture strength","Puncture strength to flashover voltage","Working voltage to flashover voltage","Breakdown voltage to rated line voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Safety factor = (Puncture strength) / (Flashover voltage). It is designed to be around 10 so that in the event of an extreme overvoltage surge, the insulator flashes over safely through air rather than puncturing its solid porcelain shell."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Murray Loop test is widely employed on underground power cables for locating:","options":["Open-circuit cable conductor faults","Short-circuit and earth faults","Insulation resistance deterioration only","Dielectric loss breakdown"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Murray Loop test operates on the Wheatstone bridge principle to accurately pin down the distance to phase-to-ground or phase-to-phase short-circuit faults along an underground cable route."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In power system stability analysis, 'Critical Clearing Time' represents the maximum allowable time duration for which:","options":["A generator can operate above rated synchronous speed","A fault can persist on the network without the system losing transient stability","Circuit breaker contacts take to extinguish an arc","A transmission line can operate above its thermal ampacity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Critical clearing time is the maximum time a fault can remain on the system before circuit breakers open; if cleared after this time, the rotor angle exceeds the critical limit and synchronism is lost."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The surge impedance (Zc) of an ideal lossless transmission line is independent of:","options":["Line series inductance per unit length","Line shunt capacitance per unit length","Transmission line physical length","Conductor diameter and spacing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Surge impedance Zc = √(L / C), where L and C are per-unit-length quantities. Hence, surge impedance is purely a function of line geometry and dielectric permittivity, completely independent of total physical line length."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A grounding (earthing) transformer with a zig-zag connected winding is installed in a power network to:","options":["Step down voltage for auxiliary substation loads","Provide an artificial neutral point for grounding and zero-sequence fault current return","Trap 3rd harmonic currents in closed delta","Improve system voltage regulation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In an ungrounded system (such as a delta-connected network), a zig-zag transformer provides a path of low impedance to zero-sequence fault currents, establishing an artificial neutral reference."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Subsynchronous Resonance (SSR) is a dynamic phenomenon typically encountered in transmission systems utilizing:","options":["Shunt reactors for voltage control","Series capacitors for line reactance compensation","Underground DC cables","Synchronous condensers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Series capacitor compensation creates an electrical resonant frequency below the fundamental synchronous frequency (f_er < f0). If this frequency matches the natural mechanical torsional oscillation of the turbine-generator shaft, shaft damage can occur."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"According to Peek's formula under fair weather conditions, the corona power loss (P) in an overhead transmission line is proportional to:","options":["(f + 25) * (V - Vd)²","(f + 50) * (V - Vd)","f² * (V - Vd)²","f * √(V - Vd)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peek's empirical formula states: P = (242.2 / δ) * (f + 25) * √(r / d) * (V - Vd)² × 10⁻⁵ kW/km/phase, showing loss is directly proportional to (f + 25) and the square of excess voltage (V - Vd)²."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a Star-Delta (Y-Δ) transformer with the star neutral solidly grounded, its zero-sequence equivalent circuit features:","options":["Star-side switch closed to reference ground bus, delta-side switch open to external transmission line but closed to reference bus","Star-side switch open to reference bus, delta-side switch closed to line","Both sides series switches closed to external line","Zero-sequence circuit completely open with infinite impedance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Grounding the star neutral provides a return path from the reference bus into the star winding. The delta winding isolates zero-sequence currents from entering the downstream transmission line while providing an internal circulating path to reference."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a 4-conductor bundled phase with sub-conductor fictitious self-radius r' and square bundle spacing d, the equivalent self Geometric Mean Radius (Ds) is:","options":["1.09 * (r' * d³)^(1/4)","√(r' * d)","1.09 * (r' * d)","(r' * d³)^(1/4)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a 4-conductor square arrangement of spacing d: Ds = (r' * d * d * d√2)^(1/4) = [2^(1/8)] * (r' * d³)^(1/4) ≈ 1.09 * (r' * d³)^(1/4)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The swing equation governing rotor dynamics of a synchronous machine on a per-unit basis (with angles in electrical radians) is expressed as:","options":["(H / πf) * (d²δ / dt²) = Pm - Pe","(2H / ωs) * (d²δ / dt²) = Pm - Pe","(M * dδ/dt) = Pm - Pe","(H / 2πf) * (d²δ / dt²) = Pe - Pm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In per-unit form with angle δ in electrical radians: M = H / (π * f0). The mechanical balance yields: (H / πf0) * (d²δ / dt²) = Pm - Pe (pu)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An HVDC link consisting of two conductors—one operating at positive polarity (+V) and the other at negative polarity (-V) with respect to ground—is classified as a:","options":["Monopolar link","Bipolar link","Homopolar link","Back-to-back link"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A bipolar link utilizes two insulated conductors of opposite polarity (+V and -V) with a grounded neutral. If one line trips, the other conductor continues carrying half-rated power using ground return."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For long transmission lines (typically exceeding 300 km), line power transfer capability is primarily constrained by:","options":["Thermal conductor ampacity limit","Transient and steady-state stability limits","Corona loss limits","Voltage drop limit at 0.95 pu"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Short lines (<80 km) are limited by thermal conductor limits; medium lines (80-300 km) by voltage regulation; and long lines (>300 km) are governed primarily by steady-state and transient rotor angle stability limits."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The skin depth (depth of penetration δ) in a metallic conductor carrying alternating current is inversely proportional to:","options":["Frequency (f)","Square root of frequency (√f)","Square of frequency (f²)","Conductivity (σ) linearly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Skin depth is mathematically given by δ = √(1 / (π * f * μ * σ)). Hence, depth of current penetration decreases inversely with the square root of frequency (1 / √f)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In power flow analysis, a Slack (Swing) bus is essential because:","options":["System real power losses cannot be determined precisely prior to obtaining the complete voltage solution","Voltage angle must be fixed at 90° for stability","All generation must be concentrated at one bus","It is the only bus where reactive power limits exist"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because line I²R power losses depend on bus voltages and phase angles that are unknown before convergence, the slack bus must float to balance the mismatch between total generation, load, and transmission losses."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A suspension string has 3 identical insulator discs. The pin-to-earth capacitance is 10% (k = 0.1) of mutual disc capacitance. The string efficiency is approximately:","options":["86.8%","79.2%","92.5%","68.4%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"V1 = V1; V2 = V1*(1 + k) = 1.1 V1; V3 = V2 + (V1 + V2)*k = 1.1 V1 + 0.21 V1 = 1.31 V1. Total voltage V = V1 + V2 + V3 = 3.41 V1. String efficiency η = V / (3 * V3) = 3.41 / (3 * 1.31) = 3.41 / 3.93 ≈ 86.8%."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 200 km lossless transmission line has phase velocity v = 3 × 10⁵ km/s and operates at 50 Hz. If no-load receiving-end voltage is 220 kV, sending-end voltage (Vs) is approximately:","options":["215.2 kV","225.5 kV","200.0 kV","230.8 kV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase constant β = ω / v = (2π * 50) / (3 × 10⁵) = π / 3000 rad/km. For l = 200 km: βl = 200π / 3000 = π / 15 rad = 12°. A = cos(βl) = cos(12°) ≈ 0.9781. Vs = Vr * A = 220 kV * 0.9781 ≈ 215.2 kV."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 50 MVA, 11 kV alternator with Xd'' = 20% is connected through a 50 MVA, 11/132 kV transformer with X = 10% to a 132 kV transmission line. For a 3-phase fault at the 132 kV bus, fault current (in Amperes) is approximately:","options":["729 A","1250 A","450 A","2187 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Z_total = j0.20 + j0.10 = j0.30 pu. Base current on 132 kV side I_base = 50 MVA / (√3 * 132 kV) ≈ 218.7 A. Fault current If = I_base / Z_pu = 218.7 / 0.30 ≈ 729 A."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A power system has positive sequence reactance X1 = 0.30 pu, negative sequence reactance X2 = 0.30 pu, and zero sequence reactance X0 = 0.60 pu. The ratio of Single Line-to-Ground (L-G) fault current to Three-Phase (3-Φ) fault current at the same bus is:","options":["0.75","1.00","1.25","0.50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If_3ph = E / X1 = 1.0 / 0.30 = 3.33 pu. If_LG = 3E / (X1 + X2 + X0) = 3 / (0.30 + 0.30 + 0.60) = 3 / 1.20 = 2.50 pu. Ratio = 2.50 / 3.33 = 0.75 (75%)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two generators have incremental fuel costs: dC1/dP1 = 0.1 P1 + 20 and dC2/dP2 = 0.15 P2 + 15 (Rs/MWh). For economic dispatch supplying a 300 MW load (neglecting losses), generator outputs P1 and P2 are:","options":["P1 = 160 MW, P2 = 140 MW","P1 = 150 MW, P2 = 150 MW","P1 = 180 MW, P2 = 120 MW","P1 = 140 MW, P2 = 160 MW"],"answer":0,"explanation":"0.1 P1 + 20 = 0.15 P2 + 15 => P1 - 1.5 P2 = -50. Substitute P1 = 300 - P2: (300 - P2) - 1.5 P2 = -50 => 2.5 P2 = 350 => P2 = 140 MW, and P1 = 300 - 140 = 160 MW."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"If the supply frequency of a transmission line increases from 50 Hz to 60 Hz under identical voltage and conductor geometry, according to Peek's formula, the corona power loss increases by approximately:","options":["13.3%","20.0%","44.0%","0.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peek's formula specifies corona loss P ∝ (f + 25). At 50 Hz: (50 + 25) = 75. At 60 Hz: (60 + 25) = 85. Relative increase = (85 - 75) / 75 = 10 / 75 = 13.33%."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Shunt capacitors are installed at receiving-end substations primarily to:","options":["Increase line current","Supply reactive power (VARs) locally and improve load power factor","Absorb excess reactive power under light load","Reduce conductor skin effect"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shunt capacitors deliver leading reactive VARs directly at the load terminal, reducing the lagging VAR demand on generators and transmission lines, thereby raising terminal voltage and improving power factor."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A run-of-river hydroelectric plant without pondage is technically best suited to operate as a:","options":["Peak-load plant","Base-load plant","Standby reserve plant","Pumped-storage unit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without water storage (pondage), the plant cannot regulate water flow to follow load fluctuations; it must generate electricity steadily whenever river water flows, making it ideal as a base-load plant."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which insulator type is specifically used on dead-ends, sharp corners, and river crossings of overhead lines?","options":["Pin insulator","Shackle insulator","Strain insulator","Post insulator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Strain insulators (strings of suspension discs mounted horizontally) are mechanically designed to withstand severe longitudinal mechanical line tension at dead-ends, anchor towers, and sharp angle turns."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The visual critical voltage (Vv) of an overhead line compared to the critical disruptive voltage (Vd) is:","options":["Always lower (Vv < Vd)","Always higher (Vv > Vd)","Exactly equal (Vv = Vd)","Zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Air breakdown begins invisibly at critical disruptive voltage Vd. Visual corona glow requires a higher electric field gradient over a finite surrounding air sheath; hence, Vv is always higher than Vd."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Arcing horns fitted on overhead line insulator strings are designed to:","options":["Increase string capacitance","Divert power arc flashovers away from the porcelain insulator surface","Prevent conductor sag","Suppress corona discharge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During an overvoltage flashover, arcing horns establish a path of lower breakdown potential in air, guiding the high-temperature power arc away from porcelain shells to avoid cracking the discs."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an underground cable, the layer provided immediately over the metallic lead sheath to protect it from mechanical injury and soil corrosion is:","options":["Bedding","Armoring","Serving","Dielectric insulation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bedding (fibrous jute or hessian tape dipped in bitumen) sits directly on the lead sheath to protect it against corrosion and cushion it from mechanical indentation by the overlying steel armor."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During foul (rainy or stormy) weather conditions, the corona power loss on an overhead line:","options":["Remains unchanged","Decreases significantly","Increases substantially (by several times)","Drops to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Raindrops and surface moisture roughen the effective conductor profile and decrease the air density irregularity factor (m0), lowering disruptive critical voltage and multiplying corona power losses."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the receiving-end power circle diagram of a transmission line, the radius of the circle is proportional to:","options":["|Vs| * |Vr| / |B|","|Vs|² / |B|","|Vr|² / |A|","|Vs| * |Vr| / |A|"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In ABCD circle diagram formulation, the radius of the receiving-end circle in MVA is given by R = (|Vs| * |Vr|) / |B|, where |B| is the magnitude of the transfer impedance parameter."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When a transmission line is terminated in a load impedance equal to its surge impedance (ZL = Zc):","options":["The voltage reflection coefficient is zero and no standing waves are formed","Voltage doubles at the receiving end","Total reflection of voltage occurs with reversed sign","Sending-end current is strictly zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With ZL = Zc, reflection coefficient Γ = (ZL - Zc) / (ZL + Zc) = 0. The line behaves as an infinite line: all traveling wave energy is completely absorbed by the load with zero reflected wave."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"High-speed auto-reclosing circuit breakers are employed on EHV transmission lines primarily to:","options":["Limit subtransient fault MVA","Improve transient stability following the clearing of transient faults","Reduce skin effect","Equalize disc insulator voltages"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Over 80% of EHV faults are transient (lightning flashovers). Rapidly tripping and reclosing line breakers before the rotor angle swings too far restores synchronism and improves transient stability."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a power system, the zero-sequence impedance of a synchronous generator is:","options":["Always greater than positive sequence impedance","Much smaller than positive or negative sequence impedance","Equal to direct-axis synchronous reactance (Xd)","Infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because zero-sequence currents in the three stator phases are in-phase and displaced 120° in space, their MMF waves largely cancel in the air gap, resulting in very low armature reaction and small zero-sequence impedance."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Static VAR Compensator (SVC) typically consists of a shunt combination of:","options":["Thyristor-Controlled Reactor (TCR) and Thyristor-Switched Capacitors (TSC)","Series capacitor and series reactor","Rotary synchronous condenser and induction generator","DC chopper and inverter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An SVC coordinates Thyristor-Switched Capacitors (TSC) for step-wise reactive power generation with Thyristor-Controlled Reactors (TCR) for smooth continuous reactive power absorption."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The generalized ABCD parameter 'B' of a transmission line represents its:","options":["Short-circuit transfer impedance (in Ohms)","Open-circuit reverse voltage ratio (dimensionless)","Open-circuit transfer admittance (in Siemens)","Short-circuit forward current ratio (dimensionless)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From the transmission equation Vs = A*Vr + B*Ir, when the receiving end is short-circuited (Vr = 0), B = Vs / Ir, which is the short-circuit transfer impedance in Ohms."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a symmetrical 3-phase line, charging reactive power (VARs) generated by line capacitance is proportional to:","options":["Operating line voltage (V)","Square of operating line voltage (V²)","Inverse of line voltage (1/V)","Square root of line voltage (√V)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reactive power generated by line capacitance is Qc = ω * C * V_ph² = ω * C * (V_line / √3)² = (ω * C * V_line²) / 3. Per 3-phase: Qc_total = ω * C * V_line² (proportional to V²)."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A traveling voltage wave of 100 kV travels on a line of surge impedance Z1 = 400 Ω and enters an underground cable of Z2 = 50 Ω. The magnitude of the transmitted (refracted) voltage wave into the cable is:","options":["22.22 kV","50.00 kV","100.00 kV","177.78 kV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Refraction coefficient a = 2*Z2 / (Z1 + Z2) = (2 * 50) / (400 + 50) = 100 / 450 = 2/9. Transmitted voltage Vt = a * Vi = (2/9) * 100 kV ≈ 22.22 kV."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 50 Hz, 4-pole turbo-generator rated 100 MVA has an inertia constant H = 4.0 MJ/MVA. If mechanical input is 80 MW and electrical output is 50 MW, the accelerating power and rotor acceleration are:","options":["Pa = 30 MW, α = 675 electrical deg/sec²","Pa = 30 MW, α = 375 electrical deg/sec²","Pa = 130 MW, α = 450 electrical deg/sec²","Pa = 15 MW, α = 300 electrical deg/sec²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Accelerating power Pa = 80 - 50 = 30 MW = 0.30 pu. Rotor acceleration α = (180 * f / H) * Pa_pu = (180 * 50 / 4.0) * 0.30 = 2250 * 0.30 = 675 electrical deg/sec²."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A plant generation is P1 = 100 MW and system transmission loss is Ploss = 0.001 P1². The penalty factor (L1) for plant 1 is:","options":["1.00","1.25","1.11","0.80"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incremental loss ∂Ploss / ∂P1 = 2 * 0.001 * P1 = 0.002 * 100 = 0.20. Penalty factor L1 = 1 / [1 - (∂Ploss / ∂P1)] = 1 / [1 - 0.20] = 1 / 0.80 = 1.25."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a transmission line with generalized constants A = 0.98 ∠1° and B = 100 ∠75° Ω, operating at |Vs| = 220 kV and |Vr| = 220 kV, maximum steady-state real power receivable at the receiving end is approximately:","options":["353.3 MW","484.0 MW","242.5 MW","130.7 MW"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PR_max = (|Vs|*|Vr| / |B|) - (|A|*|Vr|² / |B|) * cos(β - α) = (220*220 / 100) - (0.98*220² / 100) * cos(75° - 1°) = 484 - 474.32 * cos(74°) = 484 - 474.32 * 0.2756 ≈ 353.3 MW."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase 50 Hz line has conductor radius r = 1 cm and spacing D = 2 m. The line-to-neutral capacitance per km is approximately:","options":["10.5 nF/km","5.25 nF/km","21.0 nF/km","1.05 nF/km"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cn = 2πε0 / ln(D/r) = (2π * 8.854 × 10⁻¹² * 10³) / ln(200 / 1) = (5.563 × 10⁻⁸) / ln(200) = (55.63 nF) / 5.298 ≈ 10.5 nF/km."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two generators with droop characteristics R1 = 4% and R2 = 6% operate in parallel. When a load increment of 100 MW occurs, the share taken by unit 1 (P1) and unit 2 (P2) is:","options":["P1 = 60 MW, P2 = 40 MW","P1 = 40 MW, P2 = 60 MW","P1 = 50 MW, P2 = 50 MW","P1 = 75 MW, P2 = 25 MW"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Load sharing is inversely proportional to droop: ΔP1 / ΔP2 = R2 / R1 = 6 / 4 = 1.5. Since ΔP1 + ΔP2 = 100 MW: 1.5 ΔP2 + ΔP2 = 100 => 2.5 ΔP2 = 100 => ΔP2 = 40 MW, and ΔP1 = 60 MW."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The pick-up value of an electrical protective relay is defined as the:","options":["Maximum current at which the relay contacts open","Minimum value of operating quantity (current/voltage) at which the relay initiates operation","Value of current during normal full-load conditions","Current value during final breaker trip"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pick-up value is the threshold magnitude of the actuating quantity (current or voltage) above which the relay starts its movement to close its trip contacts."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Thermal overload relays used for the protection of 3-phase induction motors operate on the principle of:","options":["Electromagnetic attraction","Bending of a bimetallic strip due to heating","Eddy current induction","Magnetostriction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thermal overload relays utilize bimetallic strips composed of two metals with different thermal expansion coefficients. Sustained overload current heats and bends the strip, mechanically releasing the trip latch."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In high-voltage substations, lightning arresters are connected:","options":["In series between phase conductor and circuit breaker","Between phase line and earth (in parallel with equipment to be protected)","In series with the transformer neutral","Across the circuit breaker contacts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lightning arrester (surge diverter) is installed between the line conductor and ground, providing a non-linear low-resistance discharge path to ground during overvoltage surges."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The reset (or drop-out) ratio of an overcurrent protective relay is the ratio of:","options":["Pick-up value to reset value","Reset value to pick-up value","Operating time to reset time","Fault current to pick-up current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reset ratio = (Reset value) / (Pick-up value). For an overcurrent relay, this ratio is always slightly less than 1.0 (typically 0.90 to 0.95) to prevent chattering."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Core Balance Current Transformer (CBCT) is specifically used for:","options":["Overload protection of transformers","Sensitive earth leakage (zero-sequence ground fault) protection","Overvoltage protection of busbars","Differential protection of alternators"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A CBCT encircles all three phase conductors. Under healthy conditions, the vector sum of phase currents is zero. During an earth fault, zero-sequence current creates a net flux that induces secondary current to trip the relay."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In oil circuit breakers, the arc is primarily extinguished by:","options":["Cooling and deionizing the arc through hydrogen gas generated from oil decomposition","Compressing air across the contact gap","Condensing oil vapors onto the contacts","Absorbing electrons via carbon powder"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The extreme heat of the arc vaporizes the surrounding transformer oil into a high-pressure gas bubble composed of ~70% hydrogen. Hydrogen has superior thermal conductivity and rapidly diffuses heat to deionize the arc."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An instantaneous overcurrent relay has an intentional operating time delay of:","options":["0.1 to 0.5 seconds","Zero seconds (operates in 10 to 30 milliseconds without intentional delay)","1 to 2 seconds","5 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instantaneous relays have no built-in time delay mechanism; they complete contact closure within 10 to 30 milliseconds as soon as the current exceeds the pick-up threshold."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Current chopping in circuit breakers occurs predominantly when interrupting:","options":["Heavy 3-phase symmetrical short-circuit currents","Low inductive currents (such as un-loaded transformers or shunt reactors)","Pure capacitive charging currents of long cables","Unity power factor resistive loads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When interrupting small inductive currents, the arc-quenching capability of air-blast or vacuum breakers can extinguish the arc prematurely before natural current zero, causing current chopping and severe transient overvoltages."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Transverse (split-phase) differential protection of an alternator is specifically provided to detect:","options":["Phase-to-phase external terminal faults","Inter-turn short-circuits within the same stator phase winding","Rotor earth faults","Over-frequency conditions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard longitudinal differential protection cannot detect inter-turn faults within the same phase because total input and output currents remain equal. Transverse protection compares currents between parallel paths of the same phase winding."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Over-fluxing protection in large generator transformers is achieved using a relay sensitive to the ratio of:","options":["Current to frequency (I / f)","Voltage to frequency (V / f)","Active power to reactive power (P / Q)","Negative sequence current to positive sequence current (I2 / I1)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core magnetic flux density is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to frequency (B ∝ V / f). A V/f relay protects the transformer core from overheating due to overexcitation."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When interrupting capacitive currents of an unloaded long transmission line, a circuit breaker is prone to:","options":["Severe current chopping","Voltage restrikes and dangerous voltage escalation across the contacts","Arc failure to initiate","Complete loss of dielectric strength in the bus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At current zero, capacitive voltage is at its peak. Half a cycle later, voltage across opening contacts reaches twice peak line voltage (2*V_peak). If the dielectric gap breaks down, high-frequency restrikes occur, causing severe overvoltage escalation."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The maximum torque angle (MTA) of a directional induction-cup overcurrent relay designed for phase faults is typically chosen to be:","options":["0° (current in phase with voltage)","30° to 45° current lagging voltage","90° current lagging voltage","45° current leading voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Short-circuit fault currents in power systems are highly inductive with power factor angles lagging by 60° to 75°. Relays are designed with an internal phase shift of 30° to 45° lagging so that maximum operating torque coincides with typical fault currents."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In pilot-wire feeder protection, the 'Translay' relay operates on the principle of:","options":["Voltage balance system with opposing induced secondary voltages","Circulating current system through long pilot wires","Carrier frequency modulation","Thermal replica matching"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Translay protection scheme utilizes a voltage-balance principle. Under through-fault conditions, voltages induced in the secondary windings at both line ends oppose each other, preventing circulating current in the pilot wires."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A reverse power relay on a grid-connected turbo-alternator operates to prevent:","options":["Alternator overspeeding","Motorization of the alternator (motoring action) and mechanical damage to turbine blades","Stator earth fault burns","Loss of synchronism due to weak field"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If prime-mover steam input fails, the alternator draws active power from the grid and runs as a synchronous motor. In steam turbines, the churning of entrapped steam causes severe overheating and damage to the low-pressure blades."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A circuit breaker chops an inductive current of 10 A in a system with L = 0.4 H and shunt capacitance C = 0.001 µF (1 nF). The prospective transient overvoltage appearing across the breaker contacts is:","options":["20 kV","50 kV","100 kV","200 kV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electrostatic energy trapped equals magnetic energy: 0.5 * C * e² = 0.5 * L * i² => e = i * √(L / C). Here: e = 10 * √(0.4 / 10⁻⁹) = 10 * √(4 × 10⁸) = 10 * 20,000 = 200,000 V = 200 kV."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 50 Hz power system, a circuit breaker interrupts fault current where effective system inductance up to the fault is L = 1.0 H and capacitance C = 0.01 µF. The natural frequency of restriking voltage (fn) is approximately:","options":["1591.5 Hz","3183.0 Hz","5000.0 Hz","50.0 Hz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Natural frequency fn = 1 / (2π * √(L * C)) = 1 / (2π * √(1.0 * 10⁻⁸)) = 1 / (2π * 10⁻⁴) = 10,000 / (2π) ≈ 1591.55 Hz."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A percentage biased differential relay has a slope setting of 20%. Secondary currents entering and leaving the protected zone during an external fault are 12 A and 10 A respectively. Will the relay trip?","options":["Yes, because spill current is 2 A and restraint is 1.1 A","No, because operating current (2 A) is less than restraining threshold (2.2 A)","Yes, because any non-zero spill current causes operation","No, because restraining current is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Operating current I_op = |I1 - I2| = |12 - 10| = 2 A. Restraining current I_rest = (I1 + I2) / 2 = (12 + 10) / 2 = 11 A. For a 20% slope setting, minimum operating current required is 0.20 * 11 = 2.2 A. Since 2 A < 2.2 A, the relay remains stable and does not trip."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An IDMT relay with a Time Multiplier Setting (TMS) of 0.5 has an operating time of 4.0 seconds at TMS = 1.0 for a given Plug Setting Multiplier (PSM). The actual operating time of the relay will be:","options":["1.0 second","2.0 seconds","4.0 seconds","8.0 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Operating time is directly proportional to the Time Multiplier Setting: Operating time = (Time at TMS = 1.0) * TMS = 4.0 * 0.5 = 2.0 seconds."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The knee-point voltage (Vk) of a protection-class current transformer (Class PS) required for a differential scheme with secondary fault current If = 20 A, CT secondary resistance Rct = 1.5 Ω, and total lead resistance Rl = 1.0 Ω (relay burden Rb = 0) is at least:","options":["50 V","100 V","70 V","25 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To avoid saturation on through-faults with full DC offset, the knee-point voltage must satisfy Vk ≥ 2 * If * (Rct + 2*Rl + Rb) = 2 * 20 * (1.5 + 2*1.0 + 0) = 40 * 2.5 = 100 V."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"On an R-X diagram, the operating characteristic of an offset mho relay is represented by:","options":["A circle centered at the origin","A circle passing through the origin","A circle whose perimeter encloses the origin","A straight line parallel to the R-axis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An offset mho relay characteristic is shifted so that its circular boundary encompasses the origin, providing directional backup protection for busbar faults behind the relay location."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The fusing factor of an HRC (High Rupturing Capacity) fuse is always:","options":["Less than 1.0","Greater than 1.0 (typically 1.2 to 1.5)","Equal to 1.0","Zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fusing factor is the ratio of minimum fusing current to rated current. Because a fuse must carry its rated current continuously without blowing, minimum fusing current is always higher, making the fusing factor greater than 1.0 (typically 1.2 to 1.5)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Sulfur hexafluoride (SF6) gas is widely used in high-voltage circuit breakers primarily because of its:","options":["High flammability and low density","High dielectric strength and superior electronegative arc-quenching capability","High carbon content","Ability to conduct electricity easily"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SF6 is a heavy, electronegative gas that readily absorbs free electrons from the electric arc path to form stable, immobile negative ions, rapidly building dielectric strength across breaker contacts at current zero."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Buchholz relay can be installed only on power transformers that are:","options":["Dry-type air-cooled","Oil-immersed with a conservator tank","Hermetically sealed without conservator","Outdoor natural air cooled only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Buchholz relay is a gas-actuated safety device installed in the inclined connecting pipe between the transformer main tank and the conservator tank of oil-immersed transformers."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a power system protection scheme, backup protection operates only when:","options":["The primary protection fails to clear the fault within a set time","System voltage rises by 10%","Load current drops below full load","The fault occurs outside the power station boundary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Backup protection operates with an intentional time delay as a second line of defense whenever the primary protective relays or main circuit breakers fail to isolate a fault."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An overcurrent relay having an Inverse Definite Minimum Time (IDMT) characteristic operates:","options":["Instantly for all current magnitudes","Faster for higher fault currents with a minimum definite operating time limit","Slower as fault current increases","Only when the direction of power flow reverses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An IDMT relay exhibits an inverse time-current relationship where higher fault current yields a shorter operating time, approaching a flat (definite) minimum operating limit for very heavy faults."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why must the secondary winding of an energized Current Transformer (CT) never be open-circuited?","options":["It produces dangerously high secondary induced voltages and core saturation","It causes secondary current to rise to infinity","It eliminates primary current completely","It causes mechanical vibration of primary conductors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opening the CT secondary removes the demagnetizing secondary MMF. The entire primary current then acts as excitation current, causing massive flux density, extreme core heating, and dangerously high induced voltage peaks across the open secondary terminals."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Vacuum Circuit Breakers (VCBs) are most commonly employed in which operating voltage range?","options":["Up to 1.1 kV (Low Voltage)","11 kV to 33 kV (Medium Voltage)","132 kV to 400 kV (EHV)","765 kV and above (UHV)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vacuum circuit breakers feature fast dielectric recovery over a very small contact separation gap, making them the standard choice for medium-voltage industrial and utility distribution switchgear (typically 3.3 kV to 33 kV)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The rated symmetrical making current of a high-voltage circuit breaker is related to its rated symmetrical breaking current by a factor of approximately:","options":["1.0","1.8","2.55","√2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Making current accounts for the maximum possible asymmetric peak offset during the first cycle of a short-circuit: I_make = √2 * 1.8 * I_break(RMS) ≈ 2.55 * I_break."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In percentage-biased differential protection for power transformers, the restraining coil carries:","options":["The difference of secondary CT currents |I1 - I2|","The sum or average of secondary CT currents (|I1 + I2| / 2)","Zero sequence current only","Fault current of phase R only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The operating coil carries the differential spill current |I1 - I2|, while the restraining coil carries the through-fault current (|I1 + I2| / 2) to prevent false tripping caused by CT saturation or tap-changer ratio mismatches during external faults."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Restriking voltage in a circuit breaker is defined as the:","options":["Power-frequency RMS voltage appearing across contacts after final arc extinction","High-frequency transient voltage appearing across contacts at current zero during the arcing period","Peak voltage during a direct lightning surge","Steady-state busbar voltage prior to fault occurrence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Restriking voltage is the transient high-frequency voltage that appears across circuit breaker contacts immediately at current zero during the arcing phase, attempting to re-establish the arc."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In air-blast circuit breakers, resistance switching (connecting a resistor in parallel with the main contacts) is used to:","options":["Increase total arcing time","Damp transient oscillations and reduce the Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage (RRRV)","Increase breaking capacity in DC circuits","Prevent contact bounce during closing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shunting a resistor across the main interrupting gap damps high-frequency transient recovery oscillations, limits switching overvoltage surges, and reduces the Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage (RRRV)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For the differential protection of a Star-Delta (Y-Δ) power transformer, the Current Transformers (CTs) on the star and delta sides must be connected respectively in:","options":["Star on star side, Delta on delta side","Delta on star side, Star on delta side","Star on both sides","Delta on both sides"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To compensate for the 30° phase shift introduced by the main transformer windings and eliminate zero-sequence spill currents on external ground faults, CTs are connected in Delta on the Star side and in Star on the Delta side."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Negative phase sequence (NPS) protection is provided on large alternators to protect against:","options":["Rotor overspeeding","Severe overheating of the rotor surface and damper windings caused by unbalanced stator currents","Loss of DC field excitation","High winding temperature due to balanced symmetrical overload"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unbalanced stator currents produce negative-sequence magnetic flux rotating opposite to the rotor at double synchronous speed (2*f), inducing large double-frequency currents that overheat rotor teeth, wedges, and retaining rings."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a three-zone distance protection scheme for transmission lines, Zone 1 is set to protect approximately:","options":["100% of the line instantaneously","80% to 90% of the protected line length instantaneously","120% of the line length with intentional time delay","50% of the line length only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To account for instrument transformer errors and line parameter variations without overreaching into the next line section, Zone 1 is set to cover 80% to 90% of the line length instantaneously (with no intentional time delay)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 132 kV, 3-phase circuit breaker system with phase-to-ground natural oscillation frequency fn = 20 kHz and peak factor 1.0, the maximum Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage (RRRV_max) is:","options":["3.38 kV/µs","6.77 kV/µs","13.54 kV/µs","23.45 kV/µs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Peak phase voltage V_peak = (132 / √3) * √2 ≈ 107.78 kV. Natural frequency ωn = 2π * fn = 2π * 20,000 rad/s = 0.12566 rad/µs. Maximum rate of rise RRRV_max = V_peak * ωn = 107.78 * 0.12566 ≈ 13.54 kV/µs."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"To achieve critical damping of transient recovery voltage oscillations in a circuit breaker interrupting a circuit with inductance L and capacitance C, the shunting resistance R should be:","options":["R = 2 * √(L / C)","R = 0.5 * √(L / C)","R = √(L / C)","R = 0.5 * √(C / L)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a parallel RLC resonant circuit during arc quenching, critical damping occurs when the damping ratio ζ = 1, which requires R = 0.5 * √(L / C)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An overcurrent relay with a CT ratio of 400/5 A and a plug setting of 125% experiences a fault current of 4000 A. The Plug Setting Multiplier (PSM) is:","options":["6.4","8.0","10.0","12.5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secondary fault current = 4000 * (5 / 400) = 50 A. Relay pickup current = 5 * 1.25 = 6.25 A. PSM = (Secondary fault current) / (Relay pickup current) = 50 / 6.25 = 8.0."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 33 kV, 3-phase circuit breaker has a rated symmetrical breaking current of 20 kA. Its rated 3-phase symmetrical breaking capacity is approximately:","options":["660 MVA","1143 MVA","1980 MVA","381 MVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rated 3-phase symmetrical breaking capacity = √3 * V_line * I_break = √3 * 33 kV * 20 kA ≈ 1.732 * 660 = 1143.15 MVA."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A star-connected generator neutral is grounded through a resistor R. If the differential relay is set to operate at a minimum earth fault current equal to 10% of full load, the percentage of the stator winding protected against phase-to-ground faults is:","options":["10%","50%","90%","100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because fault voltage is zero at the neutral point, faults near the neutral cannot produce enough current through the neutral resistor to trip the relay. If 10% of generated phase voltage is needed to operate the relay, the bottom 10% remains unprotected, meaning 90% of the winding is protected."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a carrier-current phase comparison protection scheme for an EHV transmission line, an internal fault is detected when:","options":["Carrier signals sent from both line terminals arrive in exact 180° phase opposition","Carrier signal transmission blocks tripping at both terminals","Fault currents at both line ends flow inward into the line (in-phase currents)","Carrier signal frequency drops by 50%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"During an internal fault, fault current feeds inward from both terminal ends into the line section (currents are approximately in phase). The transmitted carrier pulse blocks overlap rather than interleave, allowing the phase comparator to trigger breaker trip signals at both ends."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The arc chute in an air circuit breaker (ACB) extinguishes the arc primarily by:","options":["Compressing air into a supersonic blast","Splitting the arc into small series segments using splitter plates to increase arc resistance","Immersing contacts in mineral oil","Absorbing arc ions using charcoal filters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Metallic splitter plates inside the arc chute divide the long single arc into numerous short arcs in series. This introduces multiple anode-cathode voltage drops, elongates the arc, and rapidly cools it, elevating arc resistance until extinction."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Power Line Carrier Communication (PLCC) system, a line trap (wave trap) is connected:","options":["In parallel with the coupling capacitor","In series with the transmission line conductor","Between the line conductor and ground","Across the station lightning arrester"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A line trap is an LC parallel resonant tank connected in series with the high-voltage phase conductor. It offers negligible impedance to 50 Hz power frequency while presenting high impedance to high-frequency carrier signals (50 to 500 kHz), preventing them from leaking into substation equipment."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Restricted Earth Fault (REF) protection on a star-connected transformer winding is an example of:","options":["Non-unit backup protection","Unit protection with a strictly defined physical zone","Overcurrent grading protection","Thermal replica protection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"REF protection is a high-speed unit differential scheme that operates exclusively for internal ground faults occurring between the transformer star-neutral bushing and the phase CT terminals, remaining completely stable for external out-of-zone faults."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The operating torque in an induction-disc overcurrent relay is produced by the interaction between:","options":["Two in-phase magnetic fluxes","Each alternating magnetic flux and the eddy currents induced in the disc by the other flux","Static permanent magnet flux and leakage currents","Stray electric fields and disc capacitance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Torque develops because two spatially and temporally displaced AC magnetic fluxes induce eddy currents in the metallic disc; the interaction between each flux and the eddy currents induced by the other produces a net unidirectional driving torque proportional to sin(α)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Frame leakage protection is predominantly used for the safety and protection of:","options":["Transmission line towers","Indoor metal-clad switchgear busbars","Hydro turbine blades","Underground cable terminations only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Frame leakage (Howard) protection insulates the entire metal switchgear framework from concrete footings with a single bonding cable passing through a CT to ground, providing sensitive detection of busbar-to-enclosure insulation breakdown."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The dielectric strength across separating contacts recovers most rapidly in which circuit breaker medium?","options":["High-pressure air blast","Transformer mineral oil","High vacuum (< 10⁻⁶ torr)","Atmospheric air"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a high vacuum, dielectric recovery is exceptionally fast (up to tens of kV/µs) because metal vapor atoms emitted from contacts condense within microseconds onto surrounding shields, restoring vacuum insulation almost instantly at current zero."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Single-phasing of an energized 3-phase induction motor leads to:","options":["Immediate rotor overspeeding","Severe rotor and stator overheating caused by heavy negative-sequence currents","Zero current drawn from the remaining phases","Reversal of rotation direction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If one supply phase opens while running, the motor attempts to carry shaft load on the remaining two phases. This creates large negative-sequence current components that induce double-frequency rotor eddy currents, causing severe thermal overload."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Loss of excitation (loss of field) in a large grid-connected synchronous generator is reliably detected by a relay measuring:","options":["Zero-sequence current magnitude","Impedance/admittance looking into the generator terminals (offset mho relay)","Stator winding thermal resistance","Rotor mechanical acceleration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When field excitation is lost, the machine slips poles and absorbs large reactive power (VARs) from the grid, operating as an induction generator. Terminal impedance moves into the lower half (negative X) of the R-X diagram, entering the operating boundary of an offset mho relay."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Power Swing Blocking (PSB) functionality is incorporated in transmission distance relays to:","options":["Accelerate Zone 1 tripping during symmetrical 3-phase faults","Block distance relay tripping during stable power swings while allowing tripping on actual faults","Trip the breaker if power factor drops below 0.8","Prevent CT saturation during through-faults"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During electromechanical power swings, the apparent impedance locus moves slowly across relay impedance zones. A real fault causes an instantaneous jump. A PSB scheme measures impedance transition time (dZ/dt) and blocks tripping on slow swings."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Complete 100% stator earth fault protection of a large generator (including the neutral point) is achieved by utilizing:","options":["Standard percentage differential relays alone","Third harmonic voltage differential comparison between neutral and generator terminals","Inverse definite minimum time (IDMT) overcurrent relays","Negative-sequence directional relays"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard ground fault relays have a dead zone near the neutral where fundamental 50 Hz voltage is zero. Under normal conditions, 3rd harmonic voltage exists at the neutral; a ground fault near the neutral collapses this 3rd harmonic voltage, triggering the 100% protection relay."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The coupling capacitor in a Power Line Carrier Communication (PLCC) installation serves to:","options":["Provide high impedance to 50 Hz power current and low impedance to carrier frequencies","Provide low impedance to 50 Hz power and isolate carrier waves","Step down transmission line voltage for metering","Eliminate line charging currents"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capacitive reactance is Xc = 1 / (2πfC). For 50 Hz, Xc is several mega-ohms (blocking high voltage). For high-frequency carrier signals (100 to 500 kHz), Xc drops to a few ohms, injecting the carrier signal into the phase line."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In high-impedance differential protection of substation busbars, stability during severe external through-faults with one CT fully saturated is ensured by:","options":["Adding intentional 2-second time delays","Setting the operating voltage above the maximum prospective voltage across the relay circuit","Using iron-core CTs with low knee-point voltages","Shorting the CT secondary during external faults"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During through-faults with a fully saturated CT, all current is driven through the saturated CT's secondary resistance. By placing a stabilizing resistor in series, the relay voltage setting is placed higher than this worst-case voltage, ensuring through-fault stability."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A quadrilateral distance relay is often preferred over a circular mho relay for short lines and ground faults because:","options":["It occupies less panel space","Its resistive (R) and reactive (X) reach boundaries can be set independently, accommodating higher fault/arc resistance","It does not require voltage transformer (VT) inputs","It has zero operating time for all zones"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A quadrilateral characteristic allows independent adjustment of top reactive reach (X) and horizontal resistive reach (R). This prevents under-reaching caused by ground arc and tower footing resistance without risking overreach from heavy load encroachment."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an SF6 puffer-type circuit breaker, the blast of SF6 gas required to extinguish the arc is generated by:","options":["An external auxiliary gas compressor operating continually","The relative mechanical motion of the puffer cylinder and fixed piston during contact separation","Chemical decomposition of solid Teflon nozzles","Vaporization of liquid nitrogen inside the tank"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Puffer circuit breakers eliminate external compressors. When the breaker receives a trip command, the operating mechanism drives the moving cylinder against a stationary piston, compressing entrapped SF6 and blasting it through an insulating nozzle directly onto the arc."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A circuit breaker has contacts shunted by a resistor R for critical damping. If the equivalent system inductance up to the breaker is L = 10 mH and line capacitance is C = 0.01 µF, the required value of R is:","options":["250 Ω","500 Ω","1000 Ω","2000 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For critical damping of transient recovery voltage (TRV) oscillations, damping factor ζ = 1 requires: R = 0.5 * √(L / C) = 0.5 * √( (10 × 10⁻³) / (0.01 × 10⁻⁶) ) = 0.5 * √(10⁶) = 0.5 * 1000 = 500 Ω."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The transient recovery voltage across circuit breaker contacts is expressed as v(t) = V_max * [1 - cos(ωn * t)]. If the natural frequency of the circuit is fn = 10 kHz, the time taken to reach the first peak of restriking voltage is:","options":["25 µs","50 µs","100 µs","500 µs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Peak restriking voltage occurs when cos(ωn * t) = -1, which happens at ωn * t = π => 2π * fn * t = π => t = 1 / (2 * fn). For fn = 10 kHz (10,000 Hz): t = 1 / (2 * 10,000) = 1 / 20,000 s = 50 × 10⁻⁶ s = 50 µs."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a coordinated protective scheme, upstream Relay A backs up downstream Relay B. Relay B has an operating time of 0.35 s, breaker interrupting time is 0.10 s, relay overshoot time is 0.05 s, and safety margin is 0.10 s. The minimum operating time setting for Relay A should be:","options":["0.45 s","0.50 s","0.60 s","0.75 s"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total Time Grading Margin (TGM) = Breaker time + Overshoot time + Safety margin = 0.10 + 0.05 + 0.10 = 0.25 s. Operating time of upstream backup Relay A = (Time of Relay B) + TGM = 0.35 + 0.25 = 0.60 s."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A Class PS Current Transformer (CT) used in differential protection must deliver an internal secondary fault current of 40 A. If CT secondary winding resistance is 0.5 Ω, lead loop resistance is 1.0 Ω, and relay burden is 0.5 Ω, the minimum Knee Point Voltage (Vk) required is:","options":["40 V","80 V","160 V","200 V"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To prevent saturation under full DC offset conditions, minimum knee-point voltage is calculated as: Vk = 2 * If_sec * (Rct + Rlead + Rrelay) = 2 * 40 * (0.5 + 1.0 + 0.5) = 80 * 2.0 = 160 V."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a double-float Buchholz relay, a severe internal flashover fault producing a rapid surge of oil towards the conservator operates:","options":["The upper mercury switch to sound a warning alarm only","The lower hinged flap/vane to trip the circuit breaker directly","Both switches simultaneously without delay","Neither switch until oil completely evaporates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Minor incipient faults generate slow gas bubbles that collect in the top chamber, tilting the upper float to ring an alarm. A severe short-circuit generates violent oil movement (> 1.0 m/s) that directly forces the lower hinged vane, closing contacts to trip circuit breakers."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 3-phase, 220 kV (line-to-line) power system, a 3-phase ungrounded fault occurs at zero power factor. Assuming a first-pole-to-clear factor of 1.5 and an amplitude peak factor of 1.8, the prospective peak Transient Recovery Voltage (TRV) is approximately:","options":["359 kV","485 kV","560 kV","220 kV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Peak phase-to-neutral voltage V_ph_pk = (220 kV / √3) * √2 ≈ 179.63 kV. Peak TRV across the first opening pole = V_ph_pk * (First pole factor) * (Peak factor) = 179.63 * 1.5 * 1.8 ≈ 485.0 kV."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The ceramic cartridge of a High Rupturing Capacity (HRC) fuse is filled with packed quartz sand primarily to:","options":["Absorb heat, quench the arc, and chemically form high-resistance glass (silver silicate)","Conduct electric current efficiently","Provide insulation between fuse tags","Prevent mechanical expansion of the fuse wire"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the silver element vaporizes under short-circuit, the hot silver vapor reacts with the quartz sand filling to form an insulating bead of non-conducting silver silicate, rapidly quenching the arc."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Merz-Price circulating current scheme provides internal fault protection based on the principle of:","options":["Overcurrent sensing","Differential comparison of currents entering and leaving the protected zone","Negative-sequence voltage detection","Direction of power flow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Merz-Price protection is a unit differential system where identical current transformers (CTs) compare the current entering and leaving the winding. Any difference (spill current) triggers the operating coil."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Preece's law, the fusing current (I) of a round metallic fuse wire is related to its diameter (d) by:","options":["I ∝ d","I ∝ d²","I ∝ d^(1.5)","I ∝ √d"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Preece's formula states that under steady thermal equilibrium (heat generated = heat dissipated from surface), fusing current satisfies I = k * d^(1.5) (or d^(3/2))."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The secondary winding of a Current Transformer (CT) and Potential Transformer (PT) is always grounded to:","options":["Ensure safety of personnel and instruments against high primary voltages during insulation breakdown","Complete the secondary measuring circuit","Prevent CT core saturation","Eliminate secondary lead resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Grounding instrument transformer secondaries provides a low-impedance path to earth, ensuring substation operators and sensitive meters are not exposed to hazardous primary line voltages if inter-winding insulation fails."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The total interrupting time of a high-voltage circuit breaker is the time duration from:","options":["Fault initiation to contact separation","Trip coil energization to final arc extinction across all poles","Contact separation to maximum restriking voltage peak","Relay pickup to contact touch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total breaker clearing time consists of mechanism opening time plus arcing time, measured from the instant the trip coil is energized until final arc extinction in all three phases."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The burden of an instrument transformer (CT or PT) is expressed in:","options":["Ohms only","Volt-Amperes (VA)","Watts (W)","Amperes (A)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Burden represents the total electrical load connected to the secondary terminals, conventionally rated in Volt-Amperes (VA) at rated secondary current (e.g., 5 VA or 15 VA at 5 A)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"De-ion grid plates in an air circuit breaker extinguish the electric arc by:","options":["Splitting the arc and utilizing magnetic forces to drive it into cool metal plates","Injecting compressed CO2 gas","Flooding the contact chamber with transformer oil","Evaporating liquid nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Magnetic field forces drive the drawn arc upward into a series stack of steel de-ion plates, splitting it into series sub-arcs that rapidly cool and deionize at the metallic surface boundaries."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"On an R-X diagram (impedance plane), the operating characteristic of a directional Mho (admittance) relay is:","options":["A circle with its center at the origin","A circle passing through the origin and lying predominantly in the first quadrant","A straight line parallel to the resistance (R) axis","A parabola enclosing the origin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A standard mho relay is inherently directional; its operating locus is a circle that passes directly through the origin of the R-X plane, responding only to forward faults lying inside the circle."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When a circuit breaker interrupts an inductive short-circuit fault at zero power factor lagging, the power frequency recovery voltage across the contacts at current zero is:","options":["Zero","Equal to peak system phase voltage (V_max)","Equal to RMS phase voltage","Half of peak voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At zero power factor lagging, current lags system voltage by 90°. When fault current naturally passes through zero, system supply voltage is at its maximum peak value (V_peak = √2 * V_rms), stressing the contact gap severely."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To completely neutralize line capacitance during a single line-to-ground fault, a Peterson coil (arc suppression coil) of inductance L must be tuned according to:","options":["L = 1 / (3 * ω² * C0)","L = 3 / (ω² * C0)","L = 1 / (ω² * C0)","L = ω * C0 / 3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For resonant grounding, the inductive neutral current must equal the total 3-phase capacitive charging fault current: IL = 3 * IC0 => V_ph / (ωL) = 3 * V_ph * (ωC0) => L = 1 / (3 * ω² * C0)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Static protective relays provide which primary operational advantage over conventional electromechanical induction-disc relays?","options":["Higher thermal overload tolerance","Lower burden on instrument transformers, absence of moving parts, and faster response times","Zero sensitivity to electrostatic discharge","Immunity to power-frequency harmonics without filters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Static and microprocessor relays utilize semiconductor logic circuits; they eliminate mechanical friction, inertia, and contact bounce while imposing an extremely low burden (VA draw) on measuring CTs."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Distance relays for transmission line protection are fed with inputs from:","options":["Line CTs only","Busbar PTs only","Both line Current Transformers (CTs) and Potential Transformers (PTs)","DC station batteries only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Distance relays evaluate fault distance by computing line impedance Z = V / I, requiring instantaneous voltage signals from PTs/CVTs and fault current signals from line CTs."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Field failure (loss of excitation) protection of a synchronous generator is vital because the unexcited rotor:","options":["Draws heavy reactive power from the grid, accelerating to supersynchronous speed and overheating the rotor as an induction generator","Stops instantly due to reverse torque","Generates excessive DC offset voltage","Causes high 3rd harmonic currents in the neutral"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An unexcited generator slips poles and continues delivering active power by running as an induction generator above synchronous speed, drawing heavy reactive VARs from the grid and rapidly overheating rotor retaining rings and teeth."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An overcurrent relay is equipped with a high-set instantaneous element in addition to its IDMT element to:","options":["Isolate severe close-in faults instantaneously without waiting for IDMT grading delay","Improve relay thermal accuracy","Prevent nuisance tripping on transformer inrush currents","Measure earth leakage current"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Close-in faults generate massive fault currents; an instantaneous high-set element trips the breaker in 10-20 ms, preventing severe mechanical and thermal damage without compromising downstream IDMT time grading."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An impedance relay has a secondary setting of 10 Ω. It is connected via a CT ratio of 500/5 A and a VT ratio of 132 kV / 110 V. The primary impedance reach of the relay is:","options":["120 Ω","12 Ω","0.833 Ω","1200 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Primary impedance Z_pri = Z_sec * (VTR / CTR). Here VTR = 132,000 / 110 = 1200, and CTR = 500 / 5 = 100. Therefore: Z_pri = 10 * (1200 / 100) = 10 * 12 = 120 Ω."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An air-blast circuit breaker chops an inductive magnetizing current of 15 A before natural current zero. If circuit inductance is L = 0.2 H and line capacitance is C = 500 pF, the prospective peak overvoltage across the contacts is:","options":["150 kV","200 kV","300 kV","450 kV"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By energy conservation: 0.5 * C * V_peak² = 0.5 * L * i² => V_peak = i * √(L / C). Substituting values: V_peak = 15 * √(0.2 / (500 × 10⁻¹²)) = 15 * √(4 × 10⁸) = 15 * 20,000 = 300,000 V = 300 kV."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 11 kV, 50 Hz alternator has its neutral grounded through a 2 Ω resistor. The differential relay operates when secondary out-of-balance current exceeds 0.2 A with CT ratio 1000/5 A. The percentage of stator winding unprotected against single line-to-ground faults is approximately:","options":["3.15%","6.30%","12.60%","1.25%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Primary operating current I_op = 0.2 * (1000 / 5) = 40 A. Phase voltage V_ph = 11,000 / √3 ≈ 6351 V. If x is the unprotected fraction, induced voltage is x * V_ph. Fault current If = (x * V_ph) / R => 40 = (x * 6351) / 2 => x = 80 / 6351 ≈ 0.0126 (1.26%). If R = 10 Ω, x becomes 6.30%."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An IDMT relay with plug setting 5 A and CT ratio 200/5 A operates at a Plug Setting Multiplier (PSM) of 10. The primary fault current circulating in the line is:","options":["1000 A","2000 A","4000 A","5000 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secondary operating current = PSM * Relay plug setting = 10 * 5 A = 50 A. Primary fault current = Secondary current * (CT ratio) = 50 * (200 / 5) = 50 * 40 = 2000 A."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a dual-slope percentage-biased differential relay, the slope is increased beyond rated through-fault current to:","options":["Increase relay operating speed on light internal faults","Prevent false tripping caused by severe CT saturation during heavy external through-faults","Compensate for low power-factor loads","Detect secondary open circuits in potential transformers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under heavy external faults, current transformers can saturate non-linearly, generating large false spill currents. A dual-slope characteristic increases the restraint slope (e.g., from 20% to 80%) at high currents, maintaining absolute stability."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage (RRRV) across a circuit breaker contact gap reaches its maximum theoretical value when the undamped transient recovery voltage passes through:","options":["Its peak amplitude","Zero voltage crossing (t = 0+)","One-quarter of natural oscillation cycle","Final power frequency recovery peak"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transient recovery voltage is v(t) = V_max * [1 - cos(ωn * t)]. The rate of rise is d[v(t)]/dt = V_max * ωn * sin(ωn * t). This rate peaks when sin(ωn * t) = 1 (at t = π / (2ωn)), yielding RRRV_max = V_max * ωn."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Electrical interlocking between an isolator and its associated circuit breaker ensures that:","options":["The isolator can only be opened or closed when the circuit breaker is open","The circuit breaker opens automatically when the isolator closes","Both open simultaneously on fault","The isolator breaks the arc before the breaker opens"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mechanical and electrical interlocks prevent operating an isolator while it is carrying current; the isolator can only open or close when the breaker is open (no-load condition)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Unlike a surge diverter (lightning arrester), a surge absorber protects equipment by:","options":["Diverting surge energy to earth directly","Reducing the steepness of the wave front (rate of rise of voltage)","Increasing line surge impedance","Opening the circuit breaker contacts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A surge absorber (such as a series choke inductor shunted by a resistor or a capacitor to ground) flattens and absorbs the steep front of traveling overvoltage waves, reducing dV/dt stress on winding insulation."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary operating function of a protective relay in a power system is to:","options":["Interrupt the short-circuit fault current directly","Continuously sense abnormal operating conditions and issue a trip command to the circuit breaker","Regulate system voltage and frequency","Supply reactive power during faults"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A protective relay is a sensing and decision-making device; it detects abnormal electrical parameters and energizes the trip coil of a circuit breaker, which performs the physical current interruption."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Standard differential protection using pilot wires is generally impractical for protecting very long transmission lines (e.g., > 30 km) due to:","options":["High cost, resistance, and charging capacitance of long pilot wire cables","Absence of identical CTs","Zero-sequence current mismatch","High system frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pilot wire resistance causes high burden and signal attenuation, while inter-core capacitance leads to charging currents that can cause false tripping on through-faults over long distances."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A directional overcurrent relay requires which two quantities to determine the direction of power flow?","options":["Two line voltages","Operating current and a polarizing reference quantity (voltage or current)","Two line currents from the same phase","Frequency and power factor angle only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Directional relays compare the phase angle between the fault operating current and a reference polarizing quantity (typically line voltage from a PT or neutral current from a grounding CT)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which circuit breaker requires the least maintenance and has the longest electrical contact life?","options":["Minimum Oil Circuit Breaker (MOCB)","Air Blast Circuit Breaker (ABCB)","Vacuum Circuit Breaker (VCB)","Bulk Oil Circuit Breaker (BOCB)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vacuum circuit breakers have hermetically sealed interrupting bottles with zero oxidation, negligible contact erosion, and no gas or oil handling, giving them long contact life with virtually zero contact maintenance."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The time-current characteristic curve of a modern numerical overcurrent relay is programmed using:","options":["Mechanical spring tension adjustment","Mathematical equations standardized by IEC 60255 or IEEE C37.112","Oil dashpot viscosity variations","Copper shading ring position"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Numerical and digital relays calculate operating time using standard mathematical equations (such as Standard Inverse, Very Inverse, and Extremely Inverse defined by IEC 60255 or IEEE standards)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The thermal capability of a synchronous generator rotor to withstand unbalanced stator negative-sequence current is expressed by the law:","options":["I2 * t = K","I2² * t = K","I2 * t² = K","I2 / t = K"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Joule heating produced in the rotor teeth, wedges, and retaining rings by double-frequency eddy currents is proportional to I2² * t = K, where K is a machine-specific withstand constant (typically 5 to 30 s)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In parallel feeder protection, why are non-directional overcurrent relays used at the sending end, while directional overcurrent relays are used at the receiving end?","options":["To ensure faults on either feeder do not cause unnecessary tripping of the healthy parallel feeder via backfeed","Because sending-end fault current is always leading","Because receiving-end voltage is zero during faults","To reduce the cost of potential transformers at the sending end"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A fault on feeder 1 is fed directly from the source via feeder 1, and backfed from the receiving bus via feeder 2. Directional relays at the receiving end trip only for power flowing outward into the faulty feeder, preventing the healthy feeder from tripping."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In transmission line distance protection, an intermediate infeed of fault current between the relay and the fault causes the relay to:","options":["Overreach (measure less impedance than actual)","Underreach (measure more impedance than actual)","Trip instantaneously for all zones","Lose directional sensitivity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Current injected from an intermediate source increases the voltage drop across the downstream line section. The relay at the sending end sees an apparent impedance higher than actual (Z_apparent > Z_actual), causing it to underreach."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Rotor field winding ground faults in an alternator are typically detected using:","options":["Standard phase differential relays","DC voltage injection or AC low-frequency injection into the isolated field circuit","Negative-sequence current filters","Core balance current transformers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the excitation field circuit is isolated from ground under normal operation, a single earth fault produces no fault current. An auxiliary low-frequency AC or DC voltage is injected between the field circuit and shaft ground to detect leakage current."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The asymmetrical breaking current rating of a circuit breaker differs from the symmetrical rating because:","options":["It includes the DC offset component of short-circuit current present at the instant of contact separation","It accounts for harmonic distortion only","It measures peak value instead of RMS value","It assumes zero arc resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Short-circuit currents contain an exponentially decaying DC offset component depending on the fault inception angle. The asymmetrical breaking current RMS value equals √(I_ac² + I_dc²)."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Permissive Underreach Transfer Trip (PUTT) distance teleprotection scheme, the permissive trip signal is initiated by:","options":["Zone 2 element only","Zone 1 underreaching element","Reverse-looking Zone 4 element","Overvoltage relay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In PUTT, when a breaker's instantaneous Zone 1 (underreach) element detects a fault, it simultaneously trips its local breaker and transmits a carrier permissive signal to accelerate the remote terminal's Zone 2 clearing."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To prevent severe saturation in current transformers subjected to fully offset asymmetric fault currents, Class TPZ cores are designed with:","options":["Solid high-permeability nickel-iron laminations without air gaps","Small non-magnetic air gaps distributed in the core to provide a linear magnetizing characteristic","Massive copper shielding around the primary","Superconducting secondary windings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"IEC Class TPZ transient cores feature small air gaps that dramatically reduce remnant flux and linearize magnetic reluctance, keeping the AC secondary response linear even in the presence of long DC offset time constants."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 3-phase, 50 Hz power system, a circuit breaker clears a 3-phase fault at current zero. If effective inductance is L = 5 mH and capacitance to neutral is C = 0.02 µF, the Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage peaks at time t equal to:","options":["15.7 µs","31.4 µs","49.3 µs","98.7 µs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Natural frequency ωn = 1 / √(L*C) = 1 / √(5×10⁻³ * 0.02×10⁻⁶) = 1 / √(10⁻¹⁰) = 10⁵ rad/s. RRRV = V_pk * ωn * sin(ωn * t). The rate of rise reaches its maximum peak when sin(ωn * t) = 1 => ωn * t = π / 2 => t = π / (2 * ωn) = π / (2 × 10⁵) = 3.1416 / 200,000 s ≈ 15.7 µs."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A generator has a negative-sequence capability constant K = I2² * t = 10 s. If an unbalanced fault produces a sustained negative-sequence current of 0.5 pu, the maximum allowable clearing time before rotor damage occurs is:","options":["20 s","40 s","5 s","2.5 s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"From I2² * t = K, with I2 = 0.5 pu and K = 10 s: (0.5)² * t = 10 => 0.25 * t = 10 => t = 10 / 0.25 = 40 seconds."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"On two parallel identical transmission lines sharing common towers, zero-sequence mutual coupling (X0m) between the circuits causes distance relays on a single line to:","options":["Underreach for ground faults when both lines are in service, and overreach when the parallel line is grounded at both ends","Overreach under all operating configurations","Remain completely unaffected because X0m is zero","Lose directional sensitivity for phase faults"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mutual coupling induces an opposing voltage drop into the faulted line when both lines carry zero-sequence current in the same direction, making the line appear longer (underreach). When the parallel line is out of service and grounded at both ends, circulating current reverses the effect, causing overreach."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In an IDMT relay conforming to the IEC standard inverse equation t = 0.14 * TMS / [ (I/Is)^0.02 - 1 ], with TMS = 1.0 and Plug Setting Multiplier PSM = (I/Is) = 10, the operating time is approximately:","options":["2.97 s","1.50 s","0.50 s","4.25 s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For PSM = 10: 10^0.02 = 1.04713. Denominator = 1.04713 - 1 = 0.04713. Operating time t = (0.14 * 1.0) / 0.04713 ≈ 2.97 seconds."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 50 Hz numerical relay samples voltage and current signals at 1600 Hz (32 samples per cycle). To prevent anti-aliasing errors when computing fundamental phasors using discrete Fourier transform (DFT), the analog anti-aliasing low-pass filter cutoff frequency (fc) must be less than:","options":["50 Hz","400 Hz","800 Hz","1600 Hz"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem, to prevent frequency folding (aliasing), the analog pre-filter must eliminate all input frequency components above half the sampling frequency: fc ≤ fs / 2 = 1600 / 2 = 800 Hz."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 400 kV circuit breaker has a total 3-phase symmetrical breaking capacity of 25,000 MVA. The rated symmetrical breaking current (RMS) is approximately:","options":["36.08 kA","62.50 kA","25.00 kA","18.04 kA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Breaking MVA = √3 * V_line * I_break => 25,000 MVA = √3 * 400 kV * I_break => I_break = 25,000 / (1.73205 * 400) = 25,000 / 692.82 ≈ 36.08 kA."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) at an electrical junction is a direct consequence of the conservation of:","options":["Energy","Electric charge","Linear momentum","Magnetic flux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KCL states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a node is zero, which is based on the fundamental principle of conservation of electric charge (charge cannot accumulate at a node)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) around any closed loop is a direct consequence of the conservation of:","options":["Electric charge","Energy","Electric power","Angular momentum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KVL states that the directed sum of electrical potential differences (voltages) around any closed loop is zero, representing the principle of conservation of energy."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The form factor of a pure sinusoidal alternating voltage wave is defined as the ratio of:","options":["Peak value to RMS value (1.414)","RMS value to average value (1.11)","Average value to RMS value (0.90)","Peak value to peak-to-peak value (0.50)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Form Factor = (RMS value) / (Average value) = (Vm / √2) / (2Vm / π) = π / (2√2) ≈ 1.11."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The time constant (τ) of a series R-L circuit excited by a DC voltage is given by:","options":["R * L","L / R","R / L","1 / (R * L)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The time constant of an R-L series circuit is τ = L / R (in seconds), which is the time required for the current to reach 63.2% of its final steady-state value."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Two resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω are connected in parallel. Their equivalent resistance is:","options":["9 Ω","2 Ω","4.5 Ω","18 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Req = (R1 * R2) / (R1 + R2) = (6 * 3) / (6 + 3) = 18 / 9 = 2 Ω."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current phasor:","options":["Leads the applied voltage by 90°","Lags the applied voltage by 90°","Is in phase with the applied voltage","Lags the applied voltage by 45°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In an ideal pure inductor, induced back EMF opposes current growth, causing current to lag the applied terminal voltage by exactly 90° (power factor = 0 lagging)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The dual pair of resistance (R), inductance (L), and capacitance (C) in network duality are respectively:","options":["Conductance (G), Capacitance (C), and Inductance (L)","Admittance (Y), Reactance (X), and Susceptance (B)","Conductance (G), Resistance (R), and Impedance (Z)","Impedance (Z), Admittance (Y), and Current (I)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In planar network duality, resistance R is dual to conductance G, inductance L is dual to capacitance C, and mesh loops are dual to nodes."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"According to the Maximum Power Transfer Theorem, maximum active power is delivered from an AC source with internal impedance Zth = Rth + jXth to a variable load impedance ZL when:","options":["ZL = Zth","ZL = Rth - jXth (ZL = Zth*)","ZL = Rth + j0","ZL = jXth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For maximum active power transfer in an AC network with variable complex load impedance, the load impedance must be the complex conjugate of the source Thevenin impedance: ZL = Zth* = Rth - jXth."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a series RLC resonant circuit, the impedance at resonance is:","options":["Purely reactive and maximum","Purely resistive and minimum (Z = R)","Zero","Purely capacitive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At series resonance, inductive reactance cancels capacitive reactance (XL = XC), leaving net impedance purely resistive and at its minimum value (Zmin = R), resulting in maximum circuit current."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Superposition Theorem is valid only for circuits consisting of:","options":["Linear, bilateral, and active/passive elements","Non-linear and unilateral elements","Linear, time-invariant elements with dependent sources only","Elements with exponential V-I characteristics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Superposition theorem relies strictly on the mathematical property of linearity (homogeneity and additivity) and bilateral circuit characteristics."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a two-port network to be reciprocal, the transmission (ABCD) parameters and impedance (Z) parameters must satisfy respectively:","options":["AD - BC = 1 and z12 = z21","A = D and z11 = z22","AD - BC = 0 and z12 = -z21","AB - CD = 1 and z12 = z21"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A two-port network is reciprocal if z12 = z21 for Z-parameters, y12 = y21 for Y-parameters, h12 = -h21 for hybrid parameters, and AD - BC = 1 for ABCD parameters."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"At the instant of switching (t = 0+) for an initially uncharged network, an inductor and a capacitor behave respectively as:","options":["Short circuit and Open circuit","Open circuit and Short circuit","Short circuit and Short circuit","Open circuit and Open circuit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because current through an inductor cannot change instantaneously (i(0+) = i(0-) = 0), it behaves as an open circuit. Because voltage across a capacitor cannot change instantaneously (v(0+) = v(0-) = 0), it acts as a short circuit."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Millman's Theorem is primarily used to find the equivalent voltage source for a combination of:","options":["Several parallel voltage sources having internal series impedances","Several series voltage sources with parallel resistances","A closed loop containing only inductors","A delta-connected purely reactive load"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Millman's theorem directly reduces multiple parallel branches, each containing an ideal voltage source in series with an internal impedance, into a single equivalent voltage source Veq = (Σ Vi*Yi) / (Σ Yi)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In graph theory of an electrical network having 'n' nodes and 'b' branches, the number of fundamental cut-sets (tree branches) and fundamental loops (links) are respectively:","options":["(n - 1) and (b - n + 1)","(b - n + 1) and (n - 1)","n and (b - n)","(n + 1) and (b - n - 1)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A tree of a connected graph contains (n - 1) tree branches (twigs), defining (n - 1) fundamental cut-sets. The remaining links l = b - (n - 1) = b - n + 1 define the fundamental loop ties."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a series RLC circuit excited by a step DC voltage, the condition for critical damping is given by:","options":["R = 2 * √(L / C)","R < 2 * √(L / C)","R > 2 * √(L / C)","R = 0.5 * √(L / C)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The characteristic equation is s² + (R/L)s + (1/LC) = 0. Critical damping occurs when damping factor ζ = 1 (roots are real and equal), yielding (R / 2L)² = 1 / LC => R = 2 * √(L / C)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A series RLC circuit has R = 10 Ω, L = 0.5 H, and C = 50 µF. The resonant frequency (f0) and Quality factor (Q0) are approximately:","options":["f0 = 31.83 Hz, Q0 = 10.0","f0 = 50.00 Hz, Q0 = 5.0","f0 = 31.83 Hz, Q0 = 31.62","f0 = 100.0 Hz, Q0 = 1.0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resonant angular frequency ω0 = 1 / √(L*C) = 1 / √(0.5 * 50×10⁻⁶) = 1 / √(25×10⁻⁶) = 1 / (5×10⁻³) = 200 rad/s. Resonant frequency f0 = 200 / (2π) ≈ 31.83 Hz. Quality factor Q0 = (ω0 * L) / R = (200 * 0.5) / 10 = 100 / 10 = 10.0."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For an ideal parallel RLC circuit with elements R, L, and C connected across a current source, the bandwidth (BW in rad/s) and Quality factor (Qp) are given by:","options":["BW = 1 / (R * C), Qp = R * √(C / L)","BW = R / L, Qp = (1/R) * √(L / C)","BW = R * C, Qp = R / (ω0 * L)","BW = 1 / √(LC), Qp = ω0 * R * C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a parallel RLC circuit, damping factor α = 1 / (2RC). Bandwidth BW = 2α = 1 / (RC) rad/s. Quality factor Qp = ω0 / BW = ω0 * R * C = R * √(C / L)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A DC voltage of 100 V is suddenly applied at t = 0 to a series circuit with R = 20 Ω and L = 0.1 H. The initial rate of rise of current (di/dt at t = 0+) is:","options":["0 A/s","500 A/s","1000 A/s","2000 A/s"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At t = 0+, current through the inductor is zero (i(0+) = 0), so voltage drop across resistor is 0 V. The entire 100 V appears across the inductor: L * (di/dt) = V => 0.1 * (di/dt) = 100 => di/dt = 100 / 0.1 = 1000 A/s."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For an LC driving-point impedance function Z(s) to be physically realizable (Foster's Reactance Theorem), its poles and zeros must:","options":["Lie strictly in the right-half of the s-plane","Lie on the imaginary (jω) axis, alternate with each other, and have strictly positive real residues","Lie on the negative real axis and alternate","Be identical multiple roots on the jω axis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"According to Foster's theorem for pure lossless LC networks, all poles and zeros are simple, lie exclusively on the jω (imaginary) axis, alternate with each other, and dX/dω is strictly positive."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a two-port network, the hybrid parameters are h11 = 20 Ω, h12 = 0.05, h21 = -50, and h22 = 0.2 mS. The parameter z22 is:","options":["5000 Ω","20 Ω","1000 Ω","250 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From two-port parameter conversion relationships: z22 = 1 / h22. Substituting h22 = 0.2 mS = 0.2 × 10⁻³ S: z22 = 1 / (0.2 × 10⁻³) = 1000 / 0.2 = 5000 Ω."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Thevenin's theorem, any linear bilateral two-terminal network can be replaced by:","options":["An ideal current source in parallel with an impedance","A single voltage source in series with an impedance","An ideal current source in series with an impedance","A single voltage source in parallel with an impedance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thevenin's theorem reduces any linear two-terminal circuit to an open-circuit voltage source (Vth) in series with an equivalent internal impedance (Zth)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Norton's equivalent circuit across two terminals consists of:","options":["A short-circuit current source in parallel with Norton impedance","An open-circuit voltage source in series with Norton impedance","An ideal current source in series with Norton resistance","An ideal voltage source in parallel with Norton resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Norton's theorem states that any linear active network can be simplified to an equivalent short-circuit current source (In) in parallel with an equivalent internal impedance (Zn = Zth)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an AC circuit, active power (P) is related to apparent power (S) and power factor (cos φ) by:","options":["P = S / cos φ","P = S * cos φ","P = S * sin φ","P = S * tan φ"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Active (real) power is the product of apparent power (S = V * I) and the circuit power factor: P = V * I * cos φ = S * cos φ (expressed in Watts)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The energy stored in the magnetic field of an inductor carrying current I is given by:","options":["0.5 * L * I","0.5 * L * I²","L * I²","0.5 * L² * I"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The magnetic energy stored in an inductor of inductance L conducting a steady current I is E = (1/2) * L * I² Joules."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The electrostatic energy stored in a capacitor of capacitance C charged to voltage V is:","options":["0.5 * C * V²","0.5 * C² * V","C * V²","0.5 * Q * V²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The electrostatic potential energy stored in an electric field between capacitor plates is E = (1/2) * C * V² = (1/2) * Q * V = Q² / (2C) Joules."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three identical resistors of resistance R are connected in delta. The equivalent resistance of each branch in an equivalent star connection is:","options":["3R","R / 3","R / √3","R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a balanced delta-to-star conversion: R_star = (R * R) / (R + R + R) = R² / (3R) = R / 3."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a series R-C circuit connected to a constant DC voltage source, the charging time constant (τ) is:","options":["R / C","C / R","R * C","1 / (R * C)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The time constant of a series R-C circuit is τ = R * C (in seconds), which is the time taken for the capacitor voltage to rise to 63.2% of its maximum steady-state DC voltage."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A practical AC source has internal impedance Zs = Rs + jXs. If the load is constrained to be a pure variable resistance (RL), maximum power is delivered to the load when:","options":["RL = Rs","RL = Xs","RL = √(Rs² + Xs²)","RL = Rs + Xs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the load is restricted to pure resistance, reactive tuning is impossible; maximum active power transfer occurs when load resistance equals the magnitude of the source impedance: RL = |Zs| = √(Rs² + Xs²)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Tellegen's theorem is applicable to any lumped electrical network provided that:","options":["The elements are strictly linear and time-invariant","Kirchhoff's current and voltage laws (KCL and KVL) are satisfied, regardless of linearity or bilateral behavior","The circuit operates solely under sinusoidal steady-state AC","The network contains no independent sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tellegen's theorem relies strictly on network topology and energy conservation: Σ (vk * ik) = 0. It holds for linear, non-linear, active, passive, time-variant, or time-invariant circuits as long as KCL and KVL are satisfied."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Reciprocity theorem fails to apply to circuits containing:","options":["Dependent sources or unilateral/non-linear devices","Pure inductors and capacitors","Transformers with ideal magnetic coupling","Multiple independent voltage sources"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reciprocity requires the network to consist exclusively of linear, passive, and bilateral components. Dependent sources, operational amplifiers, and unilateral non-linear devices violate reciprocity."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a parallel resonant circuit (anti-resonance) consisting of an inductor coil (R in series with L) in parallel with capacitor C, the dynamic impedance at resonance is:","options":["R / (L * C)","L / (C * R)","√(L / C)","C / (L * R)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At parallel resonance, reactive branch currents cancel out. The dynamic impedance peaks at its maximum purely resistive value: Zd = L / (C * R) Ohms."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Compensation Theorem is particularly useful in circuit analysis for calculating:","options":["Norton equivalent current of a three-phase generator","The change in branch currents resulting from a small impedance variation in one branch","The total energy stored in a coupled inductor network","Resonant frequency of a double-tuned filter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The compensation theorem states that an impedance variation ΔZ in a branch can be replaced by an opposing compensation voltage source Vc = I * ΔZ, allowing straightforward calculation of modified branch currents."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a two-port network, the hybrid parameter h21 expressed in terms of open-circuit impedance parameters (Z) is:","options":["-z21 / z22","z12 / z11","z21 / z11","-z12 / z22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By definition, h21 = (I2 / I1) when V2 = 0. From the second impedance equation: V2 = z21*I1 + z22*I2 = 0 => z22*I2 = -z21*I1 => h21 = I2 / I1 = -z21 / z22."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For an R-C driving-point impedance function Z(s), its poles and zeros must satisfy which condition?","options":["Lie on the negative real axis, alternate with each other, and have a pole closest to the origin","Lie on the negative real axis, alternate with each other, and have a zero closest to the origin","Lie strictly on the imaginary (jω) axis","Lie in the right half of the complex s-plane"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an RC driving-point impedance Z_RC(s), poles and zeros lie strictly on the negative real axis and interlace. Because a capacitor behaves as an open circuit at DC (s = 0), the critical frequency closest to (or at) the origin must be a pole."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A series RLC circuit is excited by a sinusoidal voltage v(t) = 100 sin(1000t) V. If R = 30 Ω, L = 50 mH, and C = 20 µF, the steady-state current i(t) is:","options":["3.33 sin(1000t) A","2.00 sin(1000t - 45°) A","3.33 sin(1000t + 90°) A","1.41 sin(1000t) A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At ω = 1000 rad/s: XL = ω*L = 1000 * 0.05 = 50 Ω. XC = 1 / (ω*C) = 1 / (1000 * 20×10⁻⁶) = 50 Ω. Because XL = XC = 50 Ω, the circuit is at resonance. Net impedance Z = R = 30 Ω. Current i(t) = (100 / 30) sin(1000t) = 3.33 sin(1000t) A."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In the two-wattmeter method measuring balanced 3-phase power, wattmeter readings are W1 = 10 kW and W2 = 5 kW. The total reactive power (Q) of the load is approximately:","options":["8.66 kVAR","5.00 kVAR","15.00 kVAR","2.89 kVAR"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total 3-phase reactive power in the two-wattmeter method is Q = √3 * (W1 - W2) = √3 * (10 - 5) = √3 * 5 = 1.732 * 5 ≈ 8.66 kVAR."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 100 µF capacitor is charged to 50 V and then discharged across a 10 kΩ resistor at t = 0. The time required for the capacitor voltage to decay to approximately 18.4 V is:","options":["0.5 s","1.0 s","2.0 s","5.0 s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Discharge voltage follows v(t) = V0 * e^(-t / τ). Time constant τ = R * C = 10,000 * (100 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.0 s. At t = τ = 1.0 s, v(1.0) = 50 * e⁻¹ = 50 * 0.3679 ≈ 18.39 V ≈ 18.4 V."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The Laplace transform of a current is I(s) = (2s + 5) / [s(s + 1)(s + 2)]. Using the Final Value Theorem, the steady-state current i(∞) is:","options":["0 A","2.5 A","1.25 A","5.0 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By the Final Value Theorem: i(∞) = lim_{s->0} [s * I(s)] = lim_{s->0} [ (2s + 5) / ((s + 1)(s + 2)) ] = (0 + 5) / ((1)(2)) = 5 / 2 = 2.5 A (valid since poles of s*I(s) lie at s = -1 and s = -2 in the LHP)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a connected linear graph having 6 nodes and 9 branches, the rank of its reduced incidence matrix is:","options":["4","5","6","9"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The rank of the incidence matrix (and the reduced incidence matrix) of any connected graph with n nodes is (n - 1). Here n = 6, so rank = 6 - 1 = 5."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two identical two-port networks, each having a transmission matrix T = [[1, 2], [0, 1]], are connected in cascade. The resulting overall transmission matrix T_total is:","options":["[[1, 4], [0, 1]]","[[2, 4], [0, 2]]","[[1, 2], [0, 1]]","[[1, 0], [4, 1]]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For cascaded two-port networks, overall transmission matrices multiply: T_total = T1 * T2 = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] * [[1, 2], [0, 1]] = [[(1*1 + 2*0), (1*2 + 2*1)], [(0*1 + 1*0), (0*2 + 1*1)]] = [[1, 4], [0, 1]]."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In magnetically coupled coils, if current enters the dotted terminal of both coils, the mutually induced EMF:","options":["Aids the self-induced EMF (+M)","Opposes the self-induced EMF (-M)","Is always zero","Becomes independent of frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to the dot convention, when current enters the dotted terminal of both coils, mutual flux aids the self-flux of each coil, making the mutual term positive (+M)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under the condition of maximum power transfer to a load, the efficiency of power transfer is:","options":["100%","75%","50%","25%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At maximum power transfer, internal source resistance equals load resistance (Rth = RL), causing equal power dissipation in both source and load, which yields an efficiency of exactly 50%."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An ideal independent current source has an internal resistance of:","options":["Zero","Infinite","1.0 Ω","Negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ideal current source delivers its rated current irrespective of terminal voltage, requiring its internal shunt resistance to be infinite (open circuit)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three identical resistors of 10 Ω each are connected in a star network. The resistance of each branch in the equivalent delta network is:","options":["3.33 Ω","10 Ω","30 Ω","60 Ω"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a balanced star-to-delta conversion: R_delta = 3 * R_star = 3 * 10 = 30 Ω."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the two-wattmeter method measuring 3-phase power, if the load power factor is 0.5 lagging, the reading of one of the wattmeters is:","options":["Zero","Double the other wattmeter","Negative","Equal to the other wattmeter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power factor cos φ = 0.5 means φ = 60°. Wattmeter readings are W1 = VL*IL*cos(30° - 60°) = VL*IL*cos(-30°) and W2 = VL*IL*cos(30° + 60°) = VL*IL*cos(90°) = 0."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a balanced star-connected 3-phase AC system, line voltage (VL) relates to phase voltage (Vph) as:","options":["VL = Vph","VL = √3 * Vph and leads Vph by 30°","VL = Vph / √3","VL = 3 * Vph"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a star system, line voltage is the phasor difference between two phase voltages: VL = √3 * Vph, and the line voltage phasor leads its component phase voltage phasor by 30°."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Nodal analysis of an electrical network is fundamentally formulated using:","options":["Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)","Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) and Ohm's Law","Superposition Theorem","Maximum Power Transfer Theorem"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nodal analysis assigns node voltages and applies KCL at every non-reference node, expressing branch currents in terms of node voltages using Ohm's Law."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A two-port network is electrically symmetrical if which conditions are satisfied?","options":["z11 = z22 and A = D","z12 = z21 and AD - BC = 1","z11 = -z22 and B = C","y11 = y22 and AD - BC = 0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Symmetry means the network appears identical when viewed from either port: z11 = z22 for Z-parameters, y11 = y22 for Y-parameters, and A = D for transmission (ABCD) parameters."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a series RLC resonant circuit, at the half-power cutoff frequencies (f1 and f2), circuit impedance magnitude is:","options":["R / √2","√2 * R","2 * R","R / 2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At resonance, Z = R and current is Imax. At half-power frequencies, current drops to Imax / √2, which requires total impedance to rise to |Z| = √2 * R (power factor angle φ = ±45°)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two coupled coils have self-inductances L1 = 4 H and L2 = 9 H. If mutual inductance is M = 4.8 H, the coupling coefficient (k) is:","options":["0.53","0.80","0.60","0.72"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coupling coefficient k = M / √(L1 * L2) = 4.8 / √(4 * 9) = 4.8 / √36 = 4.8 / 6 = 0.80."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Substitution Theorem states that any branch in a linear bilateral network carrying current I with terminal voltage V can be replaced by:","options":["A short circuit only","An ideal voltage source of value V or an ideal current source of value I","An open circuit only","A resistor of arbitrary value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If branch voltage V and current I are known, the branch can be substituted by an independent voltage source V or current source I without altering any voltages or currents elsewhere in the network."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A polynomial P(s) is classified as a Hurwitz polynomial if:","options":["All of its roots lie strictly in the open left-half of the complex s-plane","All of its roots lie on the imaginary axis","Its coefficients have alternating signs","All coefficients are negative"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A polynomial P(s) is Hurwitz if all its roots have real parts that are strictly negative (lying in the open left-half of the s-plane), which is a necessary condition for network stability."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The topological dual of a series RLC circuit excited by a voltage source v(t) is:","options":["A parallel RLC circuit excited by a current source i(t)","A series RLC circuit excited by a current source","A parallel R-L circuit with DC excitation","A bridge network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By duality principles, a series mesh becomes a parallel node pair, a voltage source maps to a current source, and series R, L, C elements map to parallel G, C, L elements."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For an ideal transformer with turns ratio 1:n, which of the following two-port parameter matrices does NOT exist?","options":["ABCD transmission parameters","Open-circuit impedance (Z) parameters and short-circuit admittance (Y) parameters","Hybrid (h) parameters","Inverse hybrid (g) parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ideal transformer has V1 = V2/n and I1 = -n*I2. Because it cannot be open-circuited or short-circuited without singular indeterminate relationships, its Z and Y parameter matrices do not exist."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A capacitor C = 10 µF has an initial voltage V(0-) = 20 V. At t = 0, it discharges across a resistor R = 50 kΩ. The initial current i(0+) and current at t = 0.5 s are:","options":["i(0+) = 0.4 mA, i(0.5s) = 0.147 mA","i(0+) = 2.0 mA, i(0.5s) = 0.736 mA","i(0+) = 0.4 mA, i(0.5s) = 0.054 mA","i(0+) = 4.0 mA, i(0.5s) = 1.47 mA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"i(0+) = V(0+) / R = 20 / 50,000 = 0.4 mA. Time constant τ = R * C = 50,000 * (10 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.5 s. At t = 0.5 s = 1τ: i(0.5) = i(0+) * e⁻¹ = 0.4 * 0.36788 ≈ 0.147 mA."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two identical inductors of 5 H each are connected in series. In series-aiding, total inductance is 14 H. In series-opposing, equivalent inductance is:","options":["6 H","8 H","10 H","4 H"],"answer":0,"explanation":"L_aiding = L1 + L2 + 2M => 14 = 5 + 5 + 2M => 2M = 4 H (M = 2 H). L_opposing = L1 + L2 - 2M = 5 + 5 - 4 = 6 H."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A series RLC circuit has a resonant frequency of 100 kHz and a bandwidth of 5 kHz. The Quality Factor (Q) and upper cutoff frequency (f2) are:","options":["Q = 20, f2 = 102.5 kHz","Q = 50, f2 = 105.0 kHz","Q = 20, f2 = 105.0 kHz","Q = 10, f2 = 102.5 kHz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Q = f0 / BW = 100 kHz / 5 kHz = 20. Upper cutoff frequency f2 = f0 + (BW / 2) = 100 + 2.5 = 102.5 kHz."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An uncharged LC circuit (L = 1 H, C = 1 F) is excited by a unit step voltage source u(t) at t = 0. The expression for current i(t) for t > 0 is:","options":["sin(t) A","cos(t) A","(1 - cos(t)) A","e^(-t) sin(t) A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"V(s) = 1/s. Z(s) = sL + 1/(sC) = s + 1/s = (s² + 1)/s. Current I(s) = V(s) / Z(s) = (1/s) / [(s² + 1)/s] = 1 / (s² + 1). Taking the inverse Laplace transform yields i(t) = sin(t) A."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a connected linear graph having 4 nodes and 6 branches, the number of fundamental loops and fundamental cutsets are respectively:","options":["3 and 3","2 and 4","4 and 2","5 and 1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Number of twigs (tree branches) = n - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3, which equals the number of fundamental cutsets. Number of links = b - n + 1 = 6 - 4 + 1 = 3, which equals the number of fundamental loops."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A driving-point impedance function is given by Z(s) = (s + 2) / (s² + 2s + 2). This network is:","options":["Strictly passive and stable because all poles and zeros lie in the left half of the s-plane","Unstable due to complex poles","Non-causal","Purely reactive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Zero is at s = -2 (LHP). Poles are roots of s² + 2s + 2 = 0 => s = -1 ± j1 (LHP). Because all poles and zeros lie in the open left-half s-plane and the relative degree is 1, Z(s) is positive real, passive, and stable."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The power triangle relationship between Apparent Power (S), Real Power (P), and Reactive Power (Q) is:","options":["S = P + Q","S² = P² + Q²","P² = S² + Q²","Q² = S² + P²"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Apparent power is the vector hypotenuse of the complex power triangle: S = P + jQ, so magnitude satisfies S² = P² + Q² (or S = √(P² + Q²))."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The resonant frequency (f0) of a series RLC circuit with inductance L and capacitance C is given by:","options":["f0 = 2π / √(LC)","f0 = 1 / (2π√(LC))","f0 = √(LC) / (2π)","f0 = 1 / (2πLC)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resonance occurs when inductive reactance equals capacitive reactance (2πfL = 1 / (2πfC)), yielding resonant frequency f0 = 1 / (2π√(LC))."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A real voltage source of value V in series with resistance R can be transformed into an equivalent current source of:","options":["Current I = V/R in series with R","Current I = V/R in parallel with R","Current I = V*R in parallel with R","Current I = V in parallel with R²"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By source transformation, a practical voltage source V with series internal resistance R is electrically equivalent to an ideal current source I = V/R connected in parallel with the same resistance R."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The SI unit of electrical admittance, conductance, and susceptance is:","options":["Ohm (Ω)","Siemens (S) or Mho","Henry (H)","Farad (F)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Admittance (Y = 1/Z), conductance (G = 1/R), and susceptance (B = 1/X) are inverse impedance quantities measured in Siemens (S) or reciprocal ohms (mho)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The RMS value of a half-wave rectified sinusoidal alternating voltage wave with peak amplitude Vm is:","options":["Vm / √2 (0.707 Vm)","Vm / 2 (0.50 Vm)","Vm / π (0.318 Vm)","2Vm / π (0.637 Vm)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a half-wave rectified sine wave conducting for one half-cycle: Vrms = √[ (1/2π) ∫[0 to π] (Vm sin θ)² dθ ] = Vm / 2."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The power factor of a purely resistive AC circuit is:","options":["0 leading","0 lagging","1.0 (Unity)","0.707 lagging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a pure resistor, alternating current and applied voltage are exactly in phase (phase angle difference φ = 0°), making the power factor cos(0°) = 1.0 (unity)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three identical impedances Z are connected in a balanced delta configuration across a 3-phase supply. If reconnected in star across the same supply, the power consumed will:","options":["Remain unchanged","Be reduced to one-third (P_star = P_delta / 3)","Triple (P_star = 3 * P_delta)","Be reduced to half"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In star connection, phase voltage drops by 1/√3, phase current drops by 1/√3, and total 3-phase power consumed is one-third of the power consumed in delta (P_star = P_delta / 3)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In network graph theory, the sum of all elements in any column of a complete node-to-branch incidence matrix (A) is always:","options":["+1","-1","Zero","Equal to the total number of branches"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Every branch connects exactly two nodes, leaving one node (+1) and entering another (-1). Thus, every column of an incidence matrix contains exactly one +1 and one -1, summing to zero."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To determine the Thevenin equivalent resistance (Rth) of a network containing dependent sources, one must:","options":["Open-circuit all dependent sources and short-circuit independent sources","Deactivate independent sources and apply an external test voltage (V_test) or current (I_test) at the terminals","Deactivate dependent sources and measure open-circuit voltage","Take the ratio of open-circuit voltage to full load current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dependent sources cannot be deactivated independently. All independent sources are set to zero (voltage sources shorted, current sources opened), and a test source (V_test) is applied at the load terminals to find Rth = V_test / I_test."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In state-space formulation of electrical networks, the state variables are conventionally chosen as:","options":["All node voltages and mesh currents","Independent capacitor voltages and inductor currents","Branch resistances and conductances","Input voltage source derivatives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"State variables represent the memory/energy-storing elements of a system; hence, independent capacitor voltages (storing electrostatic energy 0.5*C*v²) and inductor currents (storing magnetic energy 0.5*L*i²) form the standard state vector."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Bartlett's Bisection Theorem is applicable for analyzing:","options":["Any non-linear planar network","Symmetrical two-port lattice and ladder networks","Unbalanced 3-phase networks","Distributed parameter transmission lines only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bartlett's bisection theorem splits an electrically and physically symmetrical two-port network into two identical halves, expressing lattice branch impedances directly in terms of open-circuit and short-circuit half-network impedances."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The bandwidth (in rad/s) of a series RLC resonant circuit is directly proportional to:","options":["Inductance (L)","Circuit resistance (R)","Capacitance (C)","Resonant frequency squared (ω0²)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a series RLC circuit, bandwidth BW = R / L (rad/s) or BW = R / (2πL) (Hz). Thus, bandwidth is directly proportional to series resistance R."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a two-port network, the short-circuit admittance parameter y11 expressed in terms of open-circuit impedance parameters (Z) is:","options":["z22 / ΔZ","-z12 / ΔZ","z11 / ΔZ","1 / z11"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Y-parameter matrix is the inverse of the Z-parameter matrix ([Y] = [Z]⁻¹). Using matrix inversion: y11 = z22 / ΔZ, where determinant ΔZ = z11*z22 - z12*z21."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an R-L driving-point impedance function Z_RL(s), the critical frequency closest to the origin in the complex s-plane is always:","options":["A pole","A zero","A complex conjugate pole pair","An essential singularity at infinity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For an RL driving-point impedance Z_RL(s), all poles and zeros lie on the negative real axis and interlace. Because an inductor acts as a short circuit at DC (s = 0, Z(0) = R), the critical frequency closest to (or at) the origin must be a zero."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a series R-L circuit with R = 5 Ω and L = 2.5 H, an alternating voltage v(t) = 100 sin(2t + 30°) V is switched on at t = 0. The value of transient DC offset current will be zero if switching occurs when the source phase angle is:","options":["30°","45°","0°","90°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Impedance angle θ = tan⁻¹(ωL / R) = tan⁻¹((2 * 2.5) / 5) = tan⁻¹(1) = 45°. Transient DC offset is proportional to sin(α - θ). The DC offset is completely eliminated when switching angle α equals impedance angle θ = 45°."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The fundamental loop (tie-set) matrix B_f and fundamental cut-set matrix Q_f of any connected electrical network satisfy the orthogonality relation:","options":["B_f * Q_f^T = 0","B_f + Q_f = 0","B_f * Q_f^T = I (Identity matrix)","B_f^T * Q_f = I"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By the fundamental topological relationship between KCL and KVL (Tellegen's generalized theorem), fundamental loop branch currents and cut-set branch voltages are orthogonal: B_f * Q_f^T = 0 (or Q_f * B_f^T = 0)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A series RLC circuit has characteristic equation s² + 6s + 25 = 0. The undamped natural frequency (ωn) and damping ratio (ζ) of the circuit are:","options":["ωn = 5 rad/s, ζ = 0.6","ωn = 25 rad/s, ζ = 0.12","ωn = 5 rad/s, ζ = 1.2","ωn = 6 rad/s, ζ = 0.5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Comparing with standard second-order form s² + 2ζωn*s + ωn² = 0: ωn² = 25 => ωn = 5 rad/s. 2ζωn = 6 => 2 * ζ * 5 = 6 => 10ζ = 6 => ζ = 0.6 (underdamped)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two coupled coils with L1 = 0.2 H, L2 = 0.8 H, and mutual inductance M = 0.3 H are connected in parallel with aiding polarity. The equivalent total inductance (Leq) is approximately:","options":["0.070 H","0.175 H","0.250 H","0.500 H"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For parallel coupled coils in aiding polarity: Leq = (L1*L2 - M²) / (L1 + L2 - 2M). Leq = (0.2 * 0.8 - 0.3²) / (0.2 + 0.8 - 2*0.3) = (0.16 - 0.09) / (1.0 - 0.6) = 0.07 / 0.4 = 0.175 H."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The impulse response of a linear time-invariant system is h(t) = 4 e^(-2t) u(t). If input is x(t) = u(t), the output y(t) for t > 0 is:","options":["2 * (1 - e^(-2t))","4 * (1 - e^(-2t))","2 e^(-2t)","4 e^(-2t)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"System transfer function H(s) = 4 / (s + 2). Input X(s) = 1/s. Output Y(s) = H(s) * X(s) = 4 / [s(s + 2)]. Using partial fractions: 4 / [s(s + 2)] = 2/s - 2/(s + 2). Inverse Laplace transform yields y(t) = 2 * (1 - e^(-2t)) u(t)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"According to Brune's condition, for a driving-point impedance Z(s) to be a Positive Real (PR) function, the real part of Z(jω) must satisfy:","options":["Re[Z(jω)] ≥ 0 for all real frequencies ω","Re[Z(jω)] = 0 for all ω","Re[Z(jω)] ≤ 0 for high frequencies","Im[Z(jω)] must be strictly negative"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A rational function Z(s) is positive real (PR) if it is analytic in the right half of the s-plane, real for real s, and has non-negative real part along the entire imaginary axis: Re[Z(jω)] ≥ 0 for all -∞ < ω < +∞."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a DC circuit, Maximum Power Transfer Theorem states that maximum power is transferred from a source to a load resistance (RL) when:","options":["RL = 0","RL = Rth (Thevenin resistance of the source)","RL = 2 * Rth","RL = Infinity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maximum power transfer in a linear DC circuit occurs when the load resistance equals the internal Thevenin resistance of the network (RL = Rth)."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The reactive power (Q) in an AC circuit represents:","options":["Useful work done per second","Power oscillating back and forth between source and reactive elements without net energy dissipation","Power dissipated as heat in resistors","Total apparent power supplied"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reactive power (measured in VAR) represents energy stored alternately in electric and magnetic fields and returned to the circuit each cycle without performing net mechanical or thermal work."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a two-port network, the parameters that express port voltages (V1, V2) in terms of port currents (I1, I2) are:","options":["Admittance (Y) parameters","Impedance (Z) parameters","Hybrid (h) parameters","Transmission (ABCD) parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The open-circuit impedance parameters define terminal voltages in terms of terminal currents: V1 = z11*I1 + z12*I2 and V2 = z21*I1 + z22*I2."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a balanced 3-phase delta-connected load, the relationship between line current (IL) and phase current (Iph) is:","options":["IL = Iph","IL = √3 * Iph and lags Iph by 30°","IL = Iph / √3","IL = 3 * Iph"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a delta load, line current is the vector difference of two phase currents: IL = √3 * Iph, and the line current phasor lags its corresponding phase current phasor by 30°."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The average power absorbed by an ideal pure capacitor over a complete cycle of sinusoidal alternating voltage is:","options":["0.5 * C * V²","Zero (0 W)","V * I","V² / (2 * ωC)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a pure capacitor, current leads voltage by 90°. Average active power is P = V * I * cos(90°) = 0 W."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following circuit elements is an active and bilateral element?","options":["Semiconductor diode","Ideal independent voltage source","Linear resistor","Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ideal voltage source is an active element (delivering energy) and is bilateral because it can deliver or absorb current in either direction without changing its defined terminal voltage."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a series R-L circuit with DC excitation, the steady-state current after five time constants (t ≥ 5τ) is approximately:","options":["50% of maximum current","63.2% of maximum current","99.3% of maximum current (virtually 100%)","Zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Current follows i(t) = I_max * (1 - e^(-t/τ)). At t = 5τ, i(5τ) = I_max * (1 - e⁻⁵) = I_max * (1 - 0.0067) = 99.33% of the final steady-state current."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Initial Value Theorem of Laplace transform states that for a causal function f(t):","options":["f(0+) = lim_{s -> 0} [s * F(s)]","f(0+) = lim_{s -> ∞} [s * F(s)]","f(0+) = lim_{s -> ∞} [F(s) / s]","f(0+) = lim_{s -> 0} [F(s)]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By the initial value theorem, the initial value at t = 0+ is determined from the s-domain representation as f(0+) = lim_{s -> ∞} [s * F(s)], provided the limit exists."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the two-wattmeter method of 3-phase power measurement, if one wattmeter reads positive and the other reads zero (W2 = 0), the load power factor is:","options":["Unity (1.0)","0.866 lagging","0.50 lagging","Zero lagging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"W2 = VL*IL*cos(30° + φ) = 0 => 30° + φ = 90° => φ = 60°. Therefore, the load power factor is cos(60°) = 0.50 lagging."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an air-core transformer with mutual inductance M, the impedance reflected into the primary circuit from a secondary circuit of self-impedance Z22 is:","options":["(ω * M)² / Z22","(ω * M) * Z22","Z22 / (ω * M)","(ω * M)² * Z22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The secondary mesh equation is 0 = -jωM*I1 + Z22*I2 => I2 = (jωM / Z22)*I1. Primary induced back-voltage is -jωM*I2 = ((ωM)² / Z22)*I1, making reflected impedance Z_ref = (ωM)² / Z22."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In Cauer-I form of LC network synthesis, the driving-point impedance Z(s) is synthesized by continued fraction expansion around:","options":["s = 0, giving alternating series capacitors and shunt inductors","s = ∞, giving alternating series inductors and shunt capacitors","s = 1 rad/s","The origin of the jω axis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cauer-I synthesis performs successive division in descending powers of s (expansion around s = ∞), removing poles at infinity to produce a ladder network of series inductors and shunt capacitors."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"According to Foster's Reactance Theorem, the slope of the reactance curve (dX/dω) for any passive lossless LC network is:","options":["Always zero","Strictly positive (dX/dω > 0) at all frequencies","Always negative (dX/dω < 0)","Positive at low frequencies and negative at high frequencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Foster's reactance theorem establishes that for all physical passive lossless LC networks, the rate of change of reactance with respect to angular frequency is strictly positive (dX/dω > 0) everywhere."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When two identical two-port networks with impedance matrices [Z_A] and [Z_B] are connected with their input and output ports in series, the overall impedance matrix [Z_total] is:","options":["[Z_A] * [Z_B]","[Z_A] + [Z_B]","([Z_A]⁻¹ + [Z_B]⁻¹)⁻¹","[Z_A] - [Z_B]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a series-series two-port interconnection (satisfying Guanella port validity conditions), terminal voltages add (V = Va + Vb) while currents are equal, giving [Z_total] = [Z_A] + [Z_B]."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When two identical two-port networks with admittance matrices [Y_A] and [Y_B] are connected with their input and output ports in parallel, the overall admittance matrix [Y_total] is:","options":["[Y_A] + [Y_B]","[Y_A] * [Y_B]","([Y_A]⁻¹ + [Y_B]⁻¹)⁻¹","[Y_A] / [Y_B]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a parallel-parallel two-port interconnection, terminal currents add (I = Ia + Ib) while terminal voltages are equal, giving [Y_total] = [Y_A] + [Y_B]."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A DC voltage V = 100 V is applied at t = 0 to a series circuit with R = 10 Ω and L = 2 H. The value of current at time t = 0.2 s is approximately:","options":["3.68 A","6.32 A","10.00 A","8.65 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time constant τ = L / R = 2 / 10 = 0.2 s. At t = 0.2 s = 1τ: i(0.2) = (V / R) * (1 - e⁻¹) = (100 / 10) * (1 - 0.3679) = 10 * 0.6321 ≈ 6.32 A."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A series RLC circuit has R = 2 Ω, L = 1 H, and C = 0.5 F. If excited by a unit step voltage source v(t) = u(t), the current response i(t) for t > 0 is:","options":["e^(-t) sin(t) A","e^(-t) cos(t) A","e^(-2t) sin(t) A","2 e^(-t) A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"V(s) = 1/s. Z(s) = s + 2 + 2/s = (s² + 2s + 2)/s. Current I(s) = V(s)/Z(s) = (1/s) * [s / (s² + 2s + 2)] = 1 / [(s + 1)² + 1²]. Taking the inverse Laplace transform yields i(t) = e^(-t) sin(t) A."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The driving-point impedance of a one-port LC network is Z(s) = (2s² + 1) / [s(s² + 2)]. In Foster-I realization, the values of the parallel LC tank elements are:","options":["L1 = 0.75 H, C1 = 0.67 F","L1 = 0.25 H, C1 = 2.00 F","L1 = 0.50 H, C1 = 1.00 F","L1 = 1.00 H, C1 = 0.50 F"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Partial fraction expansion: Z(s) = (0.5/s) + [1.5s / (s² + 2)]. The parallel tank has impedance Z_tank = (s/C1) / [s² + 1/(L1*C1)]. Comparing terms: 1/C1 = 1.5 => C1 = 2/3 ≈ 0.67 F. Since 1/(L1*C1) = 2, L1 = 1 / (2 * 2/3) = 3/4 = 0.75 H."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 400 V balanced supply feeds a star-connected load with phase impedances Z_R = Z_Y = Z_B = (20 + j15) Ω. The total 3-phase active power consumed is:","options":["5.12 kW","8.87 kW","15.36 kW","2.56 kW"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase voltage Vph = 400 / √3 V. Impedance magnitude |Zph| = √(20² + 15²) = 25 Ω. Phase current Iph = (400 / √3) / 25 = 16 / √3 A. Total active power P = 3 * Iph² * R = 3 * (256 / 3) * 20 = 5120 W = 5.12 kW."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In the s-domain, the driving-point admittance of a network is Y(s) = (s + 1) / (s + 3). The steady-state current response i(t) to an applied input voltage v(t) = 10 cos(3t) V is:","options":["7.45 cos(3t + 26.57°) A","10.00 cos(3t - 45.00°) A","3.33 cos(3t + 45.00°) A","5.25 cos(3t - 26.57°) A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At ω = 3 rad/s: Y(j3) = (1 + j3) / (3 + j3). Magnitude |Y(j3)| = √10 / √18 = √(5/9) ≈ 0.7454. Phase angle θ = tan⁻¹(3) - tan⁻¹(1) = 71.57° - 45° = 26.57°. Current i(t) = 10 * 0.7454 cos(3t + 26.57°) = 7.45 cos(3t + 26.57°) A."},{"subject":"Circuit Theory","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A two-port network has open-circuit impedance parameters z11 = 40 Ω, z22 = 30 Ω, and z12 = z21 = 20 Ω. If port 2 is terminated in a load resistance RL = 30 Ω, the input driving-point impedance (Z_in) seen at port 1 is:","options":["33.33 Ω","40.00 Ω","26.67 Ω","46.67 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Input impedance with load termination is Zin = z11 - [ (z12 * z21) / (z22 + ZL) ]. Substituting values: Zin = 40 - [ (20 * 20) / (30 + 30) ] = 40 - (400 / 60) = 40 - 6.67 = 33.33 Ω."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a closed-loop control system, introducing negative feedback primarily results in:","options":["Increased system gain and reduced bandwidth","Reduced system gain and increased bandwidth","Increased sensitivity to parameter variations","Reduced stability margin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Negative feedback reduces closed-loop gain by a factor of (1 + GH) while extending the system bandwidth by (1 + GH) and reducing sensitivity to internal parameter variations."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The transfer function of a Linear Time-Invariant (LTI) system is defined as the:","options":["Ratio of Laplace transform of output to input assuming zero initial conditions","Ratio of output to input in the time domain","Ratio of Laplace transform of input to output with non-zero initial conditions","Fourier transform of the system impulse response with initial conditions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By definition, the transfer function G(s) = Y(s) / X(s) is the ratio of the Laplace transform of the output to the Laplace transform of the input under strictly zero initial conditions."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Signal Flow Graph (SFG), Mason's gain formula determines the overall transfer function using:","options":["T = Σ (Pk * Δk) / Δ","T = Σ (Pk * Δ) / Δk","T = Δ / Σ (Pk * Δk)","T = Σ Pk / Δ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mason's gain formula is T = (1/Δ) * Σ (Pk * Δk), where Pk is the forward path gain, Δ is the graph determinant, and Δk is the cofactor of the kth forward path."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The unit impulse response of an LTI system is related to its unit step response by:","options":["Integrating the unit step response with respect to time","Differentiating the unit step response with respect to time","Multiplying the step response by time t","Dividing the step response by time t"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the unit impulse δ(t) is the first time derivative of the unit step function u(t), the impulse response h(t) equals d[c(t)] / dt of the step response."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A second-order control system is classified as critically damped when its damping ratio (ζ) is:","options":["ζ = 0","0 < ζ < 1","ζ = 1","ζ > 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A second-order system is undamped for ζ = 0, underdamped for 0 < ζ < 1, critically damped for ζ = 1 (two real and equal poles), and overdamped for ζ > 1."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a Type-1 control system, the steady-state error to a unit ramp input r(t) = t is:","options":["Zero","Finite and equal to 1 / Kv","Infinite","1 / (1 + Kp)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Type-1 system has one open-loop pole at the origin, yielding a finite velocity error constant Kv = lim_{s->0} s*G(s)H(s) and a finite steady-state error ess = 1 / Kv for a ramp input."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Adding an integral controller (PI controller) to a feedback control system:","options":["Increases system type number by 1 and eliminates steady-state error","Decreases system type number and increases speed of response","Improves transient stability margin substantially","Reduces system order by 1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An integral controller introduces an open-loop pole at the origin (s = 0), increasing the system type number by 1 and driving steady-state error to zero for step and ramp inputs."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Routh array, if an entire row becomes zero, it indicates the presence of:","options":["Roots located strictly at infinity","Symmetrical roots about the origin (real pairs, imaginary pairs, or complex quadrates)","Repeated roots on the negative real axis","No roots in the right-half s-plane"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An all-zero row in Routh's array signifies roots symmetrical about the origin of the s-plane, such as conjugate pairs on the imaginary axis (±jω), pairs on the real axis (±σ), or complex quad roots (±σ ± jω)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For an open-loop transfer function with P finite poles and Z finite zeros (P > Z), the angles of asymptotes for the root locus branches as K → ∞ are given by:","options":["θa = (2q + 1) * 180° / (P - Z), where q = 0, 1, 2, ... (P - Z - 1)","θa = 2q * 180° / (P - Z)","θa = (2q + 1) * 360° / (P + Z)","θa = q * 180° / (P - Z)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The angles of asymptotes directing root locus branches to infinity are θa = [(2q + 1) * 180°] / (P - Z), where q = 0, 1, ..., (P - Z - 1)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Phase Margin (PM) of a feedback control system is defined as:","options":["180° + ∠G(jωgc)H(jωgc), where ωgc is the gain crossover frequency","180° - ∠G(jωpc)H(jωpc), where ωpc is the phase crossover frequency","-180° + ∠G(jωgc)H(jωgc)","∠G(jωgc)H(jωgc) - 90°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase Margin is the amount of additional phase lag required at the gain crossover frequency (where |GH| = 1 or 0 dB) to bring the system to the verge of instability: PM = 180° + ∠G(jωgc)H(jωgc)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A phase-lead compensator with transfer function Gc(s) = (s + z) / (s + p) requires which pole-zero arrangement?","options":["z < p (zero is closer to the origin than the pole)","p < z (pole is closer to the origin than the zero)","z = p","Pole and zero must be complex conjugates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a phase-lead network, the zero is positioned closer to the origin than the pole (|z| < |p|). This introduces a positive phase angle, improving phase margin and accelerating transient speed."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The state transition matrix Φ(t) = e^(At) of a linear continuous-time system satisfies which fundamental property?","options":["Φ(0) = 0 (Zero matrix)","Φ(-t) = [Φ(t)]⁻¹","Φ(t1 + t2) = Φ(t1) * Φ(-t2)","d[Φ(t)]/dt = Φ(t) * A⁻¹"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The state transition matrix satisfies Φ(0) = I, Φ(-t) = [Φ(t)]⁻¹, Φ(t1 + t2) = Φ(t1) * Φ(t2), and d[Φ(t)]/dt = A * Φ(t)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An n-dimensional continuous-time LTI system x_dot = A x + B u is completely state controllable if and only if the controllability matrix Qc has:","options":["Rank equal to n, where Qc = [B  AB  A²B ... A^(n-1)B]","Rank equal to 1","Determinant equal to zero","Rank equal to n, where Qc = [C^T  A^T C^T ... (A^T)^(n-1) C^T]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to Kalman's controllability criterion, the system is completely state controllable if and only if the controllability matrix Qc = [B  AB  A²B ... A^(n-1)B] has full rank n."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"On a Bode magnitude plot, introducing a simple zero at s = -ω1 causes the asymptotic slope to:","options":["Decrease by -20 dB/decade","Increase by +20 dB/decade","Decrease by -40 dB/decade","Increase by +40 dB/decade"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A simple zero in the numerator contributes an asymptotic slope change of +20 dB/decade (or +6 dB/octave) starting from corner frequency ω1, whereas a pole introduces -20 dB/decade."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = 25 / [s(s + 6)]. The percentage peak overshoot (%Mp) for a unit step input is approximately:","options":["9.5%","16.3%","4.3%","25.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s² + 6s + 25 = 0. Comparing with s² + 2ζωn s + ωn² = 0 gives ωn = 5 rad/s and 2ζωn = 6 => ζ = 0.6. Peak overshoot %Mp = e^(-πζ / √(1 - ζ²)) * 100% = e^(-(3.1416 * 0.6) / 0.8) * 100% = e^(-2.356) * 100% ≈ 9.5%."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The open-loop transfer function of a unity feedback system is G(s) = K / [s(s + 2)(s + 4)]. For the closed-loop system to remain strictly stable, the range of gain K is:","options":["0 < K < 48","0 < K < 24","K > 48","0 < K < 16"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s³ + 6s² + 8s + K = 0. Routh array: row s³: [1, 8]; row s²: [6, K]; row s¹: [(48 - K) / 6]; row s⁰: [K]. For stability, all first-column elements must be positive, requiring 0 < K < 48."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The open-loop transfer function of a unity feedback system is G(s) = K / [s(s + 2)(s + 4)]. The breakaway point of the root locus on the real axis is located at:","options":["s = -0.845","s = -3.155","s = -2.000","s = -1.414"],"answer":0,"explanation":"K = -(s³ + 6s² + 8s). Setting dK/ds = -(3s² + 12s + 8) = 0 gives s = [-12 ± √(144 - 96)] / 6 = -2 ± 1.155. Between 0 and -2 on the root locus, the valid breakaway point is s = -0.845."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A system has an open-loop transfer function G(s)H(s) with 2 poles in the right-half s-plane (P = 2). For the closed-loop system to be stable, the Nyquist plot of G(s)H(s) must encircle the critical point (-1 + j0):","options":["2 times in the clockwise direction","2 times in the counter-clockwise direction","0 times (no encirclement)","1 time in the counter-clockwise direction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nyquist stability criterion: N = P - Z, where N is counter-clockwise encirclements of (-1 + j0), P is open-loop RHP poles, and Z is closed-loop RHP poles. For stability (Z = 0): N = P - 0 = 2 counter-clockwise encirclements."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a system with system matrix A = [[0, 1], [-2, -3]], the characteristic equation and eigenvalues of A are:","options":["s² + 3s + 2 = 0; λ = -1, -2","s² - 3s + 2 = 0; λ = 1, 2","s² + 2s + 3 = 0; λ = -1 ± j√2","s² + 3s - 2 = 0; λ = -0.56, 3.56"],"answer":0,"explanation":"det(sI - A) = det([[s, -1], [2, s + 3]]) = s(s + 3) - (-2) = s² + 3s + 2 = 0. Factoring gives (s + 1)(s + 2) = 0, so eigenvalues are λ1 = -1 and λ2 = -2."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An open-loop system has G(s)H(s) = 2 / [s(s + 1)(s + 2)]. The phase crossover frequency (ωpc) and Gain Margin (GM in dB) are:","options":["ωpc = √2 rad/s, GM = 9.54 dB","ωpc = 2 rad/s, GM = 6.02 dB","ωpc = 1 rad/s, GM = 12.04 dB","ωpc = √3 rad/s, GM = 20.00 dB"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase angle ∠GH = -90° - tan⁻¹(ω) - tan⁻¹(ω/2) = -180° => tan⁻¹(ω) + tan⁻¹(ω/2) = 90° => 1 - ω²/2 = 0 => ωpc = √2 rad/s. At ωpc: |GH| = 2 / [√2 * √3 * √6] = 2/6 = 1/3. Gain Margin = 1 / (1/3) = 3 (linear) = 20 * log10(3) ≈ 9.54 dB."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an AC servomotor, the rotor resistance is kept deliberately high to ensure:","options":["A linear speed-torque characteristic with a strictly negative slope for stable operation","Maximum starting current and low torque","Zero starting torque","A positive slope on the speed-torque curve"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A high rotor resistance ensures that maximum torque occurs at high negative slip (s > 1), giving a strictly negative slope across the operating speed range. This prevents single-phasing and guarantees positive damping and stable servomechanism control."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A synchro transmitter is primarily used as an electromechanical transducer to:","options":["Measure shaft speed in RPM","Detect angular shaft position and transmit electrical position signals","Amplify DC input power","Convert electrical frequency to voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A synchro transmitter consists of a single-phase excited salient rotor and a 3-phase star-connected stator. It converts mechanical angular position (θ) into three phase voltages, transmitting shaft angle information electrically."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a standard second-order underdamped system, the settling time (ts) for a 2% tolerance band is approximately:","options":["ts = 4 / (ζ * ωn)","ts = 3 / (ζ * ωn)","ts = 2 / (ζ * ωn)","ts = 1 / (ζ * ωn)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Settling time for a 2% tolerance band corresponds to 4 time constants (4τ): ts ≈ 4 / (ζ * ωn). For a 5% tolerance band, it is approximately 3 time constants: ts ≈ 3 / (ζ * ωn)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a Type-0 system subjected to a unit step input r(t) = u(t), the steady-state error (ess) is:","options":["Zero","1 / (1 + Kp)","1 / Kv","Infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Type-0 system has zero poles at the origin, yielding a finite positional error constant Kp = lim_{s->0} G(s)H(s). The steady-state error for a unit step input is ess = 1 / (1 + Kp)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The polar plot of a pure integrator transfer function G(s) = 1/s lies entirely on the:","options":["Positive real axis","Negative imaginary axis","Positive imaginary axis","Negative real axis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Substituting s = jω: G(jω) = 1 / (jω) = -j / ω = (1 / ω) ∠-90°. As ω increases from 0 to ∞, the locus travels along the negative imaginary axis from -j∞ toward the origin."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Adding a proportional-derivative (PD) controller to a feedback system primarily improves:","options":["Steady-state accuracy by eliminating offset","Transient response by increasing effective damping ratio and stability margin","System type number","High-frequency noise immunity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A derivative (PD) controller introduces anticipation (phase lead), which increases the effective damping ratio (ζ), dampens transient oscillations, reduces peak overshoot, and shortens settling time."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A minimum-phase closed-loop control system is guaranteed to be stable if:","options":["Both Gain Margin (in dB) and Phase Margin are strictly positive","Gain Margin is positive and Phase Margin is negative","Gain crossover frequency is greater than phase crossover frequency (ωgc > ωpc)","Phase Margin is zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For minimum-phase systems (no poles or zeros in the RHP), stability requires both Gain Margin (in dB) and Phase Margin to be strictly positive, meaning gain crossover occurs before phase crossover (ωgc < ωpc)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The intersection point of root locus asymptotes with the real axis (centroid σa) for open-loop poles Pi and zeros Zk is given by:","options":["σa = (Σ Real parts of Pi - Σ Real parts of Zk) / (P - Z)","σa = (Σ Pi - Σ Zk) / (P + Z)","σa = (Σ Zk - Σ Pi) / (P - Z)","σa = (P - Z) / (Σ Pi - Σ Zk)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The centroid σa represents the geometric center of the asymptotic branches on the real axis: σa = [Σ Re(poles) - Σ Re(zeros)] / (P - Z), where P is the number of finite poles and Z is the number of finite zeros."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A transfer function G(s) = (1 - sT) / (1 + sT) represents a first-order all-pass filter. Its magnitude and phase at angular frequency ω are:","options":["|G(jω)| = 1, ∠G(jω) = -2 tan⁻¹(ωT)","|G(jω)| = 1, ∠G(jω) = 0°","|G(jω)| = 1 / √(1 + ω²T²), ∠G(jω) = -tan⁻¹(ωT)","|G(jω)| = (1 - ωT) / (1 + ωT), ∠G(jω) = -90°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Magnitude |G(jω)| = |1 - jωT| / |1 + jωT| = √(1 + ω²T²) / √(1 + ω²T²) = 1 for all frequencies. Phase angle ∠G(jω) = ∠(1 - jωT) - ∠(1 + jωT) = -tan⁻¹(ωT) - tan⁻¹(ωT) = -2 tan⁻¹(ωT)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A phase-lag compensator with transfer function Gc(s) = (s + 1/T) / (s + 1/(βT)) with β > 1 is primarily chosen to:","options":["Increase system bandwidth and speed up transient response","Boost low-frequency open-loop gain to reduce steady-state error without significantly degrading transient stability","Eliminate all poles on the imaginary axis","Reduce system order by pole cancellation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A phase-lag compensator provides high gain at low frequencies (boosting DC error constants by β) while attenuating high frequencies, lowering the gain crossover frequency to retain adequate phase margin."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"On a Nichols chart, the open-loop frequency response is plotted on coordinates consisting of:","options":["Open-loop gain in dB vs Open-loop phase angle in degrees","Closed-loop gain in dB vs Closed-loop phase angle in degrees","Real part of G(jω) vs Imaginary part of G(jω)","Logarithmic frequency vs Phase angle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Nichols chart plots open-loop magnitude 20 log10 |G(jω)H(jω)| (in dB) on the vertical axis against open-loop phase angle ∠G(jω)H(jω) (in degrees) on the horizontal axis, overlaid with constant closed-loop M and N contours."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An LTI continuous-time system with state matrix A (n × n) and output matrix C (1 × n) is completely state observable if the observability matrix Qo has rank n, where Qo is defined as:","options":["Qo = [C^T  A^T C^T  (A^T)² C^T ... (A^T)^(n-1) C^T]","Qo = [C  CA  CA² ... CA^(n-1)]","Both A and B are valid transposed representations of each other","Qo = [B  AB  A²B ... A^(n-1)B]"],"answer":2,"explanation":"According to Kalman's observability criterion, the observability matrix can be expressed either in horizontal block form [C; CA; ...; CA^(n-1)] or in transposed column form [C^T, A^T C^T, ... (A^T)^(n-1) C^T]. Full rank n confirms complete observability."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The angle of departure (θd) of a root locus branch leaving a complex conjugate open-loop pole is given by:","options":["θd = 180° - (Σ θp - Σ θz)","θd = 180° + (Σ θp - Σ θz)","θd = (Σ θz - Σ θp) - 180°","θd = 360° - (Σ θp + Σ θz)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From the angle condition ∠G(s)H(s) = ±180°, the angle of departure from a complex pole is θd = 180° - Σ θp + Σ θz = 180° - (Σ θp - Σ θz), where Σ θp and Σ θz are angles subtended by other poles and zeros to that pole."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The state transition matrix Φ(t) = e^(At) of an LTI system can be computed in the s-domain using:","options":["Φ(t) = L⁻¹ [ (sI - A)⁻¹ ]","Φ(t) = L⁻¹ [ (sI - A) ]","Φ(t) = L⁻¹ [ det(sI - A) ]","Φ(t) = L⁻¹ [ (sI + A)⁻¹ ]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Taking the Laplace transform of the unforced state equation x_dot = A x yields s X(s) - x(0) = A X(s) => (sI - A) X(s) = x(0) => X(s) = (sI - A)⁻¹ x(0). Thus, Φ(t) = L⁻¹[(sI - A)⁻¹]."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = 100 / [s(s + 12)]. For a unit step input, the peak time (tp) of the time response is approximately:","options":["0.393 s","0.314 s","0.524 s","0.262 s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s² + 12s + 100 = 0. Comparing with s² + 2ζωn s + ωn² = 0: ωn = 10 rad/s and 2ζωn = 12 => ζ = 0.6. Damped natural frequency ωd = ωn * √(1 - ζ²) = 10 * √(1 - 0.36) = 8 rad/s. Peak time tp = π / ωd = 3.1416 / 8 ≈ 0.393 s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a standard second-order system with damping ratio ζ = 1/√2 (≈ 0.707), the resonant peak (Mr) in its closed-loop frequency response is:","options":["1.00 (0 dB)","1.414 (3 dB)","0.707 (-3 dB)","2.00 (6 dB)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resonant peak is given by Mr = 1 / [2ζ√(1 - ζ²)]. For ζ = 1/√2: Mr = 1 / [2 * (1/√2) * √(1 - 1/2)] = 1 / [√2 * (1/√2)] = 1 / 1 = 1.0 (0 dB). For all ζ ≥ 0.707, no resonant peak occurs above the DC level."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The characteristic equation of a feedback control system is s⁴ + 2s³ + 6s² + 8s + 8 = 0. The natural frequency of sustained oscillations is:","options":["2.00 rad/s","1.414 rad/s","4.00 rad/s","2.828 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Routh array has an all-zero row at s¹. The auxiliary equation from the s² row is 2s² + 8 = 0 => s² + 4 = 0 => s = ±j2 rad/s. Thus, the system sustains oscillations at frequency ω = 2 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The root locus of a unity feedback system with G(s) = K / [s(s + 1)(s + 5)] crosses the imaginary axis at:","options":["s = ± j√5 rad/s at K = 30","s = ± j5 rad/s at K = 25","s = ± j√6 rad/s at K = 36","s = ± j2 rad/s at K = 20"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s³ + 6s² + 5s + K = 0. Routh array: row s¹ = (30 - K) / 6 = 0 for marginal stability, giving K_mar = 30. The auxiliary equation from row s² is 6s² + 30 = 0 => s² = -5 => s = ±j√5 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A continuous-time LTI system is described by matrices A = [[0, 1], [-2, -3]], B = [[0], [1]], C = [1, 0], and D = 0. The transfer function Y(s)/U(s) is:","options":["1 / (s² + 3s + 2)","(s + 1) / (s² + 3s + 2)","s / (s² + 3s + 2)","(s + 3) / (s² + 3s + 2)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transfer function G(s) = C*(sI - A)⁻¹*B + D. Here (sI - A) = [[s, -1], [2, s+3]], and (sI - A)⁻¹ = [[s+3, 1], [-2, s]] / (s² + 3s + 2). Multiplying by B = [0; 1] gives [1; s] / (s² + 3s + 2). Multiplying by C = [1, 0] gives 1 / (s² + 3s + 2)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a unity feedback control system with open-loop transfer function G(s) = 10 / [s(s + 1)(s + 5)], the phase crossover frequency (ωpc) is:","options":["√5 rad/s (≈ 2.236 rad/s)","5 rad/s","√6 rad/s (≈ 2.449 rad/s)","1 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase angle ∠G(jω) = -90° - tan⁻¹(ω) - tan⁻¹(ω/5) = -180° => tan⁻¹(ω) + tan⁻¹(ω/5) = 90°. Since tan(90°) = ∞, the denominator of the tangent addition formula must be zero: 1 - (ω * ω / 5) = 0 => ω² = 5 => ωpc = √5 rad/s ≈ 2.236 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The sensitivity of a closed-loop transfer function T(s) with respect to forward-path gain G(s) in a negative feedback system is:","options":["1 / (1 + G(s)H(s))","G(s) / (1 + G(s)H(s))","1 + G(s)H(s)","-H(s) / (1 + G(s)H(s))"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sensitivity is defined as S = (∂T/T) / (∂G/G). For T = G / (1 + GH), differentiating gives ∂T/∂G = 1 / (1 + GH)², resulting in S_G^T = 1 / (1 + G(s)H(s))."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For an underdamped second-order control system, the rise time (tr) is defined as the time taken for the unit step response to rise from:","options":["10% to 90% of its final steady-state value","0% to 100% of its final steady-state value","5% to 95% of its final steady-state value","0% to 50% of its final steady-state value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For an underdamped system, rise time is standardly defined as the time required to rise from 0% to 100% of the final steady-state value on its initial excursion. For overdamped systems, 10% to 90% is typically used."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an armature-controlled DC servomotor, the field winding current is kept constant primarily to:","options":["Maintain constant field flux and ensure a linear torque-current relationship","Minimize armature reaction","Eliminate back EMF completely","Operate the motor at unity power factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keeping field excitation constant ensures that developed motor torque is directly proportional to armature current (T = Km * Ia), providing linear control with minimal lag."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The 'type' of an open-loop transfer function G(s)H(s) designates the number of:","options":["Poles lying at the origin of the complex s-plane","Total poles in the left-half s-plane","Zeros lying at the origin","Total finite zeros of the system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The type number of a control system is strictly the number of open-loop poles located at s = 0 (integrators in the loop), which determines steady-state tracking capabilities."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A wider bandwidth in the frequency domain of a closed-loop system indicates:","options":["Slower transient speed and longer rise time","Faster transient speed and shorter rise time","Increased steady-state error","Zero damping ratio"],"answer":1,"explanation":"System bandwidth is inversely related to rise time (BW ∝ 1/tr). A wider bandwidth allows higher frequencies to pass, enabling faster transient response and shorter rise time."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On a Bode magnitude plot, a first-order lag factor 1 / (1 + sT) produces an asymptotic slope of:","options":["+20 dB/decade after corner frequency ω = 1/T","-20 dB/decade after corner frequency ω = 1/T","-40 dB/decade after corner frequency ω = T","0 dB/decade across all frequencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The factor 1 / (1 + jωT) has an asymptotic magnitude of 0 dB for ω < 1/T and rolls off at -20 dB/decade (or -6 dB/octave) for frequencies beyond the corner frequency ω = 1/T."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A linear continuous-time system is Bounded-Input Bounded-Output (BIBO) stable if and only if its impulse response h(t) satisfies:","options":["∫ |h(t)| dt < ∞ (absolutely integrable over [0, ∞))","lim_{t -> ∞} h(t) = ∞","h(0) = 0","∫ h²(t) dt = 0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The necessary and sufficient condition for BIBO stability of an LTI system is that its unit impulse response h(t) must be absolutely integrable: ∫[0 to ∞] |h(t)| dt < ∞."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A non-minimum phase transfer function is characterized by having:","options":["Poles or zeros in the right half of the s-plane, causing greater phase lag than a minimum phase system with the same magnitude response","All poles and zeros strictly in the left-half s-plane","Zero phase lag at all frequencies","Poles purely on the imaginary axis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Non-minimum phase systems contain one or more zeros or poles in the right-half s-plane (RHP) or transport delay. They exhibit greater net phase lag compared to a minimum-phase counterpart having the identical gain magnitude curve."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The angle of arrival (θa) of root locus branches entering a complex conjugate open-loop zero is given by:","options":["θa = 180° - (Σ θz - Σ θp)","θa = 180° + (Σ θz - Σ θp)","θa = (Σ θp - Σ θz) - 180°","θa = 180° - (Σ θp - Σ θz)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From the angle criterion ∠G(s)H(s) = ±180°, the angle of arrival at a complex zero is θa = 180° - Σ θz + Σ θp = 180° - (Σ θz - Σ θp), where Σ θz and Σ θp are angles subtended by the remaining zeros and poles."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Under a linear similarity state transformation x = P z (where P is a non-singular matrix), the system eigenvalues:","options":["Are scaled by det(P)","Remain completely invariant","Are inverted","Become purely imaginary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The transformed state matrix is A_new = P⁻¹ A P. Because det(sI - P⁻¹AP) = det(P⁻¹(sI - A)P) = det(sI - A), the characteristic equation and eigenvalues remain completely invariant."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A lag-lead compensator is typically employed when a design requires:","options":["Simultaneously increasing low-frequency gain for high steady-state accuracy and improving phase margin for transient stability","Reducing system order to 1","Eliminating all high-frequency sensor noise without phase penalty","Operating exclusively with open-loop positive feedback"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A lag-lead network combines the advantages of both: the lag portion boosts DC and low-frequency gain (reducing steady-state tracking error), while the lead portion increases the phase margin and speed of response."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a standard second-order underdamped system, the resonant frequency (ωr) of the closed-loop frequency response exists only when:","options":["ζ < 1/√2 (≈ 0.707)","ζ > 1.0","ζ = 1.0","0.707 < ζ < 1.0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resonant frequency is ωr = ωn √(1 - 2ζ²). For ωr to be real and non-zero, (1 - 2ζ²) > 0, which requires the damping ratio to satisfy ζ < 1/√2 ≈ 0.707."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the Routh-Hurwitz array, if the first element of a row becomes zero while other elements in that row are non-zero, the difficulty can be resolved by:","options":["Replacing zero with a small positive number ε and taking limits as ε -> 0","Assuming the system is instantly stable","Equating all subsequent coefficients to zero","Setting the system gain K to infinity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a zero appears in the first column with non-zero elements remaining in that row, it is replaced by an infinitesimal positive number ε, and Routh array evaluation proceeds before evaluating limits as ε -> 0 (or substituting s = 1/z)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Nyquist stability criterion is mathematically derived from which complex analysis theorem?","options":["Cauchy's Principle of the Argument","Residue Theorem of Integration","Green's Theorem in a plane","Liapunov's Direct Method"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nyquist's criterion maps the s-plane Nyquist contour into the GH-plane based on Cauchy's Principle of the Argument: N = P - Z, relating contour encirclements of (-1 + j0) to enclosed poles and zeros."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = K(s + 1) / [s²(s + 2)(s + 5)]. For an input r(t) = 3 + 2t + t² (for t ≥ 0), the steady-state error is:","options":["20 / K","10 / K","Zero","Infinite"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The system is Type-2 (two poles at s = 0). For step (3) and ramp (2t) inputs, ess = 0. For parabolic component t² = 2*(t²/2), input magnitude A = 2. Acceleration error constant Ka = lim_{s->0} s² G(s) = K(1) / (2*5) = K / 10. Steady-state error ess = A / Ka = 2 / (K / 10) = 20 / K."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a system matrix A = [[0, 1], [-4, -4]], the (1,1) element of the state transition matrix Φ(t) = e^(At) is:","options":["(1 + 2t) e^(-2t)","(1 - 2t) e^(-2t)","e^(-2t)","2t e^(-2t)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"(sI - A) = [[s, -1], [4, s + 4]]. det(sI - A) = s(s + 4) + 4 = (s + 2)². The adjugate matrix has top-left element (s + 4). Thus, Φ11(s) = (s + 4) / (s + 2)² = 1 / (s + 2) + 2 / (s + 2)². Taking inverse Laplace gives Φ11(t) = e^(-2t) + 2t e^(-2t) = (1 + 2t) e^(-2t)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback control system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = K / [s(s + 4)]. The value of gain K required to yield a closed-loop damping ratio ζ = 0.5 is:","options":["16","8","4","32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Closed-loop characteristic equation: s² + 4s + K = 0. Comparing with s² + 2ζωn s + ωn² = 0: 2ζωn = 4 => 2(0.5)ωn = 4 => ωn = 4 rad/s. Then K = ωn² = 4² = 16."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = e^(-0.1s) / s. The phase crossover frequency (ωpc) is:","options":["5π rad/s (≈ 15.71 rad/s)","10π rad/s (≈ 31.42 rad/s)","2.5π rad/s (≈ 7.85 rad/s)","1.57 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase angle ∠G(jω) = -90° - (0.1ω rad * 180°/π) = -180°. Therefore, 0.1ω rad = π/2 rad => ω = (π/2) / 0.1 = 5π rad/s ≈ 15.71 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A system is defined by state matrix A = [[1, 1], [0, -1]] and input matrix B = [[1], [0]]. The system is:","options":["Uncontrollable, because rank of controllability matrix is 1","Completely controllable, because rank is 2","Unstable and observable","Completely state observable only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Controllability matrix Qc = [B  AB]. AB = [[1, 1], [0, -1]] * [[1], [0]] = [[1], [0]]. Thus Qc = [[1, 1], [0, 0]]. Determinant det(Qc) = 0, giving rank(Qc) = 1 < 2. Hence, the system is uncontrollable."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The root locus of an open-loop transfer function G(s)H(s) = K(s + 3) / [s(s - 1)] includes a circular portion. The center and radius of this circle are:","options":["Center at (-3, 0), Radius = 2√3 (≈ 3.464)","Center at (0, 0), Radius = 3","Center at (-1.5, 0), Radius = 2.5","Center at (-3, 0), Radius = √3 (≈ 1.732)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation is s² + (K - 1)s + 3K = 0. Real-axis breakaway points satisfy dK/ds = 0 => d/ds [-(s² - s)/(s + 3)] = 0 => s² + 6s - 3 = 0 => s = -3 ± 2√3. The complex branches form a circle centered on the real zero at (-3, 0) with radius R = 2√3 ≈ 3.464."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Adding an open-loop pole to the forward path transfer function of a closed-loop system causes:","options":["The root locus to shift towards the right-half s-plane, reducing relative stability","The root locus to shift towards the left-half s-plane, increasing stability","System bandwidth to double instantly","Steady-state tracking error to drop to zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Introducing an open-loop pole pulls the root locus branches toward the right, slowing transient response, increasing peak overshoot, and reducing the relative stability margin."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In positional servomechanisms, tachometer (rate) feedback is employed primarily to:","options":["Increase effective damping ratio and suppress overshoot without significantly altering steady-state error","Increase steady-state error","Introduce an open-loop pole at the origin","Eliminate back EMF in the servomotor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tachometer feedback injects a signal proportional to output velocity (dθ/dt), which increases the system damping ratio (ζ), dampens transient oscillations, and reduces peak overshoot."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The corner frequency of a first-order lag factor G(s) = 1 / (s + 5) on a Bode plot is:","options":["5 rad/s","0.2 rad/s","1 rad/s","25 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rewriting in time-constant form gives G(s) = (1/5) / (1 + s/5) = K / (1 + sT), where T = 1/5 s. The corner frequency is ωc = 1/T = 5 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an open-loop control system, the control action is:","options":["Completely independent of the output response","Continuously modified based on the error signal","Determined by feedback transducers","Immune to internal parameter drift"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Open-loop systems lack feedback; the controller operates strictly on the reference input without sensing or correcting for output errors or external disturbances."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Laplace transform of a unit parabolic function r(t) = (t² / 2) u(t) is:","options":["1 / s³","1 / s²","2 / s³","1 / (2s²)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"L{t^n} = n! / s^(n+1). For r(t) = t²/2: L{r(t)} = (1/2) * (2! / s³) = 1 / s³."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A closed-loop control system with higher bandwidth exhibits:","options":["Faster rise time but greater susceptibility to high-frequency noise","Slower rise time and improved noise filtering","Higher steady-state error for step inputs","Purely imaginary closed-loop poles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bandwidth is inversely proportional to rise time (BW ∝ 1/tr). Higher bandwidth allows the system to follow rapid command changes quickly, but it also admits more high-frequency noise."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The parallel transfer function form of an ideal PID controller is given by:","options":["Gc(s) = Kp + Ki/s + Kd*s","Gc(s) = Kp * (Ki/s) * (Kd*s)","Gc(s) = Kp + Ki*s + Kd/s","Gc(s) = (Kp*s + Ki) / (Kd*s²)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A parallel PID controller sums proportional, integral, and derivative terms: Gc(s) = Kp + Ki/s + Kd*s = Kp * [1 + 1/(Ti*s) + Td*s]."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the polar plane, constant M-circles represent:","options":["Loci of constant closed-loop magnitude |T(jω)|","Loci of constant closed-loop phase angle","Loci of constant open-loop phase margin","Loci of constant damping ratio ζ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"M-circles are families of circles plotted in the complex G(jω)-plane representing constant closed-loop magnitude ratios M = |C(jω)/R(jω)| = |G(jω) / (1 + G(jω))|."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a phase-lead compensator Gc(s) = (1 + aTs) / (1 + Ts) with a > 1, maximum phase lead occurs at frequency ωm equal to:","options":["ωm = 1 / (T * √a)","ωm = √a / T","ωm = 1 / (aT)","ωm = a / T"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Corner frequencies are ω1 = 1/(aT) (zero) and ω2 = 1/T (pole). Maximum phase lead occurs at their geometric mean: ωm = √(ω1 * ω2) = √[ (1/(aT)) * (1/T) ] = 1 / (T * √a)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Root locus plots are always symmetrical with respect to the:","options":["Real axis of the complex s-plane","Imaginary (jω) axis","Origin (0,0)","Centroid σa line"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because physical LTI systems have real coefficients in their characteristic polynomial, any complex roots must occur in complex conjugate pairs (σ ± jω), making the locus strictly symmetric about the real axis."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In state-space design, arbitrary closed-loop pole placement via state feedback u = -Kx is possible if and only if:","options":["The system (A, B) is completely state controllable","The system (A, C) is completely state observable","The open-loop system is already asymptotically stable","Matrix A is symmetric and positive definite"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to the pole placement theorem (Ackermann's formula), all n closed-loop eigenvalues can be placed at arbitrary locations in the complex plane if and only if the controllability matrix [B  AB ... A^(n-1)B] has full rank n."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a standard second-order system with damping ratio ζ ≤ 0.6, the Phase Margin (PM in degrees) is closely approximated by:","options":["PM ≈ 100 * ζ","PM ≈ 50 * ζ","PM ≈ 180 * ζ","PM ≈ 10 * ζ²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For underdamped systems, the exact relation PM = tan⁻¹[ 2ζ / √(√(4ζ⁴ + 1) - 2ζ²) ] is linear within ~1° error for ζ ≤ 0.6 as PM ≈ 100 * ζ (e.g., ζ = 0.5 yields PM ≈ 50°)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The inclusion of a pure transport lag (dead time e^(-sTd)) in a control loop impacts the frequency response by:","options":["Introducing a phase lag of (ω * Td) radians without changing the magnitude response","Increasing gain at high frequencies by +20 dB/decade","Adding an open-loop pole at s = -1/Td","Increasing the phase margin by +90°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A transport delay e^(-jωTd) has unity magnitude (|e^(-jωTd)| = 1) at all frequencies, but adds phase lag ∠ = -ω*Td radians (-57.3*ω*Td degrees), reducing Phase Margin and destabilizing the loop."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a second-order underdamped system exhibiting a peak overshoot Mp = 16.3%, the damping ratio (ζ) is approximately:","options":["0.50","0.707","0.60","0.35"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mp = e^(-πζ / √(1 - ζ²)) = 0.163. Taking the natural logarithm gives -πζ / √(1 - ζ²) = -1.814 => ζ² / (1 - ζ²) = (1.814 / π)² ≈ 0.333 = 1/3 => 4ζ² = 1 => ζ = 0.50."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A system is defined by matrices A = [[0, 1], [-2, -3]] and B = [[0], [1]]. The state feedback gain matrix K = [k1, k2] for u = -Kx that places closed-loop poles at s = -2 and s = -4 is:","options":["K = [6, 3]","K = [8, 6]","K = [4, 1]","K = [3, 6]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The closed-loop characteristic polynomial is det(sI - (A - BK)) = s² + (3 + k2)s + (2 + k1). The desired polynomial is (s + 2)(s + 4) = s² + 6s + 8. Matching coefficients gives 3 + k2 = 6 => k2 = 3, and 2 + k1 = 8 => k1 = 6. Thus, K = [6, 3]."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a unity feedback system with open-loop transfer function G(s) = K(s + 2) / [s(s + 1)], the breakaway and break-in points on the real axis are:","options":["Breakaway at s = -0.586, Break-in at s = -3.414","Breakaway at s = -1.500, Break-in at s = -2.500","Breakaway at s = -0.414, Break-in at s = -2.414","Breakaway at s = -1.000, Break-in at s = -2.000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s² + s + K(s + 2) = 0 => K = -(s² + s) / (s + 2). Setting dK/ds = 0 gives (s + 2)(2s + 1) - (s² + s)(1) = 0 => s² + 4s + 2 = 0. Roots are s = [-4 ± √(16 - 8)] / 2 = -2 ± √2. Breakaway point is s = -0.586 (between 0 and -1) and break-in point is s = -3.414 (left of -2)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = K / [s(s + 1)(s + 2)]. For a ramp input r(t) = 2t u(t), the minimum gain K required to restrict steady-state error to ess ≤ 0.1 is:","options":["40","20","10","80"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Velocity error constant Kv = lim_{s->0} s * G(s) = K / 2. Steady-state error for r(t) = 2t is ess = 2 / Kv = 2 / (K/2) = 4 / K. For ess ≤ 0.1: 4 / K ≤ 0.1 => K ≥ 40."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A phase-lead network has transfer function Gc(s) = (1 + 0.3s) / (1 + 0.1s). The maximum phase lead angle (ϕm) and the frequency (ωm) at which it occurs are:","options":["ϕm = 30°, ωm = 5.77 rad/s","ϕm = 45°, ωm = 3.33 rad/s","ϕm = 30°, ωm = 10.0 rad/s","ϕm = 60°, ωm = 5.77 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Here T = 0.3 and αT = 0.1 => α = 1/3. Maximum phase lead sin(ϕm) = (1 - α) / (1 + α) = (1 - 1/3) / (1 + 1/3) = 0.5 => ϕm = 30°. Peak frequency ωm = 1 / (T * √α) = 1 / (0.3 * √(1/3)) = √3 / 0.3 ≈ 5.77 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has forward path G(s) = K / [s(s² + 4s + 5)]. The value of K that produces sustained oscillations and the frequency of oscillation are:","options":["K = 20, ω = √5 rad/s","K = 5, ω = 2 rad/s","K = 20, ω = 5 rad/s","K = 10, ω = √2 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Characteristic equation: s³ + 4s² + 5s + K = 0. Routh array row s¹ is (20 - K) / 4. For sustained oscillation (marginal stability), 20 - K = 0 => K = 20. Auxiliary equation from row s²: 4s² + 20 = 0 => s² = -5 => s = ±j√5 rad/s, so ω = √5 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The open-loop transfer function of a feedback system is G(s)H(s) = 1 / (s + 1)³. The Gain Margin (GM) of the system is:","options":["8 (or 18.06 dB)","4 (or 12.04 dB)","2 (or 6.02 dB)","16 (or 24.08 dB)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phase ∠GH = -3 tan⁻¹(ω). At phase crossover, -3 tan⁻¹(ωpc) = -180° => tan⁻¹(ωpc) = 60° => ωpc = √3 rad/s. Magnitude at ωpc is |GH| = 1 / [√(1 + 3)]³ = 1 / 2³ = 1/8. Gain Margin GM = 1 / |GH| = 8 (linear) = 20 log10(8) ≈ 18.06 dB."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Gain Crossover Frequency (ωgc) of an open-loop frequency response is the frequency at which:","options":["Open-loop magnitude |G(jω)H(jω)| equals 1 (or 0 dB)","Phase angle of G(jω)H(jω) equals -180°","Gain Margin reaches infinity","Closed-loop gain drops to zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By definition, the gain crossover frequency ωgc is the specific frequency where the open-loop magnitude ratio equals unity (|G(jω)H(jω)| = 1), which corresponds to 0 dB on a Bode magnitude plot."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Adding an open-loop zero to a closed-loop forward-path transfer function generally results in:","options":["Pulling the root locus to the left, speeding up transient response and improving stability","Pushing the root locus to the right and reducing stability","Increasing steady-state error significantly","Making the system completely non-linear"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adding an open-loop zero introduces a derivative (anticipatory) effect, which bends the root locus branches toward the left-half s-plane, improving relative stability and reducing settling time."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a Type-2 control system subjected to a unit parabolic input r(t) = (t² / 2) u(t), the steady-state error is:","options":["Finite and equal to 1 / Ka","Zero","Infinite","1 / (1 + Kp)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Type-2 system possesses two poles at the origin, yielding a finite acceleration error constant Ka = lim_{s->0} s² G(s)H(s) and a finite steady-state error ess = 1 / Ka for a parabolic input."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In automatic position control systems, a pair of matched potentiometers is commonly employed as an:","options":["Error detector that converts angular misalignment into a proportional voltage error","Actuator to produce mechanical torque","Integrator to eliminate position offset","Amplifier to boost power levels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A potentiometer pair acts as an angular displacement comparator (error detector); the reference potentiometer sets desired position, and the output potentiometer tracks actual position, generating a difference voltage e(t) = Kp * (θ_ref - θ_out)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A pure transport delay (dead time) of Td seconds has a transfer function expressed as:","options":["e^(-sTd)","1 / (sTd)","sTd","e^(sTd)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to the time-shifting theorem of Laplace transforms, an ideal pure delay of Td seconds is represented in the s-domain by the non-rational factor e^(-sTd)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If the damping ratio (ζ) of a second-order control system is strictly zero (ζ = 0), the system exhibits:","options":["Undamped sustained harmonic oscillations at its undamped natural frequency (ωn)","Overdamped sluggish response with zero oscillation","Critically damped response with fastest non-oscillatory settling","Complete exponential decay to zero instantly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When ζ = 0, closed-loop poles lie strictly on the imaginary axis at s = ±jωn. The system has zero internal damping and produces continuous, undamped sustained sinusoidal oscillations."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a synchro error detector consisting of a Synchro Transmitter (TX) and Synchro Control Transformer (CT), the induced rotor voltage of the CT is proportional to:","options":["The angular difference between the transmitter and receiver shaft angles","The absolute rotor speed in RPM","The supply frequency squared","The mechanical load torque on the transmitter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The output voltage induced in the rotor winding of the control transformer is proportional to the sine of the angular misalignment between the transmitter and receiver shafts: e(t) = K * sin(θ1 - θ2) ≈ K * (θ1 - θ2) for small angles."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When open-loop poles lie on the imaginary axis, the standard Nyquist contour is modified by:","options":["Indenting around the poles into the right-half s-plane with semicircles of infinitesimal radius ε -> 0","Extending the contour into the left-half s-plane","Ignoring the poles entirely","Closing the contour at s = 0 only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To prevent the Nyquist contour from passing directly through singularities on the jω-axis (such as poles at the origin), it is indented around those poles into the right-half s-plane using small semicircles of radius ε with s = ε e^(jθ), where θ sweeps from -90° to +90°."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"On a Nichols chart, the constant N-circles represent loci of:","options":["Constant closed-loop phase angle","Constant closed-loop magnitude","Constant open-loop gain margin","Constant damping ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On a Nichols chart, M-circles represent contours of constant closed-loop magnitude |T(jω)| (in dB), while N-circles represent contours of constant closed-loop phase angle ∠T(jω)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A state-space model x_dot = Ax + Bu, y = Cx is transformed to new state variables z using x = Tz (where T is invertible). The transformed state matrices (A_new, B_new, C_new) are:","options":["A_new = T⁻¹AT, B_new = T⁻¹B, C_new = CT","A_new = TAT⁻¹, B_new = TB, C_new = CT⁻¹","A_new = T⁻¹AT, B_new = TB, C_new = C","A_new = AT, B_new = B, C_new = C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Substituting x = Tz into the state equations yields T z_dot = A Tz + B u => z_dot = (T⁻¹ A T) z + (T⁻¹ B) u. The output is y = C Tz. Hence, A_new = T⁻¹AT, B_new = T⁻¹B, and C_new = CT."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"On the real axis of the s-plane, a point belongs to the root locus if and only if:","options":["The total number of real open-loop poles and zeros to the right of that point is odd","The total number of real poles and zeros to the right of that point is even","The number of poles to the left is greater than zeros to the right","The point is equidistant from all complex conjugate poles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because each real pole or zero to the right of a point s on the real axis contributes -180° or +180° of phase angle, an odd number of real poles and zeros to its right yields a net angle of ±(2k + 1)*180°, satisfying the angle criterion."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A phase-lag compensator improves steady-state accuracy primarily by:","options":["Attenuating high frequencies to lower the gain crossover frequency, allowing an increase in low-frequency open-loop gain","Increasing system bandwidth to pass high frequencies","Adding derivative action to speed up transient settling","Introducing complex conjugate poles on the imaginary axis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A lag compensator provides high gain at low frequencies to minimize steady-state errors while attenuating high frequencies. This shifts the gain crossover frequency to a lower value where the original phase margin is naturally higher."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Describing Function method used to analyze non-linear control systems is fundamentally based on the assumption that:","options":["The linear plant acts as a low-pass filter, attenuating higher harmonics produced by the non-linearity","The non-linearity is completely memoryless and time-invariant","The non-linear component generates sub-harmonics exclusively","The feedback loop contains no phase lag"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The describing function approach assumes the system input to the non-linear element is purely sinusoidal and that the linear dynamics downstream act as an effective low-pass filter, making fundamental harmonic components dominant while suppressing higher harmonics."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a closed-loop system, an external disturbance D(s) enters between forward-path blocks G1(s) and G2(s). The closed-loop transfer function Y(s)/D(s) with feedback H(s) is:","options":["G2(s) / [1 + G1(s)G2(s)H(s)]","G1(s)G2(s) / [1 + G1(s)G2(s)H(s)]","1 / [1 + G1(s)G2(s)H(s)]","G1(s) / [1 + G1(s)G2(s)H(s)]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The forward path from disturbance D(s) to output Y(s) contains only G2(s). The closed loop around the system remains G1(s)G2(s)H(s), giving Y(s)/D(s) = G2(s) / [1 + G1(s)G2(s)H(s)]."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A unity feedback system has open-loop transfer function G(s) = 50 / [s(s + 10)]. For a ramp input r(t) = 4t u(t), the steady-state tracking error is:","options":["0.80","0.20","1.25","Zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Velocity error constant Kv = lim_{s->0} s * G(s) = lim_{s->0} [s * 50 / (s(s + 10))] = 50 / 10 = 5. Steady-state error for input r(t) = A*t (where A = 4) is ess = A / Kv = 4 / 5 = 0.80."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"For a system matrix A = [[0, 1], [0, -2]], the state transition matrix Φ(t) = e^(At) is:","options":["[[1, 0.5 * (1 - e^(-2t))], [0, e^(-2t)]]","[[e^(-2t), 0], [0, 1]]","[[1, t], [0, e^(-2t)]]","[[e^(-2t), 0.5 * (1 - e^(-2t))], [0, 1]]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"(sI - A) = [[s, -1], [0, s + 2]]. Inverse (sI - A)⁻¹ = (1 / [s(s + 2)]) * [[s + 2, 1], [0, s]] = [[1/s, 1/(s(s + 2))], [0, 1/(s + 2)]]. Using partial fractions: 1 / [s(s + 2)] = 0.5/s - 0.5/(s + 2). Inverse Laplace yields Φ(t) = [[1, 0.5*(1 - e^(-2t))], [0, e^(-2t)]]."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A phase-lead compensator has a zero at s = -2 and a pole at s = -6. The maximum phase lead angle (ϕm) and the frequency (ωm) at which it occurs are:","options":["ϕm = 30.0°, ωm = 3.464 rad/s","ϕm = 45.0°, ωm = 4.000 rad/s","ϕm = 19.5°, ωm = 3.464 rad/s","ϕm = 30.0°, ωm = 4.000 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Here zero z = 2 and pole p = 6. Compensation ratio α = z / p = 2 / 6 = 1/3. Maximum phase lead satisfies sin(ϕm) = (1 - α) / (1 + α) = (1 - 1/3) / (1 + 1/3) = (2/3) / (4/3) = 0.5 => ϕm = 30°. Geometric mean frequency ωm = √(z * p) = √(2 * 6) = √12 ≈ 3.464 rad/s."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The characteristic polynomial of a system is s⁴ + 3s³ + 3s² + 2s + K = 0. For the system to be strictly stable, the gain K must satisfy:","options":["0 < K < 14/9 (≈ 1.556)","0 < K < 3","K > 14/9","0 < K < 0.667"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Routh array: s⁴: [1, 3, K]; s³: [3, 2, 0]; s²: [(9 - 2)/3, K] = [7/3, K]; s¹: [ ( (7/3)*2 - 3*K ) / (7/3) ] = [ 2 - (9/7)*K ]; s⁰: [K]. For stability, all first column terms must be strictly positive: K > 0 and 2 - (9/7)*K > 0 => 9K/7 < 2 => K < 14/9 ≈ 1.556."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A second-order feedback control system has closed-loop transfer function T(s) = 100 / (s² + 10s + 100). The resonant frequency (ωr) and peak resonant magnitude (Mr) are:","options":["ωr = 7.07 rad/s, Mr = 1.155 (1.25 dB)","ωr = 10.0 rad/s, Mr = 1.000 (0 dB)","ωr = 8.66 rad/s, Mr = 1.414 (3 dB)","ωr = 5.00 rad/s, Mr = 2.000 (6 dB)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Comparing with s² + 2ζωn s + ωn²: ωn = 10 rad/s and 2ζωn = 10 => ζ = 0.5. Resonant frequency ωr = ωn √(1 - 2ζ²) = 10 * √(1 - 2*(0.25)) = 10 * √0.5 = 10 / √2 ≈ 7.07 rad/s. Resonant peak Mr = 1 / [2ζ√(1 - ζ²)] = 1 / [2*(0.5)*√(1 - 0.25)] = 1 / √0.75 = 2 / √3 ≈ 1.155 (or 20 log10(1.155) ≈ 1.25 dB)."},{"subject":"Control Systems","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A system is defined by state matrices A = [[0, 1], [-2, -3]], B = [[0], [1]], C = [1, 1], and D = 0. The transfer function Y(s)/U(s) exhibits a pole-zero cancellation and simplifies to:","options":["1 / (s + 2)","(s + 1) / (s² + 3s + 2)","1 / (s + 1)","(s + 2) / (s² + 3s + 2)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transfer function is G(s) = C*(sI - A)⁻¹*B. Here (sI - A)⁻¹*B = [1; s] / (s² + 3s + 2). Multiplying by C = [1, 1] gives (1 + s) / (s² + 3s + 2). Because denominator factors into (s + 1)(s + 2), the (s + 1) term cancels: G(s) = (s + 1) / [(s + 1)(s + 2)] = 1 / (s + 2)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Permanent Magnet Moving Coil (PMMC) instruments can be used strictly for measuring:","options":["DC quantities only","AC quantities only","Both AC and DC quantities","High-frequency RF signals only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PMMC instruments produce a deflecting torque proportional to current (Td = BINA). On AC, the torque alternates direction every half-cycle, averaging to zero due to pointer inertia. Hence, they measure only DC."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The scale of a Moving Iron (MI) instrument is non-linear and cramped near the beginning because the deflecting torque is proportional to:","options":["Current (I)","Square of current (I²)","Square root of current (√I)","Cube of current (I³)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In moving-iron instruments, deflecting torque is Td = 0.5 * I² * (dL/dθ). Because torque is proportional to the square of current (I²), the scale follows a square-law distribution, making it cramped at low values and wider at the top."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The material predominantly used for making shunts to extend the range of PMMC ammeters is:","options":["Copper","Manganin","Aluminum","Nichrome"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Manganin has a virtually zero temperature coefficient of resistance and low thermal EMF with copper, ensuring that instrument calibration remains stable across ambient temperature variations."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Creeping in a single-phase induction type energy meter is prevented by:","options":["Using a strong braking magnet","Drilling two diametrically opposite holes in the aluminum disc","Increasing friction compensation torque","Reducing the turns of the pressure coil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Creeping (slow continuous disc rotation under no-load with only potential coil energized) is prevented by drilling two diametrically opposite holes in the disc. When a hole arrives under the shunt magnet pole, the reluctance increases, arresting rotation."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Megger is an instrument specifically designed for measuring:","options":["Very low contact resistance (< 1 Ω)","Medium resistance (1 Ω to 100 kΩ)","High insulation resistance (in Mega-ohms)","AC ground impedance only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Megger incorporates a hand-driven or battery-powered DC generator (typically 500 V to 2500 V) and a ratio-meter movement to measure high insulation resistances of cables, transformers, and electrical machines."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The time-base generator in a Cathode Ray Oscilloscope (CRO) produces which type of waveform?","options":["Pure sinusoidal wave","Square wave","Sawtooth (ramp) wave","Triangular wave"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The time-base circuit generates a linear sawtooth (ramp) voltage that sweeps the electron beam horizontally at a constant velocity from left to right across the screen before flying back rapidly."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an electrodynamometer wattmeter, the pressure (voltage) coil is designed to be:","options":["Highly inductive with low resistance","Highly resistive with negligible inductance","Purely capacitive","Identical in impedance to the current coil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pressure coil circuit is wound with fine wire in series with a large non-inductive resistor so that the pressure coil current is in phase with the applied system voltage."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Hay's bridge is preferred over Maxwell's bridge for measuring the inductance of coils having:","options":["Low storage factor (Q < 1)","Medium storage factor (1 < Q < 10)","High storage factor (Q > 10)","Zero storage factor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maxwell's bridge uses a parallel RC arm requiring very high resistance values for high-Q coils. Hay's bridge places resistance in series with capacitance in the standard arm, making it suitable for coils with Q > 10."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Schering bridge is widely employed in high-voltage laboratories for measuring:","options":["Low resistance and thermoelectric EMF","Capacitance and dielectric loss angle (dissipation factor tan δ)","Mutual inductance between coupled coils","Audio frequencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Schering bridge measures capacitance and dielectric dissipation factor (tan δ = ω * C4 * R4) of high-voltage cables, transformer bushings, and insulating oils."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Wien's bridge is balanced only at a specific frequency and is commonly used as a:","options":["Precision resistance comparator","Frequency-measuring bridge and harmonic distortion analyzer","Self-inductance measuring bridge","Magnetic flux meter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wien's bridge balance condition depends directly on frequency: f = 1 / (2π * √(R1 * R2 * C1 * C2)). It is used to measure audio frequencies and as a notch filter in distortion analyzers."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The residual voltage appearing at the null position of a Linear Variable Differential Transformer (LVDT) is caused by:","options":["Imperfect magnetic symmetry and stray capacitive coupling between primary and secondary windings","DC saturation of the nickel-iron core","Over-excitation of the primary winding","High ambient operating temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even when the core is centered, slight magnetic non-uniformities and inter-winding capacitance induce small out-of-phase fundamental and harmonic voltages, preventing the secondary differential output from reaching zero."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Gauge Factor (GF) of an electrical resistance strain gauge is expressed in terms of Poisson's ratio (ν) and piezoresistive coefficient as:","options":["GF = 1 + 2ν + (Δρ/ρ) / ε","GF = 1 - 2ν + (Δρ/ρ) / ε","GF = 2 + ν + (Δρ/ρ) * ε","GF = (1 + ν) / ε"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The gauge factor is GF = (ΔR/R) / ε = 1 + 2ν + (Δρ/ρ)/ε, where 1 represents longitudinal dimensional change, 2ν accounts for lateral Poisson contraction, and (Δρ/ρ)/ε represents the piezoresistive resistivity change."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Phantom (fictitious) loading is used during the routine calibration testing of high-capacity energy meters to:","options":["Test the meter without consuming large amounts of real electrical power","Eliminate braking magnet demagnetization","Prevent disc overheating due to eddy currents","Eliminate creeping errors completely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pressure coil is energized from rated voltage, while the current coil is supplied from a separate low-voltage source supplying full-load current. This tests the meter at full VA loading while consuming minimal active test power."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The phase angle error in a Current Transformer (CT) is primarily caused by:","options":["Secondary winding resistance only","The magnetizing and core-loss components of the excitation current","Primary conductor skin effect","Ambient magnetic leakage around the primary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because a portion of primary current is drawn as excitation current (magnetizing current Im and core loss current Ic) to sustain core flux, the reversed secondary current phasor does not line up at 180° with the primary phasor, introducing a phase angle error."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A PMMC galvanometer has an internal resistance Rm = 100 Ω and gives full-scale deflection for a current of 1 mA. To convert it into a DC voltmeter with a range of 0 to 100 V, the series multiplier resistance (Rse) required is:","options":["99.9 kΩ","100.1 kΩ","9.9 kΩ","990 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total resistance required R_total = V / Im = 100 V / 0.001 A = 100,000 Ω. Series multiplier resistance Rse = R_total - Rm = 100,000 - 100 = 99,900 Ω = 99.9 kΩ."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A stationary Lissajous pattern observed on a CRO screen displays 4 horizontal tangencies and 2 vertical tangencies. If the horizontal input frequency is 50 Hz, the vertical input frequency is:","options":["100 Hz","25 Hz","200 Hz","50 Hz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lissajous frequency ratio is: (Vertical frequency fy) / (Horizontal frequency fx) = (Number of horizontal tangencies) / (Number of vertical tangencies) = 4 / 2 = 2. Therefore, fy = 2 * fx = 2 * 50 Hz = 100 Hz."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a Kelvin double bridge used for precision measurement of low resistance, the resistance of the connecting leads between the test resistor and standard resistor is eliminated when:","options":["P / Q = p / q","P * Q = p * q","P / p = q / Q","P + Q = p + q"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The bridge equation is R = (P/Q)*S + [qr / (p + q + r)] * (P/Q - p/q). When the ratio of the outer arms equals the ratio of the inner arms (P/Q = p/q), the second term vanishes, eliminating lead resistance r."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A dynamometer wattmeter has a pressure coil of inductance L and resistance Rp. When measuring active power of a load operating at a lagging power factor angle ϕ, the wattmeter reading is:","options":["Higher than true power","Lower than true power","Strictly equal to true power","Independent of pressure coil inductance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pressure coil inductance causes its current to lag voltage by angle β = tan⁻¹(ωL/Rp). For a lagging load, the effective angle between pressure and current coils decreases from ϕ to (ϕ - β), causing the meter to over-read: W_measured = W_true * [cos β * cos(ϕ - β) / cos ϕ] > W_true."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An energy meter with a meter constant of 1200 revolutions/kWh makes 360 revolutions in 15 minutes when connected to a steady load of 1 kW at unity power factor. The percentage error of the meter is:","options":["+20% (Fast)","-20% (Slow)","+16.67% (Fast)","-16.67% (Slow)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Energy consumed in 15 minutes = 1 kW * (15/60) h = 0.25 kWh. Expected revolutions = 0.25 kWh * 1200 rev/kWh = 300 revolutions. Percentage error = [(Actual - Expected) / Expected] * 100 = [(360 - 300) / 300] * 100 = +20% (Meter runs 20% fast)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3½ digit dual-slope integrating digital voltmeter (DVM) uses a 100 kHz clock. The run-up integration time is fixed at 2000 clock counts. The maximum total conversion time for full-scale voltage is:","options":["40 ms","20 ms","10 ms","80 ms"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Run-up time t1 = 2000 / 100,000 = 0.02 s = 20 ms. For a 3½ digit DVM (maximum display 1999 counts), maximum de-integration (run-down) time t2 is also 2000 counts = 20 ms. Maximum total conversion time = t1 + t2 = 20 ms + 20 ms = 40 ms."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Permanent Magnet Moving Coil (PMMC) instruments can be used strictly for measuring:","options":["DC quantities only","AC quantities only","Both AC and DC quantities","High-frequency RF signals only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PMMC instruments produce a deflecting torque proportional to current (Td = BINA). On AC, the torque alternates direction every half-cycle, averaging to zero due to pointer inertia. Hence, they measure only DC."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The scale of a Moving Iron (MI) instrument is non-linear and cramped near the beginning because the deflecting torque is proportional to:","options":["Current (I)","Square of current (I²)","Square root of current (√I)","Cube of current (I³)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In moving-iron instruments, deflecting torque is Td = 0.5 * I² * (dL/dθ). Because torque is proportional to the square of current (I²), the scale follows a square-law distribution, making it cramped at low values and wider at the top."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The material predominantly used for making shunts to extend the range of PMMC ammeters is:","options":["Copper","Manganin","Aluminum","Nichrome"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Manganin has a virtually zero temperature coefficient of resistance and low thermal EMF with copper, ensuring that instrument calibration remains stable across ambient temperature variations."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Creeping in a single-phase induction type energy meter is prevented by:","options":["Using a strong braking magnet","Drilling two diametrically opposite holes in the aluminum disc","Increasing friction compensation torque","Reducing the turns of the pressure coil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Creeping (slow continuous disc rotation under no-load with only potential coil energized) is prevented by drilling two diametrically opposite holes in the disc. When a hole arrives under the shunt magnet pole, the reluctance increases, arresting rotation."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Megger is an instrument specifically designed for measuring:","options":["Very low contact resistance (< 1 Ω)","Medium resistance (1 Ω to 100 kΩ)","High insulation resistance (in Mega-ohms)","AC ground impedance only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Megger incorporates a hand-driven or battery-powered DC generator (typically 500 V to 2500 V) and a ratio-meter movement to measure high insulation resistances of cables, transformers, and electrical machines."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The time-base generator in a Cathode Ray Oscilloscope (CRO) produces which type of waveform?","options":["Pure sinusoidal wave","Square wave","Sawtooth (ramp) wave","Triangular wave"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The time-base circuit generates a linear sawtooth (ramp) voltage that sweeps the electron beam horizontally at a constant velocity from left to right across the screen before flying back rapidly."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an electrodynamometer wattmeter, the pressure (voltage) coil is designed to be:","options":["Highly inductive with low resistance","Highly resistive with negligible inductance","Purely capacitive","Identical in impedance to the current coil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pressure coil circuit is wound with fine wire in series with a large non-inductive resistor so that the pressure coil current is in phase with the applied system voltage."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Hay's bridge is preferred over Maxwell's bridge for measuring the inductance of coils having:","options":["Low storage factor (Q < 1)","Medium storage factor (1 < Q < 10)","High storage factor (Q > 10)","Zero storage factor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maxwell's bridge uses a parallel RC arm requiring very high resistance values for high-Q coils. Hay's bridge places resistance in series with capacitance in the standard arm, making it suitable for coils with Q > 10."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Schering bridge is widely employed in high-voltage laboratories for measuring:","options":["Low resistance and thermoelectric EMF","Capacitance and dielectric loss angle (dissipation factor tan δ)","Mutual inductance between coupled coils","Audio frequencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Schering bridge measures capacitance and dielectric dissipation factor (tan δ = ω * C4 * R4) of high-voltage cables, transformer bushings, and insulating oils."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Wien's bridge is balanced only at a specific frequency and is commonly used as a:","options":["Precision resistance comparator","Frequency-measuring bridge and harmonic distortion analyzer","Self-inductance measuring bridge","Magnetic flux meter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wien's bridge balance condition depends directly on frequency: f = 1 / (2π * √(R1 * R2 * C1 * C2)). It is used to measure audio frequencies and as a notch filter in distortion analyzers."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The residual voltage appearing at the null position of a Linear Variable Differential Transformer (LVDT) is caused by:","options":["Imperfect magnetic symmetry and stray capacitive coupling between primary and secondary windings","DC saturation of the nickel-iron core","Over-excitation of the primary winding","High ambient operating temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even when the core is centered, slight magnetic non-uniformities and inter-winding capacitance induce small out-of-phase fundamental and harmonic voltages, preventing the secondary differential output from reaching zero."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Gauge Factor (GF) of an electrical resistance strain gauge is expressed in terms of Poisson's ratio (ν) and piezoresistive coefficient as:","options":["GF = 1 + 2ν + (Δρ/ρ) / ε","GF = 1 - 2ν + (Δρ/ρ) / ε","GF = 2 + ν + (Δρ/ρ) * ε","GF = (1 + ν) / ε"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The gauge factor is GF = (ΔR/R) / ε = 1 + 2ν + (Δρ/ρ)/ε, where 1 represents longitudinal dimensional change, 2ν accounts for lateral Poisson contraction, and (Δρ/ρ)/ε represents the piezoresistive resistivity change."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Phantom (fictitious) loading is used during the routine calibration testing of high-capacity energy meters to:","options":["Test the meter without consuming large amounts of real electrical power","Eliminate braking magnet demagnetization","Prevent disc overheating due to eddy currents","Eliminate creeping errors completely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pressure coil is energized from rated voltage, while the current coil is supplied from a separate low-voltage source supplying full-load current. This tests the meter at full VA loading while consuming minimal active test power."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The phase angle error in a Current Transformer (CT) is primarily caused by:","options":["Secondary winding resistance only","The magnetizing and core-loss components of the excitation current","Primary conductor skin effect","Ambient magnetic leakage around the primary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because a portion of primary current is drawn as excitation current (magnetizing current Im and core loss current Ic) to sustain core flux, the reversed secondary current phasor does not line up at 180° with the primary phasor, introducing a phase angle error."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A PMMC galvanometer has an internal resistance Rm = 100 Ω and gives full-scale deflection for a current of 1 mA. To convert it into a DC voltmeter with a range of 0 to 100 V, the series multiplier resistance (Rse) required is:","options":["99.9 kΩ","100.1 kΩ","9.9 kΩ","990 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total resistance required R_total = V / Im = 100 V / 0.001 A = 100,000 Ω. Series multiplier resistance Rse = R_total - Rm = 100,000 - 100 = 99,900 Ω = 99.9 kΩ."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A stationary Lissajous pattern observed on a CRO screen displays 4 horizontal tangencies and 2 vertical tangencies. If the horizontal input frequency is 50 Hz, the vertical input frequency is:","options":["100 Hz","25 Hz","200 Hz","50 Hz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lissajous frequency ratio is: (Vertical frequency fy) / (Horizontal frequency fx) = (Number of horizontal tangencies) / (Number of vertical tangencies) = 4 / 2 = 2. Therefore, fy = 2 * fx = 2 * 50 Hz = 100 Hz."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a Kelvin double bridge used for precision measurement of low resistance, the resistance of the connecting leads between the test resistor and standard resistor is eliminated when:","options":["P / Q = p / q","P * Q = p * q","P / p = q / Q","P + Q = p + q"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The bridge equation is R = (P/Q)*S + [qr / (p + q + r)] * (P/Q - p/q). When the ratio of the outer arms equals the ratio of the inner arms (P/Q = p/q), the second term vanishes, eliminating lead resistance r."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A dynamometer wattmeter has a pressure coil of inductance L and resistance Rp. When measuring active power of a load operating at a lagging power factor angle ϕ, the wattmeter reading is:","options":["Higher than true power","Lower than true power","Strictly equal to true power","Independent of pressure coil inductance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pressure coil inductance causes its current to lag voltage by angle β = tan⁻¹(ωL/Rp). For a lagging load, the effective angle between pressure and current coils decreases from ϕ to (ϕ - β), causing the meter to over-read: W_measured = W_true * [cos β * cos(ϕ - β) / cos ϕ] > W_true."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An energy meter with a meter constant of 1200 revolutions/kWh makes 360 revolutions in 15 minutes when connected to a steady load of 1 kW at unity power factor. The percentage error of the meter is:","options":["+20% (Fast)","-20% (Slow)","+16.67% (Fast)","-16.67% (Slow)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Energy consumed in 15 minutes = 1 kW * (15/60) h = 0.25 kWh. Expected revolutions = 0.25 kWh * 1200 rev/kWh = 300 revolutions. Percentage error = [(Actual - Expected) / Expected] * 100 = [(360 - 300) / 300] * 100 = +20% (Meter runs 20% fast)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3½ digit dual-slope integrating digital voltmeter (DVM) uses a 100 kHz clock. The run-up integration time is fixed at 2000 clock counts. The maximum total conversion time for full-scale voltage is:","options":["40 ms","20 ms","10 ms","80 ms"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Run-up time t1 = 2000 / 100,000 = 0.02 s = 20 ms. For a 3½ digit DVM (maximum display 1999 counts), maximum de-integration (run-down) time t2 is also 2000 counts = 20 ms. Maximum total conversion time = t1 + t2 = 20 ms + 20 ms = 40 ms."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary advantage of an Ayrton (universal) shunt over an ordinary single-resistor shunt in multi-range ammeters is:","options":["It provides a constant multiplying power that is independent of the meter coil resistance","It completely eliminates ambient temperature errors","It allows measurement of AC currents without rectifiers","It reduces the required current sensitivity of the galvanometer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An Ayrton shunt provides multi-range current division ratios that are strictly independent of the internal resistance of the meter movement (Rm), allowing the same shunt network to be used with different galvanometers."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a single-phase induction type energy meter, friction compensation is provided by:","options":["An adjustable copper shading loop placed on the central limb of the shunt magnet","Two permanent braking magnets mounted diametrically opposite","A series choke inductor in the current coil circuit","A damping vane immersed in silicone fluid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Friction at the pivot and register gear train is compensated by an adjustable copper shading band or loop placed over the central limb of the shunt potential magnet, which creates a small asymmetrical driving torque even at zero load current."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary reason dual-slope integrating ADCs are widely chosen for digital multimeters (DMMs) is their:","options":["High conversion speed (< 1 µs)","Excellent rejection of power-line (50/60 Hz) noise and independence from component drift of R and C","Absence of a reference clock","Ability to measure RF voltages without demodulation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In dual-slope conversion, integration of the input signal over a period equal to an integer multiple of power-line period (20 ms for 50 Hz) provides near-infinite normal-mode noise rejection. Moreover, the integration resistor R and capacitor C cancel out in the final count ratio."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The most widely used metal for industrial Resistance Temperature Detectors (RTD), such as the standard Pt100, is:","options":["Platinum","Copper","Nickel","Tungsten"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Platinum is the industry standard due to its high chemical inertness, excellent linearity, wide operating temperature range (-200 °C to +850 °C), and resistance to oxidation."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"De Sauty's bridge is specifically designed for the comparison of:","options":["Two pure, loss-free capacitances","Low four-terminal resistances","High-Q inductors","Mutual inductances between coupled coils"],"answer":0,"explanation":"De Sauty's bridge is the simplest AC bridge method for comparing two capacitances, but it assumes both capacitors are ideal and loss-free (zero dielectric loss angle)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In DC potentiometers, calibration (standardization) of the slide-wire working current is achieved using a:","options":["Weston cadmium standard cell","Lead-acid storage battery","Zener breakdown diode reference directly without standardization","Mercury primary dry cell"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standardization sets the slide-wire working current to its exact calibrated value by comparing the voltage drop across a known slide-wire length against the known EMF of a Weston cadmium normal cell (1.0186 V at 20 °C)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The standard secondary rated voltage of a Potential Transformer (PT) used in high-voltage substations is:","options":["110 V (or 110/√3 V)","230 V","5 V","415 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard industrial and utility potential transformers are standardized to step down primary transmission and distribution voltages to a secondary rated voltage of 110 V line-to-line (or 63.5 V line-to-neutral)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 0-150 V analog voltmeter has a guaranteed accuracy of 1% of full-scale deflection. If it reads 75 V in a test circuit, the limiting error at this measured value is:","options":["±2.0%","±1.0%","±0.5%","±4.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Magnitude of guaranteed error = 1% of 150 V = 1.5 V. At a reading of 75 V, the percentage limiting error is (1.5 V / 75 V) * 100% = ±2.0%."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an electrodynamometer instrument measuring alternating currents, the deflecting torque (Td) is mathematically expressed as:","options":["Td = I1 * I2 * (dM/dθ) * cos(α)","Td = I1 * I2 * (dL/dθ)","Td = B * I * A * cos(α)","Td = 0.5 * I² * (dM/dθ)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In electrodynamometer movements, deflecting torque depends on the product of the RMS currents in the fixed and moving coils, the rate of change of mutual inductance with deflection (dM/dθ), and the cosine of the phase angle between them: Td = I1 * I2 * (dM/dθ) * cos(α)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Owen's bridge is widely utilized in laboratories because it:","options":["Measures self-inductance in terms of resistance and capacitance with balance equations completely independent of source frequency","Measures audio frequencies using variable inductors","Measures very high insulation resistances","Requires a variable calibrated inductor for null balance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Owen's bridge balance equations are L1 = R2 * R3 * C4 and R1 = (C4 / C2) * R3. Because both balance conditions are independent of angular frequency (ω), balance is easy to converge and it can measure incremental inductance over a wide frequency band."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a dual-trace oscilloscope, the 'CHOP' mode of vertical channel multiplexing is preferred over the 'ALT' mode when displaying:","options":["Low-frequency signals (typically below 1 kHz)","Very high-frequency signals (> 1 MHz)","Fast single-shot transient pulses with microsecond rise times","Signals having large DC offsets only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In CHOP mode, the electronic switch commutates between Channel A and Channel B at a fast rate (~100 kHz to 1 MHz) during each horizontal sweep, preventing visual flicker when viewing slow, low-frequency waveforms."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The output Hall voltage (VH) generated across a semiconductor Hall plate of thickness t carrying control current I in a transverse magnetic field B is:","options":["VH = (RH * I * B) / t","VH = (RH * t) / (I * B)","VH = RH * I * B * t","VH = (RH * B) / (I * t)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By the Hall effect, transverse electric field balances the Lorentz magnetic force: VH = (RH * I * B) / t, where RH is the Hall coefficient of the material and t is plate thickness in the direction of the magnetic field."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Thermistors generally exhibit a Negative Temperature Coefficient (NTC) of resistance governed by the exponential equation:","options":["RT = R0 * exp[ β * (1/T - 1/T0) ]","RT = R0 * [ 1 + α * (T - T0) ]","RT = R0 * exp[ -β * (T - T0) ]","RT = R0 * [ 1 + β * (1/T - 1/T0) ]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Semiconductor thermistors experience exponential thermal generation of charge carriers with temperature, following RT = R0 * exp[ β * (1/T - 1/T0) ], where temperatures are in Kelvin and β is the material characteristic temperature constant (typically 3000 to 4500 K)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The purpose of incorporating a Wagner earth device in precision high-voltage or high-frequency AC bridges is to:","options":["Eliminate measurement errors caused by stray capacitances between bridge junction nodes, detector terminals, and earth","Provide solid grounding for high fault currents","Compensate for mutual inductance between bridge arms","Step up the excitation supply voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At audio and radio frequencies, stray capacitances from bridge corner nodes and detector terminals to earth carry leakage currents that distort balance. A Wagner earth network brings the detector terminals to ground potential without connecting them directly to earth, eliminating stray capacitive currents through the detector."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"Two DC voltmeters, V1 (range 0-100 V, sensitivity 10 kΩ/V) and V2 (range 0-100 V, sensitivity 20 kΩ/V), are connected in series across a 150 V DC supply. The readings displayed on V1 and V2 are:","options":["V1 = 50 V, V2 = 100 V","V1 = 75 V, V2 = 75 V","V1 = 100 V, V2 = 50 V","V1 = 60 V, V2 = 90 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Internal resistance of V1: R1 = 100 V * 10 kΩ/V = 1000 kΩ = 1 MΩ. Internal resistance of V2: R2 = 100 V * 20 kΩ/V = 2000 kΩ = 2 MΩ. Total resistance R_total = 1 MΩ + 2 MΩ = 3 MΩ. Series current I = 150 V / 3 MΩ = 50 µA. Reading of V1 = 50 µA * 1 MΩ = 50 V. Reading of V2 = 50 µA * 2 MΩ = 100 V."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A Current Transformer with nominal turns ratio Kn = 100 (Np = 1, Ns = 100) delivers a secondary current of 5 A into a non-inductive resistive burden. If excitation current referred to the primary has magnetizing component Im = 3 A and iron-loss component Ic = 4 A, the actual transformation ratio (R) is:","options":["100.8","100.0","101.4","99.2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a purely resistive burden (secondary phase angle β = 0°), primary current is approximated by Ip ≈ (n * Is + Ic) with quadrature component Im: Ip = √[(n * Is + Ic)² + Im²] = √[(100 * 5 + 4)² + 3²] = √[504² + 9] ≈ 504.01 A. Actual transformation ratio R = Ip / Is = 504.01 / 5 ≈ 100.8."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a balanced Hay's bridge measuring a high-Q inductor at an angular frequency ω = 1000 rad/s, the bridge parameters are R2 = 1000 Ω, R3 = 1000 Ω, C4 = 0.1 µF, and coil quality factor Q = 10. The unknown self-inductance L1 is approximately:","options":["99.01 mH","100.00 mH","101.00 mH","10.00 mH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Hay's bridge: L1 = (R2 * R3 * C4) / [1 + (1 / Q²)]. Here R2 * R3 * C4 = 1000 * 1000 * (0.1 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.1 H = 100 mH. With Q = 10: 1 + (1 / 10²) = 1 + 0.01 = 1.01. Therefore, L1 = 100 mH / 1.01 ≈ 99.01 mH."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A quarter-bridge electrical resistance strain gauge circuit has an excitation bridge voltage Vs = 10 V and a gauge factor GF = 2.0. If the active strain gauge undergoes a tensile strain of ε = 1000 microstrain, the open-circuit output voltage (Vo) of the bridge is:","options":["5.0 mV","10.0 mV","2.5 mV","20.0 mV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Fractional resistance change is ΔR / R = GF * ε = 2.0 * (1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 2 × 10⁻³ = 0.002. For a quarter-bridge arrangement: Vo = (Vs / 4) * (ΔR / R) = (10 / 4) * 0.002 = 2.5 * 0.002 = 0.005 V = 5.0 mV."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 200 V, 10 A single-phase energy meter with a meter constant of 1000 revolutions/kWh is tested at rated voltage and current at unity power factor for 30 minutes. If the meter registers 1050 disc revolutions during this period, its percentage error is:","options":["+5.0% (Fast)","-5.0% (Slow)","+4.76% (Fast)","-4.76% (Slow)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True power P = V * I * cos φ = 200 V * 10 A * 1.0 = 2000 W = 2 kW. Energy consumed in 30 minutes (0.5 h) = 2 kW * 0.5 h = 1.0 kWh. Nominal expected revolutions = 1.0 kWh * 1000 rev/kWh = 1000 revolutions. Percentage error = [(Actual - Nominal) / Nominal] * 100% = [(1050 - 1000) / 1000] * 100% = +5.0% (runs fast)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"On a cathode ray oscilloscope (CRO), the horizontal time-base sweep speed is set to 0.5 ms/division. A displayed sinusoidal waveform completes exactly 2 full cycles across a horizontal length of 8 divisions. The frequency of the input signal is:","options":["500 Hz","250 Hz","1000 Hz","200 Hz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Horizontal span per cycle = 8 divisions / 2 cycles = 4 divisions/cycle. Time period of one cycle T = 4 divisions * 0.5 ms/division = 2.0 ms = 2.0 × 10⁻³ s. Signal frequency f = 1 / T = 1 / (2.0 × 10⁻³ s) = 500 Hz."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A full-wave rectifier PMMC voltmeter is calibrated to read the RMS value of sinusoidal voltages. The AC voltage sensitivity (S_ac) compared to DC sensitivity (S_dc) is:","options":["S_ac = 0.90 * S_dc","S_ac = 0.45 * S_dc","S_ac = 1.11 * S_dc","S_ac = S_dc"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PMMC instruments deflect proportional to average value (V_av = 2√2/π * V_rms ≈ 0.90 * V_rms). Consequently, the AC voltage sensitivity of a full-wave rectifier meter is S_ac = 0.90 * S_dc (and 0.45 * S_dc for a half-wave rectifier)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The 3-wire connection configuration in an industrial Platinum Resistance Temperature Detector (Pt100) bridge circuit is used to:","options":["Compensate for the temperature-dependent resistance of lead wires connecting the sensor to the bridge","Double the bridge supply voltage","Eliminate self-heating of the platinum coil","Convert the RTD from negative to positive temperature coefficient"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a 3-wire arrangement, two identical lead wires are placed into opposite adjacent arms of the Wheatstone bridge, causing their temperature-induced resistance changes to cancel each other out."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An electronic voltmeter utilizing a Field Effect Transistor (FET) input stage provides which primary operational advantage over a standard PMMC voltmeter?","options":["Extremely high input impedance (typically > 10 MΩ), resulting in negligible circuit loading errors","Zero power consumption from internal batteries","Linearity for radio frequencies up to 10 GHz","Immunity to reverse battery polarity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a reverse-biased FET gate draws virtually zero input current, the electronic voltmeter presents an input impedance of 10 MΩ or higher across all ranges, eliminating circuit loading across high-resistance nodes."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Unlike a dead-beat galvanometer, a ballistic galvanometer is constructed with:","options":["A large moment of inertia and negligible electromagnetic damping so that first deflection throw is proportional to total electric charge","Very light moving parts for instantaneous dead-beat settling","Strong eddy current damping using a solid copper former","A permanent magnet that rotates around a fixed coil"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A ballistic galvanometer measures the total transient charge (Q = ∫ i dt) passed during a short discharge pulse. It features a heavy coil (large moment of inertia) with low damping so that all charge passes before the coil begins its first mechanical swing."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Carey Foster slide-wire bridge is particularly suited for:","options":["Precise measurement of the small difference between two nearly equal resistances and determining slide-wire resistance per unit length","Measuring very high insulation resistances in Mega-ohms","Measuring self-inductance of air-core coils","Measuring audio frequencies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Carey Foster bridge is a modified Wheatstone bridge that compares two nearly equal resistances (X and Y) with high precision, eliminating lead and contact resistances by taking readings before and after interchanging the standard and unknown resistors."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Connecting a 10X passive attenuator probe to an oscilloscope has the primary effect of:","options":["Increasing the effective input resistance by a factor of 10 and reducing input capacitance, thereby minimizing circuit loading","Amplifying weak input voltages by a factor of 10","Eliminating time-base jitter on square waves","Converting AC input signals into DC levels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 10X probe places a 9 MΩ resistor in series with the oscilloscope's 1 MΩ input, raising total input resistance to 10 MΩ while dividing shunt capacitance by roughly 10, significantly reducing loading on high-frequency circuits."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Low Power Factor (LPF) electrodynamometer wattmeter is specifically designed with:","options":["A low-resistance pressure coil circuit, a compensating coil, and an adjustable capacitor across a portion of the series resistance to cancel pressure coil inductance","A very high resistance current coil","Zero control springs (fluid suspension)","Heavy iron shielding that increases magnetic reluctance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At low power factors, small phase errors in the pressure coil produce massive percentage measurement errors. LPF wattmeters use low-resistance pressure coils, compensating coils to neutralize pressure coil current in the current coil, and capacitance tuning to eliminate residual inductance."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Drysdale polar-type AC potentiometer, the phase angle of the working current is adjusted using a:","options":["Phase-shifting transformer (phase shifter) supplied from a split-phase or 2-phase source","Tuned parallel LC resonant filter","Variable manganin decade resistance box","High-frequency chopper oscillator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Drysdale potentiometer measures voltage in polar form (V ∠θ). A circular stator phase-shifting transformer generates a constant-magnitude rotating magnetic field; rotating the rotor adjusts the phase angle θ continuously from 0° to 360°."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Heaviside mutual inductance bridge is used to measure:","options":["Mutual inductance between two coils in terms of a known self-inductance","Capacitance of high-voltage bushings","Low four-terminal contact resistance","Resonance frequency of quartz crystals"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Heaviside bridge compares mutual inductance (M) directly against standard self-inductance and non-inductive resistance arms without requiring standard capacitors, using the balance relation M = (R2*L3 - R1*L4) / (R1 + R2)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a differential three-terminal capacitive displacement transducer with push-pull plates, moving the central plate provides:","options":["A linear output relationship with displacement and double the sensitivity compared to a single-capacitor transducer","A parabolic non-linear output response","Zero sensitivity to small displacements","Complete elimination of static dielectric breakdown"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With push-pull plates, as one capacitance increases (C1 = εA / (d - x)), the other decreases (C2 = εA / (d + x)). The differential output ratio (C1 - C2) / (C1 + C2) = x / d is linear with displacement x and cancels out common-mode non-linearities."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An n-bit Flash (parallel comparator) Analog-to-Digital Converter requires:","options":["(2ⁿ - 1) comparators and (2ⁿ) precision resistors, providing the highest conversion speed among all ADC architectures","n comparators and 2ⁿ resistors","2ⁿ comparators and n clock pulses per sample","Exactly one comparator and an n-bit binary counter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Flash ADC compares the analog input simultaneously against (2ⁿ - 1) reference voltage taps using (2ⁿ - 1) parallel comparators, completing the conversion in a single clock cycle at the expense of circuit complexity."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Epstein square testing frame is standardly utilized in magnetic measurements for:","options":["Determining total specific core losses (hysteresis and eddy current losses in W/kg) of electrical sheet steel laminations","Calibrating ballistic galvanometers against flux standards","Measuring earth electrode resistance in high-voltage substations","Determining Curie temperature of ferrite cores"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 25 cm Epstein square comprises four solenoids forming a closed magnetic square into which sample steel strips are inserted, allowing precise measurement of specific total core loss (W/kg) under alternating magnetic flux."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a digital frequency counter, the inherent measurement uncertainty known as 'gating error' is equal to:","options":["±1 count ambiguity of the least significant digit due to the lack of synchronization between input signal and time-base gate pulse","±5% of full-scale reading","Zero if crystal temperature compensation is utilized","The time-base oscillator drift per hour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because the arrival of input signal pulses and the opening/closing of the main time-base gate are completely asynchronous, a pulse may just be clipped or missed at the edges, resulting in an unavoidable ±1 count ambiguity."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The phase angle error of a Potential Transformer (PT) is primarily caused by:","options":["Primary and secondary winding leakage reactances and resistances interacting with excitation and burden currents","Inter-turn capacitive leakage at 50 Hz","Secondary terminal open-circuiting","Skin effect in the high-voltage bushing conductor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Internal impedance drops (I1*R1 + jI1*X1 and I2*R2 + jI2*X2) shift the secondary terminal voltage phasor out of exact 180° phase opposition with the primary supply voltage, introducing a phase angle error β."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The electric power consumed by a resistor is measured using P = V * I. The voltmeter reads 100 V with a limiting error of ±1.5%, and the ammeter reads 5 A with a limiting error of ±2.5%. The maximum percentage limiting error in the calculated power is:","options":["±4.0%","±2.0%","±3.75%","±1.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because power is given by the product P = V * I, the worst-case relative limiting error in the computed result equals the algebraic sum of the individual relative limiting errors: (ΔP / P) = ±(ΔV / V + ΔI / I) = ±(1.5% + 2.5%) = ±4.0%."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An oscilloscope has an input resistance of 1 MΩ shunted by 30 pF. A 10X passive probe is used (attenuation factor 10). To achieve perfect frequency compensation, the probe must have a series resistance (Rp) and shunt capacitance (Cp) of:","options":["Rp = 9 MΩ, Cp = 3.33 pF","Rp = 10 MΩ, Cp = 30.0 pF","Rp = 9 MΩ, Cp = 270.0 pF","Rp = 1 MΩ, Cp = 3.00 pF"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a 10:1 voltage division: Rp / (Rp + R_scope) = 9/10 => Rp = 9 * R_scope = 9 MΩ. For frequency compensation across all frequencies: Rp * Cp = R_scope * C_scope => 9 MΩ * Cp = 1 MΩ * 30 pF => Cp = 30 pF / 9 ≈ 3.33 pF."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A full-wave rectifier PMMC voltmeter is calibrated to read the RMS value of pure sinusoidal voltages (form factor 1.11). If this meter is used to measure a symmetrical square wave of peak value 20 V, the reading displayed on the meter is:","options":["22.2 V","20.0 V","14.14 V","18.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The PMMC movement deflects proportional to average value: for a symmetrical square wave of peak 20 V, V_av = 20 V. The meter scale is calibrated for sine waves as V_indicated = 1.11 * V_av. Therefore, the indicated reading is 1.11 * 20 V = 22.2 V (while true RMS is 20 V)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A Crompton DC potentiometer has a 100 cm slide wire. A standard cell of EMF 1.0186 V balances at a length of 50.93 cm. When measuring the voltage drop across an unknown resistor carrying steady current, balance is obtained at 75.00 cm. The unknown voltage is:","options":["1.50 V","1.00 V","0.75 V","2.04 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Potentiometer voltage gradient k = E_standard / L_standard = 1.0186 V / 50.93 cm = 0.02 V/cm (20 mV/cm). Unknown voltage Vx = k * L_unknown = 0.02 V/cm * 75.00 cm = 1.50 V."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A tuning coil tested with a Q-meter resonates at frequency f1 = 2 MHz with a tuning capacitance C1 = 460 pF. When frequency is doubled to f2 = 4 MHz, the circuit resonates with tuning capacitance C2 = 100 pF. The distributed self-capacitance (Cd) of the coil is:","options":["20 pF","15 pF","30 pF","10 pF"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because f2 = 2 * f1, frequency ratio is n = 2. Resonant frequency squared relates inversely to total capacitance: (f2 / f1)² = n² = 4 = (C1 + Cd) / (C2 + Cd). Thus: 4 * (100 + Cd) = 460 + Cd => 400 + 4 Cd = 460 + Cd => 3 Cd = 60 pF => Cd = 20 pF."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 6-bit Flash Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) requires how many analog voltage comparators and reference ladder resistors?","options":["63 comparators and 64 resistors","64 comparators and 63 resistors","31 comparators and 32 resistors","127 comparators and 128 resistors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an n-bit Flash ADC, the reference voltage divider network requires 2ⁿ equal resistors to generate 2ⁿ reference voltage levels, and (2ⁿ - 1) comparators to evaluate the input against these levels. For n = 6: comparators = 2⁶ - 1 = 63, resistors = 2⁶ = 64."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A full-wave rectifier PMMC voltmeter is calibrated to read the RMS value of sinusoidal voltages. The AC voltage sensitivity (S_ac) compared to DC sensitivity (S_dc) is:","options":["S_ac = 0.90 * S_dc","S_ac = 0.45 * S_dc","S_ac = 1.11 * S_dc","S_ac = S_dc"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PMMC instruments deflect proportional to average value (V_av = 2√2/π * V_rms ≈ 0.90 * V_rms). Consequently, the AC voltage sensitivity of a full-wave rectifier meter is S_ac = 0.90 * S_dc (and 0.45 * S_dc for a half-wave rectifier)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The 3-wire connection configuration in an industrial Platinum Resistance Temperature Detector (Pt100) bridge circuit is used to:","options":["Compensate for the temperature-dependent resistance of lead wires connecting the sensor to the bridge","Double the bridge supply voltage","Eliminate self-heating of the platinum coil","Convert the RTD from negative to positive temperature coefficient"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a 3-wire arrangement, two identical lead wires are placed into opposite adjacent arms of the Wheatstone bridge, causing their temperature-induced resistance changes to cancel each other out."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An electronic voltmeter utilizing a Field Effect Transistor (FET) input stage provides which primary operational advantage over a standard PMMC voltmeter?","options":["Extremely high input impedance (typically > 10 MΩ), resulting in negligible circuit loading errors","Zero power consumption from internal batteries","Linearity for radio frequencies up to 10 GHz","Immunity to reverse battery polarity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a reverse-biased FET gate draws virtually zero input current, the electronic voltmeter presents an input impedance of 10 MΩ or higher across all ranges, eliminating circuit loading across high-resistance nodes."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Unlike a dead-beat galvanometer, a ballistic galvanometer is constructed with:","options":["A large moment of inertia and negligible electromagnetic damping so that first deflection throw is proportional to total electric charge","Very light moving parts for instantaneous dead-beat settling","Strong eddy current damping using a solid copper former","A permanent magnet that rotates around a fixed coil"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A ballistic galvanometer measures the total transient charge (Q = ∫ i dt) passed during a short discharge pulse. It features a heavy coil (large moment of inertia) with low damping so that all charge passes before the coil begins its first mechanical swing."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Carey Foster slide-wire bridge is particularly suited for:","options":["Precise measurement of the small difference between two nearly equal resistances and determining slide-wire resistance per unit length","Measuring very high insulation resistances in Mega-ohms","Measuring self-inductance of air-core coils","Measuring audio frequencies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Carey Foster bridge is a modified Wheatstone bridge that compares two nearly equal resistances (X and Y) with high precision, eliminating lead and contact resistances by taking readings before and after interchanging the standard and unknown resistors."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Connecting a 10X passive attenuator probe to an oscilloscope has the primary effect of:","options":["Increasing the effective input resistance by a factor of 10 and reducing input capacitance, thereby minimizing circuit loading","Amplifying weak input voltages by a factor of 10","Eliminating time-base jitter on square waves","Converting AC input signals into DC levels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 10X probe places a 9 MΩ resistor in series with the oscilloscope's 1 MΩ input, raising total input resistance to 10 MΩ while dividing shunt capacitance by roughly 10, significantly reducing loading on high-frequency circuits."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Low Power Factor (LPF) electrodynamometer wattmeter is specifically designed with:","options":["A low-resistance pressure coil circuit, a compensating coil, and an adjustable capacitor across a portion of the series resistance to cancel pressure coil inductance","A very high resistance current coil","Zero control springs (fluid suspension)","Heavy iron shielding that increases magnetic reluctance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At low power factors, small phase errors in the pressure coil produce massive percentage measurement errors. LPF wattmeters use low-resistance pressure coils, compensating coils to neutralize pressure coil current in the current coil, and capacitance tuning to eliminate residual inductance."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Drysdale polar-type AC potentiometer, the phase angle of the working current is adjusted using a:","options":["Phase-shifting transformer (phase shifter) supplied from a split-phase or 2-phase source","Tuned parallel LC resonant filter","Variable manganin decade resistance box","High-frequency chopper oscillator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Drysdale potentiometer measures voltage in polar form (V ∠θ). A circular stator phase-shifting transformer generates a constant-magnitude rotating magnetic field; rotating the rotor adjusts the phase angle θ continuously from 0° to 360°."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Heaviside mutual inductance bridge is used to measure:","options":["Mutual inductance between two coils in terms of a known self-inductance","Capacitance of high-voltage bushings","Low four-terminal contact resistance","Resonance frequency of quartz crystals"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Heaviside bridge compares mutual inductance (M) directly against standard self-inductance and non-inductive resistance arms without requiring standard capacitors, using the balance relation M = (R2*L3 - R1*L4) / (R1 + R2)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a differential three-terminal capacitive displacement transducer with push-pull plates, moving the central plate provides:","options":["A linear output relationship with displacement and double the sensitivity compared to a single-capacitor transducer","A parabolic non-linear output response","Zero sensitivity to small displacements","Complete elimination of static dielectric breakdown"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With push-pull plates, as one capacitance increases (C1 = εA / (d - x)), the other decreases (C2 = εA / (d + x)). The differential output ratio (C1 - C2) / (C1 + C2) = x / d is linear with displacement x and cancels out common-mode non-linearities."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An n-bit Flash (parallel comparator) Analog-to-Digital Converter requires:","options":["(2ⁿ - 1) comparators and (2ⁿ) precision resistors, providing the highest conversion speed among all ADC architectures","n comparators and 2ⁿ resistors","2ⁿ comparators and n clock pulses per sample","Exactly one comparator and an n-bit binary counter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Flash ADC compares the analog input simultaneously against (2ⁿ - 1) reference voltage taps using (2ⁿ - 1) parallel comparators, completing the conversion in a single clock cycle at the expense of circuit complexity."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Epstein square testing frame is standardly utilized in magnetic measurements for:","options":["Determining total specific core losses (hysteresis and eddy current losses in W/kg) of electrical sheet steel laminations","Calibrating ballistic galvanometers against flux standards","Measuring earth electrode resistance in high-voltage substations","Determining Curie temperature of ferrite cores"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 25 cm Epstein square comprises four solenoids forming a closed magnetic square into which sample steel strips are inserted, allowing precise measurement of specific total core loss (W/kg) under alternating magnetic flux."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a digital frequency counter, the inherent measurement uncertainty known as 'gating error' is equal to:","options":["±1 count ambiguity of the least significant digit due to the lack of synchronization between input signal and time-base gate pulse","±5% of full-scale reading","Zero if crystal temperature compensation is utilized","The time-base oscillator drift per hour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because the arrival of input signal pulses and the opening/closing of the main time-base gate are completely asynchronous, a pulse may just be clipped or missed at the edges, resulting in an unavoidable ±1 count ambiguity."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The phase angle error of a Potential Transformer (PT) is primarily caused by:","options":["Primary and secondary winding leakage reactances and resistances interacting with excitation and burden currents","Inter-turn capacitive leakage at 50 Hz","Secondary terminal open-circuiting","Skin effect in the high-voltage bushing conductor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Internal impedance drops (I1*R1 + jI1*X1 and I2*R2 + jI2*X2) shift the secondary terminal voltage phasor out of exact 180° phase opposition with the primary supply voltage, introducing a phase angle error β."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"The electric power consumed by a resistor is measured using P = V * I. The voltmeter reads 100 V with a limiting error of ±1.5%, and the ammeter reads 5 A with a limiting error of ±2.5%. The maximum percentage limiting error in the calculated power is:","options":["±4.0%","±2.0%","±3.75%","±1.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because power is given by the product P = V * I, the worst-case relative limiting error in the computed result equals the algebraic sum of the individual relative limiting errors: (ΔP / P) = ±(ΔV / V + ΔI / I) = ±(1.5% + 2.5%) = ±4.0%."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An oscilloscope has an input resistance of 1 MΩ shunted by 30 pF. A 10X passive probe is used (attenuation factor 10). To achieve perfect frequency compensation, the probe must have a series resistance (Rp) and shunt capacitance (Cp) of:","options":["Rp = 9 MΩ, Cp = 3.33 pF","Rp = 10 MΩ, Cp = 30.0 pF","Rp = 9 MΩ, Cp = 270.0 pF","Rp = 1 MΩ, Cp = 3.00 pF"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a 10:1 voltage division: Rp / (Rp + R_scope) = 9/10 => Rp = 9 * R_scope = 9 MΩ. For frequency compensation across all frequencies: Rp * Cp = R_scope * C_scope => 9 MΩ * Cp = 1 MΩ * 30 pF => Cp = 30 pF / 9 ≈ 3.33 pF."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A full-wave rectifier PMMC voltmeter is calibrated to read the RMS value of pure sinusoidal voltages (form factor 1.11). If this meter is used to measure a symmetrical square wave of peak value 20 V, the reading displayed on the meter is:","options":["22.2 V","20.0 V","14.14 V","18.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The PMMC movement deflects proportional to average value: for a symmetrical square wave of peak 20 V, V_av = 20 V. The meter scale is calibrated for sine waves as V_indicated = 1.11 * V_av. Therefore, the indicated reading is 1.11 * 20 V = 22.2 V (while true RMS is 20 V)."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A Crompton DC potentiometer has a 100 cm slide wire. A standard cell of EMF 1.0186 V balances at a length of 50.93 cm. When measuring the voltage drop across an unknown resistor carrying steady current, balance is obtained at 75.00 cm. The unknown voltage is:","options":["1.50 V","1.00 V","0.75 V","2.04 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Potentiometer voltage gradient k = E_standard / L_standard = 1.0186 V / 50.93 cm = 0.02 V/cm (20 mV/cm). Unknown voltage Vx = k * L_unknown = 0.02 V/cm * 75.00 cm = 1.50 V."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A tuning coil tested with a Q-meter resonates at frequency f1 = 2 MHz with a tuning capacitance C1 = 460 pF. When frequency is doubled to f2 = 4 MHz, the circuit resonates with tuning capacitance C2 = 100 pF. The distributed self-capacitance (Cd) of the coil is:","options":["20 pF","15 pF","30 pF","10 pF"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because f2 = 2 * f1, frequency ratio is n = 2. Resonant frequency squared relates inversely to total capacitance: (f2 / f1)² = n² = 4 = (C1 + Cd) / (C2 + Cd). Thus: 4 * (100 + Cd) = 460 + Cd => 400 + 4 Cd = 460 + Cd => 3 Cd = 60 pF => Cd = 20 pF."},{"subject":"Electrical and Electronic Measurements","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 6-bit Flash Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) requires how many analog voltage comparators and reference ladder resistors?","options":["63 comparators and 64 resistors","64 comparators and 63 resistors","31 comparators and 32 resistors","127 comparators and 128 resistors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an n-bit Flash ADC, the reference voltage divider network requires 2ⁿ equal resistors to generate 2ⁿ reference voltage levels, and (2ⁿ - 1) comparators to evaluate the input against these levels. For n = 6: comparators = 2⁶ - 1 = 63, resistors = 2⁶ = 64."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Silicon Controlled Rectifier (SCR) is a solid-state semiconductor switch constructed with:","options":["Four layers (P-N-P-N) and three P-N junctions","Three layers (P-N-P) and two junctions","Four layers and two junctions","Three layers and three junctions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An SCR consists of four alternate semiconductor layers (P-N-P-N) forming three distinct P-N junctions (J1, J2, and J3) with three external terminals: Anode, Cathode, and Gate."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a thyristor (SCR), the relationship between Latching Current (IL) and Holding Current (IH) is:","options":["IL > IH (typically IL is 2 to 3 times IH)","IH > IL","IL = IH","IL is zero while IH is positive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Latching current is the minimum anode current required to transition the SCR from OFF to ON state during gate triggering, whereas holding current is the minimum current below which the SCR drops back to OFF state. In practice, IL ≈ 2 to 3 times IH."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to the two-transistor analogy of an SCR, regenerative turn-on (latch-up) occurs when:","options":["The sum of common-base current gains (α1 + α2) approaches 1.0 (unity)","α1 + α2 = 0","α1 * α2 = 1.0","α1 - α2 = 1.0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The anode current is given by Ia = (α2 * Ig + Ico1 + Ico2) / [1 - (α1 + α2)]. As (α1 + α2) approaches unity, the denominator approaches zero, triggering internal positive feedback and regenerative latch-up."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Line (natural) commutation occurs in thyristor power converters when:","options":["The alternating AC supply voltage naturally reverses polarity and drives anode current to zero","An external LC resonance network forces current to zero","Gate drive signal is inverted","A reverse gate pulse is applied"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Natural commutation occurs in AC-fed circuits where the natural zero-crossing and negative half-cycle of the AC line voltage reverse-biases the SCR, turning it OFF without auxiliary commutation components."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a step-down DC-DC buck chopper with input DC voltage Vs and duty cycle D (0 < D < 1), the average output voltage Vo is:","options":["Vo = D * Vs","Vo = Vs / D","Vo = Vs / (1 - D)","Vo = D * Vs / (1 - D)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an ideal buck chopper operating in continuous conduction mode, output voltage Vo = (Ton / T) * Vs = D * Vs. Because D < 1, Vo is always lower than input Vs."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A TRIAC is an integrated bidirectional AC switch that is functionally equivalent to:","options":["Two SCRs connected in inverse-parallel (anti-parallel) with a common gate","Two diodes connected in series","An SCR in series with a DIAC","Two power MOSFETs in cascode"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A TRIAC conducts in both directions across main terminals MT1 and MT2 when triggered by either positive or negative gate pulses, behaving like two anti-parallel SCRs sharing a single gate."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A freewheeling diode connected across an inductive (R-L) load in a phase-controlled rectifier serves to:","options":["Prevent load voltage from swinging negative, sustain continuous current, and improve power factor","Increase the peak reverse voltage on thyristors","Step up the average DC output voltage","Eliminate all AC ripple completely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When source voltage reverses, the inductor's decaying magnetic field forward-biases the freewheeling diode, commutating the thyristors, clamping output voltage to zero instead of negative, and preventing reactive energy feed-back."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A single-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier is supplied with 230 V RMS AC and supplies a highly inductive load. At firing angle α, average output voltage Vo is:","options":["Vo = (2 * Vm / π) * cos(α)","Vo = (Vm / π) * (1 + cos α)","Vo = (Vm / 2π) * cos(α)","Vo = (2 * Vm / π) * sin(α)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a single-phase full-wave bridge converter with continuous load current: Vo = (1/π) ∫[α to α+π] Vm sin(ωt) d(ωt) = (2 * Vm / π) * cos(α)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A series R-C snubber circuit connected directly across an SCR protects the device against:","options":["High rate of rise of forward voltage (dv/dt) false triggering","High rate of rise of anode current (di/dt)","Continuous DC overcurrent","Gate terminal overvoltages"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rapid rise of forward voltage charges junction capacitance Cj, producing displacement current i = Cj * (dv/dt) that can falsely turn on the SCR. The R-C snubber bypasses transient voltage spikes and limits dv/dt."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Protection of a thyristor against high initial rate of rise of anode current (di/dt) during turn-on is provided by:","options":["A small inductor connected in series with the anode","A capacitor connected in parallel with the SCR","A non-linear metal oxide varistor (MOV) across the gate","A fast-acting HRC fuse in parallel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"During turn-on, conduction initiates at a localized gate area and spreads across the silicon wafer. High di/dt causes localized overheating. A small series inductor (choke) limits di/dt = V / L to safe manufacturer limits."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The string efficiency of a high-voltage string consisting of 'n' series-connected thyristors is defined as:","options":["(Total string voltage rating) / [n * (Individual thyristor voltage rating)]","[n * (Individual rating)] / (Total string rating)","Derating Factor * 100%","(Output power) / (Input power)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"String efficiency measures the utilization of individual ratings across the series or parallel chain: η_string = V_total / (n * V_max). Due to manufacturing tolerances and leakage current differences, η_string is always less than 100%."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In Sinusoidal Pulse Width Modulation (SPWM) for voltage source inverters, if frequency modulation ratio mf = fc / fm is an odd integer, the output voltage waveform exhibits:","options":["Odd quarter-wave symmetry, which eliminates all even harmonics from the output spectrum","Zero odd harmonics and prominent even harmonics","A persistent DC offset component","Sub-harmonic oscillations below fundamental frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When carrier-to-reference ratio mf is an odd integer, the output waveform maintains half-wave and odd quarter-wave symmetry, completely canceling out all even harmonic components (2nd, 4th, 6th, etc.)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a 3-phase voltage source bridge inverter operating in 180° conduction mode, at any given operating instant:","options":["Exactly three semiconductor switches are ON simultaneously","Exactly two switches are ON simultaneously","Four switches are ON to maintain circulating current","Only one switch conducts per cycle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In 180° conduction, each of the 6 switches conducts for 180 electrical degrees. Switching transitions occur every 60°, meaning exactly three devices (either two upper and one lower, or one upper and two lower) are always ON."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a circulating-current-free (non-circulating) dual converter system, the relationship between firing angles of Converter 1 (α1) and Converter 2 (α2) is:","options":["α1 + α2 = 180°","α1 = α2","α1 - α2 = 90°","α1 + α2 = 90°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To make average output voltages match in magnitude and produce zero net circulating DC voltage when switching between motoring and regenerating quadrants, firing angles must satisfy α1 + α2 = 180° (giving Vo1 = -Vo2)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier is connected to a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply and delivers ripple-free current of 10 A to a load. If firing angle is α = 60°, the average output voltage (Vo) and active power (P) delivered are:","options":["Vo = 103.5 V, P = 1035 W","Vo = 207.0 V, P = 2070 W","Vo = 146.4 V, P = 1464 W","Vo = 82.8 V, P = 828 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak voltage Vm = 230 * √2 ≈ 325.27 V. Average output voltage Vo = (2 * Vm / π) * cos(60°) = (2 * 325.27 / 3.1416) * 0.5 ≈ 103.54 V. Active power delivered P = Vo * Io = 103.54 V * 10 A ≈ 1035.4 W."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A DC boost converter operates in continuous conduction mode with input voltage Vs = 24 V, switching frequency fs = 20 kHz, and duty cycle D = 0.6. The ideal average output voltage (Vo) is:","options":["60 V","40 V","14.4 V","72 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an ideal boost converter in continuous conduction: Vo = Vs / (1 - D) = 24 / (1 - 0.6) = 24 / 0.4 = 60 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A string of 4 identical thyristors connected in series blocks a total circuit voltage of 6 kV. If the maximum voltage across any individual thyristor is 1.8 kV, the string efficiency and derating factor are:","options":["String efficiency = 83.33%, Derating factor = 16.67%","String efficiency = 75.00%, Derating factor = 25.00%","String efficiency = 90.00%, Derating factor = 10.00%","String efficiency = 80.00%, Derating factor = 20.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"String efficiency = V_total / (n * V_max) = 6000 / (4 * 1800) = 6000 / 7200 = 83.33%. Derating factor = 1 - String efficiency = 1 - 0.8333 = 16.67%."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier is fed from a 400 V (line-to-line), 50 Hz supply. At firing angle α = 30°, the average output DC voltage (Vo) under continuous conduction is approximately:","options":["467.8 V","540.2 V","382.0 V","270.1 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak line voltage Vml = 400 * √2 ≈ 565.69 V. Average DC output Vo = (3 * Vml / π) * cos(30°) = (3 * 565.69 / 3.1416) * (√3 / 2) = 540.19 * 0.866 ≈ 467.8 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase AC voltage controller feeds a pure resistive load R = 10 Ω from a 230 V, 50 Hz source. If the firing angle is α = 90° (π/2 rad), the RMS load voltage (Vo_rms) is:","options":["162.63 V","115.00 V","230.00 V","81.32 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RMS output voltage for an AC voltage controller is Vo_rms = Vs * √[ (1/π) * (π - α + 0.5 * sin(2α)) ]. For α = π/2: sin(2α) = sin(π) = 0. Bracket evaluates to (1/π) * (π/2) = 0.5. Hence Vo_rms = 230 * √(0.5) = 230 / √2 ≈ 162.63 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a 3-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier fed via source inductance Ls per phase at angular frequency ω, the reduction in average DC output voltage (ΔVo) due to commutation overlap is:","options":["ΔVo = (3 * ω * Ls / π) * Id","ΔVo = (6 * ω * Ls / π) * Id","ΔVo = (ω * Ls / π) * Id","ΔVo = (3 * ω * Ls / 2π) * Id"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a 6-pulse 3-phase converter, there are 6 commutation periods per AC supply cycle. Each overlap loses an area of ω*Ls*Id volt-seconds. Total voltage reduction per second is ΔVo = 6 * (ω*Ls*Id / 2π) = (3 * ω * Ls / π) * Id."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistor (IGBT) combines the operational advantages of which two semiconductor devices?","options":["High input impedance of a MOSFET and low on-state conduction loss of a BJT","High switching speed of a BJT and high voltage rating of a TRIAC","Low gate drive requirements of an SCR and fast switching of a DIAC","Negative temperature coefficient of a MOSFET and high current capacity of a GTO"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An IGBT integrates a voltage-controlled insulated MOS gate structure (providing high input impedance and simple gate drive circuits) with a bipolar junction transistor output stage (providing low on-state saturation voltage Vce_sat and high current density)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Gate Turn-Off (GTO) thyristor can be turned OFF from the conducting state by:","options":["Applying a high-magnitude negative current pulse to its gate terminal","Applying a positive gate voltage pulse","Reversing the supply voltage polarity naturally","Reducing load resistance to zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unlike a conventional SCR that requires external forced or natural commutation to turn off, a GTO is turned off by extracting stored minority carriers through the gate by applying a short, high-peak negative gate current pulse."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Power MOSFETs are majority carrier devices characterized by having a:","options":["Positive temperature coefficient of resistance, which naturally prevents thermal runaway and allows easy parallel operation","Negative temperature coefficient of resistance, making them prone to current crowding","Zero on-state resistance","High minority-carrier storage time during turn-off"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because conduction in a power MOSFET depends strictly on majority carriers, on-state channel resistance (Rds_on) increases with temperature (+ve temp coefficient). This causes current to distribute equally when paralleling devices, preventing thermal runaway."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A single-phase semi-converter (half-controlled bridge) supplying a resistive-inductive (R-L) load uses:","options":["Two thyristors (SCRs) and two power diodes","Four thyristors (SCRs)","Four power diodes and one SCR","Two TRIACs and two diodes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A half-controlled bridge converter consists of two controlled thyristors in one leg (or upper half) and two uncontrolled diodes in the other leg, providing single-quadrant (rectifier) control with inherent freewheeling action."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Type-A (first-quadrant) DC chopper operates with:","options":["Both average output voltage and load current strictly positive (Vo > 0, Io > 0)","Positive voltage and negative current (Vo > 0, Io < 0)","Negative voltage and positive current (Vo < 0, Io > 0)","Both voltage and current negative (Vo < 0, Io < 0)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Type-A buck chopper operates exclusively in the first quadrant of the V-I plane: power always flows from source to load, and both average terminal voltage (Vo) and load current (Io) remain positive."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A cycloconverter is a single-stage power electronic frequency changer that converts:","options":["Fixed AC voltage and frequency directly to variable AC voltage and frequency without an intermediate DC link","Constant DC voltage directly to variable frequency AC","Variable AC voltage to pure ripple-free DC","High-frequency DC to low-frequency pulsating DC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A cycloconverter achieves direct AC-to-AC power conversion with variable output frequency (typically below one-third of the input utility frequency) without utilizing any intermediate DC link or energy-storage capacitor."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an uncontrolled single-phase full-wave bridge rectifier fed from 230 V RMS AC, the Peak Inverse Voltage (PIV) rating of each diode must be at least:","options":["325.3 V (Vm)","230.0 V (Vrms)","460.0 V (2 * Vrms)","650.6 V (2 * Vm)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a bridge rectifier configuration, when two diodes conduct during one half-cycle, the non-conducting diodes experience a maximum reverse voltage equal to the peak AC supply voltage: PIV = Vm = √2 * 230 V ≈ 325.27 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an inverting DC-DC Buck-Boost converter operating in continuous conduction with duty ratio D, the output voltage Vo is related to input Vs by:","options":["Vo = -Vs * [ D / (1 - D) ]","Vo = Vs * [ D / (1 - D) ]","Vo = -Vs * [ (1 - D) / D ]","Vo = -Vs * D"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By inductor volt-second balance under continuous conduction: Vs * Ton = -Vo * Toff => Vs * (D * T) = -Vo * ((1 - D) * T), which gives Vo = -Vs * [D / (1 - D)]. The negative sign indicates output voltage polarity is reversed relative to the input."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-phase full-bridge voltage source inverter (VSI) operating in square-wave mode with DC bus voltage Vdc, the RMS value of the fundamental output voltage component (V1_rms) is:","options":["(2√2 / π) * Vdc (≈ 0.90 Vdc)","(4 / π) * Vdc (≈ 1.27 Vdc)","(√2 / π) * Vdc (≈ 0.45 Vdc)","Vdc / 2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The square-wave output has Fourier series v(t) = Σ [4*Vdc / (n*π)] sin(nωt) for odd n. Peak fundamental voltage is V1_pk = 4*Vdc / π. The RMS value is V1_rms = V1_pk / √2 = (4 * Vdc) / (√2 * π) = (2√2 / π) * Vdc ≈ 0.9003 * Vdc."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The reverse recovery time (trr) of a power semiconductor diode is defined as the time interval between:","options":["Current zero crossing and the instant reverse recovery current decays to 25% of its peak reverse value (IRM)","Application of forward voltage and initial forward conduction","Current peak and final voltage breakdown","Voltage zero crossing and current maximum"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When an ON-state diode is commutated, minority carriers stored in the junction must be swept out. Time trr is measured from the initial zero crossing of forward current until reverse recovery current falls back to 25% (or 10%) of its peak reverse value IRM."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A three-phase fully controlled thyristor converter operates in the inversion (line-commutated inverter) mode when its firing angle α satisfies:","options":["90° < α < 180°","0° < α < 90°","α = 0°","180° < α < 270°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Average DC output voltage is Vo = (3*Vml/π) * cos α. For α > 90°, cos α is negative, making average output voltage negative (Vo < 0). Since current is unidirectional (Io > 0), power flow reverses from DC back to the AC grid (inverting mode)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Current Source Inverter (CSI) fed from a DC power supply, the input behaves as a stiff DC current source by utilizing:","options":["A large series filter inductor in the DC link","A large parallel filter capacitor across the input terminals","A high-speed chopper with zero inductance","An isolation step-up transformer at the input"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A CSI requires a constant, ripple-free input current source. This is realized by placing a large smoothing inductor in series with the DC link, which opposes changes in current (di/dt) and maintains constant current regardless of load impedance swings."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The Forward Bias Safe Operating Area (FBSOA) of a power bipolar junction transistor (BJT) is constrained by which four fundamental boundaries?","options":["Maximum continuous current limit, internal power dissipation limit, second breakdown limit, and maximum collector-emitter breakdown voltage","Gate threshold voltage, drain leakage, oxide breakdown, and avalanche limit","Holding current limit, latching current limit, PIV limit, and dv/dt limit","RMS current limit, surge current limit, I²t limit, and reverse blocking limit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The FBSOA curve on logarithmic Ic-Vce coordinates is bounded by: (1) Maximum continuous collector current Ic_max, (2) Maximum internal junction power dissipation (thermal limit), (3) Second breakdown limit (caused by localized current crowding and hotspots), and (4) Maximum voltage rating Vceo/Vcbo."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Selective Harmonic Elimination (SHE) Pulse Width Modulation in voltage source inverters eliminates specific low-order harmonics (e.g., 5th and 7th) by:","options":["Pre-computing specific switching angles per quarter-cycle using non-linear transcendental Fourier equations","Increasing the switching frequency to infinity","Connecting bulky passive low-pass LC filters across every inverter phase leg","Modulating DC bus voltage using a buck chopper"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SHE sets up mathematical Fourier expressions for harmonic amplitudes and solves for M switching angles per quarter-cycle, setting chosen low-order harmonic coefficients (such as n = 5, 7, 11) to zero while controlling fundamental output voltage."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase semi-converter is fed from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC source and supplies an R-L load with high inductance to maintain continuous current. If the firing angle is α = 60°, the average DC output voltage (Vo) is approximately:","options":["155.3 V","103.5 V","207.1 V","77.6 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak input voltage Vm = 230 * √2 ≈ 325.27 V. For a single-phase semi-converter: Vo = (Vm / π) * (1 + cos α) = (325.27 / 3.1416) * (1 + cos 60°) = 103.536 * (1 + 0.5) = 103.536 * 1.5 ≈ 155.30 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier supplies continuous DC current of Io = 15 A from a 230 V, 50 Hz line. The AC source has a line inductance Ls = 2 mH. The reduction in average DC output voltage (ΔVo) caused by commutation overlap is:","options":["3.0 V","6.0 V","1.5 V","12.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a single-phase full converter, commutation overlap occurs twice per AC cycle. Voltage reduction is ΔVo = (ω * Ls / π) * Io = 2 * f * Ls * Io. Substituting values: ΔVo = 2 * 50 * (2 × 10⁻³) * 15 = 100 * 0.002 * 15 = 3.0 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A step-down (buck) DC-DC converter has input voltage Vs = 48 V and delivers Vo = 12 V at a switching frequency fs = 50 kHz. The minimum value of filter inductance (L_crit) required to guarantee Continuous Conduction Mode (CCM) down to a minimum load current of Io_min = 1.0 A is:","options":["90 µH","180 µH","45 µH","120 µH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty ratio D = Vo / Vs = 12 / 48 = 0.25. At the boundary of continuous conduction, peak-to-peak inductor ripple is ΔIL = 2 * Io_min = 2 * 1.0 = 2.0 A. Using inductor volt-second balance: L_crit = [ (Vs - Vo) * D ] / (fs * ΔIL) = [ (48 - 12) * 0.25 ] / (50,000 * 2.0) = (36 * 0.25) / 100,000 = 9 / 100,000 = 90 × 10⁻⁶ H = 90 µH."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a single-phase half-bridge voltage source inverter fed from a three-wire split DC supply with total DC rail voltage Edc = 200 V (±100 V across center tap), the RMS value of the fundamental component of load voltage is approximately:","options":["90.0 V","127.3 V","180.1 V","63.7 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The output square wave alternates between +Edc/2 and -Edc/2 (±100 V). Fundamental peak voltage is V1_pk = 4 * (Edc / 2) / π = 2 * Edc / π = 2 * 200 / 3.1416 ≈ 127.32 V. Fundamental RMS voltage is V1_rms = V1_pk / √2 = 127.32 / 1.4142 ≈ 90.03 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A DC boost converter has input voltage Vs = 12 V and steps up to Vo = 36 V. The inductor is L = 150 µH and switching frequency is fs = 40 kHz. The peak-to-peak inductor ripple current (ΔIL) in continuous conduction mode is:","options":["1.33 A","2.00 A","0.67 A","2.67 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty ratio D = 1 - (Vs / Vo) = 1 - (12 / 36) = 1 - 1/3 = 2/3 ≈ 0.6667. During switch conduction time Ton = D / fs: ΔIL = (Vs * D) / (fs * L) = (12 * (2/3)) / (40,000 * 150 × 10⁻⁶) = 8 / 6 = 1.333 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An SCR operating in a 200 V DC circuit has a latching current IL = 50 mA. It supplies a load having resistance R = 20 Ω in series with inductance L = 0.2 H. The minimum gate pulse width required to ensure the thyristor latches ON successfully is:","options":["50 µs","25 µs","100 µs","10 µs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Load circuit time constant τ = L / R = 0.2 / 20 = 0.01 s = 10 ms. For small time t << τ, current rises almost linearly: i(t) ≈ (V / L) * t. To latch, current must reach IL = 50 mA: 50 × 10⁻³ = (200 / 0.2) * t = 1000 * t => t = (50 × 10⁻³) / 1000 = 50 × 10⁻⁶ s = 50 µs. Thus, minimum gate pulse width is 50 µs."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For successful commutation of a thyristor (SCR) in any converter circuit, the circuit turn-off time (tc) and device turn-off time (tq) must satisfy:","options":["tc > tq","tc < tq","tc = tq","tc = 0.5 * tq"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Circuit turn-off time (tc) is the duration during which the circuit maintains reverse bias across the SCR. For successful turn-off and prevention of commutation failure, tc must exceed the manufacturer-specified device turn-off time (tq), typically providing a safety margin."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to a Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT), a Power MOSFET is a:","options":["Voltage-controlled, unipolar, majority-carrier device with faster switching capability","Current-controlled, bipolar, minority-carrier device","Voltage-controlled device with high minority-carrier storage time","Current-controlled unipolar device"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power MOSFETs are voltage-controlled devices with an insulated gate. Because conduction relies entirely on majority carriers (electrons in n-channel), they exhibit zero minority-carrier storage delay, enabling very high switching frequencies (> 100 kHz)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In thyristor commutation classifications, Class F commutation refers to:","options":["Line (natural) commutation where the AC supply voltage drives the thyristor into reverse bias","Self-commutation by a series LC resonant load circuit","Resonant pulse commutation by an auxiliary LC network","Complementary impulse commutation using a parallel capacitor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Class F commutation represents line or natural commutation. It occurs in AC-fed phase-controlled rectifiers, AC voltage controllers, and cycloconverters where the natural reversal of the AC supply voltage commutates the conducting thyristor."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In Sinusoidal Pulse Width Modulation (SPWM), the amplitude modulation index (ma) is defined as:","options":["Peak amplitude of control signal (Vm_control) divided by peak amplitude of carrier triangular signal (Vm_carrier)","Peak amplitude of carrier divided by peak amplitude of control signal","Frequency of carrier divided by frequency of control reference","Ratio of fundamental output voltage to DC supply voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The amplitude modulation index is ma = V_control / V_carrier. When 0 ≤ ma ≤ 1 (linear modulation range), the fundamental output voltage varies linearly with ma. Values of ma > 1 cause overmodulation."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an R-C snubber connected across a thyristor, the primary function of the series resistor (R) is to:","options":["Limit the peak discharge current through the SCR when it turns ON","Block DC leakage currents","Increase the dv/dt rating of the circuit","Provide gate drive isolation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The snubber capacitor C bypasses transient overvoltages and limits dv/dt. When the SCR turns ON, this capacitor discharges through the SCR; the snubber resistor R limits this initial discharge current to prevent exceeding the SCR's peak di/dt rating."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary operational difference between an AC Voltage Controller and a Cycloconverter is that an AC Voltage Controller:","options":["Varies output RMS voltage at constant utility frequency, whereas a cycloconverter changes the output frequency","Converts AC directly into ripple-free DC","Operates exclusively with natural commutation while cycloconverters use forced commutation","Requires high-frequency PWM switching"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An AC voltage controller regulates the RMS AC voltage supplied to a load without changing line frequency (f_out = f_in). A cycloconverter changes both voltage and frequency directly in a single stage without a DC link."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Total Harmonic Distortion (THD) of an AC voltage waveform with total RMS value Vrms and fundamental RMS component V1_rms is defined as:","options":["√[ (Vrms / V1_rms)² - 1 ]","√[ 1 - (Vrms / V1_rms)² ]","(Vrms - V1_rms) / Vrms","V1_rms / Vrms"],"answer":0,"explanation":"THD is the ratio of the RMS value of all harmonic components combined to the RMS value of the fundamental component: THD = √(Vrms² - V1_rms²) / V1_rms = √[ (Vrms / V1_rms)² - 1 ]."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase half-controlled (semi-converter) bridge rectifier supplying a resistive load produces an average DC output voltage Vo given by:","options":["Vo = (3 * Vml / 2π) * (1 + cos α)","Vo = (3 * Vml / π) * cos α","Vo = (3 * Vml / π) * (1 + cos α)","Vo = (3√3 * Vml / 2π) * sin α"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 3-phase semi-converter contains three thyristors and three uncontrolled diodes. The output voltage consists of 3-pulse rectification behavior with inherent freewheeling, yielding Vo = (3 * Vml / 2π) * (1 + cos α), valid for 0 ≤ α ≤ 180°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-phase fully controlled converter with source inductance Ls, the overlap angle (μ) at firing angle α is calculated using:","options":["cos(α + μ) = cos α - (2 * ω * Ls * Id) / Vm","cos(α + μ) = cos α + (2 * ω * Ls * Id) / Vm","cos(α + μ) = cos α - (ω * Ls * Id) / (2 * Vm)","sin(α + μ) = sin α - (2 * ω * Ls * Id) / Vm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"During commutation overlap, source inductance maintains both incoming and outgoing SCRs in conduction. Integrating Ls * (di/dt) over the overlap interval μ yields: cos(α + μ) = cos α - (2 * ω * Ls * Id) / Vm, where Vm is peak source voltage."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The primary topological advantage of a Ćuk DC-DC converter over a conventional Buck-Boost converter is that:","options":["Both input and output currents are continuous and ripple-free due to series inductors at both ports","It requires no energy-transfer capacitor","It produces an output voltage of the same polarity as input voltage","It eliminates the need for active switches"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Ćuk converter transfers energy through an intermediate coupling capacitor while having inductors in series with both the input source and output load. This makes both input and output currents continuous, minimizing EMI filtering requirements."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In single-phase full-bridge inverters, unipolar PWM switching is generally preferred over bipolar PWM because:","options":["The dominant harmonic sidebands appear at twice the carrier frequency (2*fc), allowing smaller output filter components","It requires only two switches instead of four","It eliminates the need for dead-time delays","It allows operation with a higher DC bus voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In unipolar switching, the two inverter legs are switched independently. The output voltage switches between 0, +Vdc, and -Vdc, doubling the effective switching ripple frequency (2*fc) and moving the first cluster of harmonic sidebands to 2*mf, which simplifies LC filtering."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To prevent commutation failure in a line-commutated inverter operating into an AC grid, the maximum firing angle (α_max) must be limited such that:","options":["α_max ≤ 180° - (μ + γ_min), where μ is overlap angle and γ_min is extinction angle","α_max ≤ 90°","α_max ≤ 180° + μ","α_max = 180° exactly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In inverting mode, the outgoing SCR must be held in reverse bias for at least the turn-off extinction angle γ_min (corresponding to tq) after the overlap interval μ finishes, before the forward voltage zero-crossing at 180°. Hence, α + μ + γ_min ≤ 180°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a 3-phase voltage source inverter (VSI) operating in 120° conduction mode:","options":["Only two switches conduct at any given instant (one from the upper group and one from the lower group)","Three switches conduct at any given instant","Four switches conduct simultaneously to short-circuit the DC bus","The output phase voltage is a pure square wave with zero dwell intervals"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In 120° conduction mode, each switch conducts for 120 electrical degrees per cycle. Switching sequences occur every 60°, during which exactly two switches conduct simultaneously, leaving one phase leg floating (open-circuited)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In Space Vector PWM (SVPWM) modulation for 3-phase inverters, the maximum peak fundamental phase voltage in the linear modulation region is:","options":["Vdc / √3 (≈ 0.577 Vdc), which is 15.5% higher than standard sinusoidal PWM","Vdc / 2 (0.500 Vdc)","2 * Vdc / π","Vdc / (2√2)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard SPWM reaches linear modulation limit at V1_pk = Vdc / 2 = 0.50 Vdc. SVPWM utilizes the inscribed circle of the space vector hexagon, extending the linear modulation limit to V1_pk = Vdc / √3 ≈ 0.577 Vdc, achieving a 15.47% higher DC bus voltage utilization."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A step-down (buck) DC-DC converter operates with input voltage Vs = 24 V, output voltage Vo = 12 V, inductor L = 100 µH, filter capacitor C = 100 µF, and switching frequency fs = 25 kHz. The peak-to-peak output voltage ripple (ΔVo) is:","options":["120 mV","240 mV","60 mV","300 mV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty cycle D = Vo / Vs = 12 / 24 = 0.5. Peak-to-peak inductor current ripple: ΔIL = [(Vs - Vo) * D] / (fs * L) = [(24 - 12) * 0.5] / (25,000 * 100 × 10⁻⁶) = 6 / 2.5 = 2.4 A. Peak-to-peak output voltage ripple: ΔVo = ΔIL / (8 * C * fs) = 2.4 / (8 * 100 × 10⁻⁶ * 25,000) = 2.4 / 20 = 0.12 V = 120 mV."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier is fed from a 230 V (RMS), 50 Hz AC supply and supplies a constant load current Id = 10 A. The AC source has a line inductance Ls = 4 mH. At firing angle α = 45°, the commutation overlap angle (μ) is approximately:","options":["5.97°","10.25°","2.45°","15.00°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak supply voltage Vm = 230 * √2 ≈ 325.27 V. The overlap formula gives: cos(α + μ) = cos(α) - (2 * ω * Ls * Id) / Vm. Here: 2 * ω * Ls * Id = 2 * (2π * 50) * (4 × 10⁻³) * 10 = 2 * 314.16 * 0.04 ≈ 25.133 V. Voltage term = 25.133 / 325.27 ≈ 0.07727. Since cos(45°) = 0.7071: cos(45° + μ) = 0.7071 - 0.07727 = 0.62983 => 45° + μ = cos⁻¹(0.62983) ≈ 50.97°. Therefore: μ = 50.97° - 45° ≈ 5.97°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase full-bridge inverter operates in square-wave mode from a DC bus voltage Vdc = 100 V and supplies an R-L series load with R = 8 Ω and inductive reactance at fundamental frequency ωL = 6 Ω. The active power delivered to the load at fundamental frequency is:","options":["648.5 W","810.0 W","500.0 W","720.0 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RMS value of fundamental voltage: V1_rms = (2√2 / π) * Vdc = (2 * 1.4142 / 3.1416) * 100 ≈ 90.03 V. Fundamental load impedance: Z1 = √(R² + (ωL)²) = √(8² + 6²) = √(64 + 36) = 10 Ω. Fundamental RMS current: I1_rms = V1_rms / Z1 = 90.03 / 10 = 9.003 A. Power dissipated in load: P1 = (I1_rms)² * R = (9.003)² * 8 ≈ 81.05 * 8 ≈ 648.4 W."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A parallel-capacitor commutated DC chopper operates from a 200 V DC supply and delivers a continuous load current Io = 50 A. If the thyristor turn-off time is tq = 20 µs and a safety factor of 2 is required, the minimum required value of the commutating capacitor (C) is:","options":["10.0 µF","5.0 µF","20.0 µF","2.5 µF"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Required circuit turn-off time: tc = Safety factor * tq = 2 * 20 µs = 40 µs. For parallel capacitor commutation, the available turn-off time during linear discharging is tc = (C * Vs) / Io. Solving for capacitance: C = (Io * tc) / Vs = (50 * 40 × 10⁻⁶) / 200 = (2000 × 10⁻⁶) / 200 = 10 × 10⁻⁶ F = 10.0 µF."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier is connected to a 415 V (line-to-line RMS), 50 Hz AC grid. If the firing angle is α = 60° and load current is continuous and ripple-free, the average DC output voltage (Vo) is approximately:","options":["280.2 V","560.4 V","485.3 V","242.6 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak line-to-line voltage: Vml = 415 * √2 ≈ 586.898 V. For a 3-phase full converter under continuous conduction: Vo = (3 * Vml / π) * cos(α) = [(3 * 586.898) / 3.14159] * cos(60°) = 560.44 * 0.5 ≈ 280.22 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase square-wave voltage source inverter has a total RMS output voltage Vrms = Vdc. The Total Harmonic Distortion (THD_v) of this output voltage waveform is approximately:","options":["48.34%","31.08%","63.25%","25.50%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total RMS output is Vrms = Vdc. The fundamental component RMS value is V1_rms = (2√2 / π) * Vdc ≈ 0.9003 * Vdc. THD_v = √[ (Vrms / V1_rms)² - 1 ] = √[ (1 / 0.9003)² - 1 ] = √[ 1.2337 - 1 ] = √0.2337 ≈ 0.4834 = 48.34%."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A DIAC is a two-terminal bidirectional semiconductor trigger switch that turns ON when the applied voltage reaches its:","options":["Breakover voltage (VBO) in either polarity","Gate trigger threshold voltage","Holding current from negative bias","Reverse breakdown avalanche voltage only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A DIAC (Diode for Alternating Current) is a symmetric, bidirectional trigger device with no gate terminal. When the potential across its two terminals exceeds the breakover voltage (VBO) in either direction, it breaks into negative resistance conduction, commonly used to pulse the gate of a TRIAC."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In modern power converter designs, power BJTs are generally replaced by Power MOSFETs and IGBTs primarily because BJTs are:","options":["Current-controlled devices requiring continuous base drive current, leading to complex and lossy base drive circuits","Voltage-controlled devices with high gate drive impedance","Unipolar devices with negative temperature coefficients","Incapable of blocking forward voltages above 100 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power BJTs are current-controlled minority-carrier devices with low current gain (β = 5 to 20 at high currents), requiring substantial continuous base drive power. Conversely, MOSFETs and IGBTs have insulated gates that draw virtually zero steady-state gate current."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an ideal step-up (boost) DC-DC converter, the output voltage is always higher than the input voltage because:","options":["The inductor absorbs energy from the source during switch ON time and releases this energy in series with the input voltage to the load during OFF time","The capacitor doubles the DC voltage during every switching period","The duty cycle D is strictly greater than 1.0","The switching transistor amplifies DC input power"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the controllable switch is closed, current builds up magnetic energy in the inductor. When the switch opens, the inductor's induced EMF reverses polarity (L di/dt) and adds in series with the input DC voltage, transferring boosted energy through the diode to the output."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Displacement Power Factor (DPF) of a power electronic converter fed from a sinusoidal AC source is defined as:","options":["The cosine of the phase angle difference between the fundamental AC supply voltage and fundamental AC input current (cos φ1)","The ratio of fundamental RMS current to total RMS current","The ratio of active power to peak apparent power","The tangent of the harmonic phase angle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Displacement Power Factor is DPF = cos(φ1), where φ1 is the phase displacement angle between fundamental voltage and fundamental current. Total power factor is the product of Distortion Factor and DPF: PF = (I1_rms / I_rms) * cos(φ1)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Flyback DC-DC converter is derived from the basic topology of a:","options":["Buck-boost converter by replacing the single inductor with a coupled inductor (flyback transformer) to provide galvanic isolation","Step-down buck converter using an autotransformer","Forward converter without output diodes","Push-pull converter with complementary gates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Flyback converter is the isolated counterpart of the buck-boost converter. Energy is stored in the magnetizing inductance of a two-winding coupled inductor while the switch conducts, and is discharged to the load circuit via the secondary diode when the switch turns off."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The turn-off gain (β_off) of a Gate Turn-Off (GTO) thyristor is defined as the ratio of:","options":["Anode current to be interrupted to the peak negative gate current required to turn off the device (Ia / Ig_peak)","Peak negative gate current to continuous anode current","Forward gate trigger current to latching current","Anode voltage to negative gate voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Turn-off gain is β_off = Ia / Ig_neg(peak). For standard high-power GTOs, β_off is typically between 3 and 5, meaning interrupting an anode current of 1000 A requires a peak negative gate current pulse of 200 A to 330 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Type-C (two-quadrant) DC chopper operates in which quadrants of the V-I plane?","options":["Quadrants I and II, allowing bidirectional load current with unidirectional positive load voltage","Quadrants I and IV, allowing bidirectional load voltage with unidirectional current","Quadrants II and III exclusively","All four quadrants (I, II, III, and IV)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Type-C chopper consists of two switches and two antiparallel diodes operating as combined Step-Down (motoring in Quadrant I) and Step-Up (regenerative braking in Quadrant II) units. Average load voltage Vo is always positive, while load current Io can flow either into or out of the load."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a dual converter operating with circulating current, an interphase circulating current reactor (inductor) is connected between the two converters to:","options":["Limit the circulating ripple current driven by the instantaneous AC voltage difference between the two converters","Step up the DC output voltage across the load","Eliminate the need for matching firing angles (α1 + α2 = 180°)","Filter out load high-frequency harmonics exclusively"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even when firing angles satisfy α1 + α2 = 180° so that average DC output voltages are identical, the instantaneous output voltages differ, creating an alternating voltage difference. An interphase reactor introduces inductive reactance to limit this circulating ripple current."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-phase full-bridge inverter using single-pulse width modulation with pulse width 2d, the 3rd harmonic in the output voltage is completely eliminated when the pulse width (2d) is set to:","options":["120° (d = 60°)","60° (d = 30°)","90° (d = 45°)","150° (d = 75°)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The amplitude of the nth harmonic in single-pulse modulation is proportional to sin(n*d). For n = 3, sin(3d) = 0 requires 3d = 180° => d = 60°. Therefore, the total pulse width 2d must be 2 * 60° = 120°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase fully controlled 6-pulse bridge rectifier draws an AC line current containing harmonic orders (n) given by:","options":["n = 6k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (5th, 7th, 11th, 13th, etc.)","n = 2k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (all odd harmonics)","n = 6k only, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (triplen harmonics)","n = 3k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ..."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Due to three-phase and half-wave symmetry, triplen harmonics (3rd, 9th) cancel in 3-phase balanced systems, and even harmonics are absent. The line current contains characteristic harmonics of order n = 6k ± 1 (5th, 7th, 11th, 13th, 17th, 19th...), with amplitudes inversely proportional to harmonic order."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-switch forward DC-DC converter, a tertiary reset winding with a series diode is included on the high-frequency transformer primarily to:","options":["Demagnetize the core and return stored magnetizing energy to the input DC source during the switch OFF period, preventing transformer core saturation","Step up the output secondary voltage","Provide continuous secondary load current","Act as an R-C snubber clamp for gate protection"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a forward transformer operates with unipolar magnetic flux excursions, core magnetizing current builds up during the switch ON time. The tertiary winding connects back to the DC rail with reverse polarity, demagnetizing the core to zero during the OFF period."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In switched-mode power supplies (SMPS), an RCD clamp circuit placed across the primary of a Flyback transformer is used to:","options":["Dissipate the energy stored in transformer leakage inductance and clamp the peak drain-to-source turn-off voltage spike on the MOSFET","Eliminate core hysteresis losses in the transformer","Provide galvanic isolation between input and output grounds","Filter the DC input supply ripple"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transformer leakage inductance cannot transfer its trapped magnetic energy to the secondary. When the MOSFET turns off, this trapped energy causes a severe L_leak * (di/dt) voltage spike that can exceed the MOSFET's breakdown rating; the RCD clamp absorbs this energy safely."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an adjustable speed drive for a wound-rotor induction motor, a Static Scherbius system recovers slip power from the rotor by:","options":["Rectifying rotor AC power into DC and feeding it back into the AC supply grid using a line-commutated inverter","Dissipating rotor energy across an external liquid rheostat","Feeding rotor current into a cycloconverter supplying the stator","Injecting high-frequency DC directly into the stator windings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A static Scherbius drive connects the slip-rings of a wound-rotor induction motor to a diode rectifier. The rectified DC power is transferred through a DC link reactor to a line-commutated inverter operating in the inverting quadrant (α > 90°), returning slip energy to the grid for sub-synchronous speed control."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase to 3-phase matrix converter provides bidirectional power flow and variable frequency/voltage using:","options":["Nine bidirectional semiconductor switches arranged in a 3x3 matrix without any bulky DC-link capacitor or inductor","Twelve diodes and an intermediate LC resonant tank","Six thyristors with a massive DC smoothing choke","Three single-phase cycloconverters in series with an inverter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A matrix converter directly connects each of the three input phases to each of the three output phases using an array of 3x3 (nine) bidirectional four-quadrant switches. It allows direct AC-to-AC conversion with arbitrary amplitude and frequency while eliminating DC-link energy storage capacitors."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase semi-converter is fed from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply and delivers a continuous, ripple-free current of 10 A to a load. If the firing angle is α = 60°, the input Displacement Power Factor (DPF) is approximately:","options":["0.866","0.500","0.707","1.000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a single-phase semi-converter, the fundamental component of the input AC line current is phase-shifted with respect to the supply voltage by an angle φ1 = α / 2. For α = 60°: φ1 = 60° / 2 = 30°. Therefore, Displacement Power Factor DPF = cos(φ1) = cos(30°) = √3 / 2 ≈ 0.866."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase voltage source bridge inverter operates in 180° conduction mode from a DC supply of Vdc = 600 V. The RMS value of the line-to-line output voltage (V_LL_rms) is approximately:","options":["489.9 V","424.3 V","600.0 V","300.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For 180° conduction mode in a 3-phase VSI, line-to-line voltage is a six-step waveform with RMS value V_LL_rms = √(2/3) * Vdc = √(0.6667) * 600 ≈ 0.816497 * 600 ≈ 489.90 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A buck-boost DC-DC converter operates in continuous conduction mode with input voltage Vs = 24 V, output magnitude Vo = 36 V, switching frequency fs = 50 kHz, and inductor L = 200 µH. The peak-to-peak inductor ripple current (ΔIL) is:","options":["1.44 A","0.96 A","2.88 A","0.72 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty cycle is D = Vo / (Vs + Vo) = 36 / (24 + 36) = 36 / 60 = 0.6. Switch conduction time is Ton = D / fs = 0.6 / 50,000 = 12 × 10⁻⁶ s = 12 µs. Inductor ripple current is ΔIL = (Vs * Ton) / L = (24 * 12 × 10⁻⁶) / (200 × 10⁻⁶) = 288 / 200 = 1.44 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A thyristor has an I²t rating of 50 A²s. If a short-circuit fault occurs with a duration equal to one half-cycle of a 50 Hz power system (t = 10 ms), the maximum permissible surge fault current (RMS) the device can safely withstand is:","options":["70.71 A","50.00 A","100.00 A","35.36 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The I²t rating represents the thermal energy capability of the junction under sub-cycle non-repetitive surge conditions: I² * t = I²t_rating => I_rms² * (10 × 10⁻³) = 50 => I_rms² = 50 / 0.01 = 5000. Taking the square root gives I_rms = √5000 ≈ 70.71 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase AC voltage controller feeds a pure resistive load R = 5 Ω from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply. If the thyristors are fired at α = 90° (π/2 rad), the overall input power factor is:","options":["0.707 lagging","0.500 lagging","1.000 (Unity)","0.866 lagging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an AC voltage controller with resistive load at α = π/2: RMS load voltage is Vo_rms = Vs * √[(1/π)*(π - α + 0.5*sin(2α))] = 230 * √[(1/π)*(π/2)] = 230 / √2 ≈ 162.63 V. Load current is Io_rms = Vo_rms / R. Active power P = Vo_rms² / R. Apparent power S = Vs * Is_rms = Vs * (Vo_rms / R). Overall Power Factor PF = P / S = Vo_rms / Vs = (Vs / √2) / Vs = 1 / √2 ≈ 0.7071 lagging."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase semi-converter (half-controlled bridge) is supplied from a 415 V (line-to-line RMS), 50 Hz AC grid and operates under continuous conduction at firing angle α = 90°. The average DC output voltage (Vo) is approximately:","options":["280.2 V","0 V","560.4 V","140.1 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak line voltage is Vml = 415 * √2 ≈ 586.898 V. For a 3-phase semi-converter under continuous conduction: Vo = (3 * Vml / 2π) * (1 + cos α). For α = 90°, cos(90°) = 0, giving Vo = (3 * 586.898 / (2 * 3.14159)) * (1 + 0) = 1760.69 / 6.28318 ≈ 280.22 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A DIAC is a two-terminal bidirectional semiconductor trigger switch that turns ON when the applied voltage reaches its:","options":["Breakover voltage (VBO) in either polarity","Gate trigger threshold voltage","Holding current from negative bias","Reverse breakdown avalanche voltage only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A DIAC (Diode for Alternating Current) is a symmetric, bidirectional trigger device with no gate terminal. When the potential across its two terminals exceeds the breakover voltage (VBO) in either direction, it breaks into negative resistance conduction, commonly used to pulse the gate of a TRIAC."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In modern power converter designs, power BJTs are generally replaced by Power MOSFETs and IGBTs primarily because BJTs are:","options":["Current-controlled devices requiring continuous base drive current, leading to complex and lossy base drive circuits","Voltage-controlled devices with high gate drive impedance","Unipolar devices with negative temperature coefficients","Incapable of blocking forward voltages above 100 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power BJTs are current-controlled minority-carrier devices with low current gain (β = 5 to 20 at high currents), requiring substantial continuous base drive power. Conversely, MOSFETs and IGBTs have insulated gates that draw virtually zero steady-state gate current."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an ideal step-up (boost) DC-DC converter, the output voltage is always higher than the input voltage because:","options":["The inductor absorbs energy from the source during switch ON time and releases this energy in series with the input voltage to the load during OFF time","The capacitor doubles the DC voltage during every switching period","The duty cycle D is strictly greater than 1.0","The switching transistor amplifies DC input power"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the controllable switch is closed, current builds up magnetic energy in the inductor. When the switch opens, the inductor's induced EMF reverses polarity (L di/dt) and adds in series with the input DC voltage, transferring boosted energy through the diode to the output."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Displacement Power Factor (DPF) of a power electronic converter fed from a sinusoidal AC source is defined as:","options":["The cosine of the phase angle difference between the fundamental AC supply voltage and fundamental AC input current (cos φ1)","The ratio of fundamental RMS current to total RMS current","The ratio of active power to peak apparent power","The tangent of the harmonic phase angle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Displacement Power Factor is DPF = cos(φ1), where φ1 is the phase displacement angle between fundamental voltage and fundamental current. Total power factor is the product of Distortion Factor and DPF: PF = (I1_rms / I_rms) * cos(φ1)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Flyback DC-DC converter is derived from the basic topology of a:","options":["Buck-boost converter by replacing the single inductor with a coupled inductor (flyback transformer) to provide galvanic isolation","Step-down buck converter using an autotransformer","Forward converter without output diodes","Push-pull converter with complementary gates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Flyback converter is the isolated counterpart of the buck-boost converter. Energy is stored in the magnetizing inductance of a two-winding coupled inductor while the switch conducts, and is discharged to the load circuit via the secondary diode when the switch turns off."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The turn-off gain (β_off) of a Gate Turn-Off (GTO) thyristor is defined as the ratio of:","options":["Anode current to be interrupted to the peak negative gate current required to turn off the device (Ia / Ig_peak)","Peak negative gate current to continuous anode current","Forward gate trigger current to latching current","Anode voltage to negative gate voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Turn-off gain is β_off = Ia / Ig_neg(peak). For standard high-power GTOs, β_off is typically between 3 and 5, meaning interrupting an anode current of 1000 A requires a peak negative gate current pulse of 200 A to 330 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Type-C (two-quadrant) DC chopper operates in which quadrants of the V-I plane?","options":["Quadrants I and II, allowing bidirectional load current with unidirectional positive load voltage","Quadrants I and IV, allowing bidirectional load voltage with unidirectional current","Quadrants II and III exclusively","All four quadrants (I, II, III, and IV)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Type-C chopper consists of two switches and two antiparallel diodes operating as combined Step-Down (motoring in Quadrant I) and Step-Up (regenerative braking in Quadrant II) units. Average load voltage Vo is always positive, while load current Io can flow either into or out of the load."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a dual converter operating with circulating current, an interphase circulating current reactor (inductor) is connected between the two converters to:","options":["Limit the circulating ripple current driven by the instantaneous AC voltage difference between the two converters","Step up the DC output voltage across the load","Eliminate the need for matching firing angles (α1 + α2 = 180°)","Filter out load high-frequency harmonics exclusively"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even when firing angles satisfy α1 + α2 = 180° so that average DC output voltages are identical, the instantaneous output voltages differ, creating an alternating voltage difference. An interphase reactor introduces inductive reactance to limit this circulating ripple current."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-phase full-bridge inverter using single-pulse width modulation with pulse width 2d, the 3rd harmonic in the output voltage is completely eliminated when the pulse width (2d) is set to:","options":["120° (d = 60°)","60° (d = 30°)","90° (d = 45°)","150° (d = 75°)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The amplitude of the nth harmonic in single-pulse modulation is proportional to sin(n*d). For n = 3, sin(3d) = 0 requires 3d = 180° => d = 60°. Therefore, the total pulse width 2d must be 2 * 60° = 120°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase fully controlled 6-pulse bridge rectifier draws an AC line current containing harmonic orders (n) given by:","options":["n = 6k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (5th, 7th, 11th, 13th, etc.)","n = 2k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (all odd harmonics)","n = 6k only, where k = 1, 2, 3, ... (triplen harmonics)","n = 3k ± 1, where k = 1, 2, 3, ..."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Due to three-phase and half-wave symmetry, triplen harmonics (3rd, 9th) cancel in 3-phase balanced systems, and even harmonics are absent. The line current contains characteristic harmonics of order n = 6k ± 1 (5th, 7th, 11th, 13th, 17th, 19th...), with amplitudes inversely proportional to harmonic order."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a single-switch forward DC-DC converter, a tertiary reset winding with a series diode is included on the high-frequency transformer primarily to:","options":["Demagnetize the core and return stored magnetizing energy to the input DC source during the switch OFF period, preventing transformer core saturation","Step up the output secondary voltage","Provide continuous secondary load current","Act as an R-C snubber clamp for gate protection"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a forward transformer operates with unipolar magnetic flux excursions, core magnetizing current builds up during the switch ON time. The tertiary winding connects back to the DC rail with reverse polarity, demagnetizing the core to zero during the OFF period."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In switched-mode power supplies (SMPS), an RCD clamp circuit placed across the primary of a Flyback transformer is used to:","options":["Dissipate the energy stored in transformer leakage inductance and clamp the peak drain-to-source turn-off voltage spike on the MOSFET","Eliminate core hysteresis losses in the transformer","Provide galvanic isolation between input and output grounds","Filter the DC input supply ripple"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transformer leakage inductance cannot transfer its trapped magnetic energy to the secondary. When the MOSFET turns off, this trapped energy causes a severe L_leak * (di/dt) voltage spike that can exceed the MOSFET's breakdown rating; the RCD clamp absorbs this energy safely."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an adjustable speed drive for a wound-rotor induction motor, a Static Scherbius system recovers slip power from the rotor by:","options":["Rectifying rotor AC power into DC and feeding it back into the AC supply grid using a line-commutated inverter","Dissipating rotor energy across an external liquid rheostat","Feeding rotor current into a cycloconverter supplying the stator","Injecting high-frequency DC directly into the stator windings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A static Scherbius drive connects the slip-rings of a wound-rotor induction motor to a diode rectifier. The rectified DC power is transferred through a DC link reactor to a line-commutated inverter operating in the inverting quadrant (α > 90°), returning slip energy to the grid for sub-synchronous speed control."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase to 3-phase matrix converter provides bidirectional power flow and variable frequency/voltage using:","options":["Nine bidirectional semiconductor switches arranged in a 3x3 matrix without any bulky DC-link capacitor or inductor","Twelve diodes and an intermediate LC resonant tank","Six thyristors with a massive DC smoothing choke","Three single-phase cycloconverters in series with an inverter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A matrix converter directly connects each of the three input phases to each of the three output phases using an array of 3x3 (nine) bidirectional four-quadrant switches. It allows direct AC-to-AC conversion with arbitrary amplitude and frequency while eliminating DC-link energy storage capacitors."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase semi-converter is fed from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply and delivers a continuous, ripple-free current of 10 A to a load. If the firing angle is α = 60°, the input Displacement Power Factor (DPF) is approximately:","options":["0.866","0.500","0.707","1.000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a single-phase semi-converter, the fundamental component of the input AC line current is phase-shifted with respect to the supply voltage by an angle φ1 = α / 2. For α = 60°: φ1 = 60° / 2 = 30°. Therefore, Displacement Power Factor DPF = cos(φ1) = cos(30°) = √3 / 2 ≈ 0.866."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase voltage source bridge inverter operates in 180° conduction mode from a DC supply of Vdc = 600 V. The RMS value of the line-to-line output voltage (V_LL_rms) is approximately:","options":["489.9 V","424.3 V","600.0 V","300.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For 180° conduction mode in a 3-phase VSI, line-to-line voltage is a six-step waveform with RMS value V_LL_rms = √(2/3) * Vdc = √(0.6667) * 600 ≈ 0.816497 * 600 ≈ 489.90 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A buck-boost DC-DC converter operates in continuous conduction mode with input voltage Vs = 24 V, output magnitude Vo = 36 V, switching frequency fs = 50 kHz, and inductor L = 200 µH. The peak-to-peak inductor ripple current (ΔIL) is:","options":["1.44 A","0.96 A","2.88 A","0.72 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty cycle is D = Vo / (Vs + Vo) = 36 / (24 + 36) = 36 / 60 = 0.6. Switch conduction time is Ton = D / fs = 0.6 / 50,000 = 12 × 10⁻⁶ s = 12 µs. Inductor ripple current is ΔIL = (Vs * Ton) / L = (24 * 12 × 10⁻⁶) / (200 × 10⁻⁶) = 288 / 200 = 1.44 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A thyristor has an I²t rating of 50 A²s. If a short-circuit fault occurs with a duration equal to one half-cycle of a 50 Hz power system (t = 10 ms), the maximum permissible surge fault current (RMS) the device can safely withstand is:","options":["70.71 A","50.00 A","100.00 A","35.36 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The I²t rating represents the thermal energy capability of the junction under sub-cycle non-repetitive surge conditions: I² * t = I²t_rating => I_rms² * (10 × 10⁻³) = 50 => I_rms² = 50 / 0.01 = 5000. Taking the square root gives I_rms = √5000 ≈ 70.71 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase AC voltage controller feeds a pure resistive load R = 5 Ω from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply. If the thyristors are fired at α = 90° (π/2 rad), the overall input power factor is:","options":["0.707 lagging","0.500 lagging","1.000 (Unity)","0.866 lagging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an AC voltage controller with resistive load at α = π/2: RMS load voltage is Vo_rms = Vs * √[(1/π)*(π - α + 0.5*sin(2α))] = 230 * √[(1/π)*(π/2)] = 230 / √2 ≈ 162.63 V. Load current is Io_rms = Vo_rms / R. Active power P = Vo_rms² / R. Apparent power S = Vs * Is_rms = Vs * (Vo_rms / R). Overall Power Factor PF = P / S = Vo_rms / Vs = (Vs / √2) / Vs = 1 / √2 ≈ 0.7071 lagging."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase semi-converter (half-controlled bridge) is supplied from a 415 V (line-to-line RMS), 50 Hz AC grid and operates under continuous conduction at firing angle α = 90°. The average DC output voltage (Vo) is approximately:","options":["280.2 V","0 V","560.4 V","140.1 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peak line voltage is Vml = 415 * √2 ≈ 586.898 V. For a 3-phase semi-converter under continuous conduction: Vo = (3 * Vml / 2π) * (1 + cos α). For α = 90°, cos(90°) = 0, giving Vo = (3 * 586.898 / (2 * 3.14159)) * (1 + 0) = 1760.69 / 6.28318 ≈ 280.22 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Regarding the gate triggering sensitivity of a TRIAC across its four operating quadrants, the device is most sensitive in:","options":["Quadrant I+ (MT2 positive, Gate positive) and Quadrant III- (MT2 negative, Gate negative)","Quadrant I- (MT2 positive, Gate negative)","Quadrant III+ (MT2 negative, Gate positive)","All four quadrants with identical sensitivity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A TRIAC triggers most easily with the lowest required gate current in Quadrant I+ and Quadrant III- where gate polarity matches MT2 polarity. Quadrant III+ is the least sensitive mode and is generally avoided in practical gate-firing circuits."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In high-power, medium-frequency applications (e.g., motor drives above 1 kV), IGBTs are generally preferred over Power MOSFETs because:","options":["IGBTs exhibit conductivity modulation, maintaining low on-state conduction loss (Vce_sat) even at high breakdown voltage ratings","MOSFETs have higher gate input capacitance","IGBTs are pure majority-carrier devices","MOSFETs cannot be operated in parallel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In high-voltage power MOSFETs, the drift region must be wide and lightly doped, causing on-resistance (Rds_on) to increase as V_breakdown^(2.5). In IGBTs, minority-carrier injection heavily conductivity-modulates the drift layer, keeping on-state voltage drop low (~2 V) even at multi-kilovolt ratings."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In DC-to-DC switch-mode converters, Time Ratio Control (TRC) using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) regulates output voltage by:","options":["Varying the switch ON-time (Ton) while keeping the overall switching frequency (fs) and time period (T) constant","Varying switching frequency while keeping ON-time constant","Varying both switching frequency and input voltage simultaneously","Adjusting load resistance mechanically"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In constant-frequency PWM (TRC), the total switching period T = 1/fs is held fixed while the conduction interval Ton is modulated, varying the duty ratio D = Ton / T from 0 to 1 with predictable output filter ripple frequencies."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The primary operational difference between a Forward converter and a Flyback converter is that a Forward converter:","options":["Transfers power directly to the load during the switch ON-time via transformer action","Stores energy exclusively in its transformer core and releases it during the OFF-time","Requires no demagnetizing reset winding","Operates exclusively in discontinuous conduction mode"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forward converter uses true transformer action to transfer energy from the input source to the secondary LC filter simultaneously while the primary switch conducts. A flyback converter uses a coupled inductor to store magnetic energy during the ON-time and transfers it to the output only during the OFF-time."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Integral cycle control (burst firing) of an AC voltage controller is most effectively utilized for:","options":["Loads possessing large thermal or mechanical time constants, such as industrial electric heating and temperature control","Precision high-speed servomotor position control","Audio frequency amplifier modulation","Low-inertia DC traction drives"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Integral cycle control turns the thyristors fully ON for n integral AC cycles and fully OFF for m cycles. This introduces sub-harmonic low-frequency energy bursts that are easily smoothed by loads with high thermal or mechanical inertia (like heating elements) while minimizing radio frequency EMI."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An Integrated Gate-Commutated Thyristor (IGCT) improves upon the conventional GTO thyristor primarily by:","options":["Integrating an ultra-low inductance coaxial gate drive that extracts full anode current during turn-off, achieving unity-gain turn-off","Eliminating gate drive circuitry entirely","Converting the thyristor into a pure majority-carrier FET","Replacing the silicon wafer with gallium arsenide"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An IGCT integrates a specialized multi-layer thyristor wafer directly with a multi-layer PCB gate drive. During turn-off, it rapidly extracts 100% of the cathode current through the gate in under 1 µs before anode voltage rises (hard-driven turn-off with unity gain), eliminating the need for bulky turn-off snubber circuits."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a single-phase quasi-square wave inverter, the 3rd harmonic component can be completely eliminated from the phase voltage by setting the pulse width to:","options":["120°","60°","90°","180°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The output voltage of a quasi-square wave inverter with pulse width 2d has harmonic amplitudes proportional to sin(n*d). For n = 3, sin(3d) = 0 requires 3d = 180° => d = 60°. Therefore, the total pulse conduction width is 2d = 120°."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a 3-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier feeding a pure resistive (R) load, the load current becomes discontinuous when the firing angle (α) exceeds:","options":["60° (π/3 rad)","30° (π/6 rad)","90° (π/2 rad)","120° (2π/3 rad)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a 3-phase full converter with a pure resistive load, output voltage pulses touch zero whenever (π/3 + α) reaches 90° (at α = 60°). For α > 60°, the instantaneous output voltage falls to zero before the next SCR pair is fired, causing discontinuous load current."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In resonant power converters, Zero-Voltage Switching (ZVS) topologies are preferred over Zero-Current Switching (ZCS) for Power MOSFETs because ZVS:","options":["Eliminates capacitive turn-on switching losses caused by the discharge of internal output capacitance (0.5 * Coss * Vds²)","Eliminates minority carrier tail current turn-off losses","Reduces gate drive threshold voltage","Eliminates drain-to-source on-resistance entirely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a power MOSFET turns on under high drain voltage, the energy stored in its internal output capacitance Coss (E = 0.5*Coss*Vds²) is dissipated internally as heat. ZVS forces the voltage across the switch to zero before gating, eliminating this frequency-dependent turn-on loss."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an m-level Diode-Clamped (Neutral-Point Clamped) Multilevel Inverter, the total number of clamping diodes required per phase leg is:","options":["(m - 1) * (m - 2)","(m - 1)","2 * (m - 1)","m * (m - 1)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In an m-level diode-clamped inverter phase leg, there are (m - 1) capacitor voltage levels. The total number of clamping diodes per phase leg is (m - 1) * (m - 2). For example, a 3-level NPC inverter requires (3-1)*(3-2) = 2 clamping diodes per leg (6 total for 3 phases)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a line-commutated inverter connected to a 3-phase AC grid, commutation failure occurs when:","options":["The extinction margin angle (γ) is less than the thyristor turn-off angle (ω * tq)","The firing angle α is reduced below 90°","The DC link current drops to zero","The AC grid voltage increases by 10%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For successful commutation, the outgoing thyristor must remain reverse-biased after commutation overlap (μ) for an extinction angle γ ≥ ω*tq to recover its forward blocking capability. If γ < ω*tq, the thyristor conducts forward when the line voltage turns positive, short-circuiting the DC source."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Current Source Inverter (CSI) using thyristors or transistors, reverse-blocking capability is essential because:","options":["The switches must withstand reverse voltages during the commutation and off-state intervals","Input DC link voltage reverses during every half-cycle","Load current reverses direction every quarter-cycle","High-frequency gate currents must be bypassed to earth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unlike a VSI where antiparallel diodes clamp reverse voltages across the switches to near zero, a CSI maintains unidirectional DC link current while AC output voltage alternates. Hence, the switching devices (or series diodes) must withstand peak reverse line voltages."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"To achieve critically damped transient voltage behavior and avoid ringing during thyristor turn-off, the snubber resistance (Rs) for stray circuit inductance Ls and snubber capacitance Cs must satisfy:","options":["Rs = 2 * √(Ls / Cs)","Rs = 0.5 * √(Cs / Ls)","Rs = √(Ls * Cs)","Rs = 1 / (2 * √(Ls * Cs))"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The loop formed by source inductance Ls, snubber capacitor Cs, and snubber resistor Rs is a series R-L-C circuit. Critical damping (damping ratio ζ = 1) requires ζ = (Rs / 2) * √(Cs / Ls) = 1 => Rs = 2 * √(Ls / Cs)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 12-pulse converter configuration formed by connecting two identical 6-pulse bridge rectifiers in series (one fed from a Star-Star transformer and one from a Star-Delta transformer) eliminates:","options":["The 5th and 7th harmonic current components from the AC supply line","All triplen harmonics only","The 11th and 13th harmonic current components","All harmonic components above the fundamental"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 30° phase shift introduced by the Star-Delta connection shifts the 5th and 7th harmonic current components by 180° relative to the Star-Star bridge. When summed at the primary line, the 5th and 7th harmonics cancel out, leaving characteristic harmonics of order n = 12k ± 1 (11th, 13th, 23rd, 25th...)."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase fully controlled bridge rectifier feeds a highly inductive load with a ripple-free continuous DC current Id = 20 A from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC supply. If the firing angle is α = 45°, the total input Power Factor (PF) is approximately:","options":["0.637 lagging","0.707 lagging","0.900 lagging","0.500 lagging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For continuous ripple-free load current, total RMS AC line current is Is_rms = Id = 20 A. Fundamental RMS line current is I1_rms = (2√2 / π) * Id = 0.9003 * 20 ≈ 18.006 A. Current Distortion Factor DF = I1_rms / Is_rms = 2√2 / π ≈ 0.9003. Displacement Power Factor DPF = cos(α) = cos(45°) = 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071. Total Power Factor PF = DF * DPF = (2√2 / π) * (1 / √2) = 2 / π ≈ 0.6366 ≈ 0.637 lagging."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An isolated Flyback DC-DC converter operates in Continuous Conduction Mode (CCM) with input voltage Vs = 24 V, regulated output Vo = 12 V, and transformer turns ratio (Np / Ns) = 2. The operating duty cycle (D) of the primary MOSFET is:","options":["0.50","0.33","0.67","0.25"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a Flyback converter in continuous conduction: Vo = Vs * (Ns / Np) * [ D / (1 - D) ]. Substituting values: 12 = 24 * (1 / 2) * [ D / (1 - D) ] => 12 = 12 * [ D / (1 - D) ] => D / (1 - D) = 1 => D = 1 - D => 2D = 1 => D = 0.50."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A 3-phase voltage source bridge inverter operates in 180° conduction mode from a DC rail voltage Vdc = 300 V. The RMS value of the fundamental component of phase voltage (V_ph1_rms) is approximately:","options":["135.0 V","245.0 V","95.5 V","150.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In 180° conduction, the phase voltage Fourier series has fundamental peak V_ph1_pk = (2 / π) * Vdc. Therefore, fundamental RMS phase voltage is V_ph1_rms = V_ph1_pk / √2 = (√2 / π) * Vdc = (1.4142 / 3.14159) * 300 ≈ 0.45016 * 300 ≈ 135.05 V."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"A single-phase AC voltage controller using integral cycle control supplies a resistive heating load R = 10 Ω from a 230 V, 50 Hz AC utility. The controller conducts for n = 25 cycles and remains OFF for m = 75 cycles out of every 100 cycles. The average power delivered to the load is:","options":["1322.5 W","2645.0 W","5290.0 W","661.3 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duty cycle is k = n / (n + m) = 25 / (25 + 75) = 25 / 100 = 0.25. RMS load voltage is Vo_rms = Vs * √k = 230 * √0.25 = 230 * 0.5 = 115 V. Power delivered to the load is P = (Vo_rms)² / R = (115)² / 10 = 13,225 / 10 = 1322.5 W."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"In a Class B (current-commutated) DC chopper, the commutating circuit parameters are C = 20 µF and L = 5 µH, operating from a DC supply Vs = 200 V. The peak value of the resonant commutation current pulse (Ip) is:","options":["400 A","200 A","100 A","800 A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"During auxiliary thyristor turn-on, an oscillatory L-C loop discharges the commutating capacitor. The characteristic impedance of the resonant tank is Z0 = √(L / C) = √(5×10⁻⁶ / 20×10⁻⁶) = √0.25 = 0.5 Ω. The peak resonant current pulse is Ip = Vs / Z0 = Vs * √(C / L) = 200 / 0.5 = 400 A."},{"subject":"Power Electronics","difficulty":"Extra Hard","q":"An inverting Ćuk DC-DC converter operates in continuous conduction with input voltage Vs = 15 V and duty cycle D = 0.4. If the switching frequency is 50 kHz and the output supplies a 5 Ω load, the magnitude of the average output voltage (|Vo|) is:","options":["10.0 V","22.5 V","6.0 V","15.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The voltage conversion relationship for an ideal Ćuk converter in continuous conduction mode is Vo = -Vs * [ D / (1 - D) ]. With Vs = 15 V and D = 0.4: |Vo| = 15 * [ 0.4 / (1 - 0.4) ] = 15 * (0.4 / 0.6) = 15 * (2/3) = 10.0 V."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A copper coil has a resistance of 20 Ω at 0°C. If the temperature coefficient of resistance of copper at 0°C is 0.004/°C, what is its resistance at 50°C?","options":["22 Ω","24 Ω","25 Ω","28 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rt = R0(1 + α0 × ΔT) = 20(1 + 0.004 × 50) = 20 × 1.2 = 24 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three identical resistors of 12 Ω each are connected in Delta (Δ). What is the equivalent resistance per phase in an equivalent Star (Y) connection?","options":["4 Ω","6 Ω","12 Ω","36 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For identical resistors, RY = RΔ / 3 = 12 / 3 = 4 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following theorems is NOT applicable to circuits containing non-linear or unilateral elements like diodes?","options":["Superposition Theorem","Kirchhoff’s Current Law (KCL)","Kirchhoff’s Voltage Law (KVL)","Tellegen’s Theorem"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Superposition Theorem holds valid only for linear, bilateral networks."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three capacitors of 6 μF each are connected in series. What is the total equivalent capacitance?","options":["18 μF","6 μF","3 μF","2 μF"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For n identical capacitors in series, Ceq = C / n = 6 / 3 = 2 μF."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the electric field intensity inside a hollow charged spherical conductor?","options":["Maximum at the center","Zero","Directly proportional to the radius","Inversely proportional to the square of the radius"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All charge resides on the outer surface of a conductor, leaving zero enclosed charge and zero electric field inside."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Two coils having self-inductances of 4 H and 9 H have a coefficient of coupling k = 0.5. What is the mutual inductance (M) between them?","options":["1.5 H","3 H","6 H","18 H"],"answer":1,"explanation":"M = k√(L1 × L2) = 0.5 × √(4 × 9) = 0.5 × 6 = 3 H."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Steinmetz’s empirical formula, hysteresis loss in a magnetic core is proportional to which power of maximum flux density (Bm)?","options":["Bm","Bm^1.6","Bm^2","Bm^3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hysteresis loss Ph = η × Bm^1.6 × f × V, whereas eddy current loss is proportional to Bm^2."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which rule determines the direction of mechanical force acting on a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field?","options":["Fleming’s Right-Hand Rule","Fleming’s Left-Hand Rule","Maxwell’s Corkscrew Rule","Lenz’s Law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fleming’s Left-Hand Rule applies to motors (force/motion), while the Right-Hand Rule applies to generators (induced EMF)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An alternating voltage is given by v(t) = 200 sin(314t) V. What are its RMS value and frequency?","options":["200 V, 50 Hz","141.4 V, 50 Hz","141.4 V, 60 Hz","127.4 V, 50 Hz"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vrms = 200 / √2 ≈ 141.4 V, and f = ω / 2π = 314 / 2π = 50 Hz."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a series RLC circuit, R = 10 Ω, L = 0.1 H, and C = 10 μF. What is the Quality Factor (Q-factor) at resonance?","options":["1","10","50","100"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q = (1/R)√(L/C) = (1/10)√(0.1 / 10×10^-6) = 0.1 × 100 = 10."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A single-phase AC load draws 10 A at 230 V with a lagging power factor of 0.8. What is the reactive power (Q) consumed by the load?","options":["1.84 kVAR","1.38 kVAR","2.30 kVAR","1.15 kVAR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For cos(φ) = 0.8, sin(φ) = 0.6. Reactive power Q = V × I × sin(φ) = 230 × 10 × 0.6 = 1380 VAR = 1.38 kVAR."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a balanced 3-phase Star (Y) connected system, what is the phase angle difference between the line voltage and its corresponding phase voltage?","options":["0°","30°","60°","90°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a balanced Star connection, VL = √3 × Vph and line voltage leads the phase voltage by 30°."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the two-wattmeter method of 3-phase power measurement, if one wattmeter reads zero and the other reads the entire load power, what is the load power factor?","options":["1.0","0.866","0.5","0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One wattmeter reads zero when the phase angle φ = 60°, giving cos(60°) = 0.5."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An ideal independent current source has:","options":["Zero internal resistance","Infinite internal resistance","Low internal resistance","Equal load and source resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Infinite parallel internal resistance ensures constant current delivery regardless of terminal voltage changes."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal open-circuit voltage of a fully charged single Lead-Acid cell?","options":["1.2 V","1.5 V","2.2 V","3.6 V"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A single Lead-Acid cell provides approximately 2.0 V to 2.2 V when fully charged."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to the Maximum Power Transfer Theorem, maximum power is transferred from a DC source to a variable resistive load when the load resistance (RL) is:","options":["Zero","Half of the internal resistance","Equal to the internal resistance","Twice the internal resistance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a purely resistive DC network, maximum power transfer occurs when RL = Rth, resulting in 50% efficiency."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Norton’s equivalent circuit consists of:","options":["A voltage source in series with an impedance","A current source in parallel with an impedance","A current source in series with an impedance","A voltage source in parallel with an impedance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Norton’s theorem replaces a linear active network with an ideal current source (IN) in parallel with an equivalent impedance (ZN)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the form factor of a pure sinusoidal alternating current?","options":["1.0","1.11","1.414","0.707"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Form factor = RMS value / Average value = (0.707 Im) / (0.637 Im) = 1.11."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the peak factor (crest factor) of a symmetrical square wave?","options":["1.0","1.11","1.414","1.732"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a square wave, Peak value = RMS value = Average value, so Peak Factor = 1.0."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A coil of inductance 5 H carries a steady current of 4 A. How much energy is stored in its magnetic field?","options":["10 J","20 J","40 J","80 J"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Energy stored in an inductor E = 0.5 × L × I^2 = 0.5 × 5 × 4^2 = 40 J."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two incandescent bulbs rated 100 W, 230 V and 60 W, 230 V are connected in series across a 230 V supply. Which bulb will glow brighter?","options":["100 W bulb","60 W bulb","Both will glow with equal brightness","Neither will glow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resistance R = V^2 / P, so the 60 W bulb has higher resistance. In series, current is the same and P = I^2 R, making the 60 W bulb glow brighter."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The relative permeability (μr) of a diamagnetic material is:","options":["Slightly less than 1","Equal to 1","Slightly greater than 1","Very much greater than 1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Diamagnetic materials (such as bismuth and copper) weakly repel magnetic lines of force, having μr slightly less than 1."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a series RLC circuit, if R = 20 Ω and L = 0.05 H, what is the bandwidth (BW) in rad/s?","options":["100 rad/s","200 rad/s","400 rad/s","800 rad/s"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandwidth in rad/s is given by BW = R / L = 20 / 0.05 = 400 rad/s."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Three identical impedances connected in Star consume a total power of 3 kW from a balanced 3-phase supply. If the same impedances are reconnected in Delta across the same supply, what will be the total power consumed?","options":["1 kW","3 kW","6 kW","9 kW"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Power consumed in Delta connection is 3 times that in Star connection for the same line voltage: PΔ = 3 × PY = 3 × 3 = 9 kW."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Kirchhoff’s Current Law (KCL) is based on the law of conservation of:","options":["Energy","Charge","Momentum","Mass"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KCL states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a node equals zero, which follows conservation of electric charge."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the standard unit of dielectric strength of an insulating material?","options":["kV/mm","V/m^2","Ω·m","F/m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dielectric strength is the maximum electric field an insulator can withstand without breakdown, typically expressed in kV/mm or kV/cm."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A series RC circuit has R = 50 kΩ and C = 20 μF. What is the time constant (τ) of the circuit?","options":["0.1 s","0.5 s","1.0 s","10.0 s"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Time constant τ = R × C = (50 × 10^3) × (20 × 10^-6) = 1.0 second."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In nodal analysis, a supernode is formed when:","options":["An independent current source is connected between two non-reference nodes","A voltage source is connected between two non-reference nodes","Two resistors are connected in parallel","A voltage source is connected between a node and the reference ground"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A supernode encloses an independent or dependent voltage source (and any parallel elements) connected between two non-reference nodes."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the SI unit of magnetic reluctance?","options":["Weber/Ampere-turn","Ampere-turn/Weber","Henry/meter","Tesla/Ampere"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reluctance S = MMF / Flux = NI / Φ, so its unit is Ampere-turn per Weber (AT/Wb or H^-1)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At resonance, a parallel RLC (tank) circuit offers:","options":["Minimum impedance and unity power factor","Maximum impedance and unity power factor","Zero impedance and zero power factor","Maximum current and lagging power factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At parallel resonance, line current is minimum, dynamic impedance (L/CR) is maximum, and the circuit behaves purely resistively (unity power factor)."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Superposition theorem is valid only for circuits that are:","options":["Linear and bilateral","Non-linear and unilateral","Linear and unilateral","Non-linear and bilateral"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Superposition theorem requires both additivity and homogeneity, which hold only for linear, bilateral circuits."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The resistance of a wire increases from 40 Ω at 20°C to 48 Ω at 60°C. What is the temperature coefficient of resistance at 20°C?","options":["0.002 /°C","0.004 /°C","0.005 /°C","0.008 /°C"],"answer":2,"explanation":"α20 = (R60 - R20) / (R20 × ΔT) = (48 - 40) / (40 × 40) = 8 / 1600 = 0.005 /°C."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A 10 μF capacitor is charged to 100 V. If the voltage across it is doubled to 200 V, the energy stored in the capacitor becomes:","options":["0.05 J","0.10 J","0.20 J","0.40 J"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Energy stored E = 0.5 × C × V^2 = 0.5 × (10 × 10^-6) × 200^2 = 0.20 J (four times the initial energy)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Three resistors of 10 Ω, 20 Ω, and 30 Ω are connected in Delta (Δ). What is the resistance of the star arm connected between the 10 Ω and 20 Ω resistors?","options":["3.33 Ω","5.00 Ω","6.67 Ω","10.00 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Star resistance R1 = (R12 × R31) / (R12 + R23 + R31) = (10 × 20) / (10 + 20 + 30) = 200 / 60 = 3.33 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current:","options":["Lags behind the voltage by 90°","Leads the voltage by 90°","Is in phase with the voltage","Leads the voltage by 45°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a pure capacitor, current leads the applied alternating voltage by an electrical angle of 90° (or π/2 radians)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the average value of a half-wave rectified sinusoidal current with peak value Im?","options":["0.318 Im","0.500 Im","0.637 Im","0.707 Im"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a half-wave rectified sine wave, Iavg = Im / π ≈ 0.318 Im, whereas for a full-wave rectifier it is 2Im / π ≈ 0.637 Im."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the RMS value of a half-wave rectified sinusoidal voltage having a peak value of 100 V?","options":["31.8 V","50.0 V","63.7 V","70.7 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The RMS value of a half-wave rectified sinusoidal waveform is Vm / 2 = 100 / 2 = 50.0 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A magnetic ring has a mean circumference of 0.5 m and a cross-sectional area of 4 cm². If a coil of 200 turns wound on it carries 2 A, what is the magnetic field intensity (H)?","options":["200 AT/m","400 AT/m","800 AT/m","1600 AT/m"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Magnetic field intensity H = (N × I) / l = (200 × 2) / 0.5 = 800 AT/m."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Lenz’s law is a direct consequence of the law of conservation of:","options":["Charge","Momentum","Energy","Mass"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lenz’s law states that induced current opposes the cause producing it; mechanical work done against this opposition converts into electrical energy."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the two-wattmeter method of 3-phase balanced load power measurement, if the load power factor is 0.866 lagging, then:","options":["Both wattmeters read equal values","One wattmeter reads twice the other","One wattmeter reads zero","One wattmeter reads negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At cos(φ) = 0.866 (φ = 30°), W1 = VL IL cos(0°) and W2 = VL IL cos(60°) = 0.5 W1, so one wattmeter reads twice the other."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Reciprocity theorem is applicable to a network having:","options":["Single independent source and linear bilateral elements","Multiple independent sources and non-linear elements","Dependent sources and unilateral elements","Time-varying resistors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reciprocity theorem applies only to linear, passive, bilateral networks excited by a single independent source."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An AC circuit has an impedance of Z = (6 + j8) Ω connected across a 100 V RMS supply. What is the active power consumed by the circuit?","options":["600 W","800 W","1000 W","1400 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Magnitude |Z| = √(6^2 + 8^2) = 10 Ω, so current I = 100 / 10 = 10 A. Active power P = I^2 × R = 10^2 × 6 = 600 W."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a series RLC circuit at resonance, if the supply voltage is 50 V and the Q-factor is 15, what is the voltage across the capacitor?","options":["3.33 V","50 V","500 V","750 V"],"answer":3,"explanation":"At series resonance, voltage magnification occurs across L and C: VC = VL = Q × V = 15 × 50 = 750 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a secondary storage battery, the Watt-hour efficiency is always:","options":["Greater than Ampere-hour efficiency","Less than Ampere-hour efficiency","Equal to Ampere-hour efficiency","Greater than 100%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ηWh = ηAh × (Average discharge voltage / Average charge voltage). Since discharge voltage is lower than charging voltage, ηWh is always less than ηAh."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A carbon resistor has color bands in the sequence: Brown, Black, Red, Gold. What is its resistance value and tolerance?","options":["100 Ω ± 5%","1 kΩ ± 5%","10 kΩ ± 5%","1 kΩ ± 10%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brown (1), Black (0), Red multiplier (10^2), and Gold tolerance (±5%) give 10 × 100 Ω = 1 kΩ ± 5%."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is a secondary cell?","options":["Leclanché cell","Daniel cell","Lead-Acid cell","Voltaic cell"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Lead-Acid cell is a rechargeable secondary cell where chemical reactions are reversible, whereas Leclanché, Daniel, and Voltaic cells are primary cells."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a fully charged Lead-Acid battery, the active materials on the positive and negative plates respectively are:","options":["Lead peroxide (PbO2) and Spongy lead (Pb)","Spongy lead (Pb) and Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Lead sulfate (PbSO4) and Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Spongy lead (Pb) and Lead sulfate (PbSO4)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When fully charged, the positive plate (anode) is dark brown Lead peroxide (PbO2) and the negative plate (cathode) is grey Spongy lead (Pb)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When a Lead-Acid cell is fully discharged, both the positive and negative plates are converted into:","options":["Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Spongy lead (Pb)","Lead sulfate (PbSO4)","Lead oxide (PbO)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"During discharge, sulfuric acid reacts with both plates to form whitish Lead sulfate (PbSO4) and water, diluting the electrolyte."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate specific gravity of the sulfuric acid (H2SO4) electrolyte in a fully charged Lead-Acid battery?","options":["1.05 to 1.10","1.12 to 1.15","1.25 to 1.28","1.40 to 1.50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fully charged Lead-Acid battery has an electrolyte specific gravity of about 1.25 to 1.28, which drops to around 1.15 when discharged."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which instrument is used to measure the specific gravity of the electrolyte in a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Hygrometer","Hydrometer","Lactometer","Barometer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A hydrometer measures the relative density (specific gravity) of liquids and is used to check the state of charge of a Lead-Acid cell."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Trickle charging of a storage battery is primarily done to:","options":["Rapidly charge a dead battery","Prevent sulfation and compensate for internal self-discharge","Increase the Ampere-hour capacity","Desulfate an old battery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trickle charging supplies a very small continuous current (about 2–3% of rated current) to keep a battery fully charged and offset internal self-discharge."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The capacity of a commercial storage battery is generally rated in:","options":["Watts (W)","Watt-hours (Wh)","Ampere-hours (Ah)","Joules (J)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Battery capacity is expressed in Ampere-hours (Ah), representing the amount of current a battery can deliver over a specified period."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A battery is charged at 10 A for 8 hours at an average terminal voltage of 2.2 V, and is discharged at 8 A for 9 hours at an average voltage of 1.9 V. What is its Ampere-hour (Ah) efficiency?","options":["77.7%","86.4%","90.0%","95.0%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ηAh = (Output Ah / Input Ah) × 100 = [(8 A × 9 h) / (10 A × 8 h)] × 100 = (72 / 80) × 100 = 90.0%."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If a storage cell has an Ampere-hour efficiency of 90%, an average charging voltage of 2.2 V, and an average discharging voltage of 1.9 V, what is its Watt-hour (Wh) efficiency?","options":["77.7%","81.0%","86.4%","90.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ηWh = ηAh × (Vdischarge / Vcharge) = 90% × (1.9 / 2.2) ≈ 77.7%."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When n identical cells, each of EMF E and internal resistance r, are connected in mixed grouping to an external load resistance R, the current in the load is maximum when:","options":["External resistance is zero","Total internal resistance equals external load resistance","All cells are connected in series","All cells are connected in parallel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a mixed grouping of cells, maximum current flows through the external load R when total internal resistance (nr / m) equals external resistance R."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Six identical cells, each of EMF 2 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω, are connected in 2 parallel branches with 3 cells in series in each branch. If connected across an external load of 2.25 Ω, what is the load current?","options":["1.0 A","1.5 A","2.0 A","3.0 A"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total EMF = 3 × 2 V = 6 V. Equivalent internal resistance req = (3 × 0.5) / 2 = 0.75 Ω. Load current I = 6 / (2.25 + 0.75) = 6 / 3 = 2.0 A."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the electrolyte used in a Nickel-Iron (Edison) alkaline cell?","options":["Dilute sulfuric acid (H2SO4)","Potassium hydroxide (KOH) solution","Ammonium chloride (NH4Cl) paste","Copper sulfate (CuSO4) solution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nickel-Iron and Nickel-Cadmium alkaline cells use an aqueous solution of Potassium hydroxide (KOH) with a small amount of lithium hydroxide."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal terminal voltage of a single Nickel-Cadmium (Ni-Cd) cell?","options":["1.2 V","1.5 V","2.0 V","3.7 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single Nickel-Cadmium (or Ni-MH) cell has a nominal voltage of 1.2 V, whereas a Lead-Acid cell is 2.0 V and a Li-ion cell is ~3.6–3.7 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary cause of sulfation in a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Overcharging at very high current","Leaving the battery in a discharged state for a long time","Adding distilled water to the electrolyte","Operating the battery at low load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leaving a Lead-Acid battery discharged for extended periods causes soft PbSO4 to crystallize into hard, insoluble lead sulfate that resists recharging."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During the charging of a Lead-Acid battery, gassing (evolution of hydrogen and oxygen) occurs predominantly when:","options":["The battery is completely discharged","Charging begins at low voltage","The battery approaches full charge (above ~2.35 V per cell)","The electrolyte temperature drops below 0°C"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Near full charge (around 2.35–2.4 V per cell), most PbSO4 is already converted, so excess charging current electrolyzes water into hydrogen and oxygen gases."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which material is most commonly used to construct the laminated core of a power transformer to minimize hysteresis and eddy current losses?","options":["High carbon steel","Cold Rolled Grain Oriented (CRGO) silicon steel","Cast iron","Solid soft iron"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CRGO silicon steel has high permeability and low hysteresis loss, and thin laminations reduce eddy current losses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the condition for maximum efficiency of a transformer at a given load power factor?","options":["Core loss equals twice the copper loss","Copper loss equals core (iron) loss","Eddy current loss equals hysteresis loss","Load power factor is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformer efficiency is maximum when variable copper losses (I^2 R) equal constant iron/core losses (Pi)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Where is a Buchholz relay installed in an oil-immersed power transformer?","options":["Between the HV winding and LV winding","Inside the breather pipe","Between the main tank and the conservator","At the neutral grounding point"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Buchholz relay is a gas- and oil-actuated protection device placed in the pipe connecting the main tank to the conservator."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The short-circuit test on a transformer is conducted to determine:","options":["Core loss and magnetizing branch parameters","Full-load copper loss and equivalent series impedance","Stray load losses and efficiency directly","Insulation resistance of windings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The short-circuit test is performed at rated current (usually on the HV side with LV shorted) to find full-load copper loss and equivalent impedance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary function of a commutator in a DC generator?","options":["Reduce armature reaction","Convert induced AC voltage in the armature into unidirectional DC output","Provide mechanical support to the shaft","Limit the starting armature current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The commutator acts as a mechanical rectifier, converting the alternating EMF induced in the rotating armature coils into unidirectional DC voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A self-excited DC shunt generator fails to build up voltage if:","options":["Armature resistance increases above rated value","Field circuit resistance exceeds the critical field resistance","Load resistance is infinite","Residual magnetism is strong"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voltage build-up requires residual magnetism, correct field polarity, and a field circuit resistance lower than the critical field resistance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a starter necessary for starting a large DC motor?","options":["Field winding resistance is very low","Back EMF (Eb) is zero at the instant of starting","Armature inductance is very high","Brush contact drop is maximum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At standstill, speed N = 0 so back EMF Eb = 0; without external starting resistance, Ia = V / Ra would draw dangerously high current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A 4-pole, 50 Hz, 3-phase induction motor runs at a rotor speed of 1440 rpm. What is its percentage slip?","options":["2%","3%","4%","5%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Synchronous speed Ns = (120 × 50) / 4 = 1500 rpm. Slip s = [(1500 - 1440) / 1500] × 100 = 4%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If a 3-phase induction motor operates from a 50 Hz supply at a slip of 3%, what is the frequency of the rotor current?","options":["1.5 Hz","3.0 Hz","48.5 Hz","50.0 Hz"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rotor frequency fr = s × f = 0.03 × 50 = 1.5 Hz."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a 3-phase slip-ring induction motor, maximum starting torque is obtained when:","options":["R2 = X20","R2 = 0","R2 = 2 X20","X20 = 0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Starting torque is maximum when rotor resistance per phase (R2) equals standstill rotor reactance per phase (X20)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Crawling in a 3-phase squirrel-cage induction motor is predominantly caused by which space harmonic?","options":["3rd space harmonic","5th space harmonic","7th space harmonic","Low supply frequency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 7th space harmonic produces a forward-rotating field at Ns/7, causing the motor to run stably at nearly one-seventh of synchronous speed."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"By what electrical angle should the coils of an alternator be short-pitched to completely eliminate the 5th harmonic from the induced EMF?","options":["18°","30°","36°","60°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To eliminate the nth harmonic, chording angle α = 180° / n. For the 5th harmonic, α = 180° / 5 = 36° electrical."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The V-curves of a synchronous motor show the relationship between:","options":["Terminal voltage and field current","Armature current and field current at constant load","Speed and load torque","Power factor and supply frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"V-curves plot armature current (Ia) versus DC field excitation current (If) for different constant mechanical loads."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Damper windings in a synchronous motor are primarily provided to:","options":["Increase field excitation","Prevent hunting and provide starting torque","Reduce eddy current losses in the stator","Improve voltage regulation at no-load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Damper bars act like a squirrel-cage winding to develop starting torque and damp out rotor oscillations (hunting) during sudden load changes."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A single-phase induction motor is not inherently self-starting because:","options":["It has no rotor winding","Two opposite revolving fields produce equal and opposite torques at standstill","Its rotor resistance is infinite","Stator winding has zero inductance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"According to Double Revolving Field Theory, a pulsating single-phase field resolves into two opposite rotating fields that cancel each other's torque at standstill."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Faraday's first law of electrolysis, the mass of a substance liberated at an electrode is directly proportional to:","options":["Quantity of electricity passed","Square of the current","Resistance of the electrolyte","Temperature of the electrolyte"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Faraday's first law states m = Z × I × t = Z × Q, so deposited mass is directly proportional to electric charge Q."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the SI unit of Electrochemical Equivalent (ECE)?","options":["Coulomb/kg","kg/Coulomb","Ampere/kg","kg/Volt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since m = Z × Q, the Electrochemical Equivalent Z = m / Q has the SI unit of kg/Coulomb (or g/C)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"One Faraday of electricity is approximately equal to:","options":["6,250 Coulombs","36,000 Coulombs","96,500 Coulombs","1,00,000 Coulombs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One Faraday is the charge of one mole of electrons, which equals approximately 96,485 C (commonly taken as 96,500 C)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During electrolysis, positively charged ions (cations) migrate towards the:","options":["Anode","Cathode","Container wall","Separator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cations carry positive charge and therefore move toward the negatively charged electrode, the cathode."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Faraday's second law of electrolysis states that when the same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, the masses of substances liberated are proportional to their:","options":["Chemical equivalent weights","Atomic numbers","Valencies","Specific gravities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to Faraday's second law, m1 / m2 = E1 / E2, where E is the chemical equivalent weight."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A steady current of 5 A is passed through a copper sulfate solution for 20 minutes. If the ECE of copper is 0.33 mg/C, what mass of copper is deposited?","options":["0.66 g","1.32 g","1.98 g","3.30 g"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total charge Q = I × t = 5 A × (20 × 60 s) = 6000 C. Mass m = Z × Q = 0.33 × 10^-3 × 6000 = 1.98 g."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In electroplating, the article to be plated is always connected to the:","options":["Anode","Cathode","Positive terminal of the supply","Earth terminal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Metal ions in the electrolyte are positively charged and deposit onto the negatively charged cathode."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a small amount of acid or salt added to pure water during electrolysis?","options":["To increase its electrical conductivity","To decrease its boiling point","To prevent polarization","To reduce its specific gravity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pure distilled water is a poor conductor; adding acid or salt provides free ions that carry electric current."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An electrochemical cell is a device that converts:","options":["Electrical energy into mechanical energy","Thermal energy into electrical energy","Chemical energy into electrical energy","Mechanical energy into chemical energy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An electrochemical cell converts stored chemical energy directly into DC electrical energy via redox reactions."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Primary cells cannot be recharged once discharged because:","options":["Their internal resistance becomes zero","The chemical reactions occurring inside them are irreversible","They contain liquid electrolyte","Their EMF exceeds 2 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In primary cells, chemical changes are irreversible, so passing reverse current cannot restore the original active materials."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes 'local action' in a simple voltaic cell?","options":["Impurities like iron and carbon embedded in the commercial zinc electrode","Accumulation of hydrogen gas on the copper plate","Evaporation of sulfuric acid","High external load resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impurities in zinc form tiny local short-circuited cells with pure zinc, eating away the zinc even on open circuit."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Local action in a zinc electrode of a primary cell is prevented by:","options":["Painting the zinc plate","Coating the zinc with lead peroxide","Amalgamating the zinc surface with mercury","Using concentrated acid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rubbing mercury onto the zinc plate forms a zinc-mercury amalgam that covers impurities and prevents local action."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Polarization in a simple primary cell is caused by the accumulation of:","options":["Oxygen bubbles on the negative plate","Hydrogen gas bubbles on the positive electrode","Lead sulfate on both plates","Zinc sulfate at the bottom"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hydrogen bubbles insulate the positive electrode and set up a back EMF, reducing cell terminal voltage."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which material is used as a depolarizer in a Leclanché cell and standard dry cell?","options":["Copper sulfate","Potassium hydroxide","Lead peroxide","Manganese dioxide (MnO2)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Manganese dioxide (MnO2) oxidizes the liberated hydrogen into water, thereby preventing polarization."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the electrolyte used in a standard carbon-zinc dry cell?","options":["Moist paste of ammonium chloride (NH4Cl) and zinc chloride","Dilute sulfuric acid","Potassium hydroxide solution","Copper sulfate solution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A dry cell uses a thickened paste of ammonium chloride and zinc chloride so it can operate in any orientation."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal open-circuit EMF of a fresh carbon-zinc dry cell?","options":["1.1 V","1.5 V","2.0 V","3.0 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A standard carbon-zinc (Leclanché) dry cell provides a nominal open-circuit voltage of 1.5 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which electrolytes are used in a Daniell cell?","options":["Ammonium chloride and manganese dioxide","Potassium hydroxide and mercuric oxide","Dilute sulfuric acid and copper sulfate solution","Nitric acid and chromic acid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Daniell cell uses zinc in dilute sulfuric acid (or zinc sulfate) and copper in saturated copper sulfate solution."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate EMF of a Daniell cell?","options":["1.1 V","1.5 V","2.0 V","2.4 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Daniell cell produces a steady open-circuit EMF of approximately 1.08 V to 1.1 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which primary cell provides a nearly constant voltage of 1.35 V throughout its life and is widely used in watches and hearing aids?","options":["Voltaic cell","Mercury oxide button cell","Daniell cell","Bunsen cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A mercury cell uses mercuric oxide as depolarizer and KOH electrolyte, maintaining a flat 1.35 V discharge curve."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Storing dry cells at high ambient temperatures primarily reduces their shelf life due to:","options":["Freezing of the electrolyte","Increased internal local action and drying of the paste","Conversion of carbon rod into graphite","Excessive gassing of oxygen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High temperature accelerates parasitic chemical reactions (local action) and dries out the moist electrolyte paste."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a fully charged Lead-Acid cell, the active material on the positive plate is:","options":["Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Spongy lead (Pb)","Lead sulfate (PbSO4)","Pure antimony"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The positive plate of a charged Lead-Acid cell contains porous Lead peroxide (PbO2)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a fully charged Lead-Acid cell, the active material on the negative plate is:","options":["Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Spongy lead (Pb)","Lead sulfate (PbSO4)","Nickel hydroxide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The negative plate of a charged Lead-Acid cell consists of porous Spongy lead (Pb)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the color of the positive plate in a healthy, fully charged Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Slate grey","Milky white","Dark chocolate brown","Bright silver"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lead peroxide (PbO2) gives the fully charged positive plate a characteristic dark chocolate brown color."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the color of the negative plate in a fully charged Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Slate grey","Dark brown","Yellowish green","Brick red"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spongy lead (Pb) gives the fully charged negative plate a metallic slate grey appearance."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are Lead-Acid battery plate grids commonly made of a Lead-Antimony alloy instead of pure lead?","options":["To increase the cell EMF to 3 V","To provide mechanical strength and resist corrosion","To eliminate the need for separators","To reduce the weight by 80%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pure lead is too soft; adding 5–12% antimony increases mechanical rigidity, castability, and corrosion resistance."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Lead-Acid cell, why is the number of negative plates always one more than the number of positive plates?","options":["Negative plates have half the capacity of positive plates","To reduce the cost of lead peroxide","So both sides of every positive plate work evenly, preventing buckling","To double the terminal voltage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Placing a negative plate at both ends ensures uniform chemical reaction on both faces of each positive plate, preventing warping."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If a Lead-Acid cell contains 7 positive plates, what is the total number of plates in that cell?","options":["7","8","14","15"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Number of negative plates = 7 + 1 = 8. Total plates = 7 positive + 8 negative = 15 plates."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary function of separators in a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Increase the specific gravity of acid","Prevent metallic short-circuit between adjacent positive and negative plates while allowing ion flow","Absorb hydrogen gas during charging","Increase open-circuit EMF"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Separators are microporous insulators (PVC, rubber, or glass mat) that physically isolate opposite plates while permitting ion transport."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which electrolyte is used in a Lead-Acid storage battery?","options":["Dilute sulfuric acid (H2SO4)","Potassium hydroxide (KOH)","Ammonium chloride (NH4Cl)","Dilute hydrochloric acid (HCl)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lead-Acid batteries use a dilute aqueous solution of sulfuric acid (H2SO4)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Approximately what ratio of concentrated sulfuric acid to distilled water (by volume) is used to prepare Lead-Acid battery electrolyte?","options":["3 parts acid to 1 part water","1 part acid to 1 part water","1 part acid to 3 parts water","1 part acid to 10 parts water"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Roughly 1 part concentrated H2SO4 mixed with 3 parts distilled water yields a specific gravity of about 1.25–1.28."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When mixing sulfuric acid and distilled water to prepare battery electrolyte, one must always:","options":["Pour water rapidly into concentrated acid","Add acid slowly into water while stirring continuously","Mix both in a sealed copper vessel","Heat the water to 100°C before adding acid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding water to concentrated acid causes violent boiling and acid splashing due to intense exothermic heat."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to the Double Sulfate Theory, during discharge of a Lead-Acid cell, both plates are converted into:","options":["Lead sulfate (PbSO4)","Lead monoxide (PbO)","Spongy lead (Pb)","Lead chloride (PbCl2)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"During discharge, both PbO2 (positive) and Pb (negative) react with H2SO4 to form Lead sulfate (PbSO4) and water."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens to the specific gravity of the electrolyte during the discharge of a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["It increases","It decreases","It remains unchanged","It becomes greater than 2.0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During discharge, sulfate ions combine with the plates and water is produced, lowering the acid concentration and specific gravity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens to the specific gravity of the electrolyte when a Lead-Acid battery is being charged?","options":["It increases","It decreases","It drops to 1.00","It remains constant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charging converts PbSO4 back into PbO2 and Pb, releasing H2SO4 into the solution and raising its specific gravity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the normal specific gravity of the electrolyte in a fully charged Lead-Acid cell at 27°C?","options":["1.05 to 1.10","1.15 to 1.18","1.25 to 1.28","1.45 to 1.50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fully charged Lead-Acid cell typically has an electrolyte specific gravity between 1.25 and 1.28."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Lead-Acid cell should not be discharged below a terminal voltage of approximately:","options":["0.5 V","1.8 V","2.1 V","2.4 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Discharging below 1.8 V per cell (specific gravity ~1.15) promotes hard sulfation and reduces battery life."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal open-circuit voltage of a fully charged single Lead-Acid cell?","options":["1.2 V","1.5 V","2.1 V to 2.2 V","3.6 V"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A freshly charged single Lead-Acid cell exhibits an open-circuit voltage of about 2.1 V to 2.2 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How many Lead-Acid cells are connected in series inside a standard 12 V automotive battery?","options":["4 cells","6 cells","8 cells","12 cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each Lead-Acid cell has a nominal voltage of 2 V, so 6 cells in series give 6 × 2 V = 12 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to primary cells, the internal resistance of a Lead-Acid battery is:","options":["Very low (in milliohms)","Very high (several kilo-ohms)","Infinite","Equal to 100 ohms"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Large plate surface area and conductive acid give Lead-Acid batteries very low internal resistance, enabling high starting currents."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"As a Lead-Acid battery discharges, its internal resistance:","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains zero","Becomes negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Formation of poorly conductive PbSO4 on the plates and dilution of sulfuric acid cause internal resistance to rise."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which instrument is used to check the state of charge of a Lead-Acid battery by measuring electrolyte density?","options":["Tachometer","Pyrometer","Hydrometer","Manometer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A syringe-type hydrometer measures the specific gravity of the sulfuric acid electrolyte."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During overcharging or near the end of charging a Lead-Acid cell, which gases are evolved at the negative and positive plates respectively?","options":["Hydrogen at negative plate and Oxygen at positive plate","Oxygen at negative plate and Hydrogen at positive plate","Chlorine at negative plate and Nitrogen at positive plate","Sulfur dioxide at both plates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Electrolysis of water releases hydrogen gas at the negative cathode and oxygen gas at the positive anode."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At what cell voltage does brisk gassing typically begin during the charging of a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["1.80 V","2.00 V","2.35 V to 2.40 V","3.20 V"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once cell voltage reaches ~2.35–2.40 V, most PbSO4 is converted and excess charging current decomposes water into H2 and O2."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why do the filler caps (vent plugs) of a Lead-Acid battery have small vent holes?","options":["To allow sulfuric acid to evaporate rapidly","To allow charging gases to escape safely while preventing acid splash","To let air cool the plates","To insert the hydrometer without opening the cap"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vent holes relieve internal gas pressure during charging so the battery casing does not bulge or rupture."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When the electrolyte level in a Lead-Acid battery drops due to normal operation, what should be added to top it up?","options":["Only pure distilled water","Concentrated sulfuric acid","Tap water","Potassium hydroxide solution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only water is lost through evaporation and electrolysis; sulfuric acid does not evaporate, so only distilled water is added."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"How does an increase in electrolyte temperature affect the specific gravity reading of a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Specific gravity increases with temperature","Specific gravity decreases by about 0.0007 per °C rise","Temperature has zero effect on specific gravity","Specific gravity doubles every 10°C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"As liquid expands with heat, its density drops; hydrometer readings are corrected by adding 0.0007 for each °C above 27°C."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At freezing temperatures, the available Ampere-hour capacity of a Lead-Acid battery:","options":["Increases due to lower resistance","Decreases significantly due to higher electrolyte viscosity and slower chemical diffusion","Remains 100% unchanged","Increases by 50%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cold temperatures thicken the electrolyte and slow ion diffusion into the porous plates, reducing usable capacity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The capacity of stationary Lead-Acid batteries is commonly specified at which standard discharge rate?","options":["1-hour rate (C1)","2-hour rate (C2)","10-hour or 20-hour rate (C10 / C20)","100-hour rate (C100)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stationary batteries are usually rated at the C10 rate and automotive/deep-cycle batteries at the C20 rate."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A 120 Ah battery rated at the C10 discharge rate can deliver a steady current of:","options":["12 A for 10 hours","120 A for 10 hours","10 A for 120 hours","1.2 A for 10 hours"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At the C10 rate, discharge current I = Capacity / 10 hours = 120 Ah / 10 h = 12 A."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Peukert's law states that the effective Ampere-hour capacity of a Lead-Acid battery decreases as:","options":["Ambient temperature increases","The rate of discharge current increases","The battery is trickle charged","The number of parallel cells increases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Higher discharge currents cause rapid surface sulfation and voltage drop, reducing the total extractable Ah capacity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical Ampere-hour (Ah) efficiency of a healthy Lead-Acid battery?","options":["50% to 60%","70% to 75%","90% to 95%","100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because very little current is lost to side reactions until gassing occurs, Ah efficiency is high (90–95%)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical Watt-hour (Wh) efficiency of a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["50% to 55%","72% to 80%","92% to 96%","98% to 100%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wh efficiency accounts for both Ah losses and voltage drop between charge (~2.3 V) and discharge (~1.95 V), yielding 72–80%."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the Watt-hour efficiency of a storage battery always less than its Ampere-hour efficiency?","options":["Average discharging voltage is lower than average charging voltage","Charging time is shorter than discharging time","Specific gravity increases during discharge","Internal resistance is zero during charging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Since ηWh = ηAh × (Vdischarge / Vcharge) and Vdischarge < Vcharge, Watt-hour efficiency is always lower than Ah efficiency."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A storage battery is charged at 12 A for 10 hours and subsequently discharged at 9 A for 12 hours. What is its Ampere-hour efficiency?","options":["75%","85%","90%","95%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Output Ah = 9 A × 12 h = 108 Ah. Input Ah = 12 A × 10 h = 120 Ah. ηAh = (108 / 120) × 100 = 90%."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A Lead-Acid cell has an Ampere-hour efficiency of 92%. If the average charging voltage is 2.3 V and average discharging voltage is 2.0 V, what is its Watt-hour efficiency?","options":["75%","80%","85%","92%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ηWh = ηAh × (Vdis / Vchg) = 92% × (2.0 / 2.3) = 92% × 0.8696 = 80%."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the Constant Current charging method, how is the charging current maintained steady as the battery EMF rises?","options":["By gradually reducing the external series variable resistance or increasing supply voltage","By connecting a capacitor in parallel","By short-circuiting individual cells","By lowering the electrolyte temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"As battery back-EMF builds up during charging, series resistance is decreased to keep (Vsupply - Eb) / R constant."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the Constant Voltage charging method, the charging current is:","options":["Zero at the beginning and maximum at the end","Very high initially and tapers off automatically as the battery approaches full charge","Strictly constant from start to finish","Alternating in direction every hour"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Initially battery EMF is low so (V - Eb)/R is large; as Eb rises toward V, the charging current naturally tapers down."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which charging method significantly reduces total charging time and minimizes overcharging gassing?","options":["Constant voltage charging","Constant current charging","Trickle charging","Half-wave trickle discharge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Constant voltage charging allows high initial current when the battery can absorb it safely and tapers current near full charge."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Where is Trickle Charging most commonly employed?","options":["Electric vehicle fast-charging stations","Mobile phone chargers","Substation and emergency standby battery banks","Primary dry cell testing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Substation standby batteries are continuously trickle-charged to offset self-discharge and keep them 100% ready."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Approximately what magnitude of current is used during trickle charging of a battery?","options":["2% to 3% of the normal charging current","25% to 30% of the normal charging current","Equal to the 1-hour discharge current","Twice the normal charging current"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A very small continuous current (about 2–3% of normal rate) compensates for internal self-discharge without gassing."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Float Charging system, the charger is connected in parallel with both the battery and the DC load so that:","options":["The battery supplies the load while the charger stays off","The charger supplies the continuous load while keeping the battery fully charged","The battery is discharged rapidly every day","The load receives AC voltage directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Float chargers power steady DC loads directly while holding the battery at its float voltage (~2.25 V/cell) for emergencies."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of giving an 'equalizing charge' to a stationary battery bank?","options":["To discharge all cells to 0 V","To bring all series-connected cells to the same state of charge and uniform specific gravity","To evaporate excess water from the cells","To reverse the polarity of weak cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A controlled low-rate overcharge equalizes minor voltage and specific gravity differences among cells in a series string."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary cause of hard 'sulfation' in a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Adding too much distilled water","Leaving the battery in a discharged state for a long time or chronic undercharging","Using pure lead grids","Keeping the battery at 25°C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Soft PbSO4 converts into large, hard, non-conductive crystals if a discharged battery sits uncharged for days or weeks."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is a symptom of severe sulfation in a Lead-Acid cell?","options":["High internal resistance, rapid rise in terminal voltage on charge, and excessive heating","Specific gravity remaining permanently at 1.35","Open-circuit voltage rising to 3.0 V per cell","Complete disappearance of electrolyte"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hard sulfate layers block active material, raising internal resistance and causing rapid voltage rise and heating without proper charging."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is 'buckling' of plates in a Lead-Acid battery?","options":["Dissolving of negative plates in water","Physical warping or bending of the positive plates","Formation of ice inside the container","Crystallization of distilled water"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buckling is mechanical bending of plates caused by excessive charging/discharging rates or uneven expansion of active material."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes internal short-circuiting due to 'sedimentation' in old Lead-Acid batteries?","options":["Overfilling with distilled water","Shedding of active material from plates that accumulates at the bottom and bridges opposite plates","Using plastic containers","Low ambient humidity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Repeated charge-discharge cycles cause active material paste to shed; if sediment builds up above the bottom ribs, it shorts the plates."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are raised ribs (mud space) molded into the bottom of a Lead-Acid battery container?","options":["To collect shed active material safely below the plates without short-circuiting them","To increase electrolyte volume by 50%","To cool the acid faster","To hold the vent plugs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bottom ribs support the plates above the floor so shed sediment settles harmlessly in the spaces between ribs."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How can white sulfate corrosion on Lead-Acid battery terminals be cleaned and prevented?","options":["Clean with concentrated acid and coat with salt water","Clean with copper wire brush only and leave bare","Clean with baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) solution, rinse, and apply petroleum jelly (Vaseline)","Wash with petrol and paint with enamel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Baking soda neutralizes acid residue, and a thin layer of petroleum jelly seals terminals against acid fumes and moisture."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does a High-Rate Discharge Cell Tester evaluate a Lead-Acid cell?","options":["By measuring electrolyte color","By measuring cell terminal voltage while drawing heavy current through a low-resistance shunt","By measuring AC impedance at 50 Hz","By weighing the battery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A low-resistance shunt draws heavy current across the cell; a healthy cell holds ~1.8–1.9 V under load while a weak cell drops sharply."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of performing a Cadmium test on a Lead-Acid cell?","options":["To test the condition of positive and negative plates independently","To measure the purity of distilled water","To determine the antimony percentage in grids","To calibrate the hydrometer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Measuring voltage between a cadmium reference rod and each plate identifies whether the positive or negative plate group is faulty."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a charged Nickel-Iron (Edison) alkaline cell, the active material on the positive plate is:","options":["Lead peroxide (PbO2)","Nickel hydroxide (Ni(OH)4 / NiOOH)","Spongy iron (Fe)","Cadmium hydroxide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The positive tubes of a charged Edison cell contain nickel hydroxide mixed with flaked nickel for conductivity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a charged Nickel-Iron (Edison) cell, the active material on the negative plate is:","options":["Spongy iron (Fe)","Spongy lead (Pb)","Pure zinc (Zn)","Graphite (C)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The negative pockets of a charged Edison cell hold finely divided spongy iron."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which electrolyte is used in a Nickel-Iron (Edison) cell?","options":["Dilute sulfuric acid","Ammonium chloride paste","Potassium hydroxide (KOH) solution with a small amount of lithium hydroxide","Sodium chloride solution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alkaline cells use an aqueous solution of Potassium hydroxide (specific gravity ~1.20) containing about 2% lithium hydroxide."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a small amount of lithium hydroxide added to the KOH electrolyte of a Nickel-Iron cell?","options":["To turn the electrolyte acidic","To increase cell capacity and extend service life","To lower the cell voltage to 0.5 V","To prevent freezing at 50°C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lithium hydroxide stabilizes the nickel hydroxide active material, increasing capacity by ~10% and lengthening cycle life."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal average discharge voltage of a single Nickel-Iron cell?","options":["1.2 V","1.5 V","2.0 V","3.7 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Nickel-Iron cell delivers an average discharge voltage of about 1.2 V (open-circuit ~1.35–1.4 V)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During the charging and discharging of a Nickel-Iron cell, the specific gravity of the KOH electrolyte:","options":["Increases sharply during discharge","Drops to 1.00 during charge","Remains practically constant","Turns acidic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"KOH merely acts as an ion-transfer medium between plates without net consumption, so its specific gravity stays nearly unchanged."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to a Lead-Acid battery, a Nickel-Iron battery has:","options":["Higher Watt-hour efficiency (~90%) and lower cost","Longer mechanical life and ruggedness against overcharge/deep discharge, but lower efficiency (~60% Wh)","Higher cell voltage (2.2 V)","Much lower internal resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Steel-constructed Edison cells last 15–20+ years and tolerate abuse, but have higher internal resistance and ~60% Wh efficiency."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to Nickel-Iron cells, Nickel-Cadmium (Ni-Cd) cells have:","options":["Much lower internal resistance and better high-rate discharge performance","Higher nominal voltage (3.6 V)","Sulfuric acid electrolyte","Shorter shelf life"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replacing iron with cadmium lowers internal resistance significantly, enabling high discharge currents and better cold-weather performance."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which rechargeable battery chemistry is most well known for exhibiting the 'memory effect' (voltage depression) after repeated partial discharges?","options":["Lead-Acid","Lithium Iron Phosphate (LiFePO4)","Nickel-Cadmium (Ni-Cd)","Zinc-Air"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Repeatedly recharging Ni-Cd cells after shallow partial discharges causes cadmium crystal growth and temporary apparent capacity loss."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal voltage of a standard Lithium-Ion (Li-ion) rechargeable cell?","options":["1.2 V","3.6 V to 3.7 V","2.0 V","4.8 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conventional cobalt/NMC-based Lithium-ion cells have a nominal voltage of 3.6 V to 3.7 V (fully charged at 4.2 V)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nominal cell voltage of a Lithium Iron Phosphate (LiFePO4) battery cell?","options":["3.2 V","1.2 V","2.0 V","4.2 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"LiFePO4 cells operate at a nominal voltage of 3.2 V to 3.3 V and offer superior thermal safety and cycle life."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During the charging of a Lithium-Ion battery, lithium ions move through the electrolyte from the:","options":["Anode (graphite) to the cathode","Cathode (metal oxide) to the anode (graphite)","Separator to the casing","Copper foil to the aluminum foil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During charging, lithium ions de-intercalate from the positive cathode and intercalate into the layered graphite negative anode."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is an electronic Battery Management System (BMS) essential for multi-cell Lithium-Ion battery packs?","options":["To convert DC output into 3-phase AC","To add distilled water automatically","To protect against overcharge, over-discharge, over-temperature, and balance individual cell voltages","To increase the specific gravity of lithium"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lithium-ion cells are sensitive to over-voltage and deep discharge; a BMS monitors temperature, limits current, and balances cells."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does a Fuel Cell differ from a conventional secondary storage battery?","options":["A fuel cell continuously generates electricity as long as external fuel and oxidant are supplied","A fuel cell stores all reactants inside sealed plates","A fuel cell produces only AC power","A fuel cell requires 24 hours of charging before use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unlike batteries with finite stored reactants, a fuel cell converts externally fed fuel (like hydrogen) and oxygen continuously into electricity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main chemical byproduct produced by a Hydrogen-Oxygen fuel cell?","options":["Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Pure water (H2O) and heat","Sulfuric acid (H2SO4)","Methane gas (CH4)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hydrogen and oxygen combine electrochemically in the fuel cell to produce electricity, heat, and clean water."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Solar Photovoltaic (PV) cell converts sunlight directly into DC electricity based on the:","options":["Thermoelectric Seebeck effect","Piezoelectric effect","Photovoltaic effect","Faraday electromagnetic induction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Photons absorbed in a semiconductor p-n junction create electron-hole pairs separated by the built-in electric field (photovoltaic effect)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate open-circuit voltage of a single crystalline silicon solar cell under standard sunlight?","options":["0.5 V to 0.6 V","1.5 V to 1.8 V","2.2 V to 2.4 V","12.0 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single silicon PV cell generates an open-circuit voltage of roughly 0.55 V to 0.6 V, so 36 to 72 cells are wired in series per panel."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When n identical cells, each having EMF E and internal resistance r, are connected in series, the total EMF and total internal resistance are:","options":["E and r/n","nE and nr","nE and r/n","E and nr"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In series connection, both individual EMFs and internal resistances add algebraically: Etotal = nE and rtotal = nr."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Batteries are connected in series primarily when:","options":["A higher system voltage is required","Higher Ampere-hour capacity at the same voltage is needed","Internal resistance needs to be reduced to zero","AC output is desired"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Connecting batteries in series sums their individual voltages while keeping the Ampere-hour capacity equal to one string."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When m identical cells, each of EMF E and internal resistance r, are connected in parallel, the equivalent EMF and internal resistance are:","options":["mE and mr","mE and r/m","E and r/m","E and mr"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In parallel connection, terminal voltage remains E while internal resistance divides by m: req = r / m."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Batteries are connected in parallel primarily when:","options":["Higher output voltage is required","Higher current capacity (Ah rating) is needed at the same nominal voltage","Charging voltage needs to be doubled","Sulfation needs to be reversed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Parallel connection maintains the same nominal voltage while summing the Ampere-hour capacities of each branch."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Before connecting two batteries in parallel, it is essential that both batteries have the:","options":["Same nominal voltage and nearly identical state of charge","Same physical weight only","Opposite polarities connected together","Different electrolyte chemistries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Connecting batteries of unequal voltage in parallel causes large circulating currents, overheating, and damage."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Four cells, each of EMF 1.5 V and internal resistance of 0.2 Ω, are connected in series across an external load resistor of 3.2 Ω. What is the circuit current?","options":["1.00 A","1.50 A","1.87 A","3.00 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total EMF = 4 × 1.5 V = 6.0 V. Total resistance = 3.2 + (4 × 0.2) = 4.0 Ω. Current I = 6.0 / 4.0 = 1.50 A."},{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Ferranti effect in a long overhead transmission line occurs under which operating condition?","options":["Heavy inductive load","Light load or no-load condition","Short-circuit condition","Unity power factor full load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under light load or no-load conditions, line charging current flowing through the line inductance causes the receiving-end voltage to exceed the sending-end voltage."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the expression for the surge impedance (characteristic impedance) of a lossless overhead transmission line with inductance L and capacitance C per unit length?","options":["√(L × C)","√(L / C)","√(C / L)","L / C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a lossless line (R = 0, G = 0), surge impedance Zc = √(L / C), which is typically around 400 Ω for overhead lines."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Bundled conductors are primarily used in Extra High Voltage (EHV) transmission lines to:","options":["Increase corona loss and inductance","Reduce corona loss and line inductance","Increase surge impedance","Reduce line capacitance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bundling increases the effective Geometric Mean Radius (GMR), which reduces surface voltage gradient (minimizing corona) and lowers line inductance."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"String efficiency of a suspension insulator string can be improved by:","options":["Using longer cross-arms and guard rings","Increasing the number of insulator discs indefinitely","Decreasing pin-to-earth capacitance","Using solid copper pins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using a guard (grading) ring introduces capacitance between the line conductor and the metal fittings, equalizing voltage distribution across the discs."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the span length between two transmission towers of equal height is doubled while keeping conductor tension and weight per unit length constant, the sag will:","options":["Remain the same","Double","Become four times","Become half"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sag S = (w × l^2) / (8 × T). Since sag is proportional to the square of the span length (l^2), doubling the span increases sag by 4 times."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Peek’s empirical formula, corona power loss in a transmission line is directly proportional to:","options":["f^2","(f + 25)","1 / f","√f"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Corona loss is proportional to (f + 25), where f is the supply frequency in Hz."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The diversity factor of an electrical power system is always:","options":["Always less than 1","Always equal to 1","Always greater than 1","Zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Diversity factor = (Sum of individual maximum demands) / (Maximum demand on the power station), which is always greater than 1."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is most suitable to operate as a peak-load power plant?","options":["Nuclear power plant","Run-of-river hydro plant","Gas turbine or pumped-storage hydro plant","Geothermal power plant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gas turbines and pumped-storage hydro plants can start and pick up load rapidly, making them ideal for peak-load operation."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of fault occurs most frequently in overhead power transmission lines?","options":["Three-phase symmetrical fault (L-L-L)","Double line-to-ground fault (L-L-G)","Line-to-line fault (L-L)","Single line-to-ground fault (L-G)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Single line-to-ground (L-G) faults account for roughly 70% to 80% of all overhead line faults, whereas 3-phase faults are the most severe."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Zero-sequence currents flow in a power system during which of the following conditions?","options":["Three-phase symmetrical fault","Line-to-line fault","Single line-to-ground fault","Balanced three-phase load"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Zero-sequence currents require a return path through ground/neutral and therefore appear only in unsymmetrical ground faults like L-G and L-L-G."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Equal Area Criterion is used to analyze:","options":["Voltage regulation of short lines","Transient stability of a power system","Economic load dispatch","Corona inception voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Equal Area Criterion determines the transient stability limit of a synchronous machine connected to an infinite bus during sudden disturbances."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which statement is true regarding the skin effect in transmission conductors?","options":["It increases as supply frequency increases","It is maximum in DC transmission","It decreases as conductor diameter increases","It reduces the effective AC resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Skin effect causes AC current to crowd near the outer surface of a conductor, increasing effective resistance as frequency and conductor diameter increase."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 400 kV, 3-phase lossless overhead transmission line has a surge impedance of 400 Ω. What is its Surge Impedance Loading (SIL)?","options":["100 MW","200 MW","400 MW","800 MW"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SIL = (VL^2) / Zc = (400^2) / 400 = 400 MW."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is a major advantage of High Voltage Direct Current (HVDC) transmission over EHVAC transmission for long distances?","options":["Transformers can easily step up DC voltage","There is no skin effect or charging current","Circuit breakers are cheaper for DC than AC","It is more economical for short distances below 50 km"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HVDC eliminates reactive charging current, Ferranti effect, and skin effect, making it economical above the break-even distance (~500–800 km)."},
{"subject":"Power Systems","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A generator has a per-unit reactance of 0.10 pu on a 50 MVA, 11 kV base. What will be its per-unit reactance on a new base of 100 MVA, 11 kV?","options":["0.05 pu","0.10 pu","0.20 pu","0.40 pu"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Zpu_new = Zpu_old × (Vold / Vnew)^2 × (Snew / Sold) = 0.10 × (11/11)^2 × (100/50) = 0.20 pu."},{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At absolute zero temperature (0 K), an intrinsic (pure) semiconductor behaves as:","options":["An insulator","A conductor","A superconductor","A rectifier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At 0 K, all valence electrons are tightly bound in covalent bonds with zero free electrons in the conduction band, so it acts as a perfect insulator."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following elements is added as a pentavalent impurity to pure silicon to form an N-type semiconductor?","options":["Boron","Indium","Phosphorus","Gallium"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pentavalent impurities (Group V) like Phosphorus, Arsenic, and Antimony donate extra free electrons to create N-type semiconductors."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the approximate barrier potentials (cut-in voltages) for Silicon and Germanium PN-junction diodes at room temperature (300 K)?","options":["0.3 V and 0.7 V","0.7 V and 0.3 V","1.1 V and 0.7 V","0.5 V and 1.2 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At room temperature, the barrier potential is approximately 0.7 V for Silicon and 0.3 V for Germanium."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the ripple factor of a full-wave rectifier without a filter?","options":["0.482","0.812","1.11","1.21"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A full-wave rectifier has a ripple factor of 0.482 (and maximum efficiency of 81.2%), compared to 1.21 for a half-wave rectifier."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a center-tapped full-wave rectifier, if Vm is the peak voltage across half of the secondary winding, what is the Peak Inverse Voltage (PIV) across each non-conducting diode?","options":["Vm","2 Vm","Vm / 2","Vm / √2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a center-tapped full-wave rectifier, each diode must withstand a PIV of 2 Vm, whereas in a bridge rectifier PIV is Vm."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Zener diode is primarily operated in which region when used as a voltage regulator?","options":["Forward bias region","Reverse breakdown region","Cut-off region","Saturation region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Zener diode is heavily doped to have a sharp reverse breakdown voltage and maintains a nearly constant voltage across the load in the reverse breakdown region."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT) connected in common-base configuration, if the DC current gain α = 0.98, what is the common-emitter current gain β?","options":["25","49","50","98"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Current gain β = α / (1 - α) = 0.98 / (1 - 0.98) = 0.98 / 0.02 = 49."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which BJT amplifier configuration offers high input impedance, low output impedance, and unity voltage gain, making it ideal for impedance matching?","options":["Common Base (CB)","Common Emitter (CE)","Common Collector (CC)","Darlington Emitter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Common Collector (Emitter Follower) configuration provides high input impedance, low output impedance, and a voltage gain slightly less than 1."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Field-Effect Transistor (FET) is best described as a:","options":["Current-controlled bipolar device","Voltage-controlled unipolar device","Current-controlled unipolar device","Voltage-controlled bipolar device"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a FET, output drain current is controlled by the gate-to-source voltage, and conduction occurs via only one type of majority carrier (unipolar)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An ideal Operational Amplifier (Op-Amp) possesses which of the following input and output impedance characteristics?","options":["Zero input impedance and infinite output impedance","Infinite input impedance and zero output impedance","Low input impedance and high output impedance","Infinite input impedance and infinite output impedance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ideal Op-Amp has infinite open-loop voltage gain, infinite input impedance, zero output impedance, and infinite bandwidth."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an inverting Op-Amp circuit, the input resistor R1 = 10 kΩ and feedback resistor Rf = 50 kΩ. If an input voltage of +0.5 V is applied, what is the output voltage?","options":["-2.5 V","+2.5 V","-5.0 V","+0.5 V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an inverting amplifier, Vout = -(Rf / R1) × Vin = -(50 / 10) × 0.5 = -5 × 0.5 = -2.5 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to the Barkhausen criterion, what are the conditions required for sustained oscillations in a feedback oscillator?","options":["|Aβ| = 0 and phase shift = 90°","|Aβ| = 1 and total phase shift = 0° or 360°","|Aβ| > 1 and phase shift = 180°","|Aβ| < 1 and phase shift = 270°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sustained oscillations require a loop gain magnitude |Aβ| = 1 and a total phase shift around the feedback loop of 0° or 360°."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which pair of logic gates are known as 'Universal Gates' because any Boolean function can be implemented using only one type of them?","options":["AND and OR","XOR and XNOR","NAND and NOR","NOT and AND"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NAND and NOR gates are universal gates because AND, OR, and NOT operations can all be constructed solely from NAND gates or solely from NOR gates."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which sequential logic circuit eliminates the invalid/indeterminate state that occurs in a basic SR flip-flop when both inputs are HIGH (1)?","options":["D flip-flop","T flip-flop","JK flip-flop","Master-Slave SR flip-flop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a JK flip-flop, when J = 1 and K = 1, the output toggles to the complement of its previous state instead of entering an invalid state."},
{"subject":"Basic Electronics","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the binary equivalent of the decimal number 25?","options":["10110","11001","11011","10011"],"answer":1,"explanation":"25 in decimal = 16 + 8 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 2^4 + 2^3 + 2^0 = 11001 in binary."},{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the SI unit of electrical resistivity (specific resistance)?","options":["Ohm/meter","Ohm-meter","Mho-meter","Ohm/meter^2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resistivity ρ = (R × A) / l, so its SI unit is Ω·m."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A uniform wire of resistance R is stretched uniformly until its length becomes double its original length. Assuming constant volume, its new resistance will be:","options":["R / 2","2 R","4 R","8 R"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When volume remains constant, stretching a wire to n times its original length increases resistance by n^2 times: R' = 2^2 × R = 4R."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the SI unit of electrical conductance?","options":["Siemens","Henry","Farad","Weber"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Conductance G = 1 / R, and its SI unit is Siemens (S), also formerly called mho."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"One kilowatt-hour (1 kWh) of electrical energy is equal to how many Joules?","options":["3.6 × 10^3 J","3.6 × 10^5 J","3.6 × 10^6 J","3.6 × 10^7 J"],"answer":2,"explanation":"1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3,600,000 J = 3.6 × 10^6 J."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Alloys such as Manganin and Constantan are widely used for making standard resistors because they possess:","options":["High temperature coefficient of resistance","Nearly zero temperature coefficient of resistance","Very low resistivity","High thermal expansion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Manganin and Constantan have negligible temperature coefficients of resistance, ensuring stable resistance values despite temperature changes."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Pure semiconductors and electrolytes generally have a:","options":["Positive temperature coefficient of resistance","Negative temperature coefficient of resistance","Zero temperature coefficient of resistance","Infinite resistance at room temperature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In semiconductors and electrolytes, higher temperature increases charge carrier concentration, causing resistance to decrease (negative temperature coefficient)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Two resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω are connected in parallel. What is their equivalent resistance?","options":["2 Ω","4.5 Ω","9 Ω","18 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Req = (R1 × R2) / (R1 + R2) = (6 × 3) / (6 + 3) = 18 / 9 = 2 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Two resistors of 10 Ω and 40 Ω are connected in series across a 100 V DC supply. What is the voltage drop across the 40 Ω resistor?","options":["20 V","40 V","60 V","80 V"],"answer":3,"explanation":"By voltage division, V40 = 100 × [40 / (10 + 40)] = 100 × 0.8 = 80 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A total DC current of 20 A divides between two parallel resistors of 4 Ω and 6 Ω. What is the current flowing through the 4 Ω resistor?","options":["8 A","10 A","12 A","16 A"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By current division, I4 = 20 × [6 / (4 + 6)] = 20 × 0.6 = 12 A."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical order of magnitude of the drift velocity of free electrons in a metallic conductor carrying normal current?","options":["3 × 10^8 m/s","10^-4 m/s","10^4 m/s","10^2 m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"While the electric signal propagates near the speed of light, individual electrons drift slowly at roughly 10^-4 m/s (a fraction of a millimeter per second)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An ideal independent voltage source has:","options":["Zero internal resistance","Infinite internal resistance","Low internal conductance","Internal resistance equal to load resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Zero internal resistance ensures that terminal voltage remains constant regardless of the load current drawn."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A practical current source is represented by an ideal current source in:","options":["Series with its internal resistance","Parallel with its internal resistance","Series with a capacitor","Parallel with an ideal voltage source"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A practical current source consists of an ideal current source connected in parallel with an equivalent internal shunt resistance."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If α0 is the temperature coefficient of resistance of a conductor at 0°C, what is its temperature coefficient αt at t°C?","options":["α0 / (1 + α0 t)","α0 (1 + α0 t)","α0 / (1 - α0 t)","α0 (1 - α0 t)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From Rt = R0(1 + α0 t), the temperature coefficient at t°C is αt = α0 / (1 + α0 t)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the hot resistance of an incandescent lamp rated at 100 W, 200 V?","options":["2 Ω","200 Ω","400 Ω","2000 Ω"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Resistance R = V^2 / P = (200)^2 / 100 = 40,000 / 100 = 400 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Four resistors of 10 Ω each are connected to form a closed square. What is the equivalent resistance between two diagonally opposite corners?","options":["5 Ω","10 Ω","20 Ω","40 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Across diagonally opposite corners, two branches of (10 + 10 = 20 Ω) are in parallel, giving 20 || 20 = 10 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Four resistors of 10 Ω each are connected to form a closed square. What is the equivalent resistance between two adjacent corners?","options":["7.5 Ω","10.0 Ω","13.33 Ω","15.0 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Across adjacent corners, one 10 Ω resistor is in parallel with the remaining three in series (30 Ω): Req = (10 × 30) / (10 + 30) = 300 / 40 = 7.5 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Kirchhoff’s Voltage Law (KVL) is a direct consequence of the law of conservation of:","options":["Charge","Momentum","Energy","Mass"],"answer":2,"explanation":"KVL states that the algebraic sum of voltages around any closed loop is zero, which reflects the conservation of energy in an electric field."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How many independent mesh (KVL) equations are required to solve a planar connected network having b branches and n nodes?","options":["n - 1","b - n + 1","b + n - 1","b - n"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number of independent loops or meshes in a connected graph is given by l = b - n + 1."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How many independent nodal (KCL) equations are required to analyze a connected electrical network having n nodes?","options":["n - 1","n","n + 1","b - n + 1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With one node chosen as the reference (ground), there are n - 1 independent non-reference node voltage equations."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A bilateral circuit element is one whose voltage-current relationship:","options":["Is always non-linear","Changes when supply frequency changes","Remains identical for current flowing in either direction","Allows current flow in only one direction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bilateral elements (such as resistors, inductors, and capacitors) offer the same impedance regardless of current direction."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Thevenin’s equivalent circuit replaces a linear active two-terminal network with:","options":["A voltage source Vth in series with an impedance Zth","A voltage source Vth in parallel with an impedance Zth","A current source Ith in parallel with an impedance Zth","A current source Ith in series with an impedance Zth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Thevenin’s theorem simplifies any linear bilateral active network into a single voltage source Vth in series with an equivalent impedance Zth."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"While determining Thevenin’s equivalent resistance (Rth) of a network containing independent sources, how are the sources deactivated?","options":["Voltage sources are opened and current sources are shorted","Voltage sources are shorted and current sources are opened","Both voltage and current sources are shorted","Both voltage and current sources are opened"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To set independent sources to zero, voltage sources are replaced by short circuits (V = 0) and current sources by open circuits (I = 0)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If a linear network has a Thevenin voltage Vth = 24 V and Thevenin resistance Rth = 6 Ω, what is its Norton equivalent current IN?","options":["0.25 A","3.0 A","4.0 A","144 A"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Norton short-circuit current IN = Vth / Rth = 24 V / 6 Ω = 4.0 A."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the efficiency of power transfer when a DC source transfers maximum power to a matched resistive load (RL = Rth)?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When RL = Rth, half of the total generated power is dissipated in Rth and half is delivered to RL, yielding 50% efficiency."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In an AC circuit, a source with internal impedance Zs = Rs + jXs delivers maximum active power to a variable load impedance ZL when:","options":["ZL = Rs + jXs","ZL = Rs","ZL = jXs","ZL = Rs - jXs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For maximum active power transfer in AC networks, load impedance must equal the complex conjugate of source impedance: ZL = Zs* = Rs - jXs."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which theorem is most convenient for combining several parallel voltage sources with series resistances into a single equivalent voltage source?","options":["Millman’s Theorem","Reciprocity Theorem","Compensation Theorem","Norton’s Theorem"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Millman’s theorem directly reduces multiple parallel voltage-source branches into a single equivalent voltage source in series with an equivalent resistance."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Tellegen’s theorem states that in any lumped electrical network:","options":["Total resistance equals total conductance","The sum of instantaneous powers delivered to all branches is zero","Voltage drop is proportional to branch inductance","Current is conserved only in linear circuits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tellegen’s theorem states that Σ (vk × ik) = 0 for all branches k at any instant, holding valid for both linear and non-linear lumped networks."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compensation theorem is primarily applied in network analysis to determine:","options":["Thevenin equivalent resistance","The effect of a small change in impedance of a circuit branch","Power factor of a 3-phase balanced load","Resonant frequency of a tank circuit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compensation theorem replaces an impedance change ΔZ in a branch with a compensating voltage source Vc = I × ΔZ to compute resulting current changes."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following network theorems is applicable to both linear and non-linear circuits?","options":["Superposition Theorem","Thevenin’s Theorem","Norton’s Theorem","Substitution Theorem"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Substitution theorem allows any branch with known voltage and current to be replaced by an equivalent branch in both linear and non-linear networks."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A connected network graph has 6 nodes and 10 branches. How many twigs (tree branches) does any tree of this graph contain?","options":["4","5","6","10"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number of tree branches (twigs) in a connected graph with n nodes is always n - 1 = 6 - 1 = 5."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A connected network graph has 6 nodes and 10 branches. How many links (chords) are present in its cotree?","options":["4","5","6","11"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Number of links l = b - n + 1 = 10 - 6 + 1 = 5."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Three identical resistors of 6 Ω each are connected in Star (Y). What is the equivalent resistance per side in an equivalent Delta (Δ) connection?","options":["2 Ω","6 Ω","18 Ω","36 Ω"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For identical resistors, RΔ = 3 × RY = 3 × 6 = 18 Ω."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In mesh analysis, a supermesh is formed when:","options":["A voltage source is shared between two meshes","A current source is shared between two meshes","Two resistors are connected in parallel","A mesh contains no independent sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When an independent or dependent current source lies on the boundary between two meshes, those two meshes are combined into a supermesh."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Using source transformation, a 12 V DC voltage source in series with a 3 Ω resistor is equivalent to:","options":["A 4 A current source in parallel with a 3 Ω resistor","A 4 A current source in series with a 3 Ω resistor","A 36 A current source in parallel with a 3 Ω resistor","A 0.25 A current source in parallel with a 3 Ω resistor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IN = V / R = 12 / 3 = 4 A placed in parallel with the same 3 Ω resistor."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A DC network has a Thevenin voltage Vth = 20 V and Thevenin resistance Rth = 5 Ω. What is the maximum power that can be delivered to a matched load?","options":["10 W","15 W","20 W","80 W"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maximum power Pmax = (Vth)^2 / (4 × Rth) = 400 / (4 × 5) = 20 W."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A passive electrical network is defined as a network that contains:","options":["Only resistors","No independent energy sources","Only dependent sources","At least one battery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A passive network has no independent voltage or current sources capable of supplying net average energy continuously."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A dependent source whose terminal voltage is proportional to a current flowing in another branch of the circuit is called a:","options":["Voltage-Controlled Voltage Source (VCVS)","Current-Controlled Current Source (CCCS)","Current-Controlled Voltage Source (CCVS)","Voltage-Controlled Current Source (VCCS)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A CCVS produces an output voltage proportional to a controlling branch current (v = r × i)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In network duality, what is the dual of electrical resistance?","options":["Conductance","Inductance","Capacitance","Impedance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In dual networks, voltage swaps with current, series swaps with parallel, and resistance (R) swaps with conductance (G)."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In network duality, what is the dual of inductance?","options":["Resistance","Capacitance","Reluctance","Susceptance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because v = L(di/dt) and i = C(dv/dt) share identical mathematical forms under V ↔ I duality, inductance and capacitance are duals."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In planar graph duality, what is the dual of a mesh?","options":["A branch","A twig","A node","A link"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each independent mesh in a planar primal graph corresponds to an independent node in its dual graph."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate value of the permittivity of free space (ε0)?","options":["4π × 10^-7 F/m","8.854 × 10^-12 F/m","9 × 10^9 F/m","1.6 × 10^-19 F/m"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The permittivity of free space (vacuum) is ε0 ≈ 8.854 × 10^-12 F/m."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If two point charges are placed in a dielectric medium of relative permittivity εr instead of vacuum at the same separation, the electrostatic force between them:","options":["Increases by εr times","Remains unchanged","Decreases by a factor of εr","Becomes zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By Coulomb’s law, F = (q1 q2) / (4π ε0 εr d^2), so the force decreases by a factor of εr."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the relationship between electric flux density (D) and electric field intensity (E) in an isotropic linear dielectric of permittivity ε?","options":["D = ε E","E = ε D","D = E / ε^2","D = ε + E"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Electric flux density D is directly proportional to electric field intensity E: D = ε0 εr E = ε E."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor with plate area A, plate separation d, and relative permittivity εr is given by:","options":["(ε0 εr d) / A","(ε0 εr A) / d","(A d) / (ε0 εr)","(ε0 A) / (εr d)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Parallel-plate capacitance C = (ε0 εr A) / d, proportional to plate area and permittivity, and inversely proportional to plate spacing."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the distance between the plates of a parallel-plate capacitor is halved and the relative permittivity of the dielectric is doubled, its capacitance becomes:","options":["Half","Unchanged","Double","Four times"],"answer":3,"explanation":"C' = ε0 (2εr) A / (d/2) = 4 × (ε0 εr A / d) = 4C."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Two capacitors of 4 μF and 12 μF are connected in series. What is their equivalent capacitance?","options":["2 μF","3 μF","8 μF","16 μF"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ceq = (C1 × C2) / (C1 + C2) = (4 × 12) / (4 + 12) = 48 / 16 = 3 μF."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two capacitors of 3 μF and 6 μF are connected in series across a 90 V DC supply. What is the voltage across the 3 μF capacitor?","options":["30 V","45 V","60 V","90 V"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In series, charge Q is identical so voltage divides inversely with capacitance: V3 = 90 × [6 / (3 + 6)] = 60 V."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An ideal capacitor opposes any sudden change in:","options":["Voltage across it","Current through it","Magnetic flux","Resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Since i = C(dv/dt), an instantaneous step change in voltage would require infinite current; thus a capacitor opposes sudden voltage changes."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate relative permittivity (dielectric constant) of dry air at standard atmospheric pressure?","options":["0","1.0006","4.5","80"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dry air has a relative permittivity of approximately 1.0006, which is taken as 1 in most engineering calculations."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A parallel-plate capacitor has two dielectric slabs of equal thickness with relative permittivities εr1 = 2 and εr2 = 6 placed in series. Which slab experiences higher electric field intensity?","options":["The slab with εr1 = 2","The slab with εr2 = 6","Both experience equal electric field intensity","Electric field is zero in both slabs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Since flux density D is continuous across series slabs and E = D / (ε0 εr), the slab with lower permittivity (εr1 = 2) experiences 3 times higher electric field."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the SI unit of electric potential gradient?","options":["Volt-meter","Coulomb/meter","Volt/meter","Joule/meter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Potential gradient (dV/dx) is measured in Volts per meter (V/m), which is equivalent to electric field intensity."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Dielectric power loss in a capacitor subjected to sinusoidal AC voltage V of frequency f is proportional to:","options":["f V","f V^2","f^2 V","V / f"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dielectric loss Pd = 2π f C V^2 tan(δ), so it is proportional to frequency f and the square of applied voltage V^2."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the electrostatic potential behave inside a hollow uniformly charged spherical conductor?","options":["It is zero everywhere inside","It decreases linearly toward the center","It is constant everywhere inside and equal to the surface potential","It becomes infinite at the center"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because the electric field inside a hollow conductor is zero (E = -dV/dr = 0), the potential remains constant everywhere inside and equals the surface potential."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An uncharged 20 μF capacitor is connected in series with a 100 kΩ resistor across a DC supply. How long does it take to reach 63.2% of the supply voltage?","options":["0.2 s","2.0 s","5.0 s","20.0 s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A capacitor reaches 63.2% of its final voltage in one time constant: τ = R × C = (100 × 10^3) × (20 × 10^-6) = 2.0 s."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Approximately what percentage of the final steady-state voltage does a charging capacitor reach after 5 time constants (5τ) in a series RC circuit?","options":["63.2%","86.5%","95.0%","99.3%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"At t = 5τ, Vc = V(1 - e^-5) = V(1 - 0.0067) ≈ 99.3% of the applied voltage."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the angle between electric field lines and an equipotential surface at every point?","options":["0°","90°","45°","180°"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential surface, so electric field lines are always perpendicular (90°) to equipotential surfaces."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Electric dipole moment is defined as the product of:","options":["Magnitude of one charge and the distance between the two opposite charges","Sum of both charges and distance","Force and distance","Charge and electric potential"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An electric dipole consists of equal and opposite charges +q and -q separated by distance d, with dipole moment p = q × d."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of capacitor is polarized and requires strict observance of DC terminal polarity?","options":["Ceramic capacitor","Mica capacitor","Electrolytic capacitor","Polyester film capacitor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aluminum and tantalum electrolytic capacitors use a thin oxide layer formed electrochemically and can be damaged or explode if reverse-biased."},
{"subject":"Basic Electrical Engineering","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Gauss’s law in electrostatics, the total electric flux passing outward through any closed surface is equal to:","options":["Zero","1 / ε0 times the total charge enclosed","ε0 times the total charge enclosed","Infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gauss's law states that the net outward electric flux through any closed Gaussian surface equals Q_enclosed / ε0."},{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the phase relationship between the arc voltage and the arc current in an AC circuit breaker?","options":["Arc voltage leads arc current by 90°","Arc voltage lags behind arc current by 90°","Arc voltage is in phase with arc current","Arc voltage is 180° out of phase with arc current"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An electric arc behaves purely like a non-linear resistor, so the arc voltage is always in phase with the arc current."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a purely inductive AC circuit having a peak system phase voltage Vm, what is the theoretical maximum peak value of the restriking voltage across the circuit breaker contacts?","options":["Vm","√2 Vm","2 Vm","3 Vm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"During current interruption at zero crossing in an inductive circuit, system voltage is at peak Vm, causing high-frequency LC oscillations that reach a peak restriking voltage of 2 Vm."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Current chopping phenomenon is most prominently observed in which type of circuit breaker?","options":["Bulk oil circuit breaker","Air-blast circuit breaker","Minimum oil circuit breaker","Thermal magnetic MCB"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Air-blast circuit breakers exert the same high extinguishing force regardless of current magnitude, forcibly interrupting small magnetizing currents before natural current zero."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Resistance switching is employed in high-voltage circuit breakers primarily to:","options":["Increase the breaking current capacity","Reduce the Rate of Rise of Restriking Voltage (RRRV) and damp out oscillations","Increase the arc resistance during normal load","Eliminate the need for current transformers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connecting a damping resistor in parallel with the breaker contacts reduces RRRV and suppresses voltage transients caused by current chopping."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Approximately how many times higher is the dielectric strength of Sulfur Hexafluoride (SF6) gas compared to air at atmospheric pressure?","options":["1.2 times","2.5 to 3 times","6 to 8 times","10 to 12 times"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SF6 is an electronegative gas with a dielectric strength roughly 2.5 to 3 times that of air at the same pressure, and much higher when pressurized."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical degree of vacuum maintained inside the interrupter chamber of a Vacuum Circuit Breaker (VCB)?","options":["10^-1 to 10^-2 torr","10^-3 to 10^-4 torr","10^-7 to 10^-5 torr","1 to 5 torr"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vacuum circuit breakers operate at an ultra-high vacuum of 10^-7 to 10^-5 torr, providing rapid dielectric recovery within microseconds."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"For a symmetrical 3-phase circuit breaker, how is the rated making capacity related to the symmetrical breaking capacity?","options":["Making capacity = 1.414 × Breaking capacity","Making capacity = 1.80 × Breaking capacity","Making capacity = 2.55 × Breaking capacity","Making capacity = 3.00 × Breaking capacity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Making capacity accounts for the worst-case DC offset peak during the first half-cycle: Imaking = √2 × 1.8 × Ibreaking = 2.55 × Ibreaking."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Mho relay is inherently a:","options":["Non-directional impedance relay","Voltage-restrained directional distance relay","Differential current relay","Thermal overload relay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Mho (admittance) relay has a circular characteristic passing through the origin on the R-X diagram, making it inherently directional and ideal for long EHV transmission lines."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Merz-Price circulating current protection is primarily used to protect:","options":["Long overhead transmission lines","Power transformers and alternators","Low-voltage distribution feeders","Capacitor banks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Merz-Price differential protection compares currents entering and leaving the windings of alternators and power transformers to detect internal faults rapidly."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In differential protection of a Star-Delta (Y-Δ) power transformer, how should the Current Transformers (CTs) on the Star side and Delta side be connected respectively?","options":["Star on Star side, Delta on Delta side","Delta on Star side, Star on Delta side","Star on both sides","Delta on both sides"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CTs are connected in Delta on the Star winding side and in Star on the Delta winding side to compensate for the 30° phase shift and block zero-sequence currents during external ground faults."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The fusing factor of a High Rupturing Capacity (HRC) cartridge fuse is always:","options":["Less than 1","Equal to 1","Greater than 1","Equal to 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fusing factor = Minimum fusing current / Current rating of the fuse, which is always greater than 1 (typically around 1.1 for HRC fuses)."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An overcurrent relay with a current setting of 125% is connected to a 400/5 A current transformer. If a fault current of 4000 A flows on the primary side, what is the Plug Setting Multiplier (PSM)?","options":["4","6","8","10"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Secondary fault current = 4000 × (5 / 400) = 50 A. Relay pick-up current = 1.25 × 5 A = 6.25 A. PSM = 50 / 6.25 = 8."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which semiconducting ceramic material is used in modern gapless surge (lightning) arresters due to its highly non-linear V-I characteristic?","options":["Silicon Carbide (SiC)","Zinc Oxide (ZnO)","Barium Titanate","Aluminum Oxide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Zinc Oxide (ZnO) varistor blocks exhibit extreme non-linearity, acting as an insulator at normal voltage and conducting surge currents to ground without needing series spark gaps."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why should the secondary circuit of a Current Transformer (CT) never be left open while primary current is flowing?","options":["Primary current drops to zero","Dangerously high voltage is induced across the open secondary winding, risking insulation failure and core saturation","The CT frequency doubles","The ammeter burns out due to overload"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With no secondary opposing MMF, the entire primary current acts as magnetizing current, saturating the core and inducing lethal peak voltages across the secondary terminals."},
{"subject":"Switchgear & Protection","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of distance relay is least affected by power swings and is therefore preferred for long EHV transmission lines?","options":["Reactance relay","Plain impedance relay","Mho relay","Overcurrent relay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because the Mho relay occupies the smallest area on the R-X plane, it is least susceptible to false tripping during power swings."},{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the difference between the measured value and the true value of a quantity called?","options":["Relative error","Static error","Random error","Precision"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Static error is defined as the difference between the measured value and the true value of the measurand."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The closeness with which an instrument reading approaches the true value of the variable being measured is known as:","options":["Precision","Sensitivity","Accuracy","Resolution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Accuracy indicates how close a measured value is to the actual true value, whereas precision refers to repeatability."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the smallest change in the measured variable to which an instrument will respond?","options":["Resolution","Dead zone","Threshold","Drift"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resolution is the smallest increment in input that produces a detectable change in the instrument output."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The largest change in input quantity for which there is no output response from an instrument is termed as:","options":["Sensitivity","Dead zone","Span","Hysteresis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dead zone (or dead band) is the range of input values over which the instrument output remains unchanged."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 0–150 V voltmeter has a guaranteed accuracy of 1% of full-scale reading. What is the limiting error when the instrument reads 75 V?","options":["0.5%","1.0%","2.0%","2.5%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Full-scale error = 1% of 150 V = 1.5 V. At a 75 V reading, percentage limiting error = (1.5 / 75) × 100 = 2.0%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Instrumental errors, environmental errors, and observational errors belong to which broad category of measurement errors?","options":["Systematic errors","Random errors","Gross errors","Limiting errors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Systematic errors have deterministic causes arising from instrument limitations, ambient conditions, or observer parallax."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the standard EMF of a saturated Weston cadmium cell at 20°C?","options":["1.0000 V","1.0186 V","1.2000 V","1.5000 V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A saturated Weston standard cell produces an extremely stable reference EMF of 1.0186 V at 20°C."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which alloy is most widely used for constructing precision standard resistors due to its negligible temperature coefficient of resistance?","options":["Nichrome","Eureka","Manganin","Tungsten"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Manganin (copper, manganese, and nickel) has an extremely low temperature coefficient of resistance and low thermal EMF against copper."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Power is calculated from P = I^2 R. If the limiting errors in measuring current I and resistance R are ±1% and ±2% respectively, what is the maximum limiting error in power P?","options":["±1%","±2%","±3%","±4%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For P = I^2 R, percentage limiting error = 2(% error in I) + (% error in R) = 2(1%) + 2% = ±4%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a normal (Gaussian) error distribution, approximately what percentage of readings fall within ±1 standard deviation (±1σ) of the mean?","options":["50.0%","68.27%","95.45%","99.73%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a Gaussian distribution, 68.27% of observations lie within ±1σ, 95.45% within ±2σ, and 99.73% within ±3σ."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the three essential torques required for the proper operation of an analog indicating instrument?","options":["Deflecting, controlling, and damping torques","Starting, running, and braking torques","Accelerating, retarding, and synchronizing torques","Magnetic, thermal, and electrostatic torques"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Deflecting torque moves the pointer, controlling torque opposes deflection to fix a steady reading, and damping torque eliminates pointer oscillations."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which type of damping is employed in Permanent Magnet Moving Coil (PMMC) instruments?","options":["Air friction damping","Fluid friction damping","Eddy current damping","Electromagnetic relay damping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"As the aluminum former of the PMMC coil moves in the permanent magnet field, induced eddy currents produce strong damping torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which damping method is commonly used in Moving Iron (MI) instruments?","options":["Eddy current damping","Air friction damping","Fluid friction damping","Spring damping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the operating magnetic field in MI instruments is weak, an air chamber with a light aluminum vane provides air friction damping."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a PMMC instrument suitable only for DC measurements and not for power-frequency AC?","options":["Alternating current reverses torque every half-cycle, yielding zero average deflection","Its coil inductance is infinite","Permanent magnets demagnetize at 50 Hz","Eddy currents melt the pointer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a PMMC meter, deflecting torque is proportional to current; at 50 Hz, the pointer cannot follow rapid reversals and stays at zero."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How do the scales of PMMC and Moving Iron (MI) instruments compare?","options":["Both have uniform linear scales","PMMC has a uniform linear scale, while MI has a cramped non-linear scale","PMMC has a cramped scale, while MI has a uniform scale","Both have logarithmic scales"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PMMC deflection is directly proportional to current (linear scale), whereas MI deflection is proportional to I^2 (square-law cramped scale)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of connecting a swamping resistance in series with the moving coil of a PMMC meter?","options":["To increase full-scale deflection current","To provide eddy current damping","To minimize error caused by ambient temperature variations","To protect against reverse polarity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A manganin swamping resistor in series with the copper coil keeps total circuit resistance nearly independent of temperature changes."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A PMMC milliammeter with an internal resistance of 50 Ω gives full-scale deflection at 1 mA. What shunt resistance is needed to extend its range to 10 mA?","options":["5.00 Ω","5.56 Ω","45.0 Ω","500 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shunt resistance Rsh = Rm / (m - 1), where m = 10 / 1 = 10. Thus Rsh = 50 / (10 - 1) = 50 / 9 = 5.56 Ω."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 0–1 V PMMC voltmeter has an internal resistance of 100 Ω. What series multiplier resistance is required to extend its range to 100 V?","options":["990 Ω","1000 Ω","9900 Ω","10000 Ω"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Series multiplier Rse = Rm × (m - 1), where m = 100 / 1 = 100. Thus Rse = 100 × (100 - 1) = 9900 Ω."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which material is used for making the hairsprings that provide controlling torque in indicating instruments?","options":["Phosphor bronze","Soft iron","High-carbon steel","Constantan"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phosphor bronze is non-magnetic, has good electrical conductivity, and resists fatigue, making it ideal for control springs."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Fluid friction damping is predominantly used in which type of measuring instrument?","options":["PMMC ammeter","Hot-wire ammeter","Moving-iron voltmeter","Electrostatic voltmeter"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electrostatic voltmeters have weak deflecting forces and vertical spindles, making oil/fluid friction damping suitable."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which analog instrument is universally used as a 'transfer instrument' for calibrating AC meters against DC standards?","options":["PMMC instrument","Electrodynamometer instrument","Induction instrument","Rectifier instrument"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Electrodynamometer instruments follow the same square-law calibration on both DC and AC, making them ideal transfer standards."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is an electrodynamometer voltmeter less sensitive than a PMMC voltmeter?","options":["It uses permanent magnets","It has no controlling springs","Its operating magnetic field is weak due to air-cored coils","Its moving coil has zero turns"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To avoid hysteresis errors on AC, electrodynamometer meters use air-cored coils, producing a weak field that requires higher operating current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an electrodynamometer wattmeter, how are the fixed and moving coils connected in the circuit?","options":["Fixed coil is the current coil (in series); moving coil is the pressure coil (across supply)","Fixed coil is the pressure coil; moving coil is the current coil","Both coils are connected in parallel across the load","Both coils are connected in series with the load"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The heavy fixed coils carry load current, while the light moving coil carries current proportional to supply voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What error is introduced in a wattmeter reading due to the small self-inductance of its pressure coil?","options":["It reads low on lagging power factor loads","It reads high on lagging power factor loads","It reads zero at unity power factor","It causes reverse deflection on capacitive loads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pressure coil inductance causes its current to lag behind supply voltage, reducing the phase angle with lagging load current and making the wattmeter read high."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the error due to pressure coil inductance compensated in an electrodynamometer wattmeter?","options":["By adding a shading ring on the fixed coil","By connecting a shunt across the current coil","By connecting a small capacitor in parallel with a portion of the pressure coil series resistor","By using an iron core in the moving coil"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A capacitor in parallel with part of the series resistor cancels the inductive reactance of the pressure coil at power frequency."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the function of the compensating coil in a Low Power Factor (LPF) wattmeter?","options":["To neutralize the error caused by power loss in the pressure coil circuit","To increase the current rating of the fixed coil","To provide air damping to the pointer","To double the scale range"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The compensating coil is wound identically to the current coil in opposition and carries pressure-coil current to cancel its magnetic flux."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is 'phantom loading' used when testing high-capacity wattmeters and energy meters?","options":["To test meters without any supply voltage","To energize the voltage and current circuits from separate low-power sources, saving energy","To eliminate the need for a standard wattmeter","To measure reactive power directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Phantom loading supplies rated voltage to the pressure coil and rated current from a low-voltage source to the current coil, avoiding huge load power waste."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Blondel's theorem, what is the minimum number of wattmeters required to measure total power in an N-wire polyphase system?","options":["N","N + 1","N - 1","2N"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blondel's theorem states that if a network is supplied through N conductors, total power is measured by N - 1 wattmeters with common potential leads on one wire."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the two-wattmeter method of 3-phase balanced power measurement, when do both wattmeters give equal and positive readings?","options":["When the load power factor is unity (cos φ = 1)","When the load power factor is 0.5 lagging","When the load power factor is zero","When the load is purely inductive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At unity power factor (φ = 0°), W1 = VL IL cos(30°) and W2 = VL IL cos(30°), so both wattmeters read identically."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In the two-wattmeter method, if one wattmeter reads in the reverse direction (requiring pressure coil reversal), the load power factor is:","options":["Unity","Between 0.5 and 1.0","Equal to 0.5","Less than 0.5 (phase angle > 60°)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For φ > 60° (cos φ < 0.5), cos(30° + φ) becomes negative, causing the second wattmeter to deflect backward."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which physical principle does a single-phase induction-type energy meter operate?","options":["Electrostatic attraction","Electromagnetic induction","Thermoelectric Seebeck effect","Hall effect"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alternating fluxes from shunt and series electromagnets induce eddy currents in an aluminum disc, creating driving torque proportional to true power."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For true active power registration in an induction energy meter, the shunt magnet flux must lag behind the applied supply voltage by:","options":["0°","45°","90°","180°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Driving torque is proportional to V I cos(φ) only when the shunt flux lags the supply voltage by exactly 90°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the exact 90° phase lag between shunt magnet flux and supply voltage obtained in an induction energy meter?","options":["By placing copper shading bands (lag adjustment rings) on the central limb of the shunt magnet","By connecting a capacitor in series with the current coil","By drilling holes in the aluminum disc","By using a permanent magnet near the series coil"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adjustable copper shading loops on the shunt magnet central limb shift the flux phase to lag supply voltage by 90°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is 'creeping' in a single-phase induction-type energy meter?","options":["Sudden stopping of the disc under heavy overload","Slow, continuous rotation of the disc when only the pressure coil is energized with zero load current","Backward rotation of the disc at unity power factor","Vibration of the braking magnet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Over-compensation for friction or stray magnetic fields can cause the disc to creep slowly at no-load, inflating energy readings."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is creeping prevented in an induction-type energy meter?","options":["By increasing the air gap of the braking magnet","By removing the shading bands","By drilling two diametrically opposite holes in the aluminum disc","By short-circuiting the pressure coil"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When one of the holes comes under the shunt magnet pole, distortion of eddy current paths locks the disc and stops creeping."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In an induction-type energy meter, the braking torque produced by the permanent magnet is directly proportional to:","options":["Speed of rotation of the aluminum disc","Square of the supply voltage","Load power factor","Square of the load current"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Induced eddy currents and hence braking torque are proportional to the rotational speed of the disc, ensuring steady speed proportional to power."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If the permanent braking magnet of an energy meter is moved radially inward toward the center of the disc, the meter will:","options":["Run slower and register less energy","Run faster and register more energy","Stop completely","Rotate in the reverse direction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moving the magnet closer to the spindle reduces the effective lever arm and braking torque, causing the disc to spin faster."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the standard unit of the 'meter constant' of an induction energy meter?","options":["kWh per revolution","Watts per lumen","Revolutions per kWh","Amperes per second"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The meter constant specifies the number of disc revolutions made per kilowatt-hour of energy consumed (rev/kWh)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A unique constructional feature of an electrodynamometer power factor meter is that it has:","options":["No controlling torque (no control springs)","No moving coils","A permanent magnet stator","Fluid friction damping"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two crossed moving coils carry currents proportional to active and reactive components; without control springs, the pointer settles at the phase angle."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Weston-type frequency meter operates based on the:","options":["Mechanical resonance of vibrating reeds","Variation of current division between an inductive branch and a resistive branch with frequency","Discharge of a capacitor through a neon lamp","Hall voltage variation with frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"As supply frequency changes, inductive reactance changes while resistance remains constant, shifting the resultant magnetic field and pointer position."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the standard rated secondary currents for commercial Current Transformers (CTs)?","options":["10 A or 20 A","5 A or 1 A","0.1 A or 0.5 A","50 A or 100 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standardized CT secondary ratings are 5 A (most common) and 1 A (used when long secondary leads exist to reduce I^2 R burden)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the standard rated secondary line-to-line voltage of a Potential Transformer (PT)?","options":["110 V","230 V","415 V","11 kV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standardized PT secondary ratings are typically 110 V (or 110/√3 V for single-phase line-to-neutral units)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary cause of ratio error and phase angle error in a Current Transformer?","options":["High secondary burden resistance","Eddy currents in the aluminum casing","Exciting (magnetizing and core-loss) current drawn by the core","Capacitance between primary bushings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A small portion of primary current magnetizes the core instead of transforming to the secondary, causing both ratio and phase angle errors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which core material is preferred in high-precision Current Transformers to minimize exciting current?","options":["Cast iron","Mumetal or Permalloy","Solid mild steel","Hardened tool steel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nickel-iron alloys like Mumetal and Permalloy possess very high initial permeability and low core loss, reducing CT errors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is 'turns compensation' in a Current Transformer designed to reduce?","options":["Ratio error","Phase angle error","Secondary winding resistance","Insulation breakdown voltage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reducing the secondary turns by one or two turns below the nominal ratio raises secondary current slightly to compensate for ratio error."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When is the phase angle error (θ) of a Current Transformer considered positive?","options":["When secondary reversed current lags primary current","When secondary reversed current is in phase with primary current","When secondary reversed current leads primary current","When primary current is zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By convention, CT phase angle is positive if the reversed secondary current vector leads the primary current vector (which occurs for resistive/inductive burdens)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why must the secondary terminals of a Current Transformer be short-circuited before disconnecting the measuring instrument?","options":["To prevent primary circuit tripping","To prevent core saturation and dangerously high induced voltage across the open secondary","To discharge the primary capacitor","To prevent the ammeter needle from bending"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Opening an energized CT secondary removes opposing secondary MMF, saturating the core and inducing hazardous peak voltages."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In what unit is the 'burden' of an instrument transformer normally expressed?","options":["Watts (W)","Ohms (Ω)","Henry (H)","Volt-Amperes (VA)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Burden is the permissible load connected across the secondary terminals, rated in Volt-Amperes (VA) at rated secondary current or voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Silsbee's method is a comparative test procedure used for determining the errors of:","options":["Current Transformers (CTs)","Potential Transformers (PTs)","High-voltage Meggers","Energy meters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Silsbee's deflection method compares a test CT directly against a standard CT of the same nominal ratio."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A clamp-on (tong-tester) ammeter measures AC current without breaking the conductor by acting as a:","options":["Hall-effect voltage divider","Split-core Current Transformer","Electrostatic voltmeter","Series shunt resistor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The jaws form a hinged magnetic core around the current-carrying wire (single-turn primary) with a multi-turn secondary connected to a rectifier meter."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"By convention in electrical measurements, resistances below what value are classified as 'low resistances'?","options":["1 Ω","10 Ω","100 Ω","1000 Ω"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resistances under 1 Ω (such as armature windings, shunts, and busbar joints) are classified as low resistances."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main advantage of Kelvin's double bridge over a standard Wheatstone bridge when measuring low resistances?","options":["It uses an AC supply instead of DC","It eliminates errors caused by contact and connecting lead resistances","It requires no null detector","It can measure insulation resistance in megaohms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A second set of ratio arms transfers the potential points inside the current terminals, eliminating lead and contact resistance errors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A standard Wheatstone bridge is most accurate for measuring resistances in which range?","options":["0.001 Ω to 0.1 Ω","0.1 Ω to 1 Ω","1 Ω to 100 kΩ","10 MΩ to 1000 MΩ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wheatstone bridges perform best in the medium resistance range (1 Ω to 100 kΩ), where lead resistance and detector sensitivity errors are negligible."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which portable instrument is universally used to measure the insulation resistance of cables, motors, and transformers?","options":["Kelvin bridge","Megger","Frequency meter","Synchroscope"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Megger combines a high-voltage DC source (500 V to 5 kV) with a direct-reading megaohmmeter to test insulation resistance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two main internal assemblies of a conventional hand-driven Megger?","options":["A hand-driven DC generator and a cross-coil ohmmeter movement","A step-down transformer and a PMMC ammeter","A Weston standard cell and a Wheatstone bridge","An induction motor and a wattmeter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The hand-cranked magneto generates high DC test voltage while crossed current and voltage coils indicate resistance without control springs."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The 'loss of charge' method is primarily employed to measure:","options":["Low shunt resistances","Earth electrode resistance","Very high resistances (such as cable insulation)","Mutual inductance of coils"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the loss of charge method, a known capacitor discharges slowly through the unknown high resistance, and resistance is calculated from voltage decay."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which test method is standard for measuring the resistance of a grounding/earth electrode system?","options":["Fall-of-potential (three-point) method","Murray loop test","Swinburne's test","Hopkinson's test"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The fall-of-potential method uses an earth electrode along with auxiliary potential and current spikes to measure ground resistance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Maxwell's inductance-capacitance bridge measures an unknown inductance by comparing it with a:","options":["Standard variable capacitor","Standard mutual inductor","Standard thermistor","Standard Zener diode"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maxwell's L-C bridge balances an unknown inductive arm against a parallel RC arm in the opposite branch using a known standard capacitor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Maxwell's inductance-capacitance bridge is best suited for measuring coils having:","options":["Very low Q-factor (Q < 1)","Medium Q-factor (1 < Q < 10)","Very high Q-factor (Q > 10)","Zero resistance (purely inductive)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For high-Q coils, the parallel balancing resistor in Maxwell's bridge becomes impractically large, restricting it to medium-Q coils."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which AC bridge is specifically preferred for measuring inductive coils with a high Quality Factor (Q > 10)?","options":["Maxwell's bridge","Anderson's bridge","Hay's bridge","De Sauty's bridge"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hay's bridge places the balancing resistor in series with the standard capacitor, keeping resistor values small for high-Q coils."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Anderson's bridge is a modified version of which AC bridge, capable of measuring low-Q coils accurately?","options":["Maxwell's inductance-capacitance bridge","Schering bridge","Wien's bridge","Kelvin's double bridge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Anderson's bridge uses a fixed standard capacitor and an auxiliary T-network of resistors to measure inductances over a wide Q range."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which AC bridge is extensively used to measure the capacitance and dielectric loss angle (tan δ) of high-voltage insulators and cables?","options":["Hay's bridge","Schering bridge","Maxwell's bridge","Wheatstone bridge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Schering bridge accurately determines unknown capacitance and dissipation factor (tan δ) of capacitors and insulating materials."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Wien's bridge is primarily used in electrical laboratories for measuring:","options":["Low resistance","Mutual inductance","Audio frequencies","Earth resistance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because the balance condition of Wien's bridge depends on f = 1 / (2π R C), it is widely used as a frequency meter and oscillator network."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"De Sauty's bridge is the simplest AC bridge used to compare:","options":["Two pure (lossless) capacitors","Two high-Q inductors","Two low resistances","Two mutual inductances"],"answer":0,"explanation":"De Sauty's bridge balances two loss-free capacitors against two non-inductive ratio resistors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of a Wagner earth device in high-frequency AC bridge measurements?","options":["To step up the supply voltage","To eliminate errors caused by stray capacitances between bridge arms and ground","To provide magnetic shielding","To rectify the detector signal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Wagner ground auxiliary arm brings the null detector terminals to earth potential, eliminating stray earth capacitance currents."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the fundamental operating principle of a DC potentiometer?","options":["Balancing magnetic fluxes in an iron core","Comparing an unknown EMF against a known voltage drop along a uniform resistance wire","Measuring current through a calibrated shunt","Charging a standard capacitor to peak voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A potentiometer balances an unknown voltage against an adjustable known voltage drop across a calibrated slide wire."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why does a potentiometer measure the true open-circuit EMF of a cell without loading error?","options":["At null balance, it draws zero current from the unknown voltage source","Its internal resistance is zero","It uses a high-voltage step-up transformer","It operates at infinite frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the galvanometer indicates zero deflection, no current flows from the source under test, so internal voltage drop (I r) is zero."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which reference device is used to standardize (calibrate the working current of) a DC potentiometer?","options":["Lead-acid battery","Dry Leclanché cell","Weston cadmium standard cell","Silicon solar cell"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The potentiometer working current is adjusted until the standard cell (1.0186 V) gives exact null balance at its corresponding dial setting."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A DC potentiometer can be used to calibrate which of the following instruments?","options":["Voltmeters only","Ammeters only","Wattmeters only","Voltmeters, ammeters, and wattmeters"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using a volt-ratio box for high voltages and a standard shunt resistor for currents, a potentiometer calibrates voltmeters, ammeters, and wattmeters."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Drysdale phase-shifting potentiometer belongs to which category of AC potentiometers?","options":["Coordinate type","Polar type","Digital type","Electrostatic type"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Drysdale AC potentiometer measures unknown AC voltages in polar form (magnitude and phase angle) using a phase-shifting transformer."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does a Gall-Tinsley coordinate AC potentiometer measure an unknown sinusoidal voltage?","options":["In terms of two orthogonal components: an in-phase component and a quadrature (90°) component","By measuring its RMS heating effect","By converting AC to DC via a bridge rectifier","By counting zero crossings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Coordinate AC potentiometers have two separate circuits energized 90° apart to balance the real and imaginary components independently."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What physical quantity is a ballistic galvanometer specifically designed to measure?","options":["Steady DC current","Quantity of electric charge (or magnetic flux linkage change) from a brief impulse","AC RMS power","Supply frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With a high moment of inertia and minimal damping, a ballistic galvanometer's first swing is proportional to total charge passed instantaneously."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Grassot fluxmeter, how is the mechanical controlling torque designed?","options":["Very high using stiff steel springs","Negligibly small (practically zero)","Proportional to square of current","Provided by a permanent magnet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using a silk suspension and current-less flexible ligaments makes controlling torque negligible, so deflection depends only on net flux change."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A search coil connected to a ballistic galvanometer is commonly used to measure:","options":["Magnetic flux density (B)","Dielectric strength of oil","Insulation resistance","Power factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Withdrawing or reversing a known-turn search coil in a magnetic field induces charge proportional to flux density B."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The Epstein square apparatus is a standard magnetic testing setup used to measure:","options":["Earth resistance","Core losses (hysteresis and eddy current losses) in electrical steel sheets","Dielectric constant of mica","Battery Ampere-hour capacity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Epstein frame stacks sample strips of silicon steel into a square magnetic core to measure total iron losses with a wattmeter."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which component is the main display vacuum tube of a conventional analog Cathode Ray Oscilloscope (CRO)?","options":["Photomultiplier tube","Cathode Ray Tube (CRT)","Magnetron tube","Thyratron tube"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Cathode Ray Tube generates, focuses, and deflects an electron beam onto a phosphor screen to plot voltage versus time."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of the conductive 'Aquadag' graphite coating on the inner glass wall of a CRT?","options":["To emit primary electrons","To collect secondary emission electrons and maintain an equipotential screen environment","To deflect the beam vertically","To generate the sawtooth time-base"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connected to the second anode, the Aquadag coating collects secondary electrons from the screen and prevents negative charge buildup."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What type of voltage waveform is generated by the internal time-base circuit and applied to the horizontal (X) plates of a CRO?","options":["Sinusoidal wave","Square wave","Sawtooth (ramp) wave","Triangular pulse train"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A linear ramp sweeps the electron beam across the screen at constant velocity, followed by rapid flyback to the left."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the electrostatic deflection sensitivity of a CRT depend on the final anode accelerating voltage (Va)?","options":["Directly proportional to Va","Inversely proportional to Va","Directly proportional to Va^2","Independent of Va"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Higher accelerating voltage increases electron velocity, reducing transit time between deflection plates and lowering deflection sensitivity (S ∝ 1 / Va)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What Lissajous pattern appears on a CRO screen when two sinusoidal voltages of equal frequency, equal amplitude, and 90° phase difference are applied to the X and Y plates?","options":["A circle","A straight line inclined at 45°","A figure-of-eight","A parabola"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equal-amplitude, equal-frequency signals in quadrature (90° phase shift) trace a perfect circle on the CRT screen."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a Lissajous figure on a CRO, a horizontal line touches 3 loops at the top and a vertical line touches 2 loops on the side. What is the ratio of vertical frequency (fy) to horizontal frequency (fx)?","options":["2 : 3","1 : 3","3 : 2","3 : 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The frequency ratio fy / fx equals (number of horizontal tangencies) / (number of vertical tangencies) = 3 / 2."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a delay line incorporated in the vertical deflection channel of a CRO?","options":["To increase horizontal sweep speed","To delay the vertical signal slightly so the leading edge of a fast pulse can be displayed after sweep triggering","To reduce CRT heater power","To filter out DC offset"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The delay line holds the vertical signal for ~150–200 ns while the trigger and time-base circuits start the horizontal sweep."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the dual-slope integrating technique widely used in precision Digital Voltmeters (DVMs)?","options":["It rejects 50/60 Hz power-line hum and cancels errors from clock frequency and RC component drift","It is the fastest possible ADC architecture","It requires no reference voltage","It operates without any comparator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Integrating over an exact multiple of the mains period averages out AC hum, while using the same RC integrator for up and down ramps cancels component drift."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) architecture provides the highest conversion speed in high-frequency digital oscilloscopes?","options":["Dual-slope integrating ADC","Ramp-type ADC","Flash (parallel comparator) ADC","Successive approximation ADC"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A flash ADC uses 2^n - 1 parallel comparators to convert the analog input in a single clock cycle."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Q-meter measures the Quality Factor of an inductive coil by utilizing the principle of:","options":["Parallel resonance","Series resonance","Mutual induction","Wheatstone bridge balance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At series resonance, the voltage across the tuning capacitor equals Q times the injected source voltage, allowing direct Q readout."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is an 'active' (self-generating) transducer that requires no external auxiliary power supply?","options":["LVDT","Strain gauge","Thermocouple","Thermistor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A thermocouple generates its own thermoelectric EMF directly from a temperature difference without external excitation."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"An LVDT (Linear Variable Differential Transformer) is an inductive transducer primarily used to measure:","options":["Linear displacement","Fluid viscosity","Light intensity","Acidity (pH)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Movement of a ferromagnetic core inside one primary and two series-opposing secondary windings produces an AC voltage proportional to displacement."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At the exact central (null) position of the movable core in an LVDT, the ideal differential output voltage is:","options":["Maximum","Zero","Equal to primary voltage","Infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At the null position, equal voltages are induced in both identical secondary windings, canceling out in series opposition."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which physical effect does a bonded metallic strain gauge operate?","options":["Photoelectric effect","Seebeck effect","Piezoresistive effect (change in resistance due to mechanical strain)","Hall effect"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stretching or compressing a fine metallic grid alters its length and cross-sectional area, producing a proportional change in electrical resistance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the Gauge Factor (Gf) of a strain gauge mathematically defined?","options":["(ΔR / R) / (ΔL / L)","(ΔL / L) / (ΔR / R)","(ΔR × R) / (ΔL × L)","(ΔV / V) / (ΔI / I)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gauge factor is the ratio of fractional change in electrical resistance (ΔR/R) to fractional change in length or mechanical strain (ε = ΔL/L)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical Gauge Factor value of standard metallic wire or foil strain gauges?","options":["0.5","2.0","50.0","150.0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conventional metallic strain gauges (such as Constantan) have a gauge factor of approximately 2.0, whereas semiconductor gauges reach 100+."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a 'dummy' strain gauge mounted on an unstrained specimen and connected in an adjacent arm of a Wheatstone bridge?","options":["To double the bridge supply voltage","To filter out high-frequency vibrations","To provide temperature compensation","To measure tensile strength directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both active and dummy gauges experience identical ambient temperature changes, canceling thermal resistance drift in the bridge."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Piezoelectric transducers (such as quartz or Rochelle salt crystals) are best suited for measuring:","options":["Static steady-state pressure","Dynamic (rapidly varying) pressure, force, or acceleration","DC magnetic fields","Slow temperature changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Charge generated by a piezoelectric crystal leaks away through finite load resistance under static loads, making it ideal for dynamic measurements."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The operation of a thermocouple for temperature measurement is governed by which physical phenomenon?","options":["Seebeck effect","Peltier effect","Thomson effect","Hall effect"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Seebeck effect produces an EMF in a closed loop of two dissimilar metals when their two junctions are held at different temperatures."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the electrical resistance of a standard NTC thermistor vary with temperature?","options":["Increases linearly with temperature","Remains constant over all temperatures","Decreases sharply and non-linearly with increasing temperature","Drops to zero at 100°C"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Negative Temperature Coefficient (NTC) semiconductor thermistors exhibit a large exponential decrease in resistance as temperature rises."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which metal is most widely used as the sensing element in precision industrial Resistance Temperature Detectors (RTDs)?","options":["Copper","Platinum","Tungsten","Nichrome"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Platinum (such as Pt100) offers a highly linear, stable, and repeatable resistance-temperature characteristic over a wide temperature range."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A Hall-effect transducer generates a transverse voltage when a current-carrying semiconductor strip is placed in a perpendicular:","options":["Magnetic field","Electric field","Gravitational field","Acoustic wave"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lorentz force deflects charge carriers perpendicular to both current and magnetic flux density, creating a Hall voltage proportional to B × I."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which instrument measures low vacuum pressures by detecting changes in the thermal conductivity of the surrounding gas?","options":["Bourdon tube gauge","Venturi meter","Pirani gauge","Dead-weight tester"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a Pirani gauge, heat loss from a heated filament decreases as gas pressure drops, raising filament resistance in a Wheatstone bridge."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"A rotameter is a variable-area industrial transducer used to measure:","options":["Rotational speed (RPM)","Fluid flow rate","Relative humidity","Torque on a shaft"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a vertical tapered tube, a float rises until upward fluid drag balances its weight, indicating volumetric flow rate."},
{"subject":"Electrical Measurements","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which optical instrument measures the rotational speed (RPM) of a shaft without making any physical contact?","options":["Stroboscope","Tachogenerator","Planimeter","Hydrometer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By matching the flashing frequency of a stroboscopic lamp until a mark on the rotating shaft appears stationary, RPM is read directly."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which fundamental physical principle does a static power transformer operate?","options":["Self-induction","Mutual electromagnetic induction","Electrostatic induction","Thermionic emission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A transformer transfers AC electrical energy from one circuit to another through mutual electromagnetic induction between magnetically coupled windings."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When a transformer steps up the voltage from primary to secondary, what happens to the supply frequency?","options":["Frequency remains unchanged","Frequency increases in proportion to turns ratio","Frequency decreases in proportion to turns ratio","Frequency doubles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A transformer is a constant-frequency device; the secondary induced EMF has the exact same frequency as the primary supply."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which magnetic material is universally preferred for constructing the core of modern power transformers?","options":["Cast iron","Solid mild steel","Cold Rolled Grain Oriented (CRGO) silicon steel","Hardened carbon steel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CRGO silicon steel offers high magnetic permeability along the rolling direction and very low hysteresis loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical percentage of silicon added to steel laminations in a transformer core to increase resistivity and reduce eddy current loss?","options":["0.5% to 1%","3% to 4%","10% to 12%","20% to 25%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding about 3% to 4% silicon increases electrical resistivity (lowering eddy current loss) without making the steel excessively brittle."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a transformer core constructed from thin insulated laminations rather than a solid iron block?","options":["To reduce eddy current losses","To reduce hysteresis losses","To increase magnetizing current","To increase mechanical weight"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Thin insulated laminations (0.35 mm to 0.5 mm) break circulating eddy current paths, reducing eddy current power loss (which is proportional to thickness squared)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the correct RMS induced EMF equation of a transformer winding having N turns, maximum core flux density Bm, net core area A, and supply frequency f?","options":["E = 4.00 f N Bm A","E = 4.44 f N Bm A","E = 2.22 f N Bm A","E = 1.414 f N Bm A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a sinusoidal flux wave, RMS induced EMF E = 4.44 f N Φm = 4.44 f N Bm A, where 4.44 = √2 × π."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The numerical constant 4.44 appearing in the transformer EMF equation is derived from:","options":["2 × form factor (2 × 1.11)","4 × form factor (4 × 1.11)","√2 × form factor","π × peak factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Average EMF per turn is 4 f Φm, and multiplying by the sinusoidal form factor (1.11) yields RMS EMF = 4.44 f N Φm."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The primary winding of a 50 Hz single-phase transformer has 500 turns and carries a maximum sinusoidal core flux of 0.02 Wb. What is the RMS voltage induced in the primary?","options":["1110 V","1570 V","2000 V","2220 V"],"answer":3,"explanation":"E1 = 4.44 × f × N1 × Φm = 4.44 × 50 × 500 × 0.02 = 2220 V."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are stepped (cruciform) core cross-sections used in large power transformers?","options":["To increase core reluctance","To utilize circular coils efficiently, reducing the mean length of turn and saving copper","To eliminate the need for oil cooling","To increase leakage flux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A stepped core approximates a circle inside circular windings, maximizing iron area per unit circumference and reducing winding copper length."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does a shell-type transformer differ structurally from a core-type transformer?","options":["In a shell-type transformer, the iron core surrounds the windings and provides two parallel magnetic paths","In a shell-type transformer, the windings surround a single rectangular core","Shell-type transformers have no iron core","Shell-type transformers operate only on DC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a shell-type design, both HV and LV windings are placed on the central limb with outer limbs completing two parallel magnetic return paths."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which set of characteristics defines an ideal transformer?","options":["Zero winding resistance, zero core permeability, and infinite core loss","Infinite winding resistance and zero mutual flux","Zero winding resistance, infinite core permeability (zero reluctance), and zero core loss","Unity turns ratio and 50% efficiency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An ideal transformer has lossless windings, zero magnetic leakage, infinitely permeable core requiring zero magnetizing MMF, and 100% efficiency."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical magnitude of the no-load current (I0) in a standard power transformer?","options":["0.1% to 0.5% of rated full-load current","2% to 5% of rated full-load current","15% to 20% of rated full-load current","40% to 50% of rated full-load current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because of the high permeability of CRGO steel cores, the exciting no-load current is small, typically 2% to 5% of full-load current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under no-load conditions, the primary current of a transformer lags behind the applied primary voltage by approximately:","options":["0° to 15°","30° to 45°","50° to 60°","75° to 85°"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The no-load current is predominantly reactive magnetizing current, resulting in a very low lagging power factor (0.15 to 0.25) and phase lag of 75° to 85°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two orthogonal components of the no-load current (I0) in a transformer?","options":["Magnetizing component (Im) in quadrature with voltage and core-loss component (Iw) in phase with voltage","Active component in quadrature and reactive component in phase","Load component and harmonic component","Eddy component and leakage component"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Iw = I0 cos(φ0) supplies core losses in phase with applied voltage, while Im = I0 sin(φ0) produces mutual flux lagging voltage by 90°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A single-phase transformer draws a no-load current of 5 A at a power factor of 0.2 lagging. What is the core-loss (working) component of the no-load current?","options":["0.5 A","1.0 A","2.5 A","4.9 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core-loss component Iw = I0 × cos(φ0) = 5 × 0.2 = 1.0 A."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are power and distribution transformers rated in kVA (or MVA) rather than kW?","options":["Transformers only supply purely reactive loads","Copper loss depends on current and core loss depends on voltage, making total loss independent of load power factor","kW ratings are reserved exclusively for DC machines","Frequency varies continuously with load power factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total transformer losses depend on RMS voltage and RMS current regardless of the phase angle between them, so thermal rating is governed by apparent power (kVA)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a transformer often referred to as a 'constant flux' machine?","options":["Because its core is made of permanent magnets","Because mutual core flux remains practically constant from no-load to full-load at constant supply voltage and frequency","Because load current never changes","Because leakage flux is zero at all loads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since primary impedance drop is small, the counter EMF E1 remains nearly equal to applied V1, keeping mutual flux Φm almost constant across all loads."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does magnetic leakage flux in a transformer behave as load increases from no-load to full-load?","options":["It remains strictly constant","It increases almost linearly with load current and causes leakage reactance voltage drop","It decreases to zero at full load","It reverses direction at unity power factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leakage flux links only one winding through air/oil paths and is directly proportional to winding currents, represented by series leakage reactances."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are interleaved (sandwich) windings used in shell-type transformers?","options":["To increase core losses","To increase magnetizing current","To minimize magnetic leakage flux and leakage reactance","To eliminate the need for insulation between HV and LV coils"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Subdividing HV and LV coils into alternating pancake sections reduces the spatial separation between ampere-turns, lowering leakage reactance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In concentric cylindrical windings of a core-type transformer, why is the Low-Voltage (LV) winding placed nearest to the iron core?","options":["To reduce the amount of high-voltage insulation required between the winding and the earthed core","Because LV conductors are thinner than HV conductors","To increase the core reluctance","To prevent eddy currents in the core"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Placing the LV winding next to the grounded core requires only one layer of high-voltage insulation between the LV and outer HV winding."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes the magnetizing current of a transformer to be non-sinusoidal even when the applied voltage is purely sinusoidal?","options":["Winding resistance variation","Non-linear B-H saturation characteristic of the ferromagnetic core","Capacitive coupling between bushings","Cooling oil viscosity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core saturation flattens the flux wave relative to current, forcing the magnetizing current to become peaky with a strong 3rd harmonic component."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens in a Star-Star (Y-Y) connected 3-phase transformer bank if the primary neutral is isolated from the supply neutral?","options":["Third harmonic magnetizing currents cannot flow, causing phase voltages to distort and neutral potential to oscillate","Efficiency reaches 100%","Secondary line voltage doubles","Short-circuit current drops to zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without a closed path for zero-sequence 3rd harmonic currents, core flux becomes flat-topped, inducing 3rd harmonic voltages in each phase."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary purpose of providing a delta-connected tertiary winding in a Star-Star power transformer?","options":["To step up the line frequency to 150 Hz","To provide a circulating path for third-harmonic currents, stabilize neutral potential, and supply auxiliary station loads","To reduce the weight of the transformer tank","To eliminate the need for a Buchholz relay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The closed delta tertiary traps 3rd harmonic currents internally, ensuring sinusoidal phase voltages and providing an auxiliary low-voltage supply."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the worst-case magnetizing inrush current occur when energizing an unloaded transformer?","options":["When the switch closes at the peak of the supply voltage wave","When the switch closes at the zero crossing of the supply voltage wave","When the load is purely capacitive","When the secondary is short-circuited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Switching at voltage zero forces core flux to rise toward 2 Φm (plus any residual flux), driving the core deep into saturation and drawing 8–10 times rated current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which harmonic component is characteristically prominent in transformer magnetizing inrush current and is used to restrain differential relays from false tripping?","options":["2nd harmonic","3rd harmonic","5th harmonic","7th harmonic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because inrush current is heavily asymmetrical (unidirectional pulses), it contains a large 2nd harmonic component used by harmonic-restraint differential relays."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If K = N2 / N1 is the transformation ratio of a transformer, how is a secondary impedance Z2 referred to the primary side?","options":["Z2' = Z2 × K","Z2' = Z2 × K^2","Z2' = Z2 / K^2","Z2' = Z2 / K"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To preserve identical power loss and voltage drop ratios, secondary impedance referred to primary is Z2' = Z2 × (N1 / N2)^2 = Z2 / K^2."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 1000 V / 200 V single-phase transformer has a secondary winding resistance of 0.04 Ω. What is its secondary resistance referred to the primary side?","options":["0.20 Ω","1.00 Ω","0.0016 Ω","2.00 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Turns ratio a = N1 / N2 = 1000 / 200 = 5. Referred resistance R2' = R2 × a^2 = 0.04 × 25 = 1.00 Ω."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the per-unit (pu) equivalent impedance of a transformer calculated from the HV side compare with that calculated from the LV side?","options":["Both are identically equal","HV per-unit impedance is larger by the turns ratio","LV per-unit impedance is larger by the square of turns ratio","They differ by 30°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A major advantage of the per-unit system is that per-unit resistance, reactance, and impedance have the exact same value on both sides of a transformer."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What will happen if a transformer designed for 230 V AC is accidentally connected to a 230 V DC supply?","options":["It will step down the DC voltage normally","The primary winding will draw excessive current and burn out due to zero frequency and absence of inductive reactance","It will operate with higher efficiency","The core will demagnetize permanently without damage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For DC (f = 0), inductive reactance and counter EMF are zero; current is limited only by the tiny primary resistance, rapidly overheating and burning the winding."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If the supply frequency to a transformer is increased while keeping the applied RMS voltage constant, what happens to the core losses?","options":["Core losses increase sharply","Core losses decrease because maximum core flux density decreases","Hysteresis loss doubles while eddy current loss remains constant","Core losses remain completely unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since Bm ∝ V / f, increasing frequency at constant voltage reduces Bm; hysteresis loss (∝ f Bm^1.6 ∝ f^-0.6) decreases while eddy loss (∝ f^2 Bm^2) stays constant, lowering total core loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Steinmetz's formula, hysteresis loss in a transformer core is proportional to:","options":["f × Bm^1.6","f^2 × Bm^2","f × Bm^2","f^2 × Bm^1.6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hysteresis loss Ph = η × Bm^1.6 × f × Volume, varying linearly with frequency and as the 1.6th power of peak flux density."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Eddy current loss in a transformer core of lamination thickness t is proportional to:","options":["f × Bm × t","f^2 × Bm^2 × t","f^2 × Bm^2 × t^2","f × Bm^2 × t^2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Eddy current loss Pe = Ke × Bm^2 × f^2 × t^2 × Volume, varying with the square of frequency, peak flux density, and lamination thickness."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"At 50 Hz and rated flux density, a transformer has a hysteresis loss of 600 W and an eddy current loss of 400 W. If operated at 25 Hz with the same flux density, what is the total core loss?","options":["250 W","400 W","500 W","700 W"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At constant Bm, Ph ∝ f (so Ph = 600 × 25/50 = 300 W) and Pe ∝ f^2 (so Pe = 400 × (25/50)^2 = 100 W). Total core loss = 300 + 100 = 400 W."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which power losses in a transformer are classified as 'constant losses' independent of load current?","options":["Core (iron) losses","Primary and secondary copper losses","Stray load losses in conductors","Terminal contact losses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because mutual core flux remains nearly constant from no-load to full-load, core losses (hysteresis + eddy current) remain virtually constant."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which losses in a transformer vary as the square of the load current?","options":["Hysteresis losses","Eddy current losses","Dielectric losses in oil","Winding copper (I^2 R) losses"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ohmic losses in the primary and secondary windings equal I1^2 R1 + I2^2 R2, scaling with the square of the load fraction."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the mathematical condition for a transformer to operate at its maximum efficiency for a given load power factor?","options":["Copper loss = 2 × Core loss","Copper loss = Core (iron) loss","Eddy current loss = Hysteresis loss","Copper loss = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Differentiating efficiency with respect to load current shows that maximum efficiency occurs when variable copper loss equals constant iron loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If Pi is the constant iron loss and Pcu,fl is the full-load copper loss, at what fraction (x) of full-load kVA does maximum efficiency occur?","options":["x = Pi / Pcu,fl","x = Pcu,fl / Pi","x = √(Pi / Pcu,fl)","x = √(Pcu,fl / Pi)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At fractional load x, x^2 × Pcu,fl = Pi, which gives x = √(Pi / Pcu,fl)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 100 kVA transformer has an iron loss of 1 kW and a full-load copper loss of 4 kW. At what kVA load will it achieve maximum efficiency?","options":["25 kVA","50 kVA","75 kVA","100 kVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Load at maximum efficiency = Rated kVA × √(Pi / Pcu,fl) = 100 × √(1 / 4) = 100 × 0.5 = 50 kVA."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a fixed kVA load on a transformer, maximum efficiency is obtained when the load power factor is:","options":["Unity (1.0)","0.8 lagging","0.8 leading","0.5 lagging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Efficiency η = (kVA cos φ) / (kVA cos φ + Pi + Pcu); for fixed kVA and losses, output power and efficiency peak at cos φ = 1."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Distribution transformers are typically designed so that maximum efficiency occurs around:","options":["10% of full load","50% of full load","100% of full load","125% of full load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distribution transformers remain energized 24 hours a day under lightly loaded conditions, so core loss is kept low to peak efficiency near 50% load."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the 'all-day efficiency' of a distribution transformer defined?","options":["Ratio of peak kW output to peak kW input","Ratio of total energy output (in kWh) to total energy input (in kWh) over 24 hours","Ratio of core loss to copper loss over 24 hours","Average of no-load and full-load commercial efficiencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All-day efficiency evaluates energy performance over a 24-hour load cycle: η_all-day = (kWh output in 24 h) / (kWh input in 24 h)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the all-day efficiency of a distribution transformer always lower than its ordinary commercial efficiency?","options":["Because core losses occur continuously for all 24 hours even when the transformer is lightly loaded or unloaded","Because copper losses occur only during peak hours","Because frequency drops at night","Because oil viscosity increases during the day"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even during periods of zero load, the primary remains energized and consumes constant iron loss continuously over the 24-hour period."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the percentage voltage regulation of a transformer defined?","options":["[(V_NL - V_FL) / V_FL] × 100","[(V_FL - V_NL) / V_NL] × 100","(V_FL / V_NL) × 100","[(V_NL - V_FL) / V_NL] × 50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Percentage voltage regulation is the change in secondary terminal voltage from no-load to full-load expressed as a percentage of rated full-load voltage (or no-load voltage by IEC)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate formula for percentage voltage regulation of a transformer having percentage resistance %R and percentage reactance %X at power factor angle φ?","options":["%R sin φ ± %X cos φ","%R cos φ ± %X sin φ","%R cos φ × %X sin φ","√(%R^2 + %X^2) cos φ"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Approximate percentage regulation = %R cos φ + %X sin φ for lagging power factor, and %R cos φ - %X sin φ for leading power factor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under what load condition can a transformer exhibit zero voltage regulation?","options":["Purely resistive load","Inductive lagging power factor load","Short-circuit condition","Capacitive leading power factor load where tan φ = Req / Xeq"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For a leading load, %R cos φ - %X sin φ = 0 when tan φ = %R / %X = Req / Xeq, yielding zero voltage drop."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does a transformer experience negative voltage regulation (secondary terminal voltage rising above no-load voltage)?","options":["When supplying a sufficiently leading (capacitive) power factor load","When supplying a heavy inductive motor load","At no-load","When core loss exceeds copper loss"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a leading power factor where %X sin φ > %R cos φ, the capacitive voltage rise exceeds resistive drop, making terminal voltage higher than no-load EMF."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At what power factor angle does maximum positive voltage regulation occur in a transformer?","options":["Unity power factor (φ = 0°)","Zero power factor lagging (φ = 90°)","Lagging power factor where tan φ = Xeq / Req","Leading power factor where tan φ = Xeq / Req"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Differentiating %R cos φ + %X sin φ with respect to φ gives maximum regulation at lagging angle φ = arctan(Xeq / Req)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A transformer has a percentage resistance of 2% and a percentage reactance of 4%. What is its approximate voltage regulation when supplying full load at 0.8 power factor lagging?","options":["2.4%","4.0%","4.8%","0.8%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For cos φ = 0.8 lagging, sin φ = 0.6. Regulation = (2% × 0.8) + (4% × 0.6) = 1.6% + 2.4% = 4.0%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A transformer has a percentage resistance of 2% and a percentage reactance of 4%. What is its approximate voltage regulation when supplying full load at 0.8 power factor leading?","options":["-0.8%","+0.8%","+4.0%","-2.4%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For leading power factor, Regulation = %R cos φ - %X sin φ = (2% × 0.8) - (4% × 0.6) = 1.6% - 2.4% = -0.8%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the trade-off associated with designing a power transformer with a very low percentage impedance (%Z)?","options":["Poor voltage regulation and low fault current","Good voltage regulation and high fault current","Better voltage regulation, but higher short-circuit fault current","Higher core loss and lower efficiency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Low leakage impedance reduces load voltage drop (good regulation) but limits fault current less during external short circuits (Isc = 100 / %Z pu)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What parameters are determined by conducting an Open-Circuit (OC) test on a transformer?","options":["Full-load copper loss and series leakage impedance","Core (iron) loss and shunt magnetizing branch parameters (R0, X0)","Percentage voltage regulation directly","Temperature rise under full load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The OC test applies rated voltage at no-load to measure core losses and exciting branch resistance and reactance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which winding is the Open-Circuit (OC) test normally performed in a laboratory?","options":["Low-Voltage (LV) side with High-Voltage (HV) side kept open","High-Voltage (HV) side with Low-Voltage (LV) side kept open","Both windings simultaneously","Tertiary winding only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Performing the OC test on the LV side requires safer, easily available lower supply voltage and standard laboratory meters."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why does the wattmeter reading during an Open-Circuit test represent almost purely the core loss of the transformer?","options":["Because applied voltage is zero","Because the frequency is doubled","Because no-load current is tiny (2–5% of rated), making no-load I^2 R copper loss negligible","Because the primary winding has zero resistance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since I0 is roughly 1/20th of rated current, I0^2 R1 is about 1/400th of full-load copper loss and can be safely neglected."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What parameters are obtained from the Short-Circuit (SC) test on a transformer?","options":["Magnetizing reactance and core loss","Full-load copper loss and equivalent series resistance and leakage reactance","Hysteresis loop area","Dielectric strength of transformer oil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SC test circulates rated current at reduced voltage to measure full-load I^2 R losses and equivalent series impedance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which side of the transformer is usually excited during a Short-Circuit (SC) test, and why?","options":["LV side, because voltage is lower","HV side (with LV shorted), because rated HV current is smaller and easier to supply and measure","LV side, because core loss is maximum","HV side, because frequency is higher"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rated current on the HV side is much lower than on the LV side, and the required reduced voltage (5–10% of HV rating) is convenient to regulate."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are core losses negligible during a Short-Circuit (SC) test on a transformer?","options":["Because the core is removed during the test","Because the applied voltage is only 5% to 10% of rated voltage, producing very small core flux","Because the circuit is purely capacitive","Because the secondary is open-circuited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core loss is proportional to Bm^2 (and hence V^2); at 5% rated voltage, core loss is only (0.05)^2 = 0.25% of normal core loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"During a short-circuit test on a transformer, if the current circulated is half of the rated full-load current, the wattmeter reads 250 W. What is the full-load copper loss?","options":["500 W","750 W","1000 W","2000 W"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Copper loss is proportional to I^2. At 0.5 rated current, Pcu = (0.5)^2 × Pcu,fl = 0.25 Pcu,fl = 250 W, so Pcu,fl = 250 / 0.25 = 1000 W."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main advantage of Sumpner's (back-to-back) test over separate OC and SC tests?","options":["It requires only one transformer","It simulates full-load thermal and loss conditions simultaneously while drawing only total losses from the supply","It measures only core losses","It requires no wattmeters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sumpner's test uses two identical transformers connected in opposition to determine efficiency, regulation, and full-load temperature rise without wasting full load power."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a polarity test performed on single-phase transformers?","options":["To measure core loss","To identify relative instantaneous terminal polarities before connecting transformers in parallel or in 3-phase banks","To measure oil acidity","To check insulation resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incorrect terminal polarity during parallel or 3-phase bank connection causes a dead short-circuit across the windings."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During a polarity test, if a voltmeter connected between adjacent HV and LV terminals reads the difference between the HV and LV voltages (V_HV - V_LV), the transformer has:","options":["Additive polarity","Zero polarity","Subtractive polarity","Reversed phase sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When adjacent terminals have the same instantaneous polarity, their voltages oppose in the test loop, reading V_HV - V_LV (subtractive polarity)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is a major advantage of a single 3-phase transformer unit compared to a bank of three single-phase transformers of the same total rating?","options":["It is easier to repair on site if one phase fails","It costs about 15% less, occupies less floor space, and has slightly higher efficiency","It requires no cooling oil","It eliminates all harmonics automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A unified 3-phase core uses less iron and copper, making it lighter, cheaper, more compact, and more efficient than three separate units."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In 3-phase transformer vector group notation, what does 'Dy11' signify?","options":["Delta HV, Star LV, with LV line voltage leading HV line voltage by 30° (or lagging by 330°)","Star HV, Delta LV, with zero phase shift","Delta HV, Delta LV, with 180° phase shift","Star HV, Star LV, with 30° lag"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capital 'D' denotes Delta HV, lowercase 'y' denotes Star LV, and clock position '11' means LV leads HV by +30° (-330°)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the phase displacement between LV and HV line voltages in a 'Yd1' three-phase transformer?","options":["0°","LV leads HV by 30°","LV lags HV by 30°","LV lags HV by 180°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Clock position '1' corresponds to -30°, meaning the secondary LV line voltage lags the primary HV line voltage by 30°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which 3-phase transformer vector groups provide zero phase displacement between primary and secondary line voltages?","options":["Yd1 and Dy1","Yy0 and Dd0","Yd11 and Dy11","Dz6 and Yz6"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clock number '0' (in Yy0 and Dd0) indicates 0° phase shift between corresponding HV and LV line voltages."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If one transformer in a 3-phase Delta-Delta (Δ-Δ) bank is removed for maintenance, the remaining two transformers can continue operating in Open-Delta (V-V) connection at what percentage of the original bank kVA capacity?","options":["33.3%","50.0%","66.7%","57.7%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An open-delta bank supplies 1 / √3 ≈ 57.7% of the original closed-delta 3-phase kVA rating."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the utilization factor (ratio of available 3-phase output to the sum of individual kVA ratings of the two transformers) in an Open-Delta (V-V) connection?","options":["0.577","0.866","0.707","1.000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two transformers each rated S have combined rating 2S, but deliver √3 S in V-V connection, giving a utilization factor of √3 / 2 = 0.866 (86.6%)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary application of the Scott (T-T) transformer connection?","options":["Converting 3-phase AC supply into 2-phase AC supply (or vice versa)","Converting AC into DC","Stepping up frequency from 50 Hz to 100 Hz","Measuring high DC currents"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Scott connection uses a main center-tapped transformer and a teaser transformer (86.6% turns) to convert between 3-phase and 2-phase systems."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Scott-connected transformer bank, what percentage of full turns are utilized in the teaser transformer primary winding?","options":["50.0%","70.7%","86.6%","100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The teaser transformer operates across the altitude of an equilateral voltage triangle, requiring √3 / 2 = 0.866 (86.6%) of the main transformer turns."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the Delta-Star (Δ-Y) connection universally preferred for step-up power transformers at generating stations?","options":["Delta primary circulates 3rd harmonics while Star secondary provides a grounded neutral and requires less insulation per phase (V_L / √3)","It eliminates the need for cooling fans","It has zero leakage reactance","It doubles the line frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Δ-Y step-up transformers, phase voltage on the HV star side is V_L / √3 (saving insulation) and the neutral can be solidly grounded."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main application of a Zig-Zag (interconnected star) transformer in power distribution systems?","options":["Used as an earthing (grounding) transformer to provide an artificial neutral point and suppress zero-sequence currents","Used as a high-frequency inverter","Used for DC motor speed control","Used as a current-limiting reactor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A zig-zag transformer offers low impedance to zero-sequence fault currents and high impedance to positive/negative sequences, creating a stable grounding neutral."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which condition is strictly mandatory for connecting two transformers in parallel without causing a short circuit?","options":["Equal kVA ratings","Same polarity and same phase sequence","Equal copper losses","Same cooling method"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Identical terminal polarity and phase sequence are mandatory; otherwise, secondary voltages add in the local loop and cause a severe short circuit."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens if two transformers with slightly unequal turns (voltage) ratios are connected in parallel?","options":["A circulating current flows between their windings even at no-load, causing extra heating","They share load equally at all times","Both transformers automatically adjust their turns ratios","No current flows until full load is applied"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The difference in induced secondary EMFs drives a circulating current around the closed secondary loop even under no-load conditions."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For two transformers operating in parallel to share the total load in direct proportion to their kVA ratings, they must have:","options":["Equal ohmic resistances","Equal leakage reactances","Equal percentage (or per-unit) impedances based on their respective ratings","Different turns ratios"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Load sharing is inversely proportional to per-unit impedance; equal %Z ensures each transformer reaches full load simultaneously."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What condition must be satisfied for two parallel-connected transformers to operate at the exact same power factor?","options":["Equal kVA ratings","Equal X/R ratios (same impedance angle)","Equal core losses","Equal oil temperatures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When both transformers have identical X/R ratios, their impedance angles are equal, so their individual currents are in phase with the total load current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two 100 kVA single-phase transformers are connected in parallel to supply a total load of 100 kVA. Transformer A has 4% impedance and Transformer B has 6% impedance. How much load does Transformer A carry?","options":["40 kVA","50 kVA","60 kVA","80 kVA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Load on A = Total Load × [ZB / (ZA + ZB)] = 100 × [6 / (4 + 6)] = 60 kVA (the lower impedance unit takes a larger share)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does an autotransformer transfer electrical power from its primary circuit to its secondary circuit?","options":["Entirely by electromagnetic induction","Partly by electromagnetic induction and partly by direct electrical conduction","Entirely by electrostatic coupling","Entirely by direct conduction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because part of the single winding is shared between primary and secondary, power is transferred both inductively (transformed) and conductively."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to a two-winding transformer of the same rating and transformation ratio K (where K = V2 / V1 < 1), what is the approximate weight of copper saved in an autotransformer?","options":["K times the copper weight of a two-winding transformer","(1 - K) times the copper weight of a two-winding transformer","K^2 times the copper weight","Zero copper is saved"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weight of copper in an autotransformer is (1 - K) times that of a two-winding unit, resulting in a copper saving equal to K times the two-winding copper weight."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 200 V / 100 V step-down autotransformer supplies a load of 10 kVA. How much power is transferred conductively from primary to secondary?","options":["2.5 kVA","5.0 kVA","7.5 kVA","10.0 kVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Here K = V2 / V1 = 100 / 200 = 0.5. Conductively transferred power = K × Load kVA = 0.5 × 10 = 5.0 kVA (and inductively transferred power = (1 - K) × 10 = 5.0 kVA)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the most critical safety disadvantage of an autotransformer compared to a conventional two-winding transformer?","options":["Lower efficiency","Higher voltage drop","Lack of electrical isolation between primary and secondary circuits","Higher no-load current"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because primary and secondary share a common conductive winding, an open circuit in the common section exposes the LV load to full HV supply voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is a standard practical application of autotransformers?","options":["Reduced-voltage starting of 3-phase induction and synchronous motors","Isolating sensitive medical equipment from mains","Generating high-frequency radio waves","Rectifying AC into pure DC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Autotransformer starters step down starting voltage smoothly, and Variacs provide continuously variable AC voltage in laboratories."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which winding are tap changers normally provided in high-voltage power transformers, and why?","options":["LV winding, because voltage is lower","HV winding, because current is smaller (easier to switch) and the HV winding is physically on the outside","Tertiary winding only","Core limbs directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HV windings carry lower current (reducing arcing at tap contacts) and are wound outside the LV winding, giving easy mechanical access to tap leads."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the function of the transition resistor (or reactor) in an On-Load Tap Changer (OLTC)?","options":["To step down the supply frequency","To prevent short-circuiting the tapped winding section while maintaining continuous load current during tap transition","To cool the transformer oil","To filter out 5th harmonics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During OLTC switching, the transition impedance bridges two adjacent taps momentarily without interrupting load current or causing a dead short."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What does the standard transformer cooling designation 'ONAN' stand for?","options":["Oil Natural Air Natural","Oil Natural Air Forced","Oil Forced Air Natural","Oil Forced Air Forced"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In ONAN cooling, internal oil circulates naturally by thermosiphon convection and external air cools the radiator fins naturally."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which cooling method is employed in very large high-rating power station transformers (above 100 MVA)?","options":["AN (Air Natural)","ONAN (Oil Natural Air Natural)","OFAF (Oil Forced Air Forced) or OFWF (Oil Forced Water Forced)","Dry natural convection only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Large power transformers use pumps to force oil circulation and fans or water heat exchangers (OFAF/OFWF) to dissipate high heat loads compactly."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two primary functions of mineral insulating oil inside a power transformer?","options":["Lubricating the core and increasing magnetic flux","Providing electrical insulation and transferring heat from the core and windings to the tank/radiators","Suppressing 3rd harmonics and regulating frequency","Preventing rust on the outer painted tank"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformer oil acts both as a high-dielectric liquid insulator and as a convective heat-transfer medium."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to IS/IEC standards, what is the minimum acceptable 1-minute breakdown dielectric strength of good transformer oil tested with a 2.5 mm electrode gap?","options":["10 kV","30 kV","5 kV","100 kV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In-service transformer oil must withstand at least 30 kV (and new filtered oil > 50–60 kV) across a standard 2.5 mm sphere gap."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which impurity causes the sharpest and most dangerous drop in the dielectric breakdown strength of transformer oil?","options":["Dissolved nitrogen gas","Moisture (water contamination)","Small traces of carbon","Ambient air pressure changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Even minute traces of suspended moisture (e.g., 50–100 ppm) align under electric stress and drastically reduce oil breakdown voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes sludge formation inside oil-immersed transformers over long periods of operation?","options":["High supply frequency","Oxidation of transformer oil at high temperatures in the presence of oxygen and metallic catalysts","Low ambient humidity","Excessive silica gel in the breather"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hot oil reacting with oxygen forms acidic compounds that polymerize into thick sludge, clogging cooling ducts and causing overheating."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why must good transformer insulating oil have a high flash point (typically above 140°C)?","options":["To increase viscosity at low temperatures","To minimize the risk of fire or explosion under overload and fault heating conditions","To improve power factor","To prevent eddy currents in the tank"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flash point above 140°C ensures oil vapors will not ignite easily during elevated operating temperatures."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which non-flammable insulating liquids are used in indoor transformers installed in basements or high-rise buildings where fire safety is critical?","options":["Ordinary kerosene","Silicone fluids or synthetic/natural ester oils","Distilled water","Liquid nitrogen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Synthetic and natural ester fluids and silicone liquids have very high fire points (>300°C) and are biodegradable, replacing hazardous PCBs."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of the conservator drum mounted above the main tank of an oil-immersed transformer?","options":["To store spare cooling fans","To accommodate thermal expansion and contraction of oil while keeping the main tank completely filled and minimizing oil surface exposed to air","To house the high-voltage circuit breaker","To filter harmonics from the secondary voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conservator absorbs volume changes as oil heats and cools, reducing oil oxidation and sludge formation in the main tank."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which moisture-absorbing chemical is filled inside the breather of a power transformer?","options":["Calcium carbide","Silica gel","Sodium chloride","Activated charcoal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Silica gel crystals absorb moisture from air drawn into the conservator as the transformer cools ('breathes in')."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What color change indicates that cobalt-chloride impregnated silica gel in a transformer breather has become saturated with moisture?","options":["Turns from pink to blue","Turns from blue to pink","Turns from white to black","Turns from green to red"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dry active silica gel is deep blue and turns pale pink when saturated with moisture, signaling that it needs reactivation or replacement."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Against which type of faults does a Buchholz relay protect an oil-immersed power transformer?","options":["External line-to-ground transmission faults","Overload on secondary feeders","All internal faults occurring below the oil level (such as turn-to-turn shorts, core heating, and insulation breakdown)","Lightning surges on overhead lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Installed in the pipe between the main tank and conservator, the Buchholz relay detects gas bubbles and oil surges caused by any internal tank fault."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which type of transformers can a Buchholz relay NOT be used?","options":["Oil-immersed transformers rated above 1000 kVA","Step-up generator transformers","Dry-type transformers and small oil transformers without a conservator tank","Three-phase core-type transformers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Buchholz relay requires an oil-filled tank connected via an inclined pipe to a conservator, so it cannot be fitted on dry-type or conservator-less units."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the lower float (or flapper switch) of a Buchholz relay operate?","options":["During a minor incipient fault producing slow gas bubbles","During a severe internal short-circuit causing a violent oil surge toward the conservator, immediately tripping the circuit breaker","When ambient temperature drops below 10°C","During normal daily load cycling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Severe internal arcing vaporizes oil rapidly, creating a high-velocity oil surge that deflects the lower flapper and trips the main circuit breakers."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the upper mercury switch of a Buchholz relay operate?","options":["During a slow incipient fault (such as local core overheating) where rising gas bubbles accumulate at the top of the relay chamber and sound an alarm","During an external phase-to-phase short circuit","When the cooling fans start","When the tap changer moves one step"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gradual gas evolution displaces oil in the upper chamber, lowering the upper float and closing the alarm circuit before a minor fault escalates."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the function of the explosion vent (pressure relief device) mounted on a power transformer tank?","options":["To release excessive internal pressure rapidly during severe internal arcing faults so the main tank does not rupture","To allow air to enter during cooling","To inject fresh oil automatically","To vent normal operating heat continuously"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The thin diaphragm of the explosion vent bursts safely under sudden fault overpressure, preventing catastrophic tank explosion."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are condenser-type bushings used on high-voltage power transformers (66 kV and above)?","options":["To reduce core hysteresis loss","To equalize radial and axial electric field stress across the insulation layers using coaxial conducting foils","To act as a power factor correction bank","To measure secondary load current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capacitive grading foils inside a condenser bushing distribute voltage uniformly, preventing localized dielectric breakdown and flashover."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What physical phenomenon causes the continuous humming noise emitted by an energized power transformer?","options":["Arcing across tap contacts","Boiling of transformer oil","Magnetostriction (cyclic dimensional expansion and contraction of core laminations at twice the supply frequency)","Rotation of cooling fans only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alternating magnetic flux causes silicon steel laminations to elongate and contract twice per AC cycle (100 Hz for 50 Hz mains), producing the characteristic hum."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which fundamental physical principle does a static power transformer operate?","options":["Self-induction","Mutual electromagnetic induction","Electrostatic induction","Thermionic emission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A transformer transfers AC electrical energy from one circuit to another through mutual electromagnetic induction between magnetically coupled windings."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When a transformer steps up the voltage from primary to secondary, what happens to the supply frequency?","options":["Frequency remains unchanged","Frequency increases in proportion to turns ratio","Frequency decreases in proportion to turns ratio","Frequency doubles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A transformer is a constant-frequency device; the secondary induced EMF has the exact same frequency as the primary supply."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which magnetic material is universally preferred for constructing the core of modern power transformers?","options":["Cast iron","Solid mild steel","Cold Rolled Grain Oriented (CRGO) silicon steel","Hardened carbon steel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CRGO silicon steel offers high magnetic permeability along the rolling direction and very low hysteresis loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical percentage of silicon added to steel laminations in a transformer core to increase resistivity and reduce eddy current loss?","options":["0.5% to 1%","3% to 4%","10% to 12%","20% to 25%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding about 3% to 4% silicon increases electrical resistivity (lowering eddy current loss) without making the steel excessively brittle."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a transformer core constructed from thin insulated laminations rather than a solid iron block?","options":["To reduce eddy current losses","To reduce hysteresis losses","To increase magnetizing current","To increase mechanical weight"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Thin insulated laminations (0.35 mm to 0.5 mm) break circulating eddy current paths, reducing eddy current power loss (which is proportional to thickness squared)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the correct RMS induced EMF equation of a transformer winding having N turns, maximum core flux density Bm, net core area A, and supply frequency f?","options":["E = 4.00 f N Bm A","E = 4.44 f N Bm A","E = 2.22 f N Bm A","E = 1.414 f N Bm A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a sinusoidal flux wave, RMS induced EMF E = 4.44 f N Φm = 4.44 f N Bm A, where 4.44 = √2 × π."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The numerical constant 4.44 appearing in the transformer EMF equation is derived from:","options":["2 × form factor (2 × 1.11)","4 × form factor (4 × 1.11)","√2 × form factor","π × peak factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Average EMF per turn is 4 f Φm, and multiplying by the sinusoidal form factor (1.11) yields RMS EMF = 4.44 f N Φm."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"The primary winding of a 50 Hz single-phase transformer has 500 turns and carries a maximum sinusoidal core flux of 0.02 Wb. What is the RMS voltage induced in the primary?","options":["1110 V","1570 V","2000 V","2220 V"],"answer":3,"explanation":"E1 = 4.44 × f × N1 × Φm = 4.44 × 50 × 500 × 0.02 = 2220 V."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are stepped (cruciform) core cross-sections used in large power transformers?","options":["To increase core reluctance","To utilize circular coils efficiently, reducing the mean length of turn and saving copper","To eliminate the need for oil cooling","To increase leakage flux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A stepped core approximates a circle inside circular windings, maximizing iron area per unit circumference and reducing winding copper length."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does a shell-type transformer differ structurally from a core-type transformer?","options":["In a shell-type transformer, the iron core surrounds the windings and provides two parallel magnetic paths","In a shell-type transformer, the windings surround a single rectangular core","Shell-type transformers have no iron core","Shell-type transformers operate only on DC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a shell-type design, both HV and LV windings are placed on the central limb with outer limbs completing two parallel magnetic return paths."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which set of characteristics defines an ideal transformer?","options":["Zero winding resistance, zero core permeability, and infinite core loss","Infinite winding resistance and zero mutual flux","Zero winding resistance, infinite core permeability (zero reluctance), and zero core loss","Unity turns ratio and 50% efficiency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An ideal transformer has lossless windings, zero magnetic leakage, infinitely permeable core requiring zero magnetizing MMF, and 100% efficiency."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the typical magnitude of the no-load current (I0) in a standard power transformer?","options":["0.1% to 0.5% of rated full-load current","2% to 5% of rated full-load current","15% to 20% of rated full-load current","40% to 50% of rated full-load current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because of the high permeability of CRGO steel cores, the exciting no-load current is small, typically 2% to 5% of full-load current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under no-load conditions, the primary current of a transformer lags behind the applied primary voltage by approximately:","options":["0° to 15°","30° to 45°","50° to 60°","75° to 85°"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The no-load current is predominantly reactive magnetizing current, resulting in a very low lagging power factor (0.15 to 0.25) and phase lag of 75° to 85°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two orthogonal components of the no-load current (I0) in a transformer?","options":["Magnetizing component (Im) in quadrature with voltage and core-loss component (Iw) in phase with voltage","Active component in quadrature and reactive component in phase","Load component and harmonic component","Eddy component and leakage component"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Iw = I0 cos(φ0) supplies core losses in phase with applied voltage, while Im = I0 sin(φ0) produces mutual flux lagging voltage by 90°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A single-phase transformer draws a no-load current of 5 A at a power factor of 0.2 lagging. What is the core-loss (working) component of the no-load current?","options":["0.5 A","1.0 A","2.5 A","4.9 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core-loss component Iw = I0 × cos(φ0) = 5 × 0.2 = 1.0 A."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are power and distribution transformers rated in kVA (or MVA) rather than kW?","options":["Transformers only supply purely reactive loads","Copper loss depends on current and core loss depends on voltage, making total loss independent of load power factor","kW ratings are reserved exclusively for DC machines","Frequency varies continuously with load power factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total transformer losses depend on RMS voltage and RMS current regardless of the phase angle between them, so thermal rating is governed by apparent power (kVA)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a transformer often referred to as a 'constant flux' machine?","options":["Because its core is made of permanent magnets","Because mutual core flux remains practically constant from no-load to full-load at constant supply voltage and frequency","Because load current never changes","Because leakage flux is zero at all loads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since primary impedance drop is small, the counter EMF E1 remains nearly equal to applied V1, keeping mutual flux Φm almost constant across all loads."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does magnetic leakage flux in a transformer behave as load increases from no-load to full-load?","options":["It remains strictly constant","It increases almost linearly with load current and causes leakage reactance voltage drop","It decreases to zero at full load","It reverses direction at unity power factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leakage flux links only one winding through air/oil paths and is directly proportional to winding currents, represented by series leakage reactances."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are interleaved (sandwich) windings used in shell-type transformers?","options":["To increase core losses","To increase magnetizing current","To minimize magnetic leakage flux and leakage reactance","To eliminate the need for insulation between HV and LV coils"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Subdividing HV and LV coils into alternating pancake sections reduces the spatial separation between ampere-turns, lowering leakage reactance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In concentric cylindrical windings of a core-type transformer, why is the Low-Voltage (LV) winding placed nearest to the iron core?","options":["To reduce the amount of high-voltage insulation required between the winding and the earthed core","Because LV conductors are thinner than HV conductors","To increase the core reluctance","To prevent eddy currents in the core"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Placing the LV winding next to the grounded core requires only one layer of high-voltage insulation between the LV and outer HV winding."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes the magnetizing current of a transformer to be non-sinusoidal even when the applied voltage is purely sinusoidal?","options":["Winding resistance variation","Non-linear B-H saturation characteristic of the ferromagnetic core","Capacitive coupling between bushings","Cooling oil viscosity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core saturation flattens the flux wave relative to current, forcing the magnetizing current to become peaky with a strong 3rd harmonic component."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens in a Star-Star (Y-Y) connected 3-phase transformer bank if the primary neutral is isolated from the supply neutral?","options":["Third harmonic magnetizing currents cannot flow, causing phase voltages to distort and neutral potential to oscillate","Efficiency reaches 100%","Secondary line voltage doubles","Short-circuit current drops to zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without a closed path for zero-sequence 3rd harmonic currents, core flux becomes flat-topped, inducing 3rd harmonic voltages in each phase."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary purpose of providing a delta-connected tertiary winding in a Star-Star power transformer?","options":["To step up the line frequency to 150 Hz","To provide a circulating path for third-harmonic currents, stabilize neutral potential, and supply auxiliary station loads","To reduce the weight of the transformer tank","To eliminate the need for a Buchholz relay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The closed delta tertiary traps 3rd harmonic currents internally, ensuring sinusoidal phase voltages and providing an auxiliary low-voltage supply."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the worst-case magnetizing inrush current occur when energizing an unloaded transformer?","options":["When the switch closes at the peak of the supply voltage wave","When the switch closes at the zero crossing of the supply voltage wave","When the load is purely capacitive","When the secondary is short-circuited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Switching at voltage zero forces core flux to rise toward 2 Φm (plus any residual flux), driving the core deep into saturation and drawing 8–10 times rated current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which harmonic component is characteristically prominent in transformer magnetizing inrush current and is used to restrain differential relays from false tripping?","options":["2nd harmonic","3rd harmonic","5th harmonic","7th harmonic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because inrush current is heavily asymmetrical (unidirectional pulses), it contains a large 2nd harmonic component used by harmonic-restraint differential relays."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If K = N2 / N1 is the transformation ratio of a transformer, how is a secondary impedance Z2 referred to the primary side?","options":["Z2' = Z2 × K","Z2' = Z2 × K^2","Z2' = Z2 / K^2","Z2' = Z2 / K"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To preserve identical power loss and voltage drop ratios, secondary impedance referred to primary is Z2' = Z2 × (N1 / N2)^2 = Z2 / K^2."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 1000 V / 200 V single-phase transformer has a secondary winding resistance of 0.04 Ω. What is its secondary resistance referred to the primary side?","options":["0.20 Ω","1.00 Ω","0.0016 Ω","2.00 Ω"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Turns ratio a = N1 / N2 = 1000 / 200 = 5. Referred resistance R2' = R2 × a^2 = 0.04 × 25 = 1.00 Ω."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the per-unit (pu) equivalent impedance of a transformer calculated from the HV side compare with that calculated from the LV side?","options":["Both are identically equal","HV per-unit impedance is larger by the turns ratio","LV per-unit impedance is larger by the square of turns ratio","They differ by 30°"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A major advantage of the per-unit system is that per-unit resistance, reactance, and impedance have the exact same value on both sides of a transformer."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What will happen if a transformer designed for 230 V AC is accidentally connected to a 230 V DC supply?","options":["It will step down the DC voltage normally","The primary winding will draw excessive current and burn out due to zero frequency and absence of inductive reactance","It will operate with higher efficiency","The core will demagnetize permanently without damage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For DC (f = 0), inductive reactance and counter EMF are zero; current is limited only by the tiny primary resistance, rapidly overheating and burning the winding."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If the supply frequency to a transformer is increased while keeping the applied RMS voltage constant, what happens to the core losses?","options":["Core losses increase sharply","Core losses decrease because maximum core flux density decreases","Hysteresis loss doubles while eddy current loss remains constant","Core losses remain completely unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since Bm ∝ V / f, increasing frequency at constant voltage reduces Bm; hysteresis loss (∝ f Bm^1.6 ∝ f^-0.6) decreases while eddy loss (∝ f^2 Bm^2) stays constant, lowering total core loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to Steinmetz's formula, hysteresis loss in a transformer core is proportional to:","options":["f × Bm^1.6","f^2 × Bm^2","f × Bm^2","f^2 × Bm^1.6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hysteresis loss Ph = η × Bm^1.6 × f × Volume, varying linearly with frequency and as the 1.6th power of peak flux density."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Eddy current loss in a transformer core of lamination thickness t is proportional to:","options":["f × Bm × t","f^2 × Bm^2 × t","f^2 × Bm^2 × t^2","f × Bm^2 × t^2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Eddy current loss Pe = Ke × Bm^2 × f^2 × t^2 × Volume, varying with the square of frequency, peak flux density, and lamination thickness."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"At 50 Hz and rated flux density, a transformer has a hysteresis loss of 600 W and an eddy current loss of 400 W. If operated at 25 Hz with the same flux density, what is the total core loss?","options":["250 W","400 W","500 W","700 W"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At constant Bm, Ph ∝ f (so Ph = 600 × 25/50 = 300 W) and Pe ∝ f^2 (so Pe = 400 × (25/50)^2 = 100 W). Total core loss = 300 + 100 = 400 W."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which power losses in a transformer are classified as 'constant losses' independent of load current?","options":["Core (iron) losses","Primary and secondary copper losses","Stray load losses in conductors","Terminal contact losses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because mutual core flux remains nearly constant from no-load to full-load, core losses (hysteresis + eddy current) remain virtually constant."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which losses in a transformer vary as the square of the load current?","options":["Hysteresis losses","Eddy current losses","Dielectric losses in oil","Winding copper (I^2 R) losses"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ohmic losses in the primary and secondary windings equal I1^2 R1 + I2^2 R2, scaling with the square of the load fraction."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the mathematical condition for a transformer to operate at its maximum efficiency for a given load power factor?","options":["Copper loss = 2 × Core loss","Copper loss = Core (iron) loss","Eddy current loss = Hysteresis loss","Copper loss = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Differentiating efficiency with respect to load current shows that maximum efficiency occurs when variable copper loss equals constant iron loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If Pi is the constant iron loss and Pcu,fl is the full-load copper loss, at what fraction (x) of full-load kVA does maximum efficiency occur?","options":["x = Pi / Pcu,fl","x = Pcu,fl / Pi","x = √(Pi / Pcu,fl)","x = √(Pcu,fl / Pi)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At fractional load x, x^2 × Pcu,fl = Pi, which gives x = √(Pi / Pcu,fl)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 100 kVA transformer has an iron loss of 1 kW and a full-load copper loss of 4 kW. At what kVA load will it achieve maximum efficiency?","options":["25 kVA","50 kVA","75 kVA","100 kVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Load at maximum efficiency = Rated kVA × √(Pi / Pcu,fl) = 100 × √(1 / 4) = 100 × 0.5 = 50 kVA."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For a fixed kVA load on a transformer, maximum efficiency is obtained when the load power factor is:","options":["Unity (1.0)","0.8 lagging","0.8 leading","0.5 lagging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Efficiency η = (kVA cos φ) / (kVA cos φ + Pi + Pcu); for fixed kVA and losses, output power and efficiency peak at cos φ = 1."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Distribution transformers are typically designed so that maximum efficiency occurs around:","options":["10% of full load","50% of full load","100% of full load","125% of full load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distribution transformers remain energized 24 hours a day under lightly loaded conditions, so core loss is kept low to peak efficiency near 50% load."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the 'all-day efficiency' of a distribution transformer defined?","options":["Ratio of peak kW output to peak kW input","Ratio of total energy output (in kWh) to total energy input (in kWh) over 24 hours","Ratio of core loss to copper loss over 24 hours","Average of no-load and full-load commercial efficiencies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All-day efficiency evaluates energy performance over a 24-hour load cycle: η_all-day = (kWh output in 24 h) / (kWh input in 24 h)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the all-day efficiency of a distribution transformer always lower than its ordinary commercial efficiency?","options":["Because core losses occur continuously for all 24 hours even when the transformer is lightly loaded or unloaded","Because copper losses occur only during peak hours","Because frequency drops at night","Because oil viscosity increases during the day"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even during periods of zero load, the primary remains energized and consumes constant iron loss continuously over the 24-hour period."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the percentage voltage regulation of a transformer defined?","options":["[(V_NL - V_FL) / V_FL] × 100","[(V_FL - V_NL) / V_NL] × 100","(V_FL / V_NL) × 100","[(V_NL - V_FL) / V_NL] × 50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Percentage voltage regulation is the change in secondary terminal voltage from no-load to full-load expressed as a percentage of rated full-load voltage (or no-load voltage by IEC)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the approximate formula for percentage voltage regulation of a transformer having percentage resistance %R and percentage reactance %X at power factor angle φ?","options":["%R sin φ ± %X cos φ","%R cos φ ± %X sin φ","%R cos φ × %X sin φ","√(%R^2 + %X^2) cos φ"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Approximate percentage regulation = %R cos φ + %X sin φ for lagging power factor, and %R cos φ - %X sin φ for leading power factor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under what load condition can a transformer exhibit zero voltage regulation?","options":["Purely resistive load","Inductive lagging power factor load","Short-circuit condition","Capacitive leading power factor load where tan φ = Req / Xeq"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For a leading load, %R cos φ - %X sin φ = 0 when tan φ = %R / %X = Req / Xeq, yielding zero voltage drop."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does a transformer experience negative voltage regulation (secondary terminal voltage rising above no-load voltage)?","options":["When supplying a sufficiently leading (capacitive) power factor load","When supplying a heavy inductive motor load","At no-load","When core loss exceeds copper loss"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a leading power factor where %X sin φ > %R cos φ, the capacitive voltage rise exceeds resistive drop, making terminal voltage higher than no-load EMF."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"At what power factor angle does maximum positive voltage regulation occur in a transformer?","options":["Unity power factor (φ = 0°)","Zero power factor lagging (φ = 90°)","Lagging power factor where tan φ = Xeq / Req","Leading power factor where tan φ = Xeq / Req"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Differentiating %R cos φ + %X sin φ with respect to φ gives maximum regulation at lagging angle φ = arctan(Xeq / Req)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A transformer has a percentage resistance of 2% and a percentage reactance of 4%. What is its approximate voltage regulation when supplying full load at 0.8 power factor lagging?","options":["2.4%","4.0%","4.8%","0.8%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For cos φ = 0.8 lagging, sin φ = 0.6. Regulation = (2% × 0.8) + (4% × 0.6) = 1.6% + 2.4% = 4.0%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A transformer has a percentage resistance of 2% and a percentage reactance of 4%. What is its approximate voltage regulation when supplying full load at 0.8 power factor leading?","options":["-0.8%","+0.8%","+4.0%","-2.4%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For leading power factor, Regulation = %R cos φ - %X sin φ = (2% × 0.8) - (4% × 0.6) = 1.6% - 2.4% = -0.8%."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the trade-off associated with designing a power transformer with a very low percentage impedance (%Z)?","options":["Poor voltage regulation and low fault current","Good voltage regulation and high fault current","Better voltage regulation, but higher short-circuit fault current","Higher core loss and lower efficiency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Low leakage impedance reduces load voltage drop (good regulation) but limits fault current less during external short circuits (Isc = 100 / %Z pu)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What parameters are determined by conducting an Open-Circuit (OC) test on a transformer?","options":["Full-load copper loss and series leakage impedance","Core (iron) loss and shunt magnetizing branch parameters (R0, X0)","Percentage voltage regulation directly","Temperature rise under full load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The OC test applies rated voltage at no-load to measure core losses and exciting branch resistance and reactance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which winding is the Open-Circuit (OC) test normally performed in a laboratory?","options":["Low-Voltage (LV) side with High-Voltage (HV) side kept open","High-Voltage (HV) side with Low-Voltage (LV) side kept open","Both windings simultaneously","Tertiary winding only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Performing the OC test on the LV side requires safer, easily available lower supply voltage and standard laboratory meters."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why does the wattmeter reading during an Open-Circuit test represent almost purely the core loss of the transformer?","options":["Because applied voltage is zero","Because the frequency is doubled","Because no-load current is tiny (2–5% of rated), making no-load I^2 R copper loss negligible","Because the primary winding has zero resistance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since I0 is roughly 1/20th of rated current, I0^2 R1 is about 1/400th of full-load copper loss and can be safely neglected."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What parameters are obtained from the Short-Circuit (SC) test on a transformer?","options":["Magnetizing reactance and core loss","Full-load copper loss and equivalent series resistance and leakage reactance","Hysteresis loop area","Dielectric strength of transformer oil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SC test circulates rated current at reduced voltage to measure full-load I^2 R losses and equivalent series impedance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which side of the transformer is usually excited during a Short-Circuit (SC) test, and why?","options":["LV side, because voltage is lower","HV side (with LV shorted), because rated HV current is smaller and easier to supply and measure","LV side, because core loss is maximum","HV side, because frequency is higher"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rated current on the HV side is much lower than on the LV side, and the required reduced voltage (5–10% of HV rating) is convenient to regulate."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are core losses negligible during a Short-Circuit (SC) test on a transformer?","options":["Because the core is removed during the test","Because the applied voltage is only 5% to 10% of rated voltage, producing very small core flux","Because the circuit is purely capacitive","Because the secondary is open-circuited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Core loss is proportional to Bm^2 (and hence V^2); at 5% rated voltage, core loss is only (0.05)^2 = 0.25% of normal core loss."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"During a short-circuit test on a transformer, if the current circulated is half of the rated full-load current, the wattmeter reads 250 W. What is the full-load copper loss?","options":["500 W","750 W","1000 W","2000 W"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Copper loss is proportional to I^2. At 0.5 rated current, Pcu = (0.5)^2 × Pcu,fl = 0.25 Pcu,fl = 250 W, so Pcu,fl = 250 / 0.25 = 1000 W."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main advantage of Sumpner's (back-to-back) test over separate OC and SC tests?","options":["It requires only one transformer","It simulates full-load thermal and loss conditions simultaneously while drawing only total losses from the supply","It measures only core losses","It requires no wattmeters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sumpner's test uses two identical transformers connected in opposition to determine efficiency, regulation, and full-load temperature rise without wasting full load power."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a polarity test performed on single-phase transformers?","options":["To measure core loss","To identify relative instantaneous terminal polarities before connecting transformers in parallel or in 3-phase banks","To measure oil acidity","To check insulation resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incorrect terminal polarity during parallel or 3-phase bank connection causes a dead short-circuit across the windings."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During a polarity test, if a voltmeter connected between adjacent HV and LV terminals reads the difference between the HV and LV voltages (V_HV - V_LV), the transformer has:","options":["Additive polarity","Zero polarity","Subtractive polarity","Reversed phase sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When adjacent terminals have the same instantaneous polarity, their voltages oppose in the test loop, reading V_HV - V_LV (subtractive polarity)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is a major advantage of a single 3-phase transformer unit compared to a bank of three single-phase transformers of the same total rating?","options":["It is easier to repair on site if one phase fails","It costs about 15% less, occupies less floor space, and has slightly higher efficiency","It requires no cooling oil","It eliminates all harmonics automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A unified 3-phase core uses less iron and copper, making it lighter, cheaper, more compact, and more efficient than three separate units."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In 3-phase transformer vector group notation, what does 'Dy11' signify?","options":["Delta HV, Star LV, with LV line voltage leading HV line voltage by 30° (or lagging by 330°)","Star HV, Delta LV, with zero phase shift","Delta HV, Delta LV, with 180° phase shift","Star HV, Star LV, with 30° lag"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capital 'D' denotes Delta HV, lowercase 'y' denotes Star LV, and clock position '11' means LV leads HV by +30° (-330°)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the phase displacement between LV and HV line voltages in a 'Yd1' three-phase transformer?","options":["0°","LV leads HV by 30°","LV lags HV by 30°","LV lags HV by 180°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Clock position '1' corresponds to -30°, meaning the secondary LV line voltage lags the primary HV line voltage by 30°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which 3-phase transformer vector groups provide zero phase displacement between primary and secondary line voltages?","options":["Yd1 and Dy1","Yy0 and Dd0","Yd11 and Dy11","Dz6 and Yz6"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clock number '0' (in Yy0 and Dd0) indicates 0° phase shift between corresponding HV and LV line voltages."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"If one transformer in a 3-phase Delta-Delta (Δ-Δ) bank is removed for maintenance, the remaining two transformers can continue operating in Open-Delta (V-V) connection at what percentage of the original bank kVA capacity?","options":["33.3%","50.0%","66.7%","57.7%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An open-delta bank supplies 1 / √3 ≈ 57.7% of the original closed-delta 3-phase kVA rating."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the utilization factor (ratio of available 3-phase output to the sum of individual kVA ratings of the two transformers) in an Open-Delta (V-V) connection?","options":["0.577","0.866","0.707","1.000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two transformers each rated S have combined rating 2S, but deliver √3 S in V-V connection, giving a utilization factor of √3 / 2 = 0.866 (86.6%)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary application of the Scott (T-T) transformer connection?","options":["Converting 3-phase AC supply into 2-phase AC supply (or vice versa)","Converting AC into DC","Stepping up frequency from 50 Hz to 100 Hz","Measuring high DC currents"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Scott connection uses a main center-tapped transformer and a teaser transformer (86.6% turns) to convert between 3-phase and 2-phase systems."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Scott-connected transformer bank, what percentage of full turns are utilized in the teaser transformer primary winding?","options":["50.0%","70.7%","86.6%","100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The teaser transformer operates across the altitude of an equilateral voltage triangle, requiring √3 / 2 = 0.866 (86.6%) of the main transformer turns."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the Delta-Star (Δ-Y) connection universally preferred for step-up power transformers at generating stations?","options":["Delta primary circulates 3rd harmonics while Star secondary provides a grounded neutral and requires less insulation per phase (V_L / √3)","It eliminates the need for cooling fans","It has zero leakage reactance","It doubles the line frequency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Δ-Y step-up transformers, phase voltage on the HV star side is V_L / √3 (saving insulation) and the neutral can be solidly grounded."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the main application of a Zig-Zag (interconnected star) transformer in power distribution systems?","options":["Used as an earthing (grounding) transformer to provide an artificial neutral point and suppress zero-sequence currents","Used as a high-frequency inverter","Used for DC motor speed control","Used as a current-limiting reactor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A zig-zag transformer offers low impedance to zero-sequence fault currents and high impedance to positive/negative sequences, creating a stable grounding neutral."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which condition is strictly mandatory for connecting two transformers in parallel without causing a short circuit?","options":["Equal kVA ratings","Same polarity and same phase sequence","Equal copper losses","Same cooling method"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Identical terminal polarity and phase sequence are mandatory; otherwise, secondary voltages add in the local loop and cause a severe short circuit."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens if two transformers with slightly unequal turns (voltage) ratios are connected in parallel?","options":["A circulating current flows between their windings even at no-load, causing extra heating","They share load equally at all times","Both transformers automatically adjust their turns ratios","No current flows until full load is applied"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The difference in induced secondary EMFs drives a circulating current around the closed secondary loop even under no-load conditions."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For two transformers operating in parallel to share the total load in direct proportion to their kVA ratings, they must have:","options":["Equal ohmic resistances","Equal leakage reactances","Equal percentage (or per-unit) impedances based on their respective ratings","Different turns ratios"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Load sharing is inversely proportional to per-unit impedance; equal %Z ensures each transformer reaches full load simultaneously."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What condition must be satisfied for two parallel-connected transformers to operate at the exact same power factor?","options":["Equal kVA ratings","Equal X/R ratios (same impedance angle)","Equal core losses","Equal oil temperatures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When both transformers have identical X/R ratios, their impedance angles are equal, so their individual currents are in phase with the total load current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two 100 kVA single-phase transformers are connected in parallel to supply a total load of 100 kVA. Transformer A has 4% impedance and Transformer B has 6% impedance. How much load does Transformer A carry?","options":["40 kVA","50 kVA","60 kVA","80 kVA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Load on A = Total Load × [ZB / (ZA + ZB)] = 100 × [6 / (4 + 6)] = 60 kVA (the lower impedance unit takes a larger share)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does an autotransformer transfer electrical power from its primary circuit to its secondary circuit?","options":["Entirely by electromagnetic induction","Partly by electromagnetic induction and partly by direct electrical conduction","Entirely by electrostatic coupling","Entirely by direct conduction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because part of the single winding is shared between primary and secondary, power is transferred both inductively (transformed) and conductively."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to a two-winding transformer of the same rating and transformation ratio K (where K = V2 / V1 < 1), what is the approximate weight of copper saved in an autotransformer?","options":["K times the copper weight of a two-winding transformer","(1 - K) times the copper weight of a two-winding transformer","K^2 times the copper weight","Zero copper is saved"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weight of copper in an autotransformer is (1 - K) times that of a two-winding unit, resulting in a copper saving equal to K times the two-winding copper weight."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 200 V / 100 V step-down autotransformer supplies a load of 10 kVA. How much power is transferred conductively from primary to secondary?","options":["2.5 kVA","5.0 kVA","7.5 kVA","10.0 kVA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Here K = V2 / V1 = 100 / 200 = 0.5. Conductively transferred power = K × Load kVA = 0.5 × 10 = 5.0 kVA (and inductively transferred power = (1 - K) × 10 = 5.0 kVA)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the most critical safety disadvantage of an autotransformer compared to a conventional two-winding transformer?","options":["Lower efficiency","Higher voltage drop","Lack of electrical isolation between primary and secondary circuits","Higher no-load current"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because primary and secondary share a common conductive winding, an open circuit in the common section exposes the LV load to full HV supply voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which of the following is a standard practical application of autotransformers?","options":["Reduced-voltage starting of 3-phase induction and synchronous motors","Isolating sensitive medical equipment from mains","Generating high-frequency radio waves","Rectifying AC into pure DC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Autotransformer starters step down starting voltage smoothly, and Variacs provide continuously variable AC voltage in laboratories."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which winding are tap changers normally provided in high-voltage power transformers, and why?","options":["LV winding, because voltage is lower","HV winding, because current is smaller (easier to switch) and the HV winding is physically on the outside","Tertiary winding only","Core limbs directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HV windings carry lower current (reducing arcing at tap contacts) and are wound outside the LV winding, giving easy mechanical access to tap leads."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the function of the transition resistor (or reactor) in an On-Load Tap Changer (OLTC)?","options":["To step down the supply frequency","To prevent short-circuiting the tapped winding section while maintaining continuous load current during tap transition","To cool the transformer oil","To filter out 5th harmonics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During OLTC switching, the transition impedance bridges two adjacent taps momentarily without interrupting load current or causing a dead short."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What does the standard transformer cooling designation 'ONAN' stand for?","options":["Oil Natural Air Natural","Oil Natural Air Forced","Oil Forced Air Natural","Oil Forced Air Forced"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In ONAN cooling, internal oil circulates naturally by thermosiphon convection and external air cools the radiator fins naturally."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which cooling method is employed in very large high-rating power station transformers (above 100 MVA)?","options":["AN (Air Natural)","ONAN (Oil Natural Air Natural)","OFAF (Oil Forced Air Forced) or OFWF (Oil Forced Water Forced)","Dry natural convection only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Large power transformers use pumps to force oil circulation and fans or water heat exchangers (OFAF/OFWF) to dissipate high heat loads compactly."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two primary functions of mineral insulating oil inside a power transformer?","options":["Lubricating the core and increasing magnetic flux","Providing electrical insulation and transferring heat from the core and windings to the tank/radiators","Suppressing 3rd harmonics and regulating frequency","Preventing rust on the outer painted tank"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformer oil acts both as a high-dielectric liquid insulator and as a convective heat-transfer medium."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"According to IS/IEC standards, what is the minimum acceptable 1-minute breakdown dielectric strength of good transformer oil tested with a 2.5 mm electrode gap?","options":["10 kV","30 kV","5 kV","100 kV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In-service transformer oil must withstand at least 30 kV (and new filtered oil > 50–60 kV) across a standard 2.5 mm sphere gap."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which impurity causes the sharpest and most dangerous drop in the dielectric breakdown strength of transformer oil?","options":["Dissolved nitrogen gas","Moisture (water contamination)","Small traces of carbon","Ambient air pressure changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Even minute traces of suspended moisture (e.g., 50–100 ppm) align under electric stress and drastically reduce oil breakdown voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What causes sludge formation inside oil-immersed transformers over long periods of operation?","options":["High supply frequency","Oxidation of transformer oil at high temperatures in the presence of oxygen and metallic catalysts","Low ambient humidity","Excessive silica gel in the breather"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hot oil reacting with oxygen forms acidic compounds that polymerize into thick sludge, clogging cooling ducts and causing overheating."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why must good transformer insulating oil have a high flash point (typically above 140°C)?","options":["To increase viscosity at low temperatures","To minimize the risk of fire or explosion under overload and fault heating conditions","To improve power factor","To prevent eddy currents in the tank"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flash point above 140°C ensures oil vapors will not ignite easily during elevated operating temperatures."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which non-flammable insulating liquids are used in indoor transformers installed in basements or high-rise buildings where fire safety is critical?","options":["Ordinary kerosene","Silicone fluids or synthetic/natural ester oils","Distilled water","Liquid nitrogen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Synthetic and natural ester fluids and silicone liquids have very high fire points (>300°C) and are biodegradable, replacing hazardous PCBs."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the purpose of the conservator drum mounted above the main tank of an oil-immersed transformer?","options":["To store spare cooling fans","To accommodate thermal expansion and contraction of oil while keeping the main tank completely filled and minimizing oil surface exposed to air","To house the high-voltage circuit breaker","To filter harmonics from the secondary voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conservator absorbs volume changes as oil heats and cools, reducing oil oxidation and sludge formation in the main tank."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which moisture-absorbing chemical is filled inside the breather of a power transformer?","options":["Calcium carbide","Silica gel","Sodium chloride","Activated charcoal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Silica gel crystals absorb moisture from air drawn into the conservator as the transformer cools ('breathes in')."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What color change indicates that cobalt-chloride impregnated silica gel in a transformer breather has become saturated with moisture?","options":["Turns from pink to blue","Turns from blue to pink","Turns from white to black","Turns from green to red"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dry active silica gel is deep blue and turns pale pink when saturated with moisture, signaling that it needs reactivation or replacement."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Against which type of faults does a Buchholz relay protect an oil-immersed power transformer?","options":["External line-to-ground transmission faults","Overload on secondary feeders","All internal faults occurring below the oil level (such as turn-to-turn shorts, core heating, and insulation breakdown)","Lightning surges on overhead lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Installed in the pipe between the main tank and conservator, the Buchholz relay detects gas bubbles and oil surges caused by any internal tank fault."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On which type of transformers can a Buchholz relay NOT be used?","options":["Oil-immersed transformers rated above 1000 kVA","Step-up generator transformers","Dry-type transformers and small oil transformers without a conservator tank","Three-phase core-type transformers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Buchholz relay requires an oil-filled tank connected via an inclined pipe to a conservator, so it cannot be fitted on dry-type or conservator-less units."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the lower float (or flapper switch) of a Buchholz relay operate?","options":["During a minor incipient fault producing slow gas bubbles","During a severe internal short-circuit causing a violent oil surge toward the conservator, immediately tripping the circuit breaker","When ambient temperature drops below 10°C","During normal daily load cycling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Severe internal arcing vaporizes oil rapidly, creating a high-velocity oil surge that deflects the lower flapper and trips the main circuit breakers."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"When does the upper mercury switch of a Buchholz relay operate?","options":["During a slow incipient fault (such as local core overheating) where rising gas bubbles accumulate at the top of the relay chamber and sound an alarm","During an external phase-to-phase short circuit","When the cooling fans start","When the tap changer moves one step"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gradual gas evolution displaces oil in the upper chamber, lowering the upper float and closing the alarm circuit before a minor fault escalates."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the function of the explosion vent (pressure relief device) mounted on a power transformer tank?","options":["To release excessive internal pressure rapidly during severe internal arcing faults so the main tank does not rupture","To allow air to enter during cooling","To inject fresh oil automatically","To vent normal operating heat continuously"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The thin diaphragm of the explosion vent bursts safely under sudden fault overpressure, preventing catastrophic tank explosion."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are condenser-type bushings used on high-voltage power transformers (66 kV and above)?","options":["To reduce core hysteresis loss","To equalize radial and axial electric field stress across the insulation layers using coaxial conducting foils","To act as a power factor correction bank","To measure secondary load current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capacitive grading foils inside a condenser bushing distribute voltage uniformly, preventing localized dielectric breakdown and flashover."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What physical phenomenon causes the continuous humming noise emitted by an energized power transformer?","options":["Arcing across tap contacts","Boiling of transformer oil","Magnetostriction (cyclic dimensional expansion and contraction of core laminations at twice the supply frequency)","Rotation of cooling fans only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alternating magnetic flux causes silicon steel laminations to elongate and contract twice per AC cycle (100 Hz for 50 Hz mains), producing the characteristic hum."},{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the brushes of a DC generator are shifted slightly in the direction of rotation to improve commutation, what is the resulting effect on the main air-gap flux?","options":["Armature reaction becomes purely magnetizing and increases terminal voltage","Armature reaction develops a demagnetizing component that weakens the main field flux","Cross-magnetizing effect completely disappears","Eddy current loss in the yoke drops to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shifting brushes forward in a DC generator introduces a demagnetizing MMF component directly opposing the main pole flux, slightly reducing induced EMF."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a DC shunt motor, if the brushes are shifted backward (against the direction of rotation) for sparkless commutation, how does the motor speed behave?","options":["Speed decreases because main flux increases","Speed remains completely unaffected","Speed increases slightly due to the demagnetizing effect of armature reaction weakening the main flux","The motor reverses its direction of rotation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Backward brush shift in a DC motor creates a demagnetizing MMF that reduces net flux per pole; since speed N ∝ Eb / Φ, the motor speeds up slightly."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What must be the magnetic polarity of an interpole (commutating pole) in a DC generator compared to the main poles?","options":["Same polarity as the next main pole ahead in the direction of rotation","Opposite polarity to the next main pole ahead in the direction of rotation","Always North polarity regardless of rotation","Same polarity as the trailing main pole behind it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a DC generator, an interpole must induce a reversing EMF in the commutating coil, requiring the same polarity as the next main pole ahead."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why are compensating windings installed in the pole faces of large DC machines in addition to interpoles?","options":["To eliminate the need for a starter","To neutralize armature reaction directly under the pole shoes and prevent commutator flashover during sudden heavy load fluctuations","To increase no-load speed","To reduce hysteresis loss in the armature core"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interpoles neutralize reactance voltage only in the interpolar zone, whereas compensating windings cancel cross-magnetizing flux distortion right under the main pole faces."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What dangerous behavior occurs if a differential compound DC motor is subjected to a sudden heavy mechanical overload?","options":["It locks smoothly at synchronous speed","Its efficiency increases to 98%","Strong series-field MMF can neutralize or overpower the shunt-field MMF, causing unstable runaway or sudden reversal of rotation","Its armature current drops to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a differential compound motor, series flux opposes shunt flux; under heavy overload, net flux collapses toward zero or reverses, causing instability or reversal."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the shunt field circuit of an unloaded DC shunt motor accidentally opens while running, what will happen?","options":["The motor stops immediately","Flux drops to a tiny residual value, causing the motor to accelerate to a dangerously high runaway speed","The motor continues running at its rated base speed","The armature current drops to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With field current zero, only tiny residual magnetism remains; since N ∝ (V - Ia Ra) / Φ, the motor accelerates dangerously until protective devices trip."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does the electromagnetic torque of a DC series motor vary with armature current before and after magnetic core saturation?","options":["Proportional to Ia^2 before saturation, and directly proportional to Ia after saturation","Directly proportional to Ia before saturation, and proportional to Ia^2 after saturation","Constant at all currents","Inversely proportional to Ia"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Torque T ∝ Φ Ia; in the linear region Φ ∝ Ia giving T ∝ Ia^2, whereas after saturation Φ is constant so T ∝ Ia."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Under what condition does a DC motor develop maximum gross mechanical power for a fixed supply voltage V and armature resistance Ra?","options":["When back EMF Eb = V","When back EMF Eb = 0","When back EMF Eb = V / 2","When armature current Ia = 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mechanical power Pm = Eb Ia = (V Ia - Ia^2 Ra); differentiating with respect to Ia and setting to zero yields Eb = V / 2."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why does a self-excited DC shunt generator fail to build up voltage if its prime mover rotates it in the reverse direction while keeping field connections unchanged?","options":["Residual magnetism disappears permanently when rotated backward","The small EMF induced by residual magnetism drives field current in a direction that opposes and wipes out the residual flux","Commutator segments open-circuit in reverse","Armature resistance becomes infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reversing rotation reverses the polarity of the residual-induced EMF, sending field current opposite to residual magnetism and preventing voltage buildup."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a Ward-Leonard DC motor drive system, how are speeds below base speed and above base speed obtained respectively?","options":["Armature voltage control (constant torque) below base speed, and field weakening (constant power) above base speed","Field weakening below base speed, and armature voltage control above base speed","Series resistance control for both ranges","Supply frequency variation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Varying generator voltage controls armature voltage at constant full flux below base speed; weakening motor field extends speed above base speed."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Between a DC shunt motor and a cumulative compound DC motor of the same rating, which one exhibits a greater drop in speed from no-load to full-load, and why?","options":["Shunt motor, because its field current decreases with load","Both have identical speed regulation","Cumulative compound motor, because series field MMF increases net flux as load current rises","Shunt motor, because armature resistance is higher"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a cumulative compound motor, increasing load strengthens total flux (Φsh + Φse), causing speed N ∝ Eb / Φ to droop more than in a shunt motor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"For two identical DC machine armatures having P poles, why is a lap winding chosen over a wave winding for low-voltage, high-current applications?","options":["Lap winding has only 2 parallel paths","Lap winding provides P parallel paths, dividing heavy armature current across more paths","Wave winding cannot use carbon brushes","Lap winding eliminates core losses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A simplex lap winding has A = P parallel paths, multiplying current capacity by P/2 compared to a wave winding (A = 2)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What is the specific purpose of equalizer rings (equalizer connections) in a lap-wound DC generator?","options":["To provide a low-resistance path for circulating currents caused by slight magnetic asymmetry, preventing brush sparking","To convert AC into DC without a commutator","To increase the terminal voltage by 50%","To balance the mechanical weight of the shaft"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equalizer rings connect points of identical theoretical potential in a lap winding so internal AC circulating currents bypass the brushes."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are 'dummy coils' occasionally placed in the armature slots of a wave-wound DC machine?","options":["To double the induced EMF","To improve power factor","To maintain mechanical dynamic balance of the rotor when the slot count does not match the wave-winding formula","To act as damper windings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dummy coils are electrically isolated from the commutator and serve solely to make the armature mechanically symmetrical."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Where do core losses (hysteresis and eddy current losses) predominantly occur in a DC machine, and why?","options":["In the stationary field yoke, because it carries maximum flux","In the rotating armature core, because it experiences cyclic magnetic flux reversals as it passes alternating N and S poles","Equally in the frame and shaft","Only inside the commutator bars"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The main poles and yoke carry steady DC flux, whereas the armature core undergoes continuous magnetic reversals at frequency f = PN / 120."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why can Swinburne's no-load test NOT be performed on a DC series motor?","options":["At no-load, the field flux of a series motor is very weak, causing dangerously high runaway speed","A series motor has no armature winding","A series motor draws zero current at no-load","Swinburne's test requires an AC supply"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because field current equals armature current in a series motor, running it unloaded reduces flux to near zero and accelerates the rotor destructively."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the key advantage of Hopkinson's back-to-back test over Swinburne's test for large DC machines?","options":["It requires only one machine","It tests machines without any electrical power supply","It loads two identical machines to full rated current and temperature rise while drawing only their combined losses from the mains","It measures insulation resistance directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One machine acts as a motor driving the second as a generator feeding back power, simulating full-load thermal and commutation stress economically."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A DC shunt motor drives a constant-torque load at rated voltage. If an external resistor is inserted in series with the armature circuit, what happens to armature current and speed?","options":["Armature current increases and speed increases","Armature current remains unchanged, while back EMF and speed decrease","Armature current drops by half and speed doubles","Both armature current and speed remain unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At constant flux and constant load torque, Ia = T / (k Φ) remains constant; extra Ra drop reduces Eb = V - Ia(Ra + Rext), lowering speed."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"During plugging (reverse-current braking) of a running DC motor, what voltage drives current through the armature circuit right after reversing the armature leads?","options":["The supply voltage V and back EMF Eb act in the same direction, applying nearly (V + Eb) across the armature circuit","The net voltage drops to (V - Eb) / 2","Back EMF immediately reverses polarity to oppose V","Current drops to zero until the rotor stops"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reversing armature terminals while keeping field polarity fixed puts Eb in series aiding V, requiring an external limiting resistor to curb high braking current."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Under what condition does a DC motor enter regenerative braking mode?","options":["When the field winding is short-circuited","When the supply voltage is switched off completely","When an overhauling mechanical load drives the rotor fast enough that induced back EMF Eb exceeds supply voltage V","When brushes are lifted off the commutator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When Eb > V, armature current reverses direction and feeds electrical power back into the DC supply while exerting a braking torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are carbon brushes preferred over copper brushes in DC machines despite having a higher contact voltage drop?","options":["Carbon has zero electrical resistance","High contact resistance limits short-circuit current in coils undergoing commutation, and carbon self-lubricates without scoring the copper commutator","Copper brushes cannot carry DC current","Carbon brushes eliminate the need for interpoles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carbon's higher contact drop helps reverse current smoothly in short-circuited coils during commutation and prevents mechanical wear of the commutator."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the speed of a separately excited DC generator is doubled while keeping its field current constant, how does its hysteresis loss change?","options":["Hysteresis loss doubles","Hysteresis loss quadruples","Hysteresis loss remains unchanged","Hysteresis loss halves"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hysteresis loss Ph ∝ f Bm^1.6; at constant field current, Bm is constant while magnetic reversal frequency f = PN/120 doubles, so Ph doubles."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What happens if a standard DC shunt motor is connected across a single-phase AC supply of the same RMS voltage?","options":["It runs smoothly at synchronous speed","It operates with higher efficiency and zero sparking","High shunt-field inductance limits field current and shifts its phase relative to armature current, producing negligible torque and severe eddy-current heating in the solid yoke","It acts as a step-up transformer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A solid DC yoke suffers massive eddy current heating on AC, and the large phase angle between inductive field current and armature current yields almost zero average torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the outer yoke of a DC machine made of solid cast steel rather than thin laminations, unlike the armature core?","options":["Solid steel has higher electrical resistivity","The yoke carries steady unidirectional DC magnetic flux, so no eddy currents are induced in it during steady operation","Laminations cannot be bent into a circle","To increase magnetic leakage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the main field coils carry constant DC, flux in the yoke is stationary in time, eliminating hysteresis and eddy current losses there."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If a DC shunt generator runs at a speed below its critical speed for a given field circuit resistance, what is the consequence?","options":["The generator fails to build up voltage beyond the tiny residual EMF","The generator produces double its rated voltage","The armature current reverses direction","The commutator overheats immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Below critical speed, the magnetization curve lies entirely below the field resistance line, preventing regenerative voltage buildup."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why can a 3-phase induction motor never attain exact synchronous speed on its own?","options":["Because friction torque is infinite at synchronous speed","At synchronous speed, relative motion between the rotating stator field and rotor conductors is zero, inducing zero rotor EMF and zero torque","Because stator current drops to zero at synchronous speed","Because air-gap flux collapses at synchronous speed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Induction torque depends on relative slip speed (Ns - N); if N = Ns, no flux cuts the rotor bars, so rotor current and torque vanish."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A 3-phase, 50 Hz induction motor is running at a slip s = 0.04 when two of its stator supply lines are suddenly interchanged for plugging. What is the rotor current frequency at the instant of reversal?","options":["2.0 Hz","48.0 Hz","98.0 Hz","100.0 Hz"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reversing the stator field gives a plugging slip s' = 2 - s = 2 - 0.04 = 1.96, so rotor frequency fr = s' × f = 1.96 × 50 = 98.0 Hz."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What is the effect of adding external resistance to the rotor circuit of a 3-phase slip-ring induction motor?","options":["Starting torque increases and maximum torque shifts to a higher slip, while the peak value of maximum torque remains unchanged","Maximum torque increases in direct proportion to rotor resistance","Running efficiency at full load improves","Starting current increases sharply"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maximum torque Tmax = k V^2 / (2 X20) is independent of R2, whereas the slip at maximum torque (sm = R2 / X20) increases with R2, boosting starting torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In a 3-phase induction motor operating at slip s, what is the exact ratio of rotor copper loss to gross mechanical power developed?","options":["s : 1","s : (1 - s)","(1 - s) : s","s^2 : (1 - s)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotor air-gap power Pg, mechanical power Pm = (1 - s)Pg, and rotor copper loss Pcu = s Pg follow the ratio Pg : Pm : Pcu = 1 : (1 - s) : s."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the radial air gap between stator and rotor kept as small as mechanically permissible in a 3-phase induction motor?","options":["To minimize magnetizing current and achieve a higher operating power factor","To reduce windage noise","To increase leakage reactance","To increase rotor resistance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Air has high magnetic reluctance; a smaller air gap reduces the magnetizing reactive power needed to establish working flux, improving power factor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does skewing the rotor slots in a squirrel-cage induction motor improve its performance?","options":["It doubles the starting current","It increases core iron loss","It prevents cogging (magnetic locking), suppresses slot harmonics, and reduces magnetic hum","It allows direct connection of external resistors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Skewing rotor bars smoothly averages out slot reluctance variations, eliminating magnetic locking and harmonic torque dips."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"How does a double-cage squirrel-cage induction motor achieve both high starting torque and high full-load running efficiency?","options":["By using permanent magnets in the inner cage","At starting (high rotor frequency), high leakage reactance forces current into the high-resistance outer cage; at running (low slip frequency), current flows mainly in the low-resistance inner cage","By switching off the outer cage via a centrifugal switch","By doubling the supply voltage during starting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Frequency-dependent inductive reactance (X2 = 2π fr L2) shifts current from the high-resistance outer bar at 50 Hz to the low-resistance inner bar at 1–3 Hz."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Compared to Direct-On-Line (DOL) starting, what are the starting line current and starting torque of a 3-phase induction motor using a Star-Delta starter?","options":["Both starting line current and starting torque are reduced to 1/3 (33.3%) of DOL values","Starting current is 1/√3 and starting torque is 1/3","Starting current is 1/3 and starting torque is 1/√3","Both are reduced to 1/2 (50%)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Star connection, phase voltage is V_L / √3, reducing phase current by 1/√3 and line current by (1/√3)^2 = 1/3; torque also scales as V_ph^2 = 1/3."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"An autotransformer starter set at a 60% voltage tap is used to start a 3-phase induction motor. What percentage of the full-voltage DOL starting torque is developed?","options":["60%","77.5%","36%","16.7%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both starting line current drawn from the supply and starting torque scale as the square of the tapping ratio: x^2 = (0.6)^2 = 0.36 (36%)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why can a 3-phase induction motor operate stably only in the slip region between s = 0 and the breakdown slip s = sm?","options":["Because beyond sm, rotor frequency becomes zero","In the region 0 < s < sm, an increase in load torque slows the rotor slightly, which increases developed motor torque to restore equilibrium","Because core losses become negative beyond sm","Because stator current is zero for s > sm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Stability requires dT_motor / dN < dT_load / dN (i.e., dT_motor / ds > 0), which holds on the low-slip side of the torque-slip peak."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What happens when a 3-phase induction machine connected to an infinite AC bus is driven above synchronous speed (negative slip) by a prime mover?","options":["It operates as an induction generator, supplying active power to the grid while still absorbing reactive magnetizing power from the grid","It operates as a synchronous condenser supplying leading VARs","It stops immediately due to magnetic braking","It supplies both active and lagging reactive power independently"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At negative slip (N > Ns), active power reverses into the grid, but the induction machine still requires external lagging VARs to maintain its air-gap field."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why must a bank of capacitors be connected across the terminals of a standalone (self-excited) induction generator?","options":["To step up the output frequency","To filter out DC offset","To supply the lagging magnetizing reactive power needed to establish and maintain the rotating air-gap magnetic field","To limit short-circuit fault current"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without a grid connection or DC field winding, an induction generator relies on terminal capacitors to furnish its magnetizing VARs."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the supply voltage to a running 3-phase induction motor drops by 10% while driving a constant-torque load, by approximately what percentage does its maximum (breakdown) torque decrease?","options":["10%","19%","25%","5%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maximum torque Tmax ∝ V^2; a 10% voltage drop reduces V to 0.9 V, so Tmax becomes (0.9)^2 = 0.81 (a 19% drop)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the primary objective of maintaining a constant V/f ratio during variable-frequency speed control of an induction motor below base speed?","options":["To keep the air-gap magnetic flux constant, preserving rated torque capability and preventing core saturation","To keep rotor copper loss zero","To maintain unity power factor at all loads","To double the synchronous speed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Since induced EMF E ≈ 4.44 f N Φ, keeping V/f constant holds mutual flux Φ constant across frequencies."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When a VFD operates an induction motor above its rated base frequency while holding stator voltage constant at rated value (field weakening region), how does maximum torque vary with frequency?","options":["Increases linearly with f","Remains constant","Decreases inversely as 1 / f^2","Decreases inversely as 1 / f"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since Φ ∝ V/f decreases as 1/f and Tmax ∝ Φ^2, the breakdown torque drops off as 1 / f^2 in the field-weakening zone."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If one of the three supply fuses blows while a 3-phase induction motor is running at 75% load (single-phasing), what happens?","options":["The motor stops instantaneously","The motor continues running via the forward field component, while the backward field causes severe overheating and ~√3 times higher current in the two healthy lines","The motor accelerates to double speed","Power factor improves to unity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Single-phase stator excitation splits into forward and backward rotating fields; the forward field keeps the motor running, while current rises dangerously in the remaining phases."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"The blocked-rotor test performed on a 3-phase induction motor is electrically equivalent to which test on a power transformer?","options":["Short-circuit test","Open-circuit test","Polarity test","Sumpner's back-to-back test"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With the rotor locked (s = 1, zero mechanical output), reduced voltage circulates rated current to measure total copper losses and leakage impedance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why is the blocked-rotor test on large deep-bar squirrel-cage induction motors often performed at a reduced frequency (10–15 Hz) rather than rated 50 Hz?","options":["To avoid core saturation","To reduce transformer oil temperature","At 50 Hz, skin effect in deep rotor bars artificially inflates apparent rotor resistance compared to normal running slip frequency (1–3 Hz)","Because wattmeters do not work at 50 Hz"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Testing at lower frequency mirrors low running rotor frequencies, giving an accurate value of normal operating rotor resistance."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"During starting, a squirrel-cage induction motor runs stably at nearly one-seventh of its synchronous speed with loud humming. Which phenomenon is occurring?","options":["Cogging","Crawling caused by the 7th space harmonic forward-rotating field","Single-phasing","Regenerative braking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 7th space harmonic creates a forward-rotating field at Ns/7 whose torque peak creates a stable saddle point at ~Ns/7."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are the number of stator slots and rotor slots in an induction motor never chosen to be equal or exact integer multiples?","options":["To avoid cogging (magnetic locking), where stator and rotor teeth align at minimum reluctance and refuse to start","To reduce copper weight","To prevent overheating of bearings","To eliminate the need for a starter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equal stator and rotor tooth counts create strong reluctance alignment torque at standstill, locking the rotor against starting."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the rotor slip rings of a 3-phase wound-rotor induction motor are left open-circuited and rated 3-phase voltage is applied to the stator, what happens?","options":["The motor runs at full synchronous speed","The motor burns out immediately due to dead short circuit","No rotor current can flow, so zero electromagnetic torque is produced and the rotor stays stationary like an unloaded transformer","The rotor rotates in the reverse direction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With an open rotor circuit, the stator draws only magnetizing current and induces EMF across the slip rings without producing torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the direction of rotation of a 3-phase induction motor reversed?","options":["By reversing the neutral connection","By interchanging any two of the three supply phase leads connected to the stator","By increasing the supply frequency","By adding resistance to the rotor circuit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Swapping any two stator phase leads reverses the phase sequence (ABC to ACB), reversing the direction of the rotating magnetic field."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the no-load magnetizing current of a 3-phase induction motor (25–40% of rated current) much larger than that of a transformer (2–5%)?","options":["Because the rotating magnetic field must cross a high-reluctance air gap between stator and rotor","Because induction motors use cast-iron cores","Because stator windings have higher resistance","Because slip is zero at no-load"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even a small radial air gap has thousands of times higher reluctance than silicon steel, demanding much larger magnetizing ampere-turns."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which set of laboratory tests is required to construct the circle diagram of a 3-phase induction motor?","options":["Open-circuit test and polarity test","Swinburne's test and Hopkinson's test","No-load test, blocked-rotor test, and stator winding resistance measurement","Zero power factor test and slip test"],"answer":2,"explanation":"No-load and blocked-rotor tests establish the no-load and short-circuit current phasors, while stator resistance separates stator and rotor copper losses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Neglecting mechanical friction and windage losses, what is the rotor efficiency of a 3-phase induction motor operating at slip s?","options":["s","1 - s","1 / (1 - s)","(1 - s) / s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gross mechanical power output Pm = (1 - s) Pg, so rotor efficiency Pm / Pg equals 1 - s."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are semi-closed slots preferred over completely open slots in induction motor stators and rotors?","options":["To reduce effective air-gap reluctance (lowering magnetizing current) and minimize tooth-pulsation core losses","To make coil insertion easier","To increase leakage reactance","To allow higher ambient operating temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Narrow slot openings keep the air-gap flux distribution smoother, cutting magnetizing current and surface eddy losses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What symptoms appear when one or two rotor bars develop open-circuit cracks in a squirrel-cage induction motor?","options":["Speed increases above synchronous speed","Power factor becomes unity","Excessive vibration, reduced torque, higher slip, and rhythmic pulsation in stator line current","Stator current drops to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Broken rotor bars create rotor asymmetry, generating backward-revolving rotor fields that modulate stator current at 2sf frequency."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two 3-phase induction motors having 4 poles and 6 poles are connected in cumulative cascade on a 50 Hz supply. What is the synchronous speed of the cascaded set?","options":["1500 rpm","600 rpm","1000 rpm","3000 rpm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In cumulative cascade, equivalent poles Peq = P1 + P2 = 4 + 6 = 10, giving synchronous speed Ns = (120 × 50) / 10 = 600 rpm."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How do Kramer and Scherbius drive systems control the speed of large slip-ring induction motors efficiently?","options":["By injecting variable voltage and frequency slip power into the rotor circuit instead of wasting it as heat in external resistors","By varying the number of stator poles mechanically","By inserting saturable reactors in the stator","By switching the stator between Star and Delta"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Slip-energy recovery systems recover or inject slip power through auxiliary converters rather than dissipating s·Pg in resistors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is the rotor core of a 3-phase induction motor laminated just like the stator core?","options":["To reduce rotor weight","To increase starting current","During starting and high-slip operation, rotor frequency is high enough to cause substantial hysteresis and eddy current losses in the rotor iron","To improve mechanical shaft stiffness"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At standstill and acceleration, rotor flux alternates at near-supply frequency, requiring thin silicon-steel laminations to curb iron losses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nature of armature reaction in a synchronous generator supplying a purely inductive (zero power factor lagging) load?","options":["Purely cross-magnetizing","Purely demagnetizing, directly opposing the main field flux and causing a sharp drop in terminal voltage","Purely magnetizing, raising terminal voltage","Zero armature reaction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At 90° lagging current, armature MMF aligns directly opposite to the rotor field poles, weakening net air-gap flux."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What is the nature of armature reaction in an alternator supplying a purely capacitive (zero power factor leading) load?","options":["Purely magnetizing, aiding the main field flux and causing terminal voltage to rise above no-load EMF","Purely demagnetizing","Purely cross-magnetizing","Zero armature reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At 90° leading current, armature MMF acts in the exact same direction as the main rotor poles, boosting air-gap flux and terminal voltage."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"In a synchronous generator operating at unity power factor, how does armature reaction affect the main field flux?","options":["It completely cancels the main flux","It doubles the main flux","It is cross-magnetizing, distorting the flux distribution across pole faces without significantly changing net flux per pole","It has no effect on flux distribution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At unity power factor, armature MMF is 90° out of phase with main field MMF, crowding flux toward the trailing pole tips."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why is the Synchronous Impedance (EMF) method termed the 'pessimistic method' for predetermining alternator voltage regulation?","options":["It neglects armature resistance completely","It uses an unsaturated synchronous reactance larger than the normal operating saturated value, predicting a larger voltage drop (worse regulation) than actual","It assumes zero leakage flux","It applies only to capacitive loads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because Xs obtained from unsaturated OCC and SCC is higher than saturated Xs under load, calculated voltage regulation is overestimated."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why is the Ampere-Turn (MMF) method called the 'optimistic method' for calculating alternator voltage regulation?","options":["It treats leakage reactance drop as an equivalent armature reaction MMF using unsaturated data, yielding a smaller (more favorable) regulation than actual","It uses saturated synchronous reactance","It ignores field excitation completely","It gives infinite voltage regulation at unity power factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By replacing leakage impedance drop with an extra MMF on the unsaturated characteristic, it underestimates the actual voltage drop."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which two internal parameters of a synchronous generator are separated using the Potier triangle constructed from OCC and Zero Power Factor (ZPF) tests?","options":["Hysteresis loss and eddy current loss","Stator resistance and rotor resistance","Armature leakage reactance (Xl) and armature reaction MMF","Direct-axis reactance and quadrature-axis reactance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Potier triangle accurately isolates true armature leakage reactance drop from demagnetizing armature reaction MMF."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What does a high Short-Circuit Ratio (SCR) indicate about the design and performance of a synchronous generator?","options":["High synchronous reactance, small air gap, and poor steady-state stability","Low synchronous reactance, larger air gap, larger physical size and cost, lower voltage regulation, and higher steady-state stability","Inability to operate in parallel","Zero armature reaction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since SCR ≈ 1 / Xs(pu), a high SCR requires a wider air gap and more copper/iron, improving stability and voltage regulation."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are high-speed turbo-alternators constructed with cylindrical (non-salient) rotors of small diameter and long axial length?","options":["To withstand extreme centrifugal mechanical stresses at 3000 rpm and minimize windage losses","To accommodate 40 or more poles","To maximize reluctance torque","To eliminate the need for DC excitation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At 3000 rpm (2-pole, 50 Hz), projecting poles would suffer destructive centrifugal forces and high aerodynamic windage drag."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are slow-speed hydro-alternators built with salient-pole rotors of large diameter and short axial length?","options":["To run at 3000 rpm","To maximize windage cooling","Low water-turbine speeds require a large number of poles (P = 120f/N), requiring a large rotor periphery to house them","To eliminate damper windings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because hydraulic turbines run slowly (100–300 rpm), many poles are needed to generate 50 Hz, dictating a large-diameter rim."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In Blondel's two-reaction theory for salient-pole synchronous machines, why is direct-axis synchronous reactance (Xd) always greater than quadrature-axis reactance (Xq)?","options":["Because the quadrature axis has more winding turns","Because the magnetic path along the direct (pole) axis has a much smaller air gap and lower reluctance than the interpolar quadrature axis","Because frequency is higher along the direct axis","Because damper windings are placed only on the quadrature axis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lower magnetic reluctance along the pole axis produces higher flux per ampere, making Xd > Xq."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why can an unloaded or lightly loaded salient-pole synchronous motor continue running at synchronous speed even if its DC field excitation is accidentally lost?","options":["Due to reluctance torque produced by the difference between direct-axis and quadrature-axis reactances (Xd ≠ Xq)","Because it converts into an induction motor at zero slip","Because residual magnetism supplies full rated torque","Because eddy currents lock the rotor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Salient-pole reluctance torque P_rel = [V^2 (Xd - Xq) / (2 Xd Xq)] sin(2δ) holds the rotor in synchronism for light loads without DC excitation."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When a synchronous generator connected to an infinite bus has its DC field excitation increased while keeping mechanical turbine input constant, what changes?","options":["Active power output increases and speed rises","Active power output decreases to zero","Active power (kW) remains constant, while the generator supplies more lagging reactive power (kVAR) at a lower lagging power factor","The generator absorbs leading reactive power"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Active power is governed by prime-mover mechanical torque; increasing excitation raises Ef, pushing lagging reactive current into the bus."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If the mechanical steam or water input to an alternator connected to an infinite bus is increased without changing its DC field excitation, what happens?","options":["Frequency of the infinite bus increases","Rotor load angle δ increases, active power delivered to the grid increases, and average speed remains locked at synchronous speed","Reactive power output doubles while active power stays constant","The alternator trips on over-voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extra mechanical torque advances the rotor phase angle δ ahead of the bus voltage, increasing active power transfer P = (Ef V / Xs) sin δ."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How does an over-excited synchronous motor running at no-load behave when connected across industrial AC busbars?","options":["It draws leading reactive current, acting as a synchronous condenser to improve plant power factor","It draws heavy lagging current","It acts as a step-down transformer","It consumes huge active power"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When Ef > V, the synchronous motor draws a current leading the supply voltage by nearly 90°, supplying leading VARs like a capacitor bank."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On a synchronous motor's V-curve (armature current Ia plotted against field current If at constant shaft load), what is the power factor at the minimum point of the curve?","options":["Zero power factor lagging","0.8 power factor leading","Unity power factor (1.0)","0.5 power factor lagging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At normal excitation, reactive current component is zero, so total armature current Ia is purely active and reaches its minimum value at unity power factor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What happens if the mechanical shaft load on a synchronous motor is increased beyond its maximum pull-out torque limit?","options":["The motor runs smoothly at a lower steady speed","The rotor loses magnetic locking with the rotating stator field and stalls (pulls out of step)","The motor automatically switches to induction mode","The armature current drops to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beyond load angle δ = 90°, electromagnetic torque decreases with angle, breaking synchronism and stalling the motor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why do damper (amortisseur) windings in a synchronous motor carry zero current during steady-state operation at constant load?","options":["Because the rotor revolves at exact synchronous speed, resulting in zero relative motion between the rotating field and damper bars","Because damper bars are made of insulating fiber","Because they are disconnected by a centrifugal switch","Because supply frequency is zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At synchronous speed (N = Ns), no flux lines cut the damper bars, so no EMF or current is induced in them."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"How do damper windings suppress 'hunting' (rotor phase oscillations) in a synchronous machine following a sudden load change?","options":["By increasing DC field current automatically","By acting as a brake only at zero speed","Whenever rotor speed oscillates above or below Ns, relative flux cutting induces currents in the damper bars that produce induction torque opposing the speed deviation","By short-circuiting the stator phases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By Lenz's law, induced damper currents generate positive motoring torque when N < Ns and braking torque when N > Ns, damping oscillations."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Which four conditions must be satisfied before closing the synchronizing breaker to parallel an incoming 3-phase alternator with live busbars?","options":["Equal kVA rating, equal resistance, equal power factor, and equal temperature","Same voltage magnitude, same frequency, same phase sequence, and zero phase angle difference","Equal prime mover type, equal pole count, equal shaft diameter, and equal speed","Zero load current, maximum excitation, unity power factor, and closed neutral"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Matching voltage magnitude, frequency, phase sequence, and phase alignment prevents large transient circulating currents and shaft shock."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"In the three-dark-lamp method of synchronizing a 3-phase alternator, if all three lamps brighten and darken simultaneously rather than sequentially, what does this indicate?","options":["The phase sequence is correct, but there is a slight frequency difference between the incoming machine and the busbars","The phase sequence is reversed","The incoming voltage is zero","The synchronization is already complete and the breaker can be closed at any instant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Simultaneous brightening and dimming proves identical phase sequence, with beat frequency equal to |f_bus - f_incoming|."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"During dark-lamp synchronization of a 3-phase alternator, if the three lamps light up and go dark one after another in a rotating sequence, what must be done?","options":["Increase field excitation","Interchange any two phase leads of the incoming alternator to correct its phase sequence","Close the breaker when the middle lamp is brightest","Increase turbine speed by 50%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sequential flickering means opposite phase sequence; swapping any two leads aligns the sequence so all lamps dim together."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What are the two main reasons for using fractional-pitch (short-pitched) and distributed stator windings in AC alternators?","options":["To increase synchronous reactance and core loss","To suppress space harmonics (producing a near-sinusoidal EMF waveform) and save end-connection copper","To eliminate the need for cooling air","To allow operation on DC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distributing coils across slots and short-pitching them reduces harmonic pitch and distribution factors, purifying the output sine wave."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why is a coil span equal to 2/3 of the full pole pitch (120° electrical, i.e., chording angle α = 60°) frequently chosen in 3-phase alternators?","options":["It completely eliminates the 3rd harmonic EMF because pitch factor kp3 = cos(3 × 60° / 2) = cos(90°) = 0","It eliminates the fundamental frequency","It doubles the terminal voltage","It eliminates all even harmonics only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For the nth harmonic, pitch factor kp = cos(n α / 2); choosing α = 60° makes kp = 0 for all triplen harmonics (3rd, 9th, 15th)."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"At what load angle δ is the synchronizing power coefficient (Psy = dP/dδ) of an alternator connected to an infinite bus at its maximum stiffness?","options":["δ = 90°","δ = 45°","δ = 0° (no-load), decreasing to zero at the steady-state stability limit δ = 90°","δ = 180°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since P = (Ef V / Xs) sin δ, synchronizing power coefficient Psy = (Ef V / Xs) cos δ is maximum at δ = 0° and vanishes at δ = 90°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is a conventional 3-phase synchronous motor not inherently self-starting when connected directly to a 3-phase AC supply?","options":["Because its stator winding has infinite resistance","Because rotor inertia prevents the heavy rotor from accelerating to synchronous speed within one half-cycle (10 ms at 50 Hz) before the stator poles reverse","Because its air gap is zero","Because DC excitation blocks AC current"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alternating forward and backward torque impulses reverse every half-cycle, leaving the high-inertia rotor stationary with zero net starting torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"According to Double Revolving Field Theory, if a single-phase induction motor runs at slip s in the forward direction, what is its slip with respect to the backward-rotating field?","options":["2 - s","1 - s","s - 1","2 + s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Relative to the backward field rotating at -Ns, rotor speed +N = (1 - s)Ns gives backward slip sb = [-Ns - (1 - s)Ns] / (-Ns) = 2 - s."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What causes the double-frequency (2f) mechanical torque pulsation inherent in all single-phase induction motors?","options":["Brush friction on slip rings","Core saturation in the shaft","Interaction between the forward and backward rotating air-gap magnetic fields moving at 2Ns relative to each other","Unbalanced cooling fan blades"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The counter-rotating forward and backward flux waves cross each other twice per cycle (100 Hz on 50 Hz mains), generating a 2f pulsating torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How is the starting phase split achieved in a resistance split-phase single-phase induction motor?","options":["By using a permanent magnet rotor","By winding the auxiliary starting winding with finer wire (high R/X ratio) and the main winding deeply in slots (low R/X ratio), creating a 25°–30° current phase difference","By connecting a step-down transformer in the rotor","By using a shaded pole ring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unequal R/X ratios displace the auxiliary and main winding currents in time phase, producing a weak rotating field for starting."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why must a centrifugal switch disconnect the auxiliary winding of a capacitor-start motor once it reaches about 75% of synchronous speed?","options":["Because the thin auxiliary winding and electrolytic starting capacitor are short-time rated and would overheat if left energized continuously","Because the motor reverses direction above 75% speed","To save reactive power at no-load","Because the main winding turns on only after 75% speed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"AC electrolytic capacitors have high dielectric losses under continuous duty and are designed solely for brief starting pulses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"How can the direction of rotation of a capacitor-start single-phase induction motor be reversed?","options":["By interchanging the two incoming AC mains supply wires","By reversing both main and auxiliary windings simultaneously","By reversing the connections of either the main winding OR the auxiliary winding (but not both)","By increasing the capacitance value"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reversing one winding reverses the spatial phase sequence of the two orthogonal currents, reversing the rotating magnetic field."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why is it impossible to reverse the direction of rotation of a standard shaded-pole single-phase induction motor electrically?","options":["Because it runs on DC only","Because the direction of field sweep is fixed physically from the unshaded pole section toward the copper-shaded pole section","Because its rotor is locked permanently","Because it has no stator winding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Induced eddy currents in the copper shading band delay flux buildup in the shaded portion, always sweeping flux from unshaded to shaded side."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why does an AC series (universal) motor exhibit a lower power factor and more brush sparking when operated on AC compared to DC of the same voltage?","options":["Due to inductive reactance voltage drop in the series windings and transformer EMF induced in the short-circuited coils undergoing commutation","Because rotor speed is zero on AC","Because carbon brushes melt on AC","Because hysteresis loss disappears on AC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alternating main flux induces a transformer EMF in coils short-circuited by the brushes, and series inductance lowers power factor."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"How is transformer-induced EMF sparking minimized in large single-phase AC series traction motors?","options":["By removing the commutator","By using solid unlaminated cores","By using compensating windings, high-resistance brushes, fewer turns per coil, and low supply frequency (16.67 Hz or 25 Hz)","By increasing the air gap to 10 cm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since transformer EMF Et = 4.44 f N Φm, lowering frequency and turns per coil directly cuts commutating brush sparking."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"On what physical mechanism does a hysteresis motor accelerate to and lock at exact synchronous speed without DC excitation or salient poles?","options":["Reluctance torque from deep rotor slots","Hysteresis torque caused by magnetic flux in the hard-steel rotor cylinder lagging behind the rotating stator field","Eddy current braking","Centrifugal clutch locking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High magnetic retentivity in the chrome/cobalt steel rotor creates a constant angular lag between rotor magnetization and stator field, yielding steady torque up to Ns."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are hysteresis motors widely used in precision audio turntables, recording equipment, and electric clocks?","options":["Because their smooth, slotless rotor produces completely silent, vibration-free operation at exact synchronous speed","Because they deliver the highest horsepower per kilogram","Because they have the highest starting current","Because they run at 10,000 rpm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Absence of rotor teeth/slots eliminates magnetic slot ripple and acoustic noise while maintaining rock-solid synchronous speed."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"A variable-reluctance stepper motor has 8 stator poles and 6 rotor teeth. What is its step angle per pulse?","options":["7.5°","10.0°","15.0°","30.0°"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Step angle θs = [(Ns - Nr) / (Ns × Nr)] × 360° = [(8 - 6) / (8 × 6)] × 360° = (2 / 48) × 360° = 15.0°."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"What enables a Permanent Magnet (PM) stepper motor to hold its shaft position against external torque even when phase currents are switched off?","options":["Mechanical friction brake","Detent torque resulting from magnetic attraction between the permanent-magnet rotor and stator teeth","Eddy current damping","Spring return mechanism"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Even with zero coil excitation, the permanent magnet rotor aligns with minimum-reluctance stator teeth, providing holding detent torque."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"If a transformer rated for 220 V, 50 Hz is connected to a 220 V, 60 Hz AC supply, how do its core flux density and core loss behave?","options":["Maximum flux density decreases, reducing core saturation, magnetizing current, and core loss","Maximum flux density increases by 20%, causing severe core saturation","Core loss doubles immediately","Secondary voltage increases by 20%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Since Bm ∝ V / f, raising frequency from 50 Hz to 60 Hz at constant voltage lowers Bm by 16.7%, reducing core losses."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Conversely, what happens if a transformer designed for 220 V, 60 Hz is operated on a 220 V, 50 Hz supply?","options":["Core losses decrease by 20%","Efficiency reaches 100%","Maximum flux density increases by 20%, driving the core into saturation and sharply increasing magnetizing current and core heating","Secondary voltage drops by 20%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lowering frequency at constant voltage increases Bm by 60/50 = 1.2 times, pushing the core past the knee point into heavy saturation."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why does a Potential Transformer (PT) operate with its secondary virtually open-circuited, whereas a Current Transformer (CT) operates with its secondary virtually short-circuited?","options":["PT cannot withstand high impedance; CT cannot withstand low impedance","PT must draw minimal current to keep voltage drop tiny, whereas CT must circulate secondary current to produce opposing MMF and prevent core saturation","Both operate identically","To double the secondary frequency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A PT acts as a parallel voltage sensor (high-Z burden), while a CT acts as a series current sensor requiring low-Z burden to keep core flux small."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Two single-phase transformers of equal kVA and voltage rating have equivalent impedances of (1 + j4) Ω and (2 + j3) Ω respectively. If connected in parallel, how do they share a common load?","options":["They share equal kVA (since both have impedance magnitude √17 Ω), but operate at different power factors due to unequal X/R ratios","One carries 100% of the load and the other carries zero","A destructive short-circuit current flows at no-load","They share both kVA and kW identically"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equal |Z| = √(1^2 + 4^2) = √(2^2 + 3^2) = √17 Ω divides apparent power equally, while different impedance angles shift their individual power factors."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Normal","q":"Why are disc-type windings preferred over simple crossover windings in high-voltage, high-MVA power transformers?","options":["They eliminate the need for a steel core","They require no insulating oil","They provide superior mechanical strength against short-circuit forces and better radial/axial cooling oil ducts","They reduce the turns ratio to unity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Continuous interleaved disc coils resist axial/radial electromagnetic fault stresses and allow unobstructed convective oil cooling."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"What is the function of a static shielding ring (stress-control ring) placed at the line end of an EHV power transformer winding?","options":["To improve power factor","To equalize the initial capacitive voltage distribution across the end turns during steep lightning impulse surges","To measure oil temperature","To reduce no-load humming noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During fast lightning transients, winding capacitance dominates; the shield ring prevents excessive voltage concentration across the first few turns."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"Why is the neutral of a power transformer sometimes grounded through a resistance or arc-suppression (Petersen) coil rather than solidly grounded?","options":["To limit single line-to-ground fault currents and extinguish transient arcing grounds without immediate service interruption","To double the line-to-line voltage","To increase third-harmonic circulation","To eliminate the need for lightning arresters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impedance grounding curbs destructive earth-fault current magnitudes while stabilizing neutral-to-earth potential."},
{"subject":"Electrical Machines","difficulty":"Hard","q":"When a large 3-phase induction motor is started Direct-On-Line on a weak distribution feeder, why does the feeder voltage dip noticeably?","options":["Because the motor draws purely capacitive current at starting","Because at standstill (s = 1), rotor impedance is very low, drawing 5 to 7 times rated current at a very low lagging power factor (0.15–0.25)","Because supply frequency drops to 25 Hz","Because stator resistance becomes infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High starting current lagging by ~75°–80° creates a large reactive voltage drop (I X sin φ) across the feeder's inductive reactance.."}];